ATSWA.put("AUD", {"intro":"Principles of Auditing & Assurance is the regulatory and procedural core of ATSWA Part III: what an audit is and why it exists, the legal/regulatory framework and ethical code that govern it, how an audit is planned, the evidence and sampling procedures that back an opinion, internal control, public sector assurance, IT-based audit techniques, and how an audit is reported and closed out. The twelve chapters move from first principles through the full life of an audit engagement — appointment, planning, fieldwork, evidence, completion and reporting — with the CAMA/BOFIA/Insurance Act statutory framework and ICAN's own Code of Ethics treated as first-class content throughout, not an afterthought.","chapters":[{"n":1,"t":"Nature, Purpose, and Scope of Auditing and Assurance","brief":"What an audit actually is, why it exists, and the vocabulary the rest of this subject is built on: the CAMA-based legal obligation to audit, the formal definition of auditing and its key terms, the objectives/general principles/scope of an audit, reasonable assurance and audit risk, the advantages/disadvantages/limitations of audit, the audit expectation gap, how an audit firm is structured, the different types of audit, the meaning of assurance, the eight-stage chronology of an audit, agency theory, and the audit committee.","outcomes":["Explain the purpose and types of audits","Understand in general terms what an audit is and put into context some basic audit techniques","Recognise that there are theoretical considerations underlying audit practice","Outline and explain the main stages of an audit","Outline the functions of an audit committee"],"secs":[{"n":"1.1","t":"Introduction","b":[{"p":"Managers of businesses and organisations prepare accounts to communicate how the entities have been managed within a given period — the accounts (financial statements) are the end product of the managers' **stewardship function**. An unqualified, favourable, or \"clean\" audit report communicates to stakeholders that their business interests have been managed well and that they can rely on the picture the financial statements portray."},{"key":"Auditing is a very onerous and demanding task, requiring a high sense of competence, integrity, skill, experience and technical knowledge — auditors must gather appropriate and sufficient audit evidence to make informed judgments about a whole range of business situations, which is only possible if they understand how organisations operate and the environment they operate in. Auditing is therefore a practical, real-life subject, not an abstract one."}]},{"n":"1.2","t":"Historical background","b":[{"p":"The evolution of auditing dates back to the medieval ages. In ancient Egypt and Rome, people were employed to review the work done by tax collectors and estate managers. In medieval Britain, an independent person was employed by the Feudal Barons to ensure returns from tenant farmers accurately reflected revenues received from the estates."},{"note":"In those eras, the emphasis was purely on the **detection of fraud and irregularities**. Auditing has since developed into a far more demanding and sophisticated discipline, shifting its central purpose toward forming an opinion on the truth and fairness of financial statements as a whole — fraud detection is now only a by-product of that wider process (see §1.6.1 and §1.18)."}]},{"n":"1.3","t":"Auditing today","b":[{"p":"Wherever a fiduciary relationship with financial implications exists, there is a need for a knowledgeable outsider to independently and objectively review the accounts of stewardship and express an opinion on their integrity."},{"def":{"t":"Account of stewardship","d":"within a company, the record of how the directors have dealt with the investments or assets of the company on behalf of the shareholders — summarised in the financial statements (Statement of Profit or Loss and Other Comprehensive Income, Statement of Financial Position, Statement of Changes in Equity, Statement of Cash Flow, Statement of Value Added, and the Directors' Report)."}},{"key":"Auditing today is rooted in the **separation of ownership from management** — shareholders own the company but are not involved in day-to-day decisions made by the board of directors, creating an urgent need to safeguard their interests."},{"p":"In Nigeria, the **Companies and Allied Matters Act (CAMA) 2020** makes it an obligation for every public limited company (Plc) to have its annual financial statements audited before circulating them to members. Section 400(1) states: \"Every Company shall at each general meeting appoint an auditor or auditors to audit the financial statements of the company...\" Other West African countries have similar legislation making the audit of public companies' financial statements a mandatory requirement."},{"p":"The appointed auditor — who must be a member of a recognised professional accountancy body, duly licensed to practise, and not disqualified by any legislation — is expected to express an opinion on the truth and fairness of the Statement of Profit or Loss and Other Comprehensive Income and the Statement of Financial Position, both prepared by the directors and presented to shareholders at the annual general meeting."}]},{"n":"1.4","t":"Definition of auditing","b":[{"def":{"t":"Auditing","d":"a process, carried out by an appointed qualified person or body, whereby the records and financial statements of an entity are subjected to independent examination in such detail as will enable the auditor to form an opinion as to the truth and fairness of the financial statements."}},{"p":"Every key word and phrase in this definition carries specific meaning:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Term","Meaning"],"rows":[["**Independent examination**","The audit must be conducted independently of the person who prepared the records and financial statements. The auditor's work must never be influenced by the client — an auditor cannot give an unbiased opinion unless independent of all parties involved. The auditor must be independent not only in fact and attitude of mind, but must also be **seen to be** independent in appearance."],["**Appointed qualified person**","The auditor must be professionally qualified, having completed the approved professional accountancy examinations, with the relevant skills, experience and integrity to carry out the examination."],["**Records and financial statements**","Written authorisation to effect transactions, the source documents, the Books of Prime Entry, the Ledgers, the Trial Balance, the Statement of Profit or Loss and OCI, the Statement of Financial Position, the Statement of Changes in Equity, the Statement of Cash Flow, the Statement of Value Added, the Directors' Report, and other reports issued in accordance with current corporate governance practice."],["**Opinion**","The expression of the auditor's professional viewpoint about the financial statements, after exercising judgment about skills and based on the evidence gathered."],["**True and fair** (truth and fairness)","That the financial statements are free from material misstatement; that proper books of accounts have been kept; that the financial statements are consistent with the underlying records; that they have been prepared in accordance with acceptable accounting standards and relevant legislation; that assets and liabilities exist, are properly valued, and pertain to the entity; and that all expenses and revenues stated relate to the period under consideration."]]}},{"key":"\"**Truth**\" and \"**fair**\" are not the same word doing double duty — the study text distinguishes them explicitly. \"Truth\" means the information is factual, conforms to reality with no material errors, and has been correctly extracted from the books and records in line with required standards and law. \"Fair\" means the information is free from discrimination and bias, complies with expected standards and rules, and reflects the **commercial substance** of the company's underlying transactions — a subtly different test from mere factual accuracy."}]},{"n":"1.5","t":"Why the need for an audit","b":[{"p":"Directors are obliged to report to shareholders on how the business has been managed. Shareholders want assurance that this report contains no errors, is not misleading, and discloses all relevant information. The way out of this credibility problem is to appoint an independent person — an auditor — to examine the report and express an opinion on it."},{"p":"Further reasons an audit is needed:"},{"ul":["Modern companies, some spanning political boundaries, prepare complex accounts encompassing various legislations, standards, conventions, policies and control systems — such accounts need independent experts with the skill and experience to examine them;","Financial statements must comply with various legislations and standards, and audit is how that compliance is assured;","Many interested parties — bankers, creditors, employees, potential investors, regulators, government agencies, donors, researchers — use financial statements as a basis for decisions, and an independent audit ensures those statements are objective, free from material misstatement, and relevant to users' needs."]}]},{"n":"1.6","t":"Objective and general principles governing an audit of financial statements","b":[{"h4":"§1.6.1 Objective of an audit"},{"p":"The primary objective of an audit of financial statements is to enable the auditor to express an opinion on whether the financial statements are prepared, in all material respects, in accordance with an identified financial reporting framework, and give \"a true and fair view\" (or \"present fairly, in all material respects\") the financial results and state of affairs of the client entity."},{"warn":"The auditor's opinion enhances the credibility of the financial statements — but the user **cannot** assume the opinion is an assurance as to the entity's future viability, nor of the efficiency or effectiveness of management's conduct. This is one of the audit expectation gap's most common misconceptions (§1.10), so the study text states it explicitly here."},{"p":"The subsidiary objectives of an audit are to:"},{"ul":["detect errors and fraud;","prevent errors and fraud; and","help the client improve their accounting and internal control systems."]},{"key":"A subtle but important distinction: audit is **not designed** to identify errors, detect fraud and discover significant weaknesses — but audit work should be carried out in such a manner as to be **able to** unearth them if they exist. Design intent and incidental capability are different things."},{"h4":"§1.6.2 General principles of an audit"},{"p":"The auditor should comply with the Code of Ethics for Members issued by the International Federation of Accountants. The ethical principles governing the auditor's professional responsibilities are:"},{"ol":["Independence;","Integrity;","Objectivity;","Professional competence and due care;","Confidentiality;","Professional behaviour/conduct; and","Technical standards."]},{"p":"The auditor should conduct an audit in accordance with **International Standards on Auditing (ISAs)**, which contain basic principles and essential procedures together with related explanatory guidance."},{"def":{"t":"Professional scepticism","d":"an attitude that includes a questioning mind, being alert to conditions that may indicate possible misstatement due to error or fraud, and a critical assessment of audit evidence — recognising that circumstances may exist that cause the financial statements to be materially misstated."}},{"note":"Professional scepticism matters throughout the audit process because it reduces the risk of: overlooking suspicious circumstances; over-generalising when drawing conclusions from audit observations; and using faulty assumptions when determining the nature, timing and extent of audit procedures and evaluating their results."},{"key":"In planning and performing an audit, the auditor neither assumes management is dishonest, nor assumes unquestioned honesty — a deliberately neutral starting point. Accordingly, management's representations are **never** a substitute for obtaining sufficient appropriate audit evidence to draw reasonable conclusions."},{"h4":"§1.6.3 Scope of an audit"},{"def":{"t":"Scope of an audit","d":"the audit procedures deemed necessary in the circumstances to achieve the audit's objective — determined by the auditor having regard to the requirements of ISAs, relevant professional bodies, legislation, regulations, and, where appropriate, the terms of the audit engagement and reporting requirements."}},{"h4":"§1.6.4 Reasonable assurance"},{"def":{"t":"Reasonable assurance","d":"a concept relating to the accumulation of audit evidence necessary for the auditor to conclude there are no material misstatements in the financial statements taken as a whole. It relates to the **whole** audit process, and is not the same as absolute assurance."}},{"p":"An auditor cannot obtain **absolute** assurance, because of unavoidable inherent limitations:"},{"ol":["**The use of testing** — the auditor takes samples for testing, rather than working on the whole dataset or total financial records;","**Inherent limitations of internal control** — e.g. the possibility of management override or collusion; and","**The persuasive, not conclusive, nature of most audit evidence.**"]},{"p":"The auditor's work is further permeated by **judgment**, regarding: the gathering of audit evidence (e.g. deciding the nature, timing and extent of audit procedures); the drawing of conclusions from the evidence gathered; and assessing the reasonableness of management's estimates in preparing the financial statements. Other limitations (e.g. related-party transactions) may also affect the persuasiveness of available audit evidence."},{"warn":"Because of all the factors above, **an audit is not a guarantee** that the financial statements are free of material misstatement — this is a deliberate conclusion the study text draws explicitly, tying §1.6.4 directly back to §1.9's limitations of an audit."},{"h4":"§1.6.5 Audit risk and materiality"},{"p":"Entities face a variety of business risks depending on the nature of their operations, industry, regulatory environment, size and complexity. Management is responsible for identifying and responding to such risks, but not all risks relate to the preparation of the financial statements — the auditor is ultimately concerned only with risks that may affect the financial statements."},{"def":{"t":"Audit risk","d":"the risk that the auditor expresses an inappropriate audit opinion when the financial statements are materially misstated."}},{"key":"The auditor **reduces** audit risk (rather than eliminating it) by designing and performing audit procedures to obtain sufficient appropriate audit evidence for drawing reasonable conclusions. Reasonable assurance is obtained precisely when audit risk has been reduced to an acceptably low level — the two concepts (§1.6.4 and §1.6.5) are two sides of the same coin."},{"p":"The auditor is concerned with **material** misstatements and is not responsible for detecting misstatements immaterial to the financial statements as a whole. The auditor considers whether identified uncorrected misstatements — individually and in aggregate — are material to the financial statements taken as a whole."},{"h4":"§1.6.6 Responsibility for the financial statements"},{"key":"While the auditor is responsible for **forming and expressing an opinion** on the financial statements, responsibility for **preparing and fairly presenting** them, in accordance with the applicable financial reporting framework, rests with **management**, with oversight from those charged with governance (the directors). The audit does **not** relieve management or those charged with governance of this responsibility."},{"p":"Directors should keep the financial statements and accounting records at the company's registered office, or any other place in the relevant country the directors deem fit."},{"h4":"§1.6.7 Public sector perspective"},{"p":"Whether an audit is conducted in the private or public sector, the **basic principles of auditing remain the same**. What may differ is the audit objective and scope, often attributable to differences in the audit mandate, legal requirements, or the form of reporting (public sector entities may need additional financial reports). Public sector auditors must also consider specific requirements of any relevant regulations, ordinances or ministerial directives, including national security issues. Public sector audit mandates may be more specific and encompass a wider range of objectives and broader scope than private sector audits, potentially affecting the auditor's discretion in establishing materiality, reporting fraud and error, and the form of the auditor's report — but these differences never amount to a difference in the **basic principles and essential procedures** themselves."}]},{"n":"1.7","t":"Advantages of an audit","b":[{"p":"Audit is carried out to lend credibility to the financial statements concerned and to assure stakeholders of their truth and fairness. Beyond this core purpose, there are further inherent advantages to having accounts audited:"},{"ol":["**Disputes between partners may be largely avoided**, especially where complicated profit-sharing arrangements subsist — independent examination of partnership accounts ensures accurate assessment and division of profits;","**Changes in ownership are facilitated** by unqualified audit reports — e.g. admitting a new partner, or two sole proprietors merging into a partnership, is made easier when previous audited accounts are available for examination;","**Audited financial statements serve as a reliable source of information** for banks and other financial institutions assessing the strength of loan/overdraft applicants;","**Audited accounts, adjusted for tax purposes**, submitted to the State Internal Revenue Service carry greater authority than unaudited accounts;","**The presence of a qualified auditor is useful for other ancillary capacities** — for a company, the auditor can additionally offer taxation, information technology, liquidation and insolvency, and other services (see §1.15 for the fuller list)."]}]},{"n":"1.8","t":"Disadvantages of an audit","b":[{"ol":["**Audit fees add extra financial burden** to businesses — it is believed that, absent a statutory obligation, many companies would not engage external auditors despite the advantages above; this is also why few partnerships and sole proprietors have their accounts audited;","**Auditing burdens the client's staff and management**, who must provide information to the auditors, which they may see as disruptive to their work plan — an external auditor should therefore plan audit work carefully to minimise this perceived disruption."]}]},{"n":"1.9","t":"Limitations of an audit","b":[{"ol":["**Not a guarantee of future viability or management efficiency** — even though the auditor pays attention to both during audit work, neither represents the main purpose of an audit;","**Audit work examines past transactions** and verifies assets/liabilities held at the end of the accounting period — there is no guarantee that what happened in the past will apply to the future;","**Auditors' primary concern is how management's decisions affect the financial statements**, and recommending control-system improvements — auditors do not conduct an extensive review of operational efficiency;","**Auditors express an \"opinion,\" not a certification of complete correctness.** What \"reasonable assurance\" means depends largely on what a user of financial statements expects from the auditor's report. This limitation stems from: (i) it is not practically possible to examine every item within an account balance or class of accounts; and (ii) the possibility of collusion and misrepresentation for fraudulent purposes, and the fact that most audit evidence is **persuasive** (indicating what is probable), not **conclusive** (indicating what is definite)."]}]},{"n":"1.10","t":"The audit expectation gap","b":[{"p":"Many people still hold misconceptions about the auditor's role and what auditing is meant to achieve:"},{"ol":["That auditors' reports are addressed to the **directors** of the company, not to the members (shareholders/owners);","That a **qualified** audit report — indicating the financial statements do not give a true and fair view — is more useful to those who appoint the auditors than an unqualified (clean) report;","That it is the auditor's **duty to detect fraud**, when in fact fraud detection is the responsibility of the directors."]},{"key":"These misconceptions point to the fact that an **\"expectation gap\"** still exists between what auditors actually do, and what the general public/law expects them to do."}]},{"n":"1.11","t":"The structure of an audit firm","b":[{"p":"Audit firms have structured management: partners generally delegate routine management decisions to the senior partner, who reports to the full partnership at regularly convened meetings. A Partnership Secretary/Administrator, reporting to the senior partner, takes responsibility for administration — agenda papers, minutes, accounting/management records, word processing — with these salaries forming part of the firm's overhead."},{"p":"Professional staff and partners maintain accurate time records so all time assigned to client affairs is properly billed at the appropriate hourly rate (itself determined by reference to salaries) — this is how the firm recovers professional staff salary cost and overheads. The **excess of audit fees over these costs** represents partnership profits, shared among partners in their profit-sharing ratios."},{"p":"Professional staff are divided into departments (taxation, audit, consultancy, information technology, accounts preparation services, liquidation, etc.) and into seniority grades. Partners are individually responsible for client service, each designated \"engagement partner,\" while detailed assignment management is the work of a manager, who in turn leads teams of seniors and juniors within the relevant departmental area."},{"p":"To run the firm effectively and profitably, the following essential features must be emphasised:"},{"ul":["Regular communication of progress on audit assignments;","Timely communication of problem areas;","Close supervision of juniors' work by seniors;","Proper planning of work and its allocation to appropriate staff;","Quality control, including evidence of work completed; and","Review of work done by managers and engagement partners."]},{"note":"It is also essential that audit firms pay particular attention to **technical training and updating of staff knowledge** — through in-house bulletins, seminars, and provision of library and data bank resources."}]},{"n":"1.12","t":"Types of audits","b":[{"h4":"§1.12.1 Private audit and statutory audit"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Private audit","Statutory audit"],"rows":[["Undertaken at the instance of an interested party (e.g. a sole trader) or parties (partners of a partnership), even though there is **no legal obligation** for an audit to be carried out. Scope may be determined as narrowly or broadly as the client wishes.","Arises under company law, making it a **statutory obligation** for every limited liability company's accounts to be audited annually by a professionally qualified auditor. Scope is largely determined by the governing legislation, which neither the client nor the auditor may vary."]]}},{"key":"Even though the appointment of an auditor is a statutory requirement, the **relationship** between the company and the auditor is still governed by contract — the statutory obligation and the contractual relationship are not the same thing."},{"h4":"§1.12.2 External and internal audit"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["External audit","Internal audit"],"rows":[["Independent persons are brought in from **outside** an organisation to review the accounts prepared by management. This is the definition and objective of audit stated in the rest of this chapter.","An organisation appoints **full-time staff** to monitor and report on the running of the company's operations, as a service to management."]]}},{"p":"A fuller comparison:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["External Auditors","Internal Auditors"],"rows":[["Independent of the organisation","Responsible to Management, the Managing Director, the Board of Directors, or the Audit Committee"],["Responsibilities are fixed by statute","Responsibilities are specified by Management"],["Report to shareholders","Report to the Director or Audit Committee"],["Perform work to enable them express an opinion on the truth and fairness of the accounts in accordance with Company Law","Work may range over many operational and financial areas and activities as determined by Management"],["Must be professionally qualified","May not be professionally qualified"]]}},{"note":"Some activities performed by internal auditors resemble those performed by external auditors, and the efficiency of the former **largely affects the workload** of the latter — a well-functioning internal audit function can reduce how much substantive testing the external auditor needs to perform."},{"h4":"§1.12.3 Complete audit, interim audit and continuous audit"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","When used"],"rows":[["**Complete audit**","Applies to smaller concerns, where transaction volume and record complexity do not require the auditor's attendance more than once a year — the single visit normally starts as soon as the financial year ends, and continues until complete and the audit report signed."],["**Interim audit**","For larger clients, the auditor proceeds on an interim basis given the volume of testing needed. Arranged with the client's co-operation, interim visits may be bi-annual, quarterly or monthly, and may occur at any time before the year ends."],["**Continuous audit**","Where a large company's internal control system displays fundamental and material weaknesses, the auditor may need to check a higher proportion of transactions than usual — in exceptional circumstances, the audit team may need to check continuously throughout the period the accounts relate to."]]}},{"h4":"§1.12.4 Systems audit and Statement of Financial Position audit"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Systems audit","Statement of Financial Position audit"],"rows":[["Review of the **internal control systems** through which transactions are processed.","Audit of the **account balances** themselves."]]}}]},{"n":"1.13","t":"Meaning of assurance","b":[{"def":{"t":"Assurance","d":"a strong feeling or confidence about being right. In an assurance engagement, a firm is engaged to express an opinion on financial statements prepared by the directors — giving company owners the assurance that those statements can be relied upon."}},{"p":"Assurance can be provided by:"},{"ul":["**Audit** — external, internal, or a combination of the two; and","**Review** — a voluntary investigation, whose resulting opinion is usually expressed as **negative assurance**."]},{"key":"Without assurance from professional accountants, shareholders may not accept that the financial statements give a true and fair view — statutory audit provides assurance as to the quality of the information displayed. The **degree** of assurance the auditor can provide depends on the amount of work performed and the results of that work."},{"h4":"Two categories of assurance"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Category","Description"],"rows":[["**Reasonable Assurance**","A **high** level of assurance, expressed in **positive** form, e.g. \"in our opinion the financial statements give a true and fair view.\" The objective of a statutory audit is to provide reasonable assurance. (The law does not recognise the internal auditor, so internal audit may not be able to express an assurance opinion.)"],["**Limited Assurance**","A **moderate** level of assurance, expressed in **negative** form, e.g. \"Based on our review, nothing has come to our attention that causes us to believe that the accompanying financial statements do not give a true and fair view.\" The objective of a review engagement is often to provide limited assurance."]]}},{"note":"The higher level of assurance from an audit report raises the credibility provided by the assurance process — but audit work takes more time than a review, and thus costs more. The two properties (assurance level and cost) move together, not independently."},{"h4":"The five elements of an assurance engagement"},{"ol":["**A tripartite relationship** — the professional accountants providing the service; the directors/responsible officers who prepared the financial statements; and the users of the assurance report (e.g. shareholders, the tax office, researchers);","**The subject matter** — the financial statements or cash flow forecast prepared by the responsible officer for the professional accountant to review;","**The suitable criteria** — \"the rules\" against which the subject matter is evaluated to reach an opinion (e.g. CAMA, IFRS, ISA);","**The evidence** — all material information used by the professional accountant to arrive at an opinion; and","**The assurance report** — the written report containing the professional accountant's opinion, presented to the users."]}]},{"n":"1.14","t":"The chronology of an audit","b":[{"p":"Auditing is a practical discipline and, in detail, each audit differs from every other — but every audit assignment follows a systematic approach, structured here as eight stages."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Stage","Objective and how it is achieved"],"rows":[["**1**","Discover the background and operational system on a **theoretical** basis: interview selected management staff; ascertain the background and nature of the business and records; refer to procedural manuals and the organisational chart; draft systems notes and systems flow charts."],["**2**","Discover the operational system **in practice**: conduct selected walk-through tests on typical transactions, and make own observations."],["**3**","Discover the strengths and weaknesses in the control systems, in detail: complete all applicable sections of the Internal Control Evaluation Questionnaire (ICEQ); conduct compliance tests as necessary; prepare memoranda on weak areas."],["**4**","Evaluate the relative effect of weaknesses in each major operational area: consider the effect of weak areas with reference to materiality and any compensating controls; complete the Internal Control Evaluation schedule once the total effect of weaknesses is noted; report the effect of major weaknesses to management."],["**5**","Confirm the reliability of the records as a basis for preparing final accounts: prepare a detailed audit programme, specifying the nature and extent of substantive tests of transactions and account balances; execute the programme using depth testing techniques."],["**6**","Ensure the draft financial statements agree with the underlying records: prepare detailed year-end schedules, summarising individual items by cross-reference to the records and Statement of Financial Position schedules; reconcile the movement of each item from the previous to the current Statement of Financial Position."],["**7**","Form an opinion on whether the accounts give a true and fair view and comply with statutory and other requirements: obtain a letter of representation from the chief executive; complete verification of assets and liabilities; execute the financial position audit programme; execute analytical review; consider the effects of changes in accounting policy and events after the year end; use checklists to cover all disclosure requirements; conduct a post-audit review of files, programmes and accounts by an independent partner."],["**8**","Express an opinion in the audit report to members: review the draft Directors' Report; consider the need for qualification; refer any qualification to independent partners; draft the final audit report for inclusion in the published accounts."]]}},{"key":"These eight stages compress into **five phases** that summarise the whole audit process:"},{"ol":["The auditor should adequately plan, control and record their work;","The auditor should ascertain the entity's system of recording and processing transactions, and assess its adequacy as a basis for preparing financial statements;","The auditor should obtain relevant and reliable audit evidence sufficient to draw reasonable conclusions from it;","If the auditor wishes to rely on internal controls, they should ascertain and evaluate those controls, and perform compliance tests on their operation; and","The auditor should review the financial statements, sufficiently and in conjunction with conclusions drawn from other audit evidence, to give a reasonable basis for the opinion on the financial statements."]}]},{"n":"1.15","t":"Auditing and other assurance services","b":[{"p":"Auditors have intimate knowledge of company operations, and may be called upon by existing and prospective clients to perform services other than auditing:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Service","What it involves"],"rows":[["**Accounting services**","Assisting with maintenance of accounting records and preparation of financial statements, especially for small firms and partnerships."],["**Taxation matters**","Assisting with preparation and agreement of tax returns for claims on taxation relief and computation of capital allowances; attending to tax office queries; handling objections and appeals over disputed assessments; processing and procuring tax clearance certificates."],["**Liquidation, receivership and trusts**","Assisting companies realise the best return on their assets during winding up, and acting in a fiduciary capacity as trustee under the terms of a trust deed."],["**Stock Exchange advisory**","Advising companies seeking quotation on the Nigerian Stock Exchange."],["**Feasibility reports**","Assisting companies to prepare feasibility reports."],["**Pension scheme advice**","Providing advice on establishing staff pension schemes."],["**Secretarial services**","Assisting clients with filing annual returns, registration and incorporation of new business, etc., with the Corporate Affairs Commission."]]}}]},{"n":"1.16","t":"The agency theory and auditing","b":[{"h4":"Agents and principals"},{"def":{"t":"Agency relationship","d":"occurs when one party, the **principal**, employs another party, the **agent**, to perform a service on the principal's behalf."}},{"p":"Modern organisational theory views an organisation as comprising various interest groups, whose relationships are often described in terms of agency theory. For example: directors are agents of shareholders; employees are agents of directors; and auditors are agents of shareholders."},{"key":"Each principal must recognise that, although employing the agent, the agent has interests of their own to protect — and may therefore not fully carry out the principal's requirements. This is the seed of every conflict discussed in the rest of this section."},{"h4":"Agency relationship conflict"},{"p":"Directors have a duty of stewardship over the company's assets, but are also interested in their own remuneration — and if remuneration increases, the company's assets decrease. Since the decision to approve directors' pay effectively lies in the directors' own hands, a conflict of interest arises."},{"p":"Auditors submit their report to shareholders, yet know that the decision to reappoint them effectively lies with the directors — creating a potential conflict between carrying out their function properly and remaining on good terms with the directors. Recognising these agency relationships allows a principal to take steps ensuring an agent works closer to the principal's interests."},{"h4":"The application of agency theory to auditing"},{"p":"Despite the conflicts of interest, agency theory predicts that, by behaving rationally, an agent would not act against the principal's interest — because shareholders will only buy shares if safeguards are in place to protect their interests. The legal requirement for audited accounts is one such necessary precondition; management, recognising the desirability of an audit, will arrange a proper one since it is in their own interest to reassure suspicious shareholders."},{"warn":"If this reasoning holds, the practical conclusion of agency theory in auditing is that **statutory regulation is unnecessary**: large companies would voluntarily pay high fees for extensive, sophisticated audits (having the greatest need to convince shareholders of management's honesty), while a small company where management and shareholders are virtually the same group would not pay for a high-quality audit — arguably a reasonable result, since such shareholders have little need for one. But this conclusion does **not** necessarily satisfy other users of accounting information, such as lenders (who lend based on the accounts) and tax authorities (who assess tax liability based on reported profit) — which is exactly why statutory audit requirements persist in practice, despite the theory's own internal logic pointing the other way."}]},{"n":"1.17","t":"Audit committee","b":[{"p":"Section 404(2) of CAMA 2020 states that Public Limited Companies (Plc) shall have Audit Committees. The intent is to create space between the external auditors and the directors of the client company."},{"def":{"t":"Audit Committee membership (CAMA s.404(3))","d":"the Audit Committee shall consist of **five members**, comprising three members and two non-executive directors, whose function is to view the company's position in a detached and dispassionate manner and to liaise effectively between the main board and the external auditors."}},{"p":"Subject to any additional functions and powers the company's articles may stipulate, the functions/responsibilities of an audit committee under Section 404(7) are to:"},{"ol":["ascertain whether the company's accounting and reporting policies are in accordance with legal requirements and agreed ethical practices;","review the scope and planning of audit requirements;","review the findings on management matters, in conjunction with the external auditor and departmental responses thereon;","keep under review the effectiveness of the company's system of accounting and internal control;","make recommendations to the board on the appointment, removal and remuneration of the company's external auditors; and","authorise the internal auditor to investigate any company activities that may be of interest or concern to the committee."]}]},{"n":"1.18","t":"Chapter summary","b":[{"ol":["Auditing has developed into a core business subject because of the need for credibility and transparency in stewardship accounting.","Company laws of many countries have made audit of financial records and statements of corporate bodies a mandatory requirement.","The objective of an audit of financial statements is to enable the auditor to express an opinion on whether the financial statements were examined in accordance with an identified financial reporting framework, and give \"a true and fair view\" (or \"present fairly, in all material respects\") the financial results and state of affairs of the client entity.","The need for audit arose from the stewardship function.","Detection of errors and fraud in financial records are only **by-products** of the audit process, not its main purpose.","The role of assurance services is to give the desired confidence to various parties."]}]},{"n":"1.19","t":"Study text questions — multiple-choice and short-answer","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with 10 questions — 5 multiple-choice and 5 short-answer — reproduced below in full with the official answers, each explained. The Auditing study text sets **no examination-type questions**; this bank is the whole of its end-of-chapter material."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"The questions, with the official answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Which of the following is correct concerning an auditor’s responsibilities regarding financial statements? (A) Making suggestions that are adopted about the form and contents of an entity’s financial statement; (B) An auditor may draft an entity’s financial statements based on information from the management accounting system; (C) The fair presentation of audited financial statements in conformity with regulation is an implicit part of the auditor’s responsibilities; (D) An auditor’s responsibilities for audited financial statements are contained in his letter of engagement; (E) The auditor must include in the audited financial statements details of his letter of engagement.","Which of the following is **not** true of assurance? (A) Assurance is a strong feeling or confidence that something is right; (B) Assurance can be provided by internal audit; (C) A review of financial statements or part thereof, as a special investigation, is an assurance service; (D) The amount of work done by the auditor is in an inverse relationship to the degree of assurance; (E) The assurance provided by the auditor is either a reasonable assurance or a limited assurance.","In expressing an opinion on the accounts of clients, the auditor does **not** accept responsibility for which of the following? (A) The consistency of the application of accounting policies; (B) The reliability of opening balances; (C) The appropriateness of the comparative figures included in the accounts; (D) The preparation of financial statements; (E) Compliance with legal requirements.","Which of these departments is **NOT** normally found in an audit firm? (A) Taxation; (B) Audit/Taxation; (C) Production; (D) Management consultancy; (E) Insolvency.","Which of the following professional services is **NOT** offered by a Chartered Accountant in addition to his service as auditor of a client? (A) Acting as a Reporting Accountant in an initial public offer; (B) Keeping the client’s accounting books; (C) Attending to tax queries; (D) Recommending remedial actions to the audit committee; (E) Participating in annual inventory taking.","Where should a company keep its accounting records, as specified by CAMA 2020?","One of the ethical principles governing the auditor’s professional responsibilities is ………","The relationship between the various interested parties in a company is often explained by the ……… theory.","The audit that is compulsorily required by law to be carried out by an organisation is called ………","An independent examination of financial statements carried out by non-employees of an entity is called ………"]}],"a":[{"ol":["**B** — an auditor may draft an entity’s financial statements from information in the management accounting system. Doing so does not transfer responsibility for them: preparation remains the **directors’** responsibility, and the auditor’s responsibility is only to form and express an opinion on them. (Note that drafting the accounts of a listed client creates a **self-review threat** — see §2.7.)","**D** — the relationship is **direct, not inverse**. The more work the auditor does, the **higher** the level of assurance that can be given; that is precisely why a full audit gives **reasonable** assurance while a review engagement, involving less work, gives only **limited** assurance.","**D** — the preparation of the financial statements. This is the **directors’** responsibility under CAMA; the auditor’s responsibility is to report an opinion on them. The distinction is stated expressly in every audit report and is the foundation of the **audit expectation gap** (§1.10).","**C** — production. An audit firm provides professional services; it manufactures nothing. The departments normally found are audit and assurance, taxation, management consultancy, insolvency/corporate recovery, and corporate finance (§1.11).","**A** — acting as Reporting Accountant in an initial public offer **for the same client**. The two roles conflict: the reporting accountant reports publicly on the historical financial information that the auditor has audited, so performing both creates an unacceptable **self-review threat** to independence.","**At its registered office, or such other place in the country as the directors think fit.**","**Any one of:** independence; integrity; objectivity; professional competence and due care; confidentiality; professional behaviour or conduct; technical standards.","**Agency** theory (§1.16).","**Statutory audit** (§1.12.1).","**External audit** (§1.12.2)."]}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"This chapter is the vocabulary foundation for the entire subject — every later chapter assumes fluency with \"reasonable assurance,\" \"audit risk,\" \"material misstatement,\" \"professional scepticism,\" and the true-vs-fair distinction. The examiner's favourite traps live in the audit expectation gap (§1.10) and the limitations of audit (§1.9): know precisely what an audit does NOT promise (future viability, management efficiency, fraud detection as a primary objective, absolute assurance). Also expect the reasonable-vs-limited-assurance distinction (positive vs. negative wording) and the five elements of an assurance engagement to be tested directly.","errors":["Treating \"reasonable assurance\" as a synonym for \"absolute assurance\" — the chapter is explicit that absolute assurance is unattainable, due to testing, inherent control limitations, and the persuasive (not conclusive) nature of most evidence.","Assuming fraud detection is a primary objective of an audit — it is explicitly only a subsidiary objective and a by-product of properly conducted audit work, not the design intent.","Confusing \"true\" with \"fair\" as though they were the same test — truth is about factual accuracy and conformity to standards/law; fairness is about freedom from bias and reflecting the commercial substance of transactions.","Mixing up reasonable assurance (positive wording, audit) with limited assurance (negative wording, review) — the wording direction is the actual test the exam uses to distinguish them.","Believing the auditor is responsible for preparing the financial statements — that responsibility belongs to management/those charged with governance; the auditor is responsible only for forming and expressing an opinion on them."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Which of the following is correct concerning an auditor's responsibilities regarding financial statements?","o":["Making suggestions that are adopted about the form and contents of an entity's financial statement","An auditor may draft an entity's financial statements based on information from the management accounting system","The fair presentation of audited financial statements in conformity with regulation is an implicit part of auditor's responsibilities","An auditor's responsibilities for audited financial statement is contained in his letter of engagement","The auditor must include in the audited financial statements details of his letter of engagement"],"a":1,"w":"An auditor, acting as a trained accountant, may be called upon to provide accounting services — including assisting with the preparation of financial statements based on information from the management accounting system, especially for smaller clients (§1.15) — while remaining distinct from the auditor's core opinion-forming responsibility.","src":"Chapter 1.15","sec":"1.15"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT true of assurance?","o":["Assurance is a strong feeling or confidence that something is right","Assurance can be provided by internal audit","A review of financial statements or part thereof, as a special investigation, is an assurance service","The amount of work done by the auditor is in an inverse relationship to the degree of assurance","The assurance provided by the auditor is either a reasonable assurance or a limited assurance"],"a":1,"w":"The law does not recognise the internal auditor in the same way as the external auditor and, as such, internal audit may not be able to express a formal assurance opinion in the reasonable-assurance sense that a statutory audit provides — so, in that strict sense, it is not true that assurance \"can be provided by internal audit.\"","src":"Chapter 1.13","sec":"1.13"},{"q":"In expressing an opinion on the accounts of clients, the auditor does not accept responsibility for which of the following?","o":["The consistency of the application of accounting policies","The reliability of opening balances","The appropriateness of the comparative figures included in the accounts","The preparation of financial statements","Compliance with legal requirements"],"a":3,"w":"Responsibility for preparing and fairly presenting the financial statements rests with management, with oversight from those charged with governance — not with the auditor, whose responsibility is limited to forming and expressing an opinion on them (§1.6.6).","src":"Chapter 1.6","sec":"1.6"},{"q":"Which of these departments is NOT normally found in an audit firm?","o":["Taxation","Audit/Taxation","Production","Management consultancy","Insolvency"],"a":2,"w":"An audit firm's professional staff are divided into departments such as taxation, audit, consultancy, information technology, accounts preparation services and liquidation — \"production\" is a manufacturing function, not something an audit firm operates (§1.11).","src":"Chapter 1.11","sec":"1.11"},{"q":"Which of the following professional services is NOT offered by a Chartered Accountant in addition to his service as auditor of a client?","o":["Acting as a Reporting Accountant in an initial public offer","Keeping client's accounting books","Attending to tax queries","Recommending remedial actions to audit committee","Participating in annual inventory taking"],"a":0,"w":"Acting as Reporting Accountant in a client's own IPO is a self-review threat to independence and is not among the ancillary services (accounting, tax, liquidation/trusts, feasibility reports, pension advice, secretarial services) the chapter lists as compatible with also being that client's auditor.","src":"Chapter 1.15","sec":"1.15"}],"theory":[{"q":"Distinguish between reasonable assurance and limited assurance, giving an example of the wording used to express each.","marks":5,"a":[{"p":"**Reasonable assurance** is a high level of assurance, expressed in **positive** form — e.g. \"in our opinion the financial statements give a true and fair view.\" It is the objective of a statutory audit."},{"p":"**Limited assurance** is a moderate level of assurance, expressed in **negative** form — e.g. \"Based on our review, nothing has come to our attention that causes us to believe that the accompanying financial statements do not give a true and fair view.\" It is often the objective of a review engagement."},{"p":"A higher level of assurance raises the credibility of the assurance process, but requires more audit work and therefore costs more than a review."}],"src":"Chapter 1.13","sec":"1.13"},{"q":"State and briefly explain the five elements of an assurance engagement.","marks":5,"a":[{"ol":["**A tripartite relationship** — the professional accountant, the responsible officer who prepared the subject matter, and the users of the assurance report.","**The subject matter** — the financial statements or cash flow forecast being reviewed.","**Suitable criteria** — the rules against which the subject matter is evaluated (e.g. CAMA, IFRS, ISA).","**Evidence** — the material information used to arrive at the opinion.","**The assurance report** — the written report containing the opinion, presented to users."]}],"src":"Chapter 1.13","sec":"1.13"}]}},{"n":2,"t":"Regulatory Framework of Auditing","brief":"The statutory and professional scaffolding an audit operates inside: proper books of accounts, mandatory audit requirements, how auditors are appointed, removed and remunerated under CAMA, their statutory rights and duties, the ethical requirements of competence/integrity/confidentiality/objectivity/independence, the procedure for accepting a new engagement, the letter of engagement, the auditor's responsibility for fraud/error and non-compliance with laws and regulations (NOCLAR), and the specific scope of audit functions under CAMA, BOFIA and the Insurance Act.","outcomes":["Outline the statutory provisions as to keeping proper books of accounts","Outline the qualifications of an external auditor","State the powers, duties and statutory rights of an auditor","Appreciate professional ethical requirements of an audit","Describe the ethical requirements relating to the appointment and removal of auditors","Outline the purpose and content of the client screening process","Detail the steps to be taken on a new client engagement","Outline the purpose and contents of an engagement letter","Understand the impact of laws and accounting standards on audit","Know the scope of audit functions under CAMA, BOFIA and the Insurance Act"],"secs":[{"n":"2.1","t":"Keeping proper books of accounts","b":[{"note":"A word on this chapter's numbering. The study text's own section numbers break down after §2.6: it runs 2.6, then 2.61, 2.6.2, 2.6.3 (sub-sections of 2.6), then **2.62, 2.63, 2.64, 2.65, 2.66, 2.67, 2.68, 2.69** for what are plainly eight further *main* sections. Those are read here as **2.7 to 2.14**, which is what they must be. The mapping, for cross-reference against the PDF: book 2.62 = §2.7 here; 2.63 = §2.8; 2.64 = §2.9; 2.65 = §2.10; 2.66 = §2.11; 2.67 = §2.12; 2.68 = §2.13; 2.69 = §2.14 (chapter summary). Sections 2.1 to 2.6 are unchanged.","lbl":"Section numbering — the book's own scheme, and the mapping used here"},{"p":"Section 374 of CAMA makes it obligatory, by statute, for every company to keep proper books of accounts. Books are **not** deemed properly kept unless they:"},{"ol":["portray a true and fair view of the state of the company's affairs;","explain transactions; and","enable the preparation of the Statement of Comprehensive Income and the Statement of Financial Position."]},{"key":"The books of accounts must always be open to inspection by the directors, secretary and auditors of the company. Each public company shall also host its audited accounts on its website."},{"p":"The books must generally show, in particular, details of:"},{"ul":["all sums received and expended, and the matters to which the receipts and expenditure relate;","all sales and purchases of property, goods and services; and","the company's assets and liabilities and the interest of shareholders."]}]},{"n":"2.2","t":"Mandatory requirement to have accounts audited","b":[{"p":"It is a mandatory requirement that the auditor's report be attached to every company's financial statements before they can be circulated to members — implying every company should have an auditor. **Section 377 of CAMA** makes it mandatory for financial statements to be prepared and audited."}]},{"n":"2.3","t":"Appointment and remuneration of auditors","b":[{"h4":"§2.3.1 Who qualifies to be an auditor? (S.403 CAMA)"},{"p":"A person qualifies for appointment as auditor of a public company only if they are a member of a recognised professional accountancy body, duly licensed to practise by that body in the home/host country, and not disqualified by any legislative instrument issued at the instance of the Registrar/Commissioner."},{"warn":"None of the following is entitled to act as auditor to either a private or a public company:"},{"ol":["an officer of the company or any associated company (a partnership with a person acting as Secretary or Registration Officer of the company, or of any associated company, does **not** count as a disqualification);","an infant;","a person found by a competent court to be of unsound mind;","a body corporate (though members of an incorporated partnership may be appointed);","an undischarged bankrupt, unless granted leave to act as auditor of that specific company by the court that adjudged them bankrupt."]},{"h4":"§2.3.2 Who appoints the auditors? (S.401 CAMA)"},{"p":"Auditors are generally appointed by the shareholders (members) in a general meeting, by ordinary resolution, to hold office from the conclusion of that meeting until the conclusion of the next AGM. The **directors** may appoint the **first** auditors of the company, and may fill any **casual vacancy** (where no auditor is appointed or re-appointed at an AGM). A person's **written consent** is necessary before they can be appointed as auditor."},{"h4":"§2.3.3 Who fixes the auditors' remuneration? (S.408 CAMA)"},{"p":"Generally, remuneration is fixed by the members in a general meeting, by ordinary resolution, or in such manner as the company may determine. For an auditor appointed by the **directors**, remuneration may instead be fixed by the directors, for the period expiring at the conclusion of the next AGM."}]},{"n":"2.4","t":"Removal and resignation of auditors","b":[{"h4":"§2.4.1 Resignation of an auditor"},{"p":"Section 412 of CAMA 2020 provides for auditor resignation:"},{"ol":["An auditor may resign by depositing a written notice at the company's registered office — the notice brings the term of office to an end on the day deposited, or on a later date it specifies;","The notice is **not effective** unless it contains either a statement that there are no circumstances connected with the resignation which should be brought to members' or creditors' notice, or a statement of what those circumstances are;","Where a notice is deposited, the company must, within 14 days, send a copy to the Commission — and if the notice contains a circumstances statement, to all persons entitled to receive copies of the company's financial statements. The company or any aggrieved person may, within 14 days of the company receiving the notice, apply to the court for an order;","If the court is satisfied the auditor is using the notice to secure needless publicity for defamatory matter, it may order that copies need not be sent out, and may order the company's costs on the application to be paid, in whole or part, by the auditor — even though the auditor is not a party to the application;","The company shall, within 14 days of the court's decision, send the Commission, shareholders and debenture holders a statement of the order's effect (if the court makes one), or a copy of the notice containing the statement (if it makes no order)."]},{"note":"Ghana's Companies Code makes virtually identical provision, with a few added procedural rights: the resigning auditor may attach a signed requisition for the directors to convene an extraordinary general meeting so the resignation circumstances can be brought to members' attention; may request the company to circulate a written statement of circumstances (read out at the meeting if this fails to happen for any reason); and retains the right to receive all notices of, attend, and speak at, both the meeting at which their term would have expired and any meeting to fill the resulting casual vacancy."},{"h4":"§2.4.2 Removal of an auditor"},{"p":"Section 409 of CAMA:"},{"ol":["A company may, by ordinary resolution, remove an auditor before the expiration of their term of office, notwithstanding anything in any agreement between the company and the auditor;","Where a removal resolution is passed at a general meeting, the company shall, within 14 days, give notice of that fact in the prescribed form to the Commission;","Removal does not deprive the auditor of compensation or damages payable to them."]},{"p":"Ghana's Companies Code makes similar, more detailed provision — for removal to take effect:"},{"ol":["There must be a resolution to remove the auditor or appoint a replacement;","The resolution must be passed at an annual general meeting;","Written notice of intent to move the resolution must be given to the company at least 35 days before the AGM (14 days, if the resolution removes/replaces a director-appointed auditor);","On receipt, the company must forthwith send a copy to the auditor concerned;","Notice of the resolution must be given to members at the same time and in the same manner as notice of the meeting itself, or, failing that, at least 21 days before the meeting (7 days for a resolution affecting a director-appointed auditor);","The auditor's right to be heard on the resolution at the meeting must not be withheld;","The company should send, with every AGM notice, copies of any written statement the auditor submits — if received too late, the company must circulate it forthwith, in the same manner as meeting notices;","The court may, on application, decide against circulating the auditor's statement if it is unreasonably long or amounts to an abuse of the right or an attempt to secure needless publicity for defamatory matter — without prejudice to the auditor's right to be heard orally, and to have the written statement read, at the meeting;","If duly passed, the resolution takes effect only after the AGM concludes."]},{"key":"The **object** of these statutory removal provisions is to preserve two things at once: the shareholders' right to appoint the auditors of their choice, and the auditors' independence from the directors — by not permitting directors who disagree with the auditors to simply dismiss them."},{"h4":"§2.4.3 Other types of departure from office"},{"p":"Auditors may also be removed by a resolution, at the AGM, to appoint new auditors — special notice is required, and the outgoing auditors may make representations beforehand as to why they should stay. Alternatively, auditors may simply decline to offer themselves for re-election, in which case they may still make representations about the resolution to appoint new auditors before the relevant meeting."}]},{"n":"2.5","t":"Rights and responsibilities of auditors","b":[{"h4":"§2.5.1 Duties of the auditor"},{"p":"The statutory duties of the auditor are:"},{"ol":["To report to members on all accounts and financial statements laid before them at the AGM;","To state whether the financial statements show a true and fair view and have been properly prepared in accordance with the Code;","To consider whether information in the Directors' Report is consistent with the accounts, and report any inconsistency;","To form an opinion on whether: proper accounting records have been kept; proper returns adequate for their audit have been received from branches not visited; the Statement of Financial Position and Statement of Comprehensive Income agree with the accounting records/returns; and whether the necessary information/explanations were obtained from company officers — these matters are reported **\"by exception only\"**: where the auditor is satisfied, no mention is made in the report;","To outline, in the report, details of directors' remuneration, loans to officers, and transactions involving directors and other connected persons, if not already disclosed in the financial statements themselves;","To make other special reports in various circumstances; and","To make a \"statement of circumstances\" when ceasing to hold office for any reason."]},{"key":"Duty 4's \"report by exception\" principle is an easy exam trap: the auditor's silence on these specific matters is not an oversight — it is the **prescribed reporting convention**. Mention only appears when the auditor is **not** satisfied."},{"h4":"§2.5.2 The rights of the auditor"},{"p":"The statutory rights of auditors are:"},{"ol":["Right of access at all times to the company's books of accounts and vouchers;","Right to require from officers, including directors, such information and explanations as deemed necessary for the auditors' duties;","Right to attend any general meeting and receive notices of, and communications relating to, any part of the meeting's business that concerns them as auditors;","Right to apply to the court for directions on any matter arising from performing their CAMA functions, with the costs of such an application met by the company unless the court directs otherwise;","Right to be notified in writing of an intended resolution to remove them or appoint another auditor, and to submit a written statement to be circulated to members (and read at the meeting, if required);","Right, before accepting appointment, to communicate with any retiring auditor; and","Right to contract with a company — beyond their statutory duties to members — to undertake obligations relating to detecting defalcations, and advice on accounting, taxation, raising finance, and other matters."]}]},{"n":"2.6","t":"Requirements for competence and integrity","b":[{"h4":"§2.6.1 The principles of due skill, care and competence"},{"ul":["Auditors should carry out professional work with skill, care, diligence and expedition, with proper regard for expected technical and professional standards;","All auditors in public practice are obliged to provide quality services;","The **degree** of skill and care depends on the work — a higher degree is required for specialised work (e.g. due diligence investigations) or where negligence is likely to cause substantial loss;","The duty of skilful act and care is **not absolute** — opinion and advice do not give rise to claims merely because they are subsequently proved wrong;","Auditors must exercise reasonable care in everything they do, and are particularly expected to apply generally accepted auditing techniques;","If auditors encounter anything that puts them upon enquiry, they must investigate it to a reasonable and satisfactory conclusion — never accepting an explanation without carrying out the investigations/tests needed to form their own judgment."]},{"h4":"§2.6.2 The principle of integrity"},{"ul":["Auditors should behave with integrity in all professional, business and personal financial relationships — integrity implies not merely honesty, but fair dealing and truthfulness; auditors should strive for **objectivity**, the state of mind having regard to all considerations relevant to the task at hand, bound up with intellectual honesty;","Auditors must not accept or perform work they are not competent to undertake, unless they obtain the advice/assistance needed to competently carry it out;","Auditors must carry out professional work with due skill, care, diligence and expedition, with proper regard for the technical and professional standards expected of them;","Auditors should behave with courtesy and consideration towards everyone they come into contact with during their work."]},{"h4":"§2.6.3 Professional duty of confidence"},{"p":"Clients expect their business affairs kept confidential — maintaining confidence may mean auditors cannot \"blow the whistle\" on client malpractice, allowing it to continue. As a general rule, information acquired in professional work should **not** be disclosed except where consent has been obtained (from the client, employer, or other proper source), or where there is a public duty, or a legal/professional right or duty, to disclose. An auditor must never use, or appear to use, information for personal advantage or a third party's advantage."},{"p":"The recognised exceptions permitting disclosure are:"},{"ol":["**Suspected treason** (obliging disclosure to a competent authority) — the same duty arises for suspicion of terrorism or drug/money laundering; the auditor should also consider whether non-compliance with laws/regulations affects the financial statements, and include in their report a statement of the resulting significant uncertainty or disagreement;","**Public duty to disclose** — e.g. where an offence contrary to the public interest has been committed;","**Disclosure compelled by law** — e.g. where a member is required in an action to give evidence or deliver documents;","**Assisting authorities** (police, SIRS, FIRS, etc.) — the auditor should be satisfied that such information is genuinely demanded under statutory authority; absent that satisfaction, they should decline until the client's authority is obtained, and if that authority is refused while the demand is pressed, the member should not accede unless advised to by their solicitor;","**Awareness of a client's wrongful act** — the auditor must ensure they have not prejudiced themselves (e.g. by relying on client-given information that later proves incorrect). It is a criminal offence for an auditor to act positively, without lawful authority or reasonable excuse, to impede with intent the arrest or prosecution of a client known or believed to have committed an offence calling for arrest."]}]},{"n":"2.7","t":"Requirements for objectivity and independence","b":[{"p":"An auditor's objectivity must be beyond question — assured only if the auditor **is, and is seen to be,** independent. Threats to independence can be reduced by the nature and extent of the precautions the practice takes. Thirteen circumstances may pose a threat:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Threat","Description and safeguard"],"rows":[["**Undue dependence on a client**","Objectivity may be threatened by undue dependence on any one audit client or group of connected clients. Recurring fee income from one client/group should generally not exceed **15%** of gross practice income (**10%** for listed/public-interest companies) — a new practice unable to meet this needs extra safeguards."],["**Overdue fees**","Significant overdue fees from a client can pose a threat akin to a loan — firms must ensure overdue fees, together with fees from current work, cannot be construed as a loan."],["**Actual or threatened litigation**","Litigation involving a client can represent a breakdown of trust, impairing independence or making directors unwilling to disclose information — though a dispute purely over audit fees may not cause such problems."],["**Associated firms — outside influences**","Pressure from associated practices/organisations or external sources (bankers, solicitors, government, introducers of business) — relevant factors include the closeness of the association and the strength of the associate's interest in the firm retaining the client."],["**Family and personal relationships**","Threatened where an officer/senior employee of a client is closely connected to the responsible partner/staff member — \"closely connected\" includes children and their spouses, siblings and their spouses, and anyone given regular financial assistance by, or indebted to, that staff member/partner. Proximity matters (the more junior, the less risk). An officer/employee of a company (or their partner) is barred from becoming its auditor, extending for **two years** after ceasing to be an officer/employee."],["**Beneficial interests in shares/investments**","Staff and partners should not hold shareholdings in client businesses (including via a spouse or minor child); shares acquired involuntarily (marriage, inheritance) should be disposed of at the earliest opportunity. Where a client's regulations require the auditor to be a shareholder, only the minimum number should be held, and the holding disclosed in the client's accounts."],["**Beneficial interest in trusts**","Threatened where a partner (or someone closely connected) has a beneficial interest in a trust holding shares in a client. If the partner is, and wishes to remain, trustee, the shareholding is treated as equivalent to a beneficial shareholding and the practice should cease to report; if not a trustee, the partner should cease personally to report as soon as aware of the holding."],["**Trustee investments**","An audit firm should not have a public company as a client if a partner (or their spouse) is trustee of a trust holding more than **10%** of that company's stated capital, or 10%+ of the trust's total assets in that company's shares."],["**Voting on audit appointments**","A partner/staff member holding shares in a client company should not vote, or be voted for, at any general meeting on the appointment, removal or remuneration of auditors."],["**Loans**","Threatened by a loan to or from an audit client — no loans or guarantees should be undertaken unless with client financial institutions in the normal course of business."],["**Goods, services or hospitality**","Threatened by accepting goods/services/hospitality from a client — acceptance on normal commercial terms, or of only modest benefit, is acceptable."],["**Provision of other services**","Threatened by providing non-audit services (accountancy, taxation, consultancy) — auditors can end up effectively making management decisions, harming independence; critics note auditors may then be unwilling to qualify a report if doing so risks losing lucrative non-audit work too."]]}},{"note":"Where the **same staff** prepare accounts and carry out the audit, the audit work must be carefully planned and recorded — the file should specifically show what audit assurance was obtained through the accountancy work. A key countermeasure: use **different staff** for accounts preparation and audit. Large firms are also barred from providing accountancy services to **public** companies (though they may still provide consultancy, corporate finance and taxation), and commonly counter the threat with fully separated departments between which no information passes."},{"h4":"§2.7.1 Integrity, objectivity and independence — review procedures"},{"p":"Every audit firm should establish review procedures (including an annual review) to guard against loss of independence — enabling the firm to satisfy itself that each engagement may properly be accepted or continued, and to identify situations where independence may be at risk. Safeguards might include:"},{"ul":["including a manager or other qualified employee in the review of the audit team/rotation of the engagement partner; and","rotation of senior members of staff."]}]},{"n":"2.8","t":"Procedure for the appointment of auditors","b":[{"p":"Having covered who qualifies, their rights/responsibilities, and the professional requirements of skill, competence, integrity and independence, this section covers the procedure of actually appointing external auditors."},{"key":"A key issue: the **prospective** auditor must communicate with the **retiring** auditor to discuss matters relating to the client — if the client refuses to permit this communication, the incoming auditor should refuse the assignment. There is also a professional requirement to seek reference about the prospective client, often via a **client screening process**, safeguarding against adverse publicity or legal action from associating with a not-doing-well or dubious client — this process identifies risks associated with accepting the client and assesses control levels. Its terminal point is the accept/decline decision; if accepted, terms are formalised via the letter of engagement."},{"h4":"§2.8.1 Pre-accepting conditions"},{"p":"Prior to accepting an appointment, the auditor must ensure the principles of integrity, objectivity, independence and other ethical requirements are not compromised, and that they have been appointed in a proper manner, with particular reference to taking over from a retiring auditor."},{"h4":"§2.8.2 Ethical consideration"},{"ul":["If fees from the appointment would exceed **15%** of the gross fees of practice (other than a new practice), the appointment should not be accepted;","Any personal relationship with an officer/employee of the company would be considered unethical, as would any financial involvement (shareholding, loan to/from the company);","Any conflict of interest should be avoided — e.g. advising two clients in dispute with each other."]},{"h4":"§2.8.3 Legal consideration"},{"p":"The Companies Code prohibits officers/servants of the company (or partners/employees of such an officer/servant) from accepting the position of auditor to it. The auditor must be a member of ICAN or another recognised professional accountancy body, and not disqualified by any statute."},{"h4":"§2.8.4 Practical consideration"},{"p":"The auditors must ensure the firm's existing resources (material, manpower, equipment) are adequate for the new client — raising questions of staff/time availability and technical expertise. It may be impossible to accept if the firm is not large enough, is heavily engaged elsewhere at the time needed, or lacks necessary expertise (e.g. in computing). The auditor may also weigh audit fees against the risks of the engagement, declining if fees cannot match the associated risk."},{"h4":"§2.8.5 Seeking reference about the client"},{"p":"The auditor must obtain sufficient knowledge of the client's business to identify and appreciate issues affecting it, making independent enquiries about the company's status. This knowledge covers:"},{"ol":["general economic factors;","industry conditions affecting the client's business;","the entity itself (board composition, management profile, etc.);","the entity's products, market, supplies, expenses and operations;","the entity's financial performance and condition; and","the reporting environment."]},{"p":"Sources of information about new clients include:"},{"ul":["previous auditors;","confidential enquiries from the prospective client's bankers, solicitors, underwriters, registrar, etc.;","prior-year financial statements;","professional rules, regulations and standards;","specific rules and regulations pertaining to the industry;","the regulations of the client's company; and","directories of incorporated companies."]},{"h4":"§2.8.6 Communicating with the present auditors"},{"p":"One ethical requirement before accepting an audit engagement is to obtain the client's permission to write to the retiring auditors, enquiring if there is any professional reason the appointment should not be accepted — protecting shareholders and serving as professional courtesy. If permission is refused, the prospective auditor should decline the nomination."},{"p":"If granted, the prospective auditor writes to the previous auditors for relevant information to inform their decision. On receiving this request, the previous auditor should in turn seek the client's permission to discuss the client's affairs freely — if that is refused, the previous auditors should report that fact to the prospective auditors (who may then decline the offer); if granted, they should discuss frankly and freely all relevant issues and fully disclose relevant information about the client's affairs."},{"h4":"§2.8.7 Post-acceptance procedures"},{"p":"After accepting the nomination:"},{"ol":["Ensure the outgoing auditor's removal, retirement or resignation was properly concluded, in line with CAMA 2020;","Ensure the new appointment is valid and formalised — obtaining a copy of the resolution passed at the general meeting;","Where part of the outgoing auditor's fees is still outstanding, join with the outgoing auditor to secure payment;","Submit a letter of engagement to the directors of the client company; and","On taking up the appointment, arrange to collect all books, documents and papers belonging to the client from the outgoing auditor — who is expected to co-operate and transfer them, unless they hold a lien over the records for outstanding fees."]}]},{"n":"2.9","t":"Letter of engagement","b":[{"p":"Prior to any audit work, the auditor should agree in writing the precise scope and nature of the work to be undertaken — this is the purpose of the letter of engagement."},{"def":{"t":"Letter of engagement","d":"a letter sent by the auditors to a client at the start of any audit, setting out the terms of the engagement and forming the basis of the contract."}},{"h4":"§2.9.1 Purposes of the letter of engagement"},{"ol":["To define clearly the extent of the auditors' and directors' responsibilities;","To help minimise the possibilities of misunderstanding between auditors and client, by formalising the terms of engagement;","To provide written confirmation of the auditors' acceptance of the appointment; and","To confirm in writing verbal arrangements on the scope of the audit, the form of their report, and the scope of any non-audit service."]},{"h4":"§2.9.2 Contents of the letter of engagement"},{"p":"The main emphasis is on the respective responsibilities of directors and auditors, and the scope of the audit — it should also identify any report the auditor must submit beyond the statutory audit report. Specific contents:"},{"ol":["The board's responsibilities for proper books of accounts and financial statements; the auditors' responsibilities to report on the financial statements; the scope and basis of the audit work;","Fees and billing arrangements (it does **not** state the fees payable);","Where appropriate, arrangements concerning other auditors/experts involved in some aspect of the audit;","Any agreement for the auditor to provide taxation services;","Arrangements, if any, with predecessor auditors;","Any restriction of the auditors' liability to the client (not possible with limited companies);","A reference to any further agreements between the auditors and client;","A proposed timetable for the engagement; and","A request for written acknowledgement of the letter."]},{"h4":"§2.9.3 Issuing and revising engagement letters"},{"ul":["The letter should be issued to a new client before audit work begins, and reviewed annually to stay up to date;","Whenever circumstances change (extra duties, new auditing guidelines, a change in the nature of services or other terms), a new letter must be issued to an existing client;","Specific factors that may necessitate a new letter: an indication the client misunderstands the objective/scope of the audit; a recent change of management, board or audit committee; a significant change in ownership (e.g. a new holding company); a relevant change in the nature/size of the client's business; and a relevant change in legal or professional requirements;","In a year where a new letter is deemed unnecessary, it may still be appropriate to remind the client of the original letter;","The letter is addressed to the board of directors or audit committee of the client company."]},{"h4":"§2.9.4 Public sector perspective"},{"p":"The engagement letter's purpose — informing the auditee of the engagement's nature and clarifying responsibilities — holds in the public sector too, though legislation generally mandates the appointment of a public sector auditor, so engagement letters may not be as widespread a practice. Nevertheless, a letter setting out the engagement's nature, or recognising an engagement not indicated in the legislative mandate, can be useful to both parties. Specific requirements may exist within the audit mandate — e.g. requiring the auditor to report directly to a minister, the legislature or the public if management attempts to limit the audit's scope."},{"h4":"§2.9.5 Example letter of engagement — structure"},{"p":"A real letter of engagement (here, from a fictitious firm, \"Babatunde & Co.,\" to a fictitious client, \"Primetime Plc\") follows a consistent structure, reproduced below by its constituent clauses:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Clause","What it states"],"rows":[["**Directors' responsibility**","The directors are responsible for maintaining proper accounting records, preparing financial statements giving a true and fair view, and making available all accounting records, other records, related information and minutes of management/shareholder meetings, as and when required."],["**Auditors' responsibility**","A statutory responsibility to report to members whether, in the auditors' opinion, the financial statements give a true and fair view and have been properly prepared under CAMA/the Companies Code — reporting specifically on: whether proper accounting records/branch returns were kept/received; whether the SFP and Statement of Profit or Loss agree with the records/returns; whether all necessary information/explanations were obtained; and whether the directors' report is consistent with the financial statements. Also covers the professional duty to report non-compliance (in any material respect) with applicable accounting standards, unless the departure is justified — considering whether the departure is needed for a true and fair view, and whether it is adequately disclosed."],["**Scope and basis of audit**","The audit will follow approved auditing standards, including tests of transactions and of existence/ownership/valuation of assets and liabilities as considered necessary; an understanding of accounting and internal control systems will be obtained to assess their adequacy and establish whether proper records were maintained; the audit is not designed to identify all significant weaknesses, but any that come to notice and warrant attention will be reported (not to third parties without prior written consent)."],["**Written representations**","The auditors may request written confirmation of oral representations received during the audit on matters with a material effect on the financial statements — noting it is an offence for a company officer to mislead the auditors."],["**Access rights**","The auditors will request sighting of all documents accompanying the financial statements (chairman's statement, operating/financial review, directors' report), and are entitled to attend all general meetings and receive notice of them."],["**Fraud/error responsibility**","Safeguarding assets and preventing/detecting fraud, error and non-compliance rests with the directors — the auditors will endeavour to plan the audit for a reasonable expectation of detecting material misstatements, but the audit should not be relied upon to disclose every such misstatement that may exist."],["**Post-report responsibility**","Once the report is issued, the auditors have no further responsibility for that financial year's statements — but expect to be informed of any material event between the report date and the AGM that may affect the financial statements."],["**Other services**","Other requested services (e.g. taxation, investment business advice) are dealt with in separate letters."],["**Fees**","Computed on time spent by partners/staff and their level of skill/responsibility, billed at appropriate intervals and due on presentation, unless otherwise agreed."],["**Applicable law**","The letter is governed by the laws of the relevant West African country, whose courts have exclusive jurisdiction over any related claim or dispute."],["**Continuity**","Once agreed, the letter remains effective from one audit appointment to the next until replaced — the client is asked to confirm agreement in writing by signing and returning a copy."]]}}]},{"n":"2.10","t":"Auditors' responsibility to consider fraud and error","b":[{"p":"When planning, performing, evaluating and reporting on audit procedures, auditors should consider the risk of material misstatement in the financial statements, including those resulting from fraud or error."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Term","Definition and examples"],"rows":[["**Fraud**","The use of deception to obtain an unjust or illegal financial advantage, and intentional misrepresentations affecting the financial statements by management, employees, or third parties — involving: manipulation, falsification or alteration of records/documents; misappropriation of assets or theft; suppression or omission of the effects of transactions from records/documents; recording of transactions without substance; or intentional misapplication of accounting policies."],["**Error**","Unintentional mistakes in financial statements — mathematical or clerical mistakes in underlying records/data; inadvertent omission or misinterpretation of accounting policies; and unintentional misapplication of accounting policies."]]}},{"key":"The **essential distinguishing feature** between fraud and error is **intent**. Responsibility for preventing and detecting both rests with the **directors**, through the implementation and continued operation of adequate accounting and internal control systems — which reduce, but never eliminate, the possibility of either."},{"h4":"§2.10.1 Responsibility of the auditors"},{"p":"**Prevention**: it is not the auditor's function to prevent fraud and error — though the fact of an annual audit may act as a deterrent."},{"p":"**Risk assessment**: when planning, the auditor assesses the risk that fraud or error may cause material misstatement. Beyond weaknesses in accounting/internal control system design and non-compliance with identified controls, conditions/events raising this risk include:"},{"ul":["questions over the integrity or competence of management;","unusual pressures within an entity;","unusual transactions; and","problems obtaining sufficient appropriate audit evidence."]},{"p":"**Detection**: based on the risk assessment, the auditor designs procedures with a reasonable expectation of detecting material misstatements from fraud or error, seeking sufficient appropriate evidence that fraud/error material to the financial statements has not occurred, or, if it has, that its effect is properly reflected or corrected."},{"key":"Detecting **errors** is ordinarily easier than detecting the **concealment** of fraud (e.g. collusion between employees, or falsification of records) — because fraud is, by definition, designed to evade detection, while error is not. Due to the inherent limitations of an audit, there remains an unavoidable risk that material misstatement from fraud (and, to a lesser extent, error) goes undetected. The **subsequent discovery** of a material misstatement after the audit report is issued does **not**, by itself, indicate the auditors failed to follow the basic principles and essential procedures of an audit."},{"note":"Unless the audit reveals contrary evidence, auditors are entitled to accept representations as truthful and records/documents as genuine — while still maintaining professional scepticism throughout. Even an effective internal control system carries some risk of failing to operate as designed, and may be wholly ineffective against fraud involving **collusion among employees**, or fraud committed by **management overriding** the very controls meant to prevent it — e.g. directing subordinates to record transactions incorrectly, conceal them, or suppress related information."},{"h4":"§2.10.2 Procedures when fraud or error is indicated"},{"p":"When audit procedures indicate fraud or error may exist, the auditor considers the potential effect on the financial statements. If it could be material, the auditor performs modified or additional procedures, with extent depending on judgment as to: the type of fraud/error indicated; its likelihood of occurrence; and the likelihood that this type of fraud/error could materially affect the financial statements."},{"key":"Unless circumstances clearly indicate otherwise, auditors **cannot assume** a suspected fraud/error is an isolated occurrence — they should adjust the nature, timing and extent of substantive procedures accordingly. If suspicion is not dispelled, the auditors discuss the matter with management/directors/audit committee, consider whether it has been properly reflected or corrected, and consider the implications for the reliability of management representations more broadly. If the suspicion is supported, the auditors consider legal consequences — whether to contact the entity's lawyers or seek their own legal advice, and what action to take."},{"h4":"§2.10.3 Reporting fraud and error to management"},{"p":"The auditors should communicate factual findings to management/the board/the audit committee as soon as practicable if they suspect fraud (even if the potential effect is immaterial) or if material error is found to exist. In choosing who to report to, they consider all circumstances — reporting to the next higher level of authority not suspected of involvement (e.g. to the audit committee, if senior management/board members are suspected). Where no higher authority exists, or the auditors are precluded from obtaining sufficient evidence, believe a report may not be acted on, or are unsure who to report to, they may seek legal advice."},{"h4":"§2.10.4 Reporting fraud and error to members"},{"p":"Auditors consider the implications for their report if they conclude a suspected fraud/error has a material effect and they disagree with the accounting treatment or disclosure (or lack of it), or if they cannot determine whether fraud/error has occurred. They should **not** refrain from qualifying their opinion merely because the position has since been corrected, or because of the possible consequences of qualification."},{"h4":"§2.10.5 Reporting fraud and error to third parties"},{"p":"Where aware of a suspected instance supported by strong evidence, and considering the matter may warrant reporting to a proper authority in the public interest, the auditors ensure the matter reaches the directors/audit committee first. Having considered any views expressed and any legal advice obtained, they may report it themselves if under statutory duty to do so, or if the entity does not report it itself (or cannot provide evidence that it has). In **extreme** cases, auditors may report directly to a proper authority, without first discussing with the entity, where the matter has caused a **loss of confidence in the integrity of the directors** — e.g. where the directors are themselves suspected or known to be involved and have not reported it."},{"key":"Confidentiality is an **implied** term of the auditor's contract, but is **not absolute** — in exceptional circumstances auditors have the right to report to a proper authority in the public interest, weighing the public interest in confidentiality against the public interest in disclosure. Auditors are protected from liability for breach of confidence where: disclosure is in the public interest; made to an appropriate body/person; and not motivated by malice. They are protected from liability for defamation where disclosure is made in their capacity as auditors of the entity, and there is no malice."},{"p":"Factors relevant to whether public-interest disclosure is justified: the extent to which the suspected fraud is likely to affect the public; whether directors have rectified or are taking effective corrective action; the extent to which non-disclosure would let the fraud recur with impunity; the gravity of the matter; and the weight of evidence/degree of the auditors' suspicion. Proper authorities may include: the Serious Fraud Office, the Police Service, the Bureau of National Investigations, recognised professional bodies, the Stock Exchange, the Central Bank, the Internal/Inland Revenue Service, the VAT Office, and the Customs, Excise & Preventive Service."},{"h4":"§2.10.6 Withdrawal from the engagement"},{"p":"Auditors may conclude withdrawal is necessary — e.g. if shareholders have not been given required information, and there is no opportunity to report it while remaining auditors. Relevant factors: implications of fraud involvement by the entity's highest authority (affecting management representation reliability), and the effects of continued association. Legal advice may be needed to reach this conclusion."},{"warn":"Resignation is a **step of last resort** — it is normally preferable for auditors to remain in place to fulfil their statutory duties, particularly where minority interests are involved. But there are circumstances with no real alternative — e.g. where directors refuse to issue financial statements, or the auditors wish to inform shareholders/creditors of concerns with no other immediate way to do so."}]},{"n":"2.11","t":"Auditors' responsibility to consider non-compliance with laws and regulations (NOCLAR)","b":[{"p":"Auditors should plan, perform and report on their audit recognising that an entity's non-compliance with law or regulations may materially affect the financial statements."},{"key":"Whether an act constitutes non-compliance is a **legal determination**, ordinarily beyond the auditor's professional competence — though training, experience and understanding of the entity/industry may let them recognise acts that may constitute non-compliance. The determination is generally based on the advice of a qualified legal expert, but can ultimately only be decided by a court of law."},{"p":"Laws and regulations relevant to the audit fall into **two categories**: (1) those relating directly to the preparation, inclusion or disclosure of specific items in the financial statements; and (2) those providing the **legal framework** within which the entity conducts business, where non-compliance may fundamentally affect operations and hence the financial statements."},{"h4":"§2.11.1 Responsibilities of the directors"},{"p":"Directors are responsible for ensuring compliance with applicable law and regulations, and for establishing effective arrangements to prevent and detect non-compliance. Neither assigning particular responsibilities to management, nor the audit process, relieves them of this. Assisting policies/procedures include:"},{"ol":["maintaining an up-to-date register of significant laws/regulations applicable to the entity's industry;","monitoring legal requirements and changes, ensuring operating procedures meet them;","instituting and operating appropriate internal control systems;","developing, publicising and following a code of conduct;","ensuring employees are properly trained and understand the code of conduct;","monitoring compliance with the code and disciplining employees who fail to comply;","engaging legal advisers to assist monitoring legal requirements; and","maintaining a record of complaints."]},{"note":"In larger entities, these policies may be supplemented by assigning responsibilities to an internal audit function, a legal department, a compliance function, and an audit committee. In some sectors (e.g. financial services), detailed laws specifically require directors to have compliance systems, breach of which could materially affect the financial statements — directors are also required to report certain instances of non-compliance to proper authorities on a timely basis."},{"h4":"§2.11.2 Responsibilities of the auditors"},{"p":"**Prevention**: it is not the auditors' function to prevent non-compliance — though the annual audit may act as a deterrent. **Detection**: auditors plan, perform and evaluate their work for a reasonable expectation of detecting material misstatement — recognising it may arise from non-compliance — but an audit cannot be expected to detect **all** possible non-compliance."},{"p":"This risk (of undetected non-compliance) is higher than for other misstatements, due to:"},{"ol":["many laws/regulations relating to operating aspects typically have no material effect on the financial statements, and their non-compliance is not captured by accounting/internal control systems;","the effectiveness of audit procedures is affected by inherent control-system limitations and the use of testing;","much of the evidence obtained is persuasive rather than conclusive; and","non-compliance may involve conduct designed to conceal it — collusion, forgery, override of controls, or intentional misrepresentation to the auditors."]},{"h4":"§2.11.3 Auditors in the public sector"},{"p":"Public sector auditors may have duties beyond those of private-sector auditors — e.g. auditors of local authorities must keep under review the legality of the authority's transactions, with a specific duty to act on prospective unlawful expenditure. Auditors of central government accounts have a statutory duty to confirm grants have been applied to their intended purpose and that expenditure conforms with the governing authority."},{"h4":"§2.11.4 Auditors' consideration of compliance"},{"p":"For laws relating directly to financial statement preparation/disclosure, the auditors should obtain sufficient appropriate audit evidence of compliance. For laws providing the entity's broader legal framework, auditors should perform procedures to help **identify** possible non-compliance, by:"},{"ol":["obtaining a general understanding of the applicable legal/regulatory framework and how the entity complies with it;","inspecting correspondence with relevant licensing/regulatory authorities;","enquiring of directors whether they are aware of possible non-compliance; and","obtaining written confirmation from directors that they have disclosed all known events involving possible non-compliance, and their actual/contingent consequences."]},{"p":"Building this general understanding uses existing knowledge of the entity's industry and regulatory environment to: enquire of management about applicable laws/regulations (and changes); enquire about the entity's compliance policies and procedures; discuss policies for identifying/evaluating litigation, claims and assessments; and discuss the legal/regulatory framework with auditors of foreign subsidiaries where relevant."},{"key":"Beyond this, auditors are **not required** to plan and perform other procedures specifically to identify non-compliance — doing so is outside the scope of a financial statement audit. But when carrying out procedures to form their opinion, they should stay alert for possible non-compliance that might affect the financial statements — via reading board/management minutes, enquiring of directors/legal counsel about litigation, and performing substantive tests of transactions/balances."},{"h4":"§2.11.5 Procedure when possible non-compliance is discovered"},{"p":"On becoming aware of possible non-compliance, the auditor obtains an understanding of the act and its circumstances, and enough information to evaluate its possible effect. Indications that non-compliance may have occurred include:"},{"ul":["investigations by a government department, or payment of fines/penalties;","payments for unspecified services, or loans to consultants/related parties/employees/government employees;","sales commissions or agents' fees appearing excessive relative to the norm;","purchasing at prices significantly above or below market price;","unusual transactions with companies not registered with tax authorities;","payments for goods/services made other than to the country of origin;","an accounting system that fails, by design or accident, to provide an adequate audit trail or sufficient evidence;","unauthorised or improperly recorded transactions; and","media comment."]},{"p":"In evaluating possible effects, the auditors consider: the potential financial consequences (fines, damages, expropriation of assets, enforced discontinuance, litigation); whether those consequences require disclosure; and whether they are serious enough to call into question the view the financial statements give."},{"p":"Where non-compliance is suspected, findings are documented (e.g. copies of records, minutes of conversations) and, subject to any third-party reporting requirement, discussed with directors. If directors do not provide sufficient information to show compliance, the auditors may seek legal advice — normally consulting the entity's own lawyers with the entity's permission, or, where that is not possible or appropriate, seeking their own advice."},{"p":"Where non-compliance is suspected or has occurred, the auditors reconsider their audit risk assessment and the validity of management representations. Relevant factors: any apparent failure of specific control procedures; the level of management/employees involved; and any concealment of the act."},{"h4":"§2.11.6 Reporting non-compliance to management"},{"p":"As soon as practicable, the auditors should communicate non-compliance that comes to their attention to management/the board/the audit committee — without delay, if in their judgment it is material or believed intentional. As with fraud, reporting goes to the next higher authority not suspected of involvement, with legal advice sought where no such authority exists or the auditors are unsure how to proceed."},{"h4":"§2.11.7 Reporting non-compliance to shareholders"},{"p":"Auditors consider report implications if they conclude a suspected instance has a material effect and disagree with the treatment/disclosure, or cannot determine whether it occurred — not refraining from qualifying the opinion merely because the position was since regularised, or because of the consequences of qualification. Even where the immediate financial effect is immaterial, disclosure may still be required because of potential **future** material consequences (e.g. an illegal payment leading to criminal proceedings or loss of business)."},{"h4":"§2.11.8 Reporting non-compliance to third parties"},{"p":"Following the same structure as fraud reporting (§2.10.5): the matter first reaches the directors/audit committee, extreme cases may warrant direct reporting to a proper authority without prior discussion where confidence in directors' integrity is lost, and the same confidentiality-vs-public-interest balancing and qualified-privilege protections apply. Proper authorities can include the Serious Fraud Office, Police Service, Securities Commission, recognised professional bodies, the Stock Exchange, Central Bank, Local Authorities, Internal Revenue Service, Customs/Excise & Preventive Service, and the Ministry of Trade and Industry."}]},{"n":"2.12","t":"Impact of laws and accounting standards on audit","b":[{"h4":"Companies and Allied Matters Act (CAMA)"},{"p":"Statutory audit in Nigeria is regulated by CAMA 2020, BOFIA 2020 and the Insurance Act 2003 — containing detailed regulation on the conduct of an audit, the accounting records the auditor works from, the financial statements reported on, and the auditor's relationship with the company."},{"p":"Schedule 2 of CAMA specifically requires every company to keep accounting records sufficient to show and explain its transactions such as to: (a) disclose, with reasonable accuracy at any time, the company's financial position; and (b) enable directors to ensure financial statements comply with CAMA's form/content requirements. Records must, in particular, contain day-to-day entries of all sums received/expended (and the matters they relate to), and a record of assets/liabilities. Where the business deals in goods, records must additionally contain: a statement of inventories held at year end; all stock-taking statements from which such inventory statements were prepared; and (except for ordinary retail sales) a statement of all goods sold and purchased, identifying buyers/sellers in sufficient detail."},{"h4":"Financial Reporting Council of Nigeria (FRCN)"},{"p":"The FRCN Act 2023 regulates financial reporting and corporate governance standards, including assurance services, strengthening audit oversight and quality assurance:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Function","What it covers"],"rows":[["**Establishment and mandate**","Empowered to develop and enforce accounting, auditing, actuarial, valuation and corporate governance standards; regulates the conduct of financial reporting/auditing professionals."],["**Registration and licensing**","Practising auditors must be registered with the FRCN; firms offering audit/assurance services must comply with FRCN registration and submit to its oversight."],["**Monitoring and inspection**","Conducts quality control inspections and practice reviews of audit firms for compliance — non-compliance can bring suspension, fines, or revocation of practice licences."],["**Adoption of standards**","Adopts and customises international standards (e.g. ISAs) for Nigeria's environment — auditors must comply when carrying out assurance engagements."],["**Whistleblowing and public interest**","Encourages whistleblowing on misconduct in financial reporting/auditing, and protects whistleblowers against retaliation."],["**Disciplinary actions**","Investigates and disciplines erring auditors/firms — sanctions may include publication of the disciplinary action, suspension, fines, or blacklisting."]]}},{"warn":"Penalties under the FRCN Act (at the board's discretion) include: a fine of **N5 million**; one year's imprisonment, or both, on conviction; and proscription or de-listing of the accountancy firm."}]},{"n":"2.13","t":"Scope of audit functions under CAMA, BOFIA and the Insurance Act","b":[{"p":"CAMA 2020 is the principal law governing incorporation and business conduct in Nigeria; BOFIA 2020 regulates banks and other financial institutions; the Insurance Act 2003 regulates insurance companies."},{"h4":"§2.13.1 Companies and Allied Matters Act"},{"p":"Section 404(1) requires the auditor to report to members on the accounts examined, and on every Statement of Financial Position, Statement of Comprehensive Income and group financial statement laid before the company in general meeting during the auditors' tenure."},{"p":"For a **public** company, the auditor must **also** report to an audit committee — consisting of an **equal number of directors and shareholder representatives** (maximum six members total), which examines the auditor's report and makes recommendations to the AGM as it sees fit. Subject to any additional powers the articles may stipulate, the audit committee's objectives/functions are to:"},{"ol":["ascertain whether the company's accounting and reporting policies accord with legal requirements and agreed ethical practices;","review the scope and planning of audit requirements;","review findings on management matters with the external auditor, and departmental responses thereon;","keep under review the effectiveness of the company's accounting/internal control systems;","make recommendations to the board on the appointment, removal and remuneration of the external auditors; and","authorise the internal auditor to investigate any activity of interest or concern to the committee."]},{"p":"Section 407(1) requires the auditor, in preparing their report, to carry out such investigations as enable an opinion on whether: (i) proper accounting records were kept, and proper returns adequate for the audit received from branches not visited; and (ii) the SFP and (if not consolidated) the Statement of Comprehensive Income agree with the accounting records and returns."},{"p":"Section 407(2): if the auditors are of the opinion that proper returns were not received, or the statements do not agree with the records/returns, they must state that fact in their report. Section 407(3): every auditor has a right of access at all times to the company's books, accounts and vouchers, and is entitled to require from company officers whatever information/explanations they think necessary. Section 407(4): it is the auditor's duty to consider whether the directors' report is consistent with the accounts, stating the fact in their report if it is not."},{"h4":"§2.13.2 Banks and Other Financial Institutions Act 2020 (BOFIA)"},{"p":"BOFIA 2020 regulates banks and other financial institutions in Nigeria, administered by the **Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN)**, aiming for high banking-practice standards and financial stability."},{"h4":"Publication of annual accounts of banks"},{"ol":["Subject to the CBN's prior written approval, a bank shall, not later than 4 months after its financial year-end: cause publication in an approved daily newspaper; exhibit the accounts conspicuously in each office/branch in Nigeria; and forward signed copies to the CBN, containing the full and correct names of the bank's directors;","Every published account must disclose in detail penalties paid for contravening the Act or any policy guideline in force during the year, and the auditor's report shall reflect such contravention;","The SFP and Statement of Comprehensive Income shall bear on their face the report of an \"approved auditor\" (one approved under s.28), and contain any statements specified by the bank from time to time;","Non-compliance is an offence, liable to a penalty of **not less than ₦5,000,000**, plus **₦100,000 for each day** the contravention continues."]},{"h4":"Contents and form of accounts"},{"ol":["Every SFP and Statement of Comprehensive Income must give a true and fair view of the bank's state of affairs as at the end of the reporting period;","Accounts forwarded to the CBN must comply with any CBN circular issued on the matter;","A director who fails to take reasonable steps to secure compliance with prescribed provisions is guilty of an offence, liable to a fine of **₦2,000,000** payable to the bank."]},{"h4":"Appointment, powers and report of the approved auditor"},{"ol":["Every bank shall annually appoint an \"approved auditor\" (approved by the CBN), whose duty is to report to shareholders on the annual SFP and Statement of Comprehensive Income, containing such other information as the CBN may prescribe;","The approved auditor must be: a member of a recognised professional body in Nigeria; approved by the CBN; resident in Nigeria; and carrying on professional practice as accountant/auditor in Nigeria;","The following are **ineligible** for appointment (and cease to hold office if they later acquire the disqualifying status): anyone with an interest in the bank other than as a depositor; a director, officer or agent of the bank; a firm in which a bank director has an interest as partner/director; or anyone indebted to the bank;","If a bank fails to appoint an approved auditor, the CBN shall appoint one and fix the remuneration payable by the bank;","An approved auditor contravening, or deliberately/negligently failing to comply with, required provisions is guilty of an offence, liable to a fine of **not less than ₦200,000 and not exceeding ₦500,000**;","The approved auditor's report is read together with the board's report at the AGM, and two copies of each (with an analysis of bad and doubtful advances) sent to the CBN;","If, in the course of duty, the approved auditor is satisfied that: (i) the Act (or any other law) has been contravened by the bank or another person; (ii) losses have substantially reduced the bank's capital funds; (iii) an irregularity jeopardises depositors'/creditors' interests; or (iv) they cannot confirm depositor/creditor claims are covered by the bank's assets — they must **immediately report** the matter to the CBN;","The approved auditor forwards two copies of a domestic report on the bank's activities to the CBN, not later than 3 months after the financial year-end;","Contravention here is an offence liable to a fine of **not less than ₦2,000,000**, and where the approved auditor is a firm, individual partners are additionally liable to imprisonment for **not less than 3 years**, or a fine of not less than ₦2,000,000, or both;","A bank cannot terminate an approved auditor's appointment without prior CBN approval (s.28)."]},{"h4":"§2.13.3 Insurance Act 2003 (as amended)"},{"p":"Insurance business is a special type of business — providing economic protection from identifiable risks over a specific period — so specific legal and regulatory requirements apply, including the Insurance Act 2020, CAMA 2020, IFRS 4 (now replaced by **IFRS 17**, effective January 2023), and relevant NAICOM circulars."},{"p":"Insurance business is classified into two main classes, with life insurance further split into three categories and general insurance into eight:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Life insurance categories","General insurance categories"],"rows":[["Individual life insurance business","Fire insurance business"],["Group life insurance and pension business","General accident insurance business"],["Health insurance business","Motor vehicle insurance business"],["—","Marine and aviation insurance business"],["—","Oil and gas insurance business"],["—","Engineering insurance business"],["—","Bonds, credit guarantee and suretyship insurance business"],["—","Miscellaneous insurance business"]]}},{"def":{"t":"Re-insurance business","d":"an advanced type of insurance business, beyond the two broad classes above, whereby insurance companies buy their own insurance against losses that might result from claims made against them by those they insure."}},{"h4":"Capital (s.10, as amended by NAICOM circular NAICOM/DPR/CIR/25/2019)"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Business type","Minimum share capital"],"rows":[["Life insurance business","Not less than N8 billion"],["General insurance business","Not less than N10 billion"],["Composite insurance business","Not less than N18 billion"],["Re-insurance business","Not less than N20 billion"]]}},{"h4":"Records of insurance companies (ss.17-19)"},{"p":"Where an insurer carries on both classes of business, separate and distinct accounts must be kept and carried to a separate insurance fund, appropriately named — each fund representing the liabilities for all contracts of that class, consisting of the reserves/provisions specified in s.19(2)(a) and (b)."},{"p":"Records to be kept and maintained at the insurer's principal office:"},{"ol":["The memorandum and articles of association (or other evidence of constitution), and a record of the names/addresses of the business owners (shareholders or otherwise);","Minutes of any meeting of the owners and of the policy-making executive (board of directors or otherwise);","A register of all policies — names/addresses of policyholders, dates policies were effected, and records of transfer/assignment/nomination the insurer has notice of;","A register of claims — date of claim, claimant's name/address, date settled (or, for a repudiated claim, the date and grounds of rejection; or, for litigation, its particulars and the court's decision);","A register of investments, showing those attributable to insurance funds and those not, and any value changes over time;","A register of assets;","A register of reinsurance ceded, showing separately those ceded within and outside Nigeria;","A cash book;","A current account book;","A register of open policies for marine insurance transactions;","Management report by external auditors; and","For life insurance business specifically: a register of assured under group policies; a register of loans on policies; a register of cash surrender values; and a register of lapsed and expired policies."]},{"h4":"Provisions and reserves (ss.20-23)"},{"p":"Every insurance company must establish and maintain:"},{"ul":["provision for unexpired risk;","provision for outstanding claims;","contingency reserves; and","a contingency reserve fund."]},{"note":"The specific rates and conditions for calculating each provision/reserve are set out in the corresponding sections of the Act — the auditor's work necessarily includes verifying these were computed on the prescribed basis."},{"h4":"Investment in assets (s.25) and returns to NAICOM (s.26)"},{"p":"Section 25 provides guidelines on how an insurance company must invest and hold Nigerian assets equivalent to, and not less than, the amount of policyholders' funds, specifying the types of property/securities those funds can be invested in."},{"p":"Section 26 requires an insurance company to submit to NAICOM, not later than 30 June each year: (a) a Statement of Financial Position as at the last financial year-end, plus the relevant profit and loss account for presentation at the AGM; (b) a revenue account for each registered class of insurance business, kept as separate receipts/payments accounts; and (c) a statement of investments representing the insurance funds. After NAICOM's approval, the company must publish its general SFP together with its Statement of Profit or Loss and OCI in at least one newspaper of wide circulation, covering general insurance, life assurance and composite business as applicable, disclosing (per class): premium recognition, expenses, claims (life only), policy liabilities (life only), valuation surplus/deficiency (life only), unexpired risk (general only), and the required financial statement disclosures."},{"key":"A composite insurer (life and general) follows the **same** disclosure requirements as above for both classes, and must additionally include a **separate Statement of Financial Position**. As with banks, the auditor should factor these insurance-specific issues into the audit from the planning stage onward, and the auditor's report should reference compliance with **both** CAMA and the Insurance Act 2003 (and relevant circulars) — not CAMA alone."}]},{"n":"2.14","t":"Chapter summary","b":[{"ol":["Every company, by law, should have its financial statements audited before circulation to members and other stakeholders.","The appointed auditor should be a professionally qualified accountant, duly licensed to practise.","Generally, the auditor is appointed at the AGM by members; where a casual vacancy exists, the directors may appoint one.","The auditor's remuneration is determined by the appointing authority — the members or the directors, as the case may be.","An auditor can resign before completing their term, giving notice to the client company.","An auditor can, alternatively, be removed from office by ordinary resolution before the expiration of their term.","The auditor, by virtue of appointment, has statutory responsibilities and rights that cannot be compromised in any way.","The auditor should demonstrate professional skill and competence, and uphold requisite professional ethics — competence, fairness, due care, objectivity and independence among the requirements.","The auditor may, in the course of work, be held liable under contract, the law of tort, and/or criminal law.","The auditor also has a responsibility towards the client's compliance with laws and regulations."]}]},{"n":"2.15","t":"Study text questions — multiple-choice and short-answer","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with 10 questions — 5 multiple-choice and 5 short-answer — reproduced below in full with the official answers, each explained. The Auditing study text sets **no examination-type questions**; this bank is the whole of its end-of-chapter material."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"The questions, with the official answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Which of the following is **NOT** a specific audit problem in insurance companies? (A) The treatment of premium income; (B) Unexpired risks; (C) The calculation of outstanding claims; (D) Outstanding directors’ remuneration; (E) Allocation of underwriting, claims handling and investment expenses.","Which of the following is **NOT** required as part of financial statements under Section 377 of CAMA? (A) A Statement of Value Added; (B) Statement of Cash Flows; (C) Statements of Accounting Policies; (D) Statement of Financial Position as at the end of the year; (E) The Chairman’s Report.","The statement that shows how the benefits of the effort of an enterprise are shared between employees, providers of capital and the amount of re-investment is known as (A) Statement of Cash Flows; (B) Statement of Value Added; (C) Financial Statement; (D) Statement of Comprehensive Income; (E) Auditor’s Report.","Which of the following letters confirms the acceptance and understanding of the audit assignment? (A) Comfort Letter; (B) Letter of Consent; (C) Engagement Letter; (D) Management Letter; (E) Letter of Representation.","Statements which show how the basic procedure contained in a standard is to be applied are described as (A) Accounting Standard; (B) Professional Pronouncements; (C) Guidelines; (D) Auditing Standards; (E) International Financial Reporting Standards.","Pronouncements or statements issued by government legislation, which regulate the implementation of auditing functions, are called ………","Those pronouncements or statements issued by professional bodies (e.g. ICAN, FRC) which state the basic procedures to be adopted by auditors when carrying out their functions of planning, examining and reporting on the financial statements prepared by the enterprise are called ………","When should an engagement letter be sent to a prospective client?","What is the penalty for laying or delivering defective financial statements, as specified by CAMA 2020?","The work of an external auditor is regulated mainly through legal and professional pronouncements. State **TWO** of the sources of these pronouncements."]}],"a":[{"ol":["**D** — outstanding directors’ remuneration. That is an ordinary audit matter for **any** company; the other four are peculiar to insurance, where the central difficulty is measuring liabilities that have not yet crystallised (§2.13.3).","**E** — the Chairman’s Report. It is customarily published in the annual report but is **not** one of the statutory financial statements, and the auditor does not report an opinion on it — though the auditor must read it and consider whether it is **inconsistent** with the audited statements.","**B** — the Statement of Value Added. It reports the wealth created by the entity and how it has been distributed among employees, government, providers of capital, and the amount retained for reinvestment.","**C** — the engagement letter (§2.9). Do not confuse it with the **management letter** (the auditor’s report to management on internal control weaknesses found during the audit) or the **letter of representation** (written confirmations obtained **from** management at the end of the audit).","**C** — guidelines. A **standard** prescribes the basic principles and essential procedures; a **guideline** gives guidance on how those procedures are to be applied in practice. Standards are mandatory; guidelines are persuasive.","**Statutory regulation.**","**Accounting standards** *(the study text’s own answer)*. Strictly, pronouncements governing the **auditor’s** procedures are **auditing standards**; accounting standards govern the **preparation** of the financial statements. Both are issued by the bodies named, which is presumably why the key gives the broader term.","**Immediately the auditor is appointed** — and in any event before the audit work begins, so that the terms are agreed in writing before either party acts on them (§2.9.3).","**A fine as specified by the Commission**, for any subsection not complied with, on every person who is a director — for a private company.","**Any two of:** (a) CAMA 2020; (b) professional pronouncements by ICAN; (c) accounting standards issued by the FRC."]}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"This is the densest, most statute-heavy chapter in the subject — the exam draws directly on specific CAMA section numbers (S.374, S.377, S.401, S.403, S.408, S.409, S.412, S.404, S.407) and specific numeric thresholds (the 15%/10% fee-dependence rule, the 10% trustee-shareholding rule, the various BOFIA/Insurance Act fines and capital minimums). Do not try to memorise every number in the BOFIA/Insurance Act subsections — but do know cold: who appoints/removes/pays auditors and under what section; the resignation vs. removal procedural differences; the 15%/10% independence fee threshold; the five recognised exceptions to confidentiality; and the letter of engagement's four purposes and nine contents. The fraud/error and NOCLAR sections (§2.10-2.11) are structurally identical to each other — learn the shared skeleton (prevention → risk assessment → detection → procedures when indicated → report to management → report to members → report to third parties → possible withdrawal) once, and both sections follow.","errors":["Confusing resignation (auditor-initiated, S.412) with removal (company-initiated by ordinary resolution, S.409) — they have different notice periods, different triggering parties, and different statutory objects.","Treating the 15% recurring-fee independence threshold as fixed at 15% for every client — it drops to 10% specifically for listed and other public-interest companies.","Assuming audit committee membership is identical between Chapter 1's general description (5 members: 3 members + 2 non-executive directors, per S.404(3)) and this chapter's public-company-specific description (an EQUAL number of directors and shareholder representatives, max 6, per S.404) — the CAMA sections describe genuinely different committee compositions depending on context, so quote the one the question is actually asking about.","Believing fraud detection is the auditor's primary duty under NOCLAR/fraud sections — prevention is explicitly NOT the auditor's function in either case; the auditor's role is detection through properly planned procedures, not prevention.","Forgetting that the engagement letter explicitly does NOT state the fees payable — only the fees and billing ARRANGEMENT (basis of computation, billing intervals) is included."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a specific audit problem in insurance companies?","o":["The treatment of premium income","Unexpired risks","The calculation of outstanding claims","Outstanding directors' remuneration","Allocation of underwriting, claims handling and investment expenses"],"a":3,"w":"Outstanding directors' remuneration is a general audit matter relevant to any company, not a problem specific to the insurance industry's own accounting peculiarities (premium recognition, unexpired risk, claims reserving, expense allocation).","src":"Chapter 2.13","sec":"2.13"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT required as part of financial statements under Section 377 of CAMA?","o":["A Statement of Value Added","Statement of Cash Flow","Statements of Accounting Policies","Statement of Financial Position as at the end of the year","The Chairman's Report"],"a":4,"w":"The Chairman's Report is not one of the required financial statement components under CAMA — the Directors' Report is, but the Chairman's Report is a distinct, non-mandatory document.","src":"Chapter 2.12","sec":"2.12"},{"q":"The statement that shows how the benefits of an enterprise's effort are shared between employees, providers of capital, and the amount of re-investment is known as","o":["Statement of Cash Flow","Statement of Value Added","Financial Statement","Statement of Comprehensive Income","Auditor's Report"],"a":1,"w":"The Statement of Value Added is precisely this — showing how the wealth generated by the enterprise's operations is distributed among employees, capital providers, government and retained for re-investment.","src":"Chapter 2.12","sec":"2.12"},{"q":"Which of the following letters confirms the acceptance and understanding of the audit assignment?","o":["Comfort Letter","Letter of Consent","Engagement Letter","Management Letter","Letter of Representation"],"a":2,"w":"The letter of engagement is precisely defined as the letter setting out the terms of the audit engagement, forming the basis of the contract, and confirming the auditors' acceptance of the appointment.","src":"Chapter 2.9","sec":"2.9"},{"q":"Statements which show how the basic procedure contained in a standard is to be applied are described as","o":["Accounting Standard","Professional Pronouncements","Guidelines","Auditing Standards","International Financial Reporting Standards"],"a":2,"w":"Guidelines are application-level statements showing how the basic procedures set out in a standard are to be applied in practice — distinct from the standard itself.","src":"Chapter 2.12","sec":"2.12"},{"q":"Under the general rule on undue dependence, recurring fee income from one audit client should not, in general, exceed what percentage of gross practice income for a listed or other public-interest company?","o":["5%","10%","15%","20%","25%"],"a":1,"w":"The general threshold is 15% of gross practice income, but this is tightened to 10% specifically for listed and other public-interest companies, given the heightened public interest in their audit independence.","src":"Chapter 2.7","sec":"2.7"},{"q":"Under CAMA, an auditor's statutory duty to report on whether proper accounting records have been kept is reported on what basis?","o":["In full detail regardless of the outcome","By exception only — mentioned only where the auditor is not satisfied","Only if requested by the audit committee","Only in years where the company is a public company","Only where fraud is suspected"],"a":1,"w":"These matters are reported \"by exception only\": where the auditor is satisfied, no mention is made in the report at all — mention appears precisely when, and only when, the auditor is not satisfied.","src":"Chapter 2.5","sec":"2.5"}],"theory":[{"q":"State the five recognised exceptions under which an auditor may disclose confidential client information.","marks":5,"a":[{"ol":["Suspected treason (also terrorism or drug/money laundering) — obliging disclosure to a competent authority.","Where there is a public duty to disclose, e.g. an offence contrary to the public interest has been committed.","Where disclosure is compelled by law, e.g. a member is required to give evidence or deliver documents in an action.","Where assisting the police, tax authorities or another authority, once satisfied the information is genuinely demanded under statutory authority.","Where the auditor knows or suspects a client has committed a wrongful act, and must ensure they have not prejudiced themselves in relation to it."]}],"src":"Chapter 2.6","sec":"2.6"},{"q":"Distinguish between fraud and error, and state who bears primary responsibility for their prevention and detection.","marks":5,"a":[{"p":"**Fraud** is the use of deception to obtain an unjust or illegal financial advantage, and intentional misrepresentation affecting the financial statements — involving manipulation/falsification of records, misappropriation of assets, suppression of transactions, recording of transactions without substance, or intentional misapplication of accounting policies."},{"p":"**Error** is an unintentional mistake — mathematical/clerical mistakes, or inadvertent/unintentional misapplication or misinterpretation of accounting policies. The distinguishing feature between the two is **intent**."},{"p":"Primary responsibility for preventing and detecting both rests with the **directors**, through adequate accounting and internal control systems (which reduce, but never eliminate, the possibility of either). It is explicitly **not** the auditor's function to prevent fraud or error, though the annual audit may act as a deterrent, and the auditor should plan work for a reasonable expectation of detecting material misstatement arising from them."}],"src":"Chapter 2.10","sec":"2.10"}]}},{"n":3,"t":"Professional Ethics and Public Interest","brief":"The ethical architecture behind every audit: the IFAC/IESBA Code and ICAN's own localised Professional Code of Conduct, the fundamental principles every member must uphold, actual vs. perceived independence, the conceptual framework of five threat categories and how safeguards address them, principles-based vs. rules-based ethics codes, the practical applications of independence and objectivity, the auditor's confidentiality obligations and their exceptions, acting in the public interest, and the NOCLAR (non-compliance with laws and regulations) whistleblowing framework.","outcomes":["Explain the fundamental principles of the IFAC and ICAN codes of Ethics and Conduct","Explain professional conduct for ICAN members","Apply the conceptual framework to identify threats to the fundamental principles and suggest appropriate safeguards across a range of common scenarios","Explain the applications of Professional Ethics responsibilities","Explain the auditor's responsibilities in relation to confidentiality","Assess ethical conflicts facing accountants in business or practice","Discuss how an accountant should respond to a suspected instance of NOCLAR"],"secs":[{"n":"3.1","t":"Fundamental principles of the ICAN Code of Ethics","b":[{"def":{"t":"Ethics","d":"a system or code of conduct based on moral duties, values and obligations that indicates how we should behave within a constituted body or society."}},{"p":"In Nigeria, auditors must comply with the rules of professional conduct issued by their professional bodies, and accounting standards issued by the Financial Reporting Council of Nigeria. This chapter is built on **IFAC's Code of Ethics for Professional Accountants**, published by the IESBA (referred to throughout as \"The Code\")."},{"key":"ICAN adopted the IESBA Code into its own localised code, the **\"Professional Code of Conduct and Guide for Members\"** — the substance remains largely unchanged. The ICAN Code must be followed by **all members (including student members)**, whether operating as external auditors, assurance providers or internal auditors — and also applies to the staff of an ICAN practice, regardless of whether they belong to ICAN or any other professional body."},{"note":"Although the guidance applies to all members, the examination is primarily concerned with how the rules apply to an **external auditor or assurance provider**. By providing a code of ethics, ICAN performs a core regulatory function — ensuring statutory audits are performed only by fit and proper persons acting with professional integrity."},{"p":"The ethical code covers:"},{"ol":["Fundamental principles;","Integrity, objectivity and independence;","Conflicts of interest;","Confidentiality;","Changes in professional appointment;","Consultancy;","Association with non-members;","Fees;","Obtaining professional work;","The names and letterheads of practising firms;","Members in business; and","Enforcement of ethical standards."]}]},{"n":"3.2","t":"Professional conduct for members of ICAN","b":[{"p":"The Professional Code of Conduct and Guide for Members guides members in the efficient discharge of their professional duties, requiring **strict observance** as a condition of Institute membership."},{"h4":"The fundamental principles"},{"ol":["A member should behave with **integrity** in all professional and business relationships — implying not mere honesty, but fair dealing and truthfulness;","A member should strive for **objectivity** in all professional and business judgments — the state of mind having regard to all considerations relevant to the task at hand, but no other;","A member should **not accept or perform work they are not competent** to undertake, unless they obtain the advice/assistance needed to competently carry it out;","A member should carry out professional work with **due skill, care, diligence and expedition**, with proper regard for expected technical and professional standards;","A member should conduct themselves with **courtesy and consideration** towards everyone they come into contact with while performing their work;","A member should respect the **confidentiality** of information acquired through professional and business relationships, and not disclose it to third parties without proper specific authority, unless there is a legal or professional right or duty to disclose."]},{"h4":"Due skill and care"},{"p":"It is a fundamental principle that ICAN members carry out work with professional competence and due care — reinforcing the basic contract-law principle that a service should be performed with a reasonable degree of skill and care."},{"key":"The implication of \"**due care**\" (or \"reasonable care\") is that audit work must be **adapted to the specific circumstances and characteristics of the client** — there is no such thing as a \"standard\" audit."},{"p":"If the auditor fails to exercise a proper degree of care, consequences may include:"},{"ol":["legal claims against the auditor in the law of contract or the law of tort (wrongdoing);","disciplinary proceedings against the auditor by ICAN; and","the auditor/firm earning a reputation in the business community for poor work standards, and consequently losing clients."]}]},{"n":"3.3","t":"Actual and perceived independence","b":[{"p":"For an audit or other assurance report to be of value, the assurance provider:"},{"ol":["must **be** independent (**actual** independence); and also","must **be seen to be** independent (**perceived** independence)."]},{"key":"An assurance provider's opinion must be independent, given by a professional with appropriate skill, and **uninfluenced by anyone else** — in particular, not influenced by the opinions or views of the client's management. This is why the fundamental principle of **objectivity** matters so much."},{"p":"Independence of the assurance provider — especially a statutory auditor — is a matter of **public confidence** in the assurance/audit process. Members must be fully aware of situations that may damage their independence and objectivity (called **\"threats to independence\"**); such threats may be reduced by safeguards an assurance firm puts in place."},{"p":"While The Code provides a conceptual framework recognising it is impossible to define every threat-creating situation and specify every safeguard, it gives specific guidance in ten key areas where independence may be, or may be seen to be, under threat:"},{"ol":["fees and pricing;","family and personal relationships;","close business relationships;","financial interests;","loans and guarantees;","employment or former employment with assurance clients;","long association of senior personnel with assurance clients;","provision of non-audit services;","gifts and hospitality; and","actual and threatened litigation."]}]},{"n":"3.4","t":"The conceptual framework: five threats to the fundamental principles","b":[{"p":"The Code considers how the fundamental principles are applied within a **conceptual framework**, acknowledging they might be threatened by a broad range of circumstances. This identifies five potential categories of threat:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Threat","Example"],"rows":[["**Self-interest**","An auditor earning a large proportion of revenue from one client may be unwilling to upset that client by issuing an unfavourable report."],["**Self-review**","An auditor performing accountancy work for a client, in addition to the audit, may find themselves reviewing their own work — and be less critical of it than of someone else's."],["**Advocacy**","Supporting the client in a legal case may lead to a perceived loss of independence."],["**Familiarity**","Acting for a client over a long period may mean the auditor becomes less critical of that client's reporting practices."],["**Intimidation**","A strong finance director may intimidate junior audit team members and persuade them not to report errors found during testing."]]}},{"key":"Members must **identify, evaluate and address** such threats. If not at an acceptable level (where a reasonable and informed third party would conclude compliance with the fundamental principles has been compromised), they must be addressed by: (i) eliminating the circumstances creating the threat; (ii) applying safeguards to reduce the threat to an acceptable level; or (iii) declining or ending the specific professional activity."},{"note":"Some threats have no adequate safeguard at all — e.g. for the advocacy-threat example above, it may be appropriate simply **not to act** for the client in that capacity, or to resign from the audit, since no safeguard reduces the threat to an acceptable level. Where a safeguard does exist, it must specifically match the threat — e.g. the familiarity threat from long audit tenure is mitigated by **rotating the engagement partner (and possibly the whole team) every few years**, not by an unrelated control. The Code also requires auditors to form an overall conclusion on whether the actions planned or taken eliminate the threats, or reduce them to an acceptable level."},{"h4":"Self-interest threats"},{"p":"Occur from the financial or other interests of members or their family. An **immediate** family member is a spouse (or equivalent) or dependant; a **close** family member is a parent, non-dependent child, brother or sister who is not an immediate family member."},{"p":"Circumstances that may give rise to self-interest threats include:"},{"ol":["financial interests, loans or guarantees;","incentive-based fee arrangements;","concern over employment security;","commercial pressure from outside the employing organisation;","inappropriate personal use of corporate assets;","close personal or business relationships;","holding a financial interest in a client, or jointly with a client; and","undue dependence on fees from a client."]},{"h4":"Self-review threats"},{"p":"Occur when a previous judgment needs re-evaluation by the member responsible for it — e.g. a member who maintained a client's accounting records may be unwilling to find fault with financial statements derived from them. Circumstances include:"},{"ol":["business decisions or data reviewed by the same person who made/prepared them;","being in a position to exert direct, significant influence over an entity's financial reports;","discovering a significant error during re-evaluation of the member's own prior work;","reporting on financial systems after being involved in designing/implementing them;","an assurance team member being, or having recently been, employed by the client in a position of direct, significant influence over the engagement's subject matter; and","performing a service that directly affects the subject matter of an assurance engagement."]},{"h4":"Advocacy threats"},{"p":"Occur when members promote a client's position or opinion to the point that subsequent objectivity may be compromised. Circumstances include:"},{"ol":["commenting publicly on future events;","situations where information is incomplete, or the argument being supported is against the law;","promoting shares in a listed company that is also an audit client; and","acting as advocate for an assurance client in litigation or dispute with third parties."]},{"warn":"If a client asks its audit firm to represent it in a legal dispute with the tax authorities over tax payable, the audit firm should **refuse** — acting as advocate in this way would put its objectivity under threat."},{"h4":"Familiarity threats"},{"p":"Occur when a close relationship makes members too sympathetic to others' interests. Circumstances include:"},{"ol":["a member able to influence financial/non-financial reporting or business decisions has an immediate family member who could benefit from those decisions;","long association with business contacts influencing business decisions;","accepting gifts or preferential treatment, unless the value is trivial and inconsequential;","over-familiarity with an organisation's management, such that professional judgment could be compromised; and","a former firm partner being a director/officer of the client, or an employee in a position of direct, significant influence over the engagement's subject matter."]},{"key":"The classic auditing example: a **senior team member (e.g. the engagement partner) working the same audit for several years** risks becoming too familiar with the client and its management, unable to take an objective view of the audit."},{"h4":"Intimidation threats"},{"p":"Occur when a member's conduct is influenced by fear or threats — e.g. encountering an aggressive, dominating individual at a client or employer. Circumstances include:"},{"ol":["threat of dismissal or replacement of the member (or a close family member) over a disagreement about an accounting principle's application, or how information is to be reported;","a dominant personality attempting to influence the member's decisions;","being threatened with litigation; and","being pressured to inappropriately reduce the amount of work performed, in order to reduce fees."]}]},{"n":"3.5","t":"Safeguards","b":[{"p":"Safeguards that may remove or reduce threats fall into three categories:"},{"ol":["safeguards created by the profession, legislation or regulation;","safeguards in the work environment; and","safeguards created by the individual."]},{"h4":"Safeguards created by the profession, legislation or regulation"},{"ul":["educational, training and experience requirements for entry into the profession;","continuing professional development requirements;","corporate governance regulations;","professional standards (such as ISAs);","professional/regulatory monitoring and disciplinary procedures (such as ICAN's own disciplinary procedures); and","external review by a legally empowered third party (such as a government-appointed regulator) of a member's reports or information."]},{"h4":"Safeguards in the work environment"},{"ul":["the employer's own monitoring, ethics and conduct programmes (internal training or mentoring);","recruitment procedures ensuring only high-calibre, competent staff are recruited;","appropriate disciplinary processes;","strong internal controls;","leadership that stresses ethical behaviour and expects it of employees;","policies/procedures to implement and monitor employee performance quality;","policies/procedures to implement and monitor engagement quality;","documented policies for identifying threats, evaluating them, and implementing appropriate safeguards;","communication of, and training on, such policies and procedures;","using different partners and engagement teams for non-assurance services to assurance clients;","policies/procedures stopping non-engagement-team individuals from inappropriately influencing an engagement's outcome;","policies/procedures empowering employees to report ethical issues to senior staff without fear of retribution;","discussing ethical issues with the client;","disclosing to the client (possibly via the audit committee) the nature of services provided and fees charged; and","consultation with another appropriate professional accountant."]},{"h4":"Safeguards created by the individual"},{"ul":["complying with continuing professional development requirements;","keeping records of contentious issues and the approach taken to decisions;","having a broader perspective on other organisations' operations, via relationships with other professionals;","using an independent mentor; and","keeping in contact with legal advisers and professional bodies."]}]},{"n":"3.6","t":"Principles-based ethics codes and rules-based ethics codes","b":[{"p":"A regulatory body could issue a code of ethics containing specific rules for specific situations — a **rules-based** code."},{"p":"Rules-based codes have several weaknesses:"},{"ol":["accountants face many different, complex and varied situations requiring an ethical decision — it is impossible to plan a rule in advance, without knowing the exact details of a situation, for every type of ethical problem;","the type of situations (ethical dilemmas) an accountant faces changes as the business environment changes, requiring frequent review and update of the rule book;","ethical views differ between countries and cultures — behaviour considered slightly unethical in one country might be perfectly normal in another, and a rule book cannot easily accommodate this."]},{"def":{"t":"Principles-based code of ethics","d":"a code that specifies general principles of ethical behaviour, requiring the professional accountant to act in accordance with those principles, using their own judgment to decide whether a particular course of action is \"proper\" or \"ethical\" in each case."}},{"key":"Both the IFAC and ICAN codes of ethics are **principles-based**, not rules-based — which is precisely why the conceptual framework of five threats and three safeguard categories (§3.4-3.5) matters so much: it is the structured way a principles-based code still gives concrete guidance without becoming a rigid, ever-incomplete rulebook."}]},{"n":"3.7","t":"Applications of professional ethics","b":[{"h4":"Independence"},{"p":"A member must exercise the objectivity and independence required of an auditor — having both **independence of mind** and **independence of appearance**, in a position to give an honest, unbiased opinion. A member must never knowingly allow their name to be associated with a misleading financial statement. To exercise independence, members in public practice must not:"},{"ol":["possess any direct or indirect beneficial interest in a company for which they or their firm act as auditors;","accept fees based on the success of an assignment, except where unavoidable due to legislation or an agreement to which they are not a party;","accept fees based on the turnover of the company they audit;","act for two opposing parties in a negotiation, claim or settlement, unless appointed as arbitrator under due process of law;","carry out audit work concurrently with work for the client in an executive capacity;","give or take loans from clients — nor should anyone closely connected with them make or receive such loans, or guarantees (overdue fees may, in some circumstances, constitute a loan);","accept goods or services from a client (or anyone closely connected) unless of modest value — acceptance of undue hospitality poses a similar threat;","establish family or personal relationships with client companies — it is desirable to avoid a professional relationship where a personal one already exists (e.g. mutual business interests among the client, the audit firm, and officers/employees/partners/staff of either);","allow the existence of actual or threatened litigation — this places the parties in an adversarial position that clearly undermines objectivity;","allow influences outside the practice, e.g. pressure from associated practices, bankers, solicitors, government, or introducers of business; and","accept any assignment that will give rise to, or create, a conflict of interest."]},{"h4":"Objectivity"},{"def":{"t":"Objectivity","d":"the state of mind having regard to all considerations relevant to the task at hand, but no other — sometimes described as \"independence of mind.\" Essential for any professional exercising professional judgment, and just as essential for members in business as for practising members."}},{"key":"Objectivity is contrasted with **subjectivity**: subjective decisions are taken from the individual's own point of view, weighing things like friendship, loyalty or self-preservation. While vital to ordinary life, such considerations have **no place in professional decision-making** — the auditor should assemble all relevant information from the books of account submitted to them, and base their opinion or decisions only on that data and the profession's guiding principles."}]},{"n":"3.8","t":"Auditors' responsibilities with regard to confidentiality","b":[{"p":"The IFAC Code states that professional accountants must respect the confidentiality of information about a client's or employer's affairs, acquired through professional services."},{"key":"The duty of confidentiality **continues even after** the relationship between the professional accountant and the client or employer has ended — it is not extinguished simply because the engagement is over."},{"p":"The ICAN Professional Code of Conduct and Guide for Members states that confidentiality obliges members in public practice to refrain from:"},{"ol":["disclosing confidential information — to persons outside the firm, or beyond a need-to-know basis within the firm/employing organisation — without proper specific authority, unless there is a legal or professional right or duty to disclose;","using confidential information for their own or a third party's personal advantage;","lapsing on confidentiality even in a social environment;","failing to stay alert to possible inadvertent disclosure, particularly given long association with a business associate or close/immediate family member;","failing to maintain confidentiality of information disclosed by a prospective client or employer;","failing to consider the need to maintain confidentiality of information within the firm or employing organisation; and","failing to take all reasonable steps to ensure staff under their control, and any advisers consulted, respect the member's duty of confidentiality."]}]},{"n":"3.9","t":"Exceptions to the duty of confidentiality","b":[{"p":"Circumstances where members are, or may be, required to disclose confidential information, or where disclosure may be appropriate:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Category","Detail"],"rows":[["**Permitted or authorised disclosure**","Disclosure is permitted by law and/or authorised by the client or employer."],["**Disclosure required by law**","E.g. producing documents or other evidence in legal proceedings; or disclosing infringements of the law to the appropriate public authorities."],["**Professional duty or right to disclose** (when not prohibited by law)","To comply with a member body's quality review; to respond to an inquiry/investigation by a member or regulatory body; or to protect a member's professional interests in legal proceedings."],["**Compliance obligation**","To comply with technical standards and ethics requirements."]]}},{"p":"When deciding whether to disclose, members should consider:"},{"ul":["whether the interests of all parties, including any third parties, could be harmed if the client or employer consents to disclosure;","whether all relevant information is known and substantiated (to the extent practicable) — where facts are unsubstantiated or information incomplete, professional judgment should determine what type of disclosure, if any, to make; and","the type of communication expected and to whom it is addressed — members should be satisfied the recipients are appropriate."]}]},{"n":"3.10","t":"Acting in the public interest","b":[{"key":"An aspect that separates a **profession** from a mere **trade** is that members of the profession are expected to act in the **public interest** — it is a responsibility of the accountancy profession not to act exclusively to satisfy the needs of a particular client or employer. When a client's or employer's demands appear contrary to the public interest, accountants should consider the public interest instead."},{"p":"Professional codes of ethics do not provide a clear definition of \"public interest,\" but it is usually associated with:"},{"ol":["detecting and reporting any serious misdemeanour or crime;","protecting health and public safety;","preventing the public from being misled by a statement or action;","exposing the misuse of public funds and corruption in government; and","revealing conflicts of interest among individuals in positions of power or influence."]},{"h4":"Ethical conflict resolution"},{"p":"In evaluating compliance with the fundamental principles, an accountant may need to resolve a conflict in their application. Consider:"},{"ol":["the relevant facts;","the ethical issues involved;","the fundamental principles related to the matter;","established internal procedures; and","alternative courses of action."]},{"p":"Having considered these, an accountant should determine the appropriate course of action, consistent with the identified fundamental principles, bearing in mind the consequences of each option. If unresolved, they should consult other appropriate persons within the firm/organisation — and, where the matter involves a conflict with or within an organisation, also consider consulting those charged with governance (e.g. the board or audit committee). Issues and relevant discussions should be **documented**."},{"h4":"Non-resolution"},{"p":"If a significant conflict cannot be resolved, an accountant may obtain professional advice from an appropriate Institute committee or legal advisers, gaining guidance on ethical issues without breaching confidentiality."},{"warn":"If, after exhausting all relevant possibilities, the ethical conflict **remains unresolved**, the accountant should, where possible, refuse to remain associated with the matter creating the conflict — determining it appropriate to withdraw from the engagement team or specific assignment, or to resign altogether from the engagement, the firm, or the employing organisation."}]},{"n":"3.11","t":"The NOCLAR (non-compliance with laws and regulations) framework","b":[{"p":"The IFAC Code provides additional guidance on how a professional accountant should respond when encountering non-compliance with laws and regulations (NOCLAR) — whether **intentional or unintentional**. Examples include:"},{"ol":["fraud, corruption and bribery;","money laundering, terrorist financing and proceeds of crime;","securities markets and trading;","banking and other financial products and services;","data protection;","tax and pension liabilities and payments;","environmental protection; and","public health and safety."]},{"p":"The NOCLAR framework explains:"},{"ol":["the process to follow when a professional accountant encounters a suspected fraud or illegal act (i.e. NOCLAR); and","the circumstances in which a professional accountant would override the fundamental principle of confidentiality and disclose the matter to an appropriate authority (**whistle-blowing**)."]},{"key":"Under the framework, auditors **cannot simply resign** from an engagement because of identified or suspected NOCLAR, without the matter first being appropriately addressed — resignation is not a substitute for following the framework's process."},{"h4":"Auditors' responsibilities"},{"p":"Where a professional accountant becomes aware of a NOCLAR-related matter, they must:"},{"ol":["obtain an understanding of the matter;","advise management to take appropriate, timely action (if not already done), to: rectify, remediate or mitigate the consequences of non-compliance; deter its commission where it has not yet occurred; or disclose the matter to an appropriate authority; and","determine whether further action is needed, by considering the appropriateness of management's response — including whether: the response is timely; the NOCLAR has been adequately investigated; action has been (or is being) taken to rectify/remediate/mitigate its consequences; action has been (or is being) taken to deter any not-yet-occurred NOCLAR; appropriate steps have been (or are being) taken to reduce the risk of recurrence (e.g. additional controls or training); and the NOCLAR has been adequately disclosed to an appropriate authority."]},{"p":"The accountant then determines whether **further action is needed in the public interest**, considering the legal/regulatory framework, the urgency of the matter, the integrity of management, and whether the NOCLAR is likely to recur. Further action might include:"},{"ol":["disclosing the matter to an appropriate authority, even absent a legal/regulatory requirement to do so; or","withdrawing from the engagement and the professional relationship, where permitted by law or regulation."]},{"p":"The professional accountant may disclose the matter to an appropriate authority if:"},{"ul":["the entity is engaged in bribery;","the entity is regulated, and the matter is significant enough to threaten its operating licence;","the matter could have adverse consequences for the financial markets;","products harmful to public health or safety are likely to be sold; or","the entity is promoting a scheme to help its clients evade taxes."]}]},{"n":"3.12","t":"Chapter summary","b":[{"ol":["This chapter dealt extensively with the professional pronouncements — guidelines, standards, codes of ethics and general conduct rules — issued by ICAN in compliance with IFAC requirements, and also covered aspects of the profession's relationship with the public.","Readers are equipped with the up-to-date fundamental principles and guidelines to follow while carrying out an audit assignment, and in other relationships with clients and people encountered during professional duties.","The chapter also discussed threats to audit independence and their resolution, and conflicts of interest — including beneficial shareholding and financial involvement of an auditor in a client's affairs."]}]},{"n":"3.13","t":"Study text questions — multiple-choice and short-answer","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with 16 questions — 8 multiple-choice and 8 short-answer — reproduced below in full with the official answers, each explained. The Auditing study text sets **no examination-type questions**; this bank is the whole of its end-of-chapter material."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"The questions, with the official answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Which of the following is **NOT** an objective of auditing standards and guidelines? (A) To minimise engagement risk; (B) To ensure quality control of an audit; (C) To enhance the initiative of the auditor; (D) To ensure uniformity in carrying out audit assignments; (E) To increase auditors’ image in the eyes of the users of the financial statements.","The professional accountant may disclose matters to an appropriate authority in the cases below **EXCEPT** …… (A) The entity engages in international business with other entities; (B) The entity is engaged in bribery; (C) The matter could result in adverse consequences to the financial markets; (D) Products harmful to public health or safety are likely to be sold; (E) The entity is promoting a scheme to its clients to assist them in evading taxes.","Which one of the following is **CONTRARY** to the concept of public interest on which the accountant is expected to act? (A) Detecting and reporting any serious misdemeanour or crime; (B) Protecting health and public safety; (C) Accommodating the activities of a client that are against public health; (D) Exposing the misuse of public funds and corruption in government; (E) Revealing the existence of any conflict of interest of individuals in a position of power or influence.","Which one of the issues below may **not** give rise to a self-interest threat? (A) Concern over employment security; (B) Commercial pressure from outside the employing organisation; (C) Inappropriate personal use of corporate assets; (D) Charging fees which are based on the work and time applied to the work; (E) Holding a financial interest in a client, or jointly holding a financial interest with a client.","The fundamental principles of professional conduct are as follows **EXCEPT** (A) A member should behave with integrity in all professional and business relationships; (B) A member should strive for objectivity in all professional and business judgment; (C) A member should not accept or perform work which he or she is not competent to undertake; (D) A member should carry out his or her professional work with due skill, care, diligence and expedition; (E) A member should always obtain the advice and guideline of senior members in all professional engagements.","Which of the following is a safeguard created by the **profession** against threats to independence, objectivity and integrity? (A) Continuing professional education requirement; (B) A corporate governance structure; (C) Peer pressures towards integrity and objectivity; (D) Partners’ high regard for their careers and reputation; (E) Emphasis on integrity and objectivity as hallmark qualities.","According to the professional code of conduct for members of ICAN, a member who discovers information which adversely affects accounts or statements that he is currently preparing or auditing should (A) Stop work on it immediately; (B) Report to the police; (C) Qualify his report on the accounts; (D) Report to the tax office; (E) Resign his appointment as auditor and refund all fees already received for the exercise.","Which of the sanctions listed below is **NOT** imposed by the Institute of Chartered Accountants of Nigeria for misconduct of its members? (A) Reprimand; (B) Payment of costs; (C) Closure of the practising office of the member; (D) Suspension from membership; (E) Removal of name from the membership register.","Where a standard or guideline issued by ICAN in Nigeria conflicts with that of an IFAC standard or guideline, which one prevails?","The way and manner in which each member is to relate to other members on the one hand and to society on the other, both from a moral perspective, is regulated by professional ………","According to the code of ethics issued by IFAC, independence requires both independence of ……… and independence of ………","A member may disclose confidential information to third parties in certain circumstances: YES/NO.","According to the ICAN professional code of conduct and guide for members, honesty, fair dealing and truthfulness imply ………","For an audit or other assurance report to be of value, the assurance provider must be seen to be ………","It is a fundamental principle that members should carry out their work with professional ……… and due care.","Among the safeguards created by the individual against threats is complying with continual ……… ……… requirements."]}],"a":[{"ol":["**C** — to enhance the initiative of the auditor. Standards exist to impose **consistency and a minimum quality**, which necessarily constrains individual initiative rather than enhancing it.","**A** — engaging in international business is **entirely lawful** and is no ground whatever for breaching confidentiality. The other four involve illegality or harm to the public interest, which may justify disclosure (§3.11).","**C** — accommodating client activities that are against public health. The accountant’s duty to the public interest **overrides** the duty to the client where the two conflict.","**D** — charging fees based on work done and time applied is the **normal, proper** basis of billing and creates no threat. (A **contingent** fee, dependent on the outcome of the work, would create a serious self-interest threat.)","**E** — always obtaining the advice of senior members is not a fundamental principle. Consultation is sensible where a matter is beyond one’s competence, but the principle is **competence**, not deference.","**A** — the continuing professional education requirement, which the **profession** imposes. (B) is a safeguard created by the **client**; (C), (D) and (E) are safeguards within the **firm’s own work environment** or personal attributes, not professional safeguards.","**C** — qualify his report on the accounts. The auditor’s remedy where a matter adversely affects the financial statements and is not properly dealt with is to **modify the opinion**, not to abandon the engagement or to report to outside bodies.","**C** — closure of a practising office. ICAN may discipline the **member** — reprimand, costs, suspension, removal from the register, withdrawal of a practising licence — but it has no power to close premises.","**The ICAN standard or guideline prevails** in Nigeria.","**Ethics.**","**Mind** and **appearance**. Independence of **mind** is the state of mind that permits an opinion without being affected by influences that compromise professional judgment; independence in **appearance** is the avoidance of facts and circumstances so significant that a reasonable and informed third party would conclude that integrity, objectivity or professional scepticism had been compromised.","**YES** — where disclosure is permitted by law and authorised by the client, required by law, or where there is a professional duty or right to disclose that is in the public interest (§3.11).","**Integrity.**","**Independent.** Note the words \"be **seen** to be\" — this is independence in **appearance**.","**Competence.**","**Professional development** (continuing professional development, CPD)."]}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"Learn the five threats (self-interest, self-review, advocacy, familiarity, intimidation) as a single memorised set — nearly every scenario-based question in this chapter reduces to \"which threat is this, and what safeguard fits it?\" The three safeguard categories (profession/legislation, work environment, individual) are the second half of that same pattern. Also know cold: the difference between actual and perceived independence; that both IFAC and ICAN codes are principles-based, not rules-based, and why (three weaknesses of rules-based codes); the confidentiality obligation surviving the end of the client relationship; and that NOCLAR forbids simply resigning without first following the framework's process (understand → advise management → assess response → determine further action).","errors":["Treating \"independence\" as a single concept — it is explicitly TWO things (independence of mind AND independence of appearance), and a threat to either one is a genuine problem, even if the auditor is in fact unbiased.","Confusing self-interest threats (the auditor's own financial stake) with self-review threats (re-evaluating the auditor's own prior work/judgment) — they sound similar but involve different mechanisms and different safeguards.","Assuming a rules-based code is more rigorous than a principles-based one — the chapter is explicit that rules-based codes have real, structural weaknesses (cannot anticipate every situation, go stale as the business environment changes, cannot flex for cultural differences) that principles-based codes are specifically designed to avoid.","Believing an auditor can simply resign when NOCLAR is suspected — resignation without first following the framework's process (understand, advise management, assess response, determine further action) is explicitly not permitted.","Forgetting that confidentiality survives the END of the client relationship — a common trap is assuming the duty lapses once the engagement or the client relationship ends."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Which of the following is NOT an objective of auditing standards and guidelines?","o":["To minimize engagement risk","To ensure quality control of an audit","To enhance the initiative of the auditor","To ensure uniformity in carrying out audit assignments","To increase auditors' image in the eyes of the users of the financial statements"],"a":2,"w":"Standards and guidelines exist to constrain and guide practice consistently — minimizing risk, ensuring quality and uniformity, and building user confidence — not to \"enhance the initiative\" of the individual auditor, which runs counter to their whole standardising purpose.","src":"Chapter 3","sec":"3.1"},{"q":"The professional accountant may disclose matters to an appropriate authority in the cases below EXCEPT","o":["The entity engages in international business with other entities","The entity is engaged in bribery","The matter could result in adverse consequences to the financial markets","Products harmful to the public health or safety are likely to be sold","The entity is promoting a scheme to its clients to assist them in evading taxes"],"a":0,"w":"Engaging in ordinary international business is not wrongdoing at all — the other four options are the genuine, listed NOCLAR disclosure triggers (bribery, threats to financial markets, harmful products, tax evasion schemes).","src":"Chapter 3.11","sec":"3.11"},{"q":"Which one of the following is CONTRARY to the concept of public interest that the accountant is expected to act on?","o":["Detecting and reporting any serious misdemeanour or crime","Protecting health and public safety","Accommodating the activities of the client that is against public health","Exposing the misuse of public funds and corruption in government","Revealing the existence of any conflict of interests of those individuals who are in a position of power or influence"],"a":2,"w":"Accommodating client activity against public health is the opposite of acting in the public interest — the other four options are all genuine public-interest actions the chapter lists.","src":"Chapter 3.10","sec":"3.10"},{"q":"Which one of the issues below may NOT give rise to a self-interest threat?","o":["Concern over employment security","Commercial pressure from outside the employing organization","Inappropriate personal use of corporate assets","Charging fees which are based on the work and time applied to the work","Holding a financial interest in a client or jointly holding a financial interest with a client"],"a":3,"w":"Ordinary time-and-work-based fees are the normal, unthreatening basis for charging — it is INCENTIVE-based fee arrangements (tied to outcomes, not time/work) that create a self-interest threat, not standard time-based billing.","src":"Chapter 3.4","sec":"3.4"},{"q":"The fundamental principles of professional conduct are as follows EXCEPT","o":["A member should behave with integrity in all professional and business relationships","A member should strive for objectivity in all professional and business judgment","A member should not accept or perform work which he or she is not competent to undertake","A member should carry out his or her professional work with due skill, care, diligence and expedition","A member should always obtain the advice and guideline of senior members in all professional engagements"],"a":4,"w":"The six fundamental principles are integrity, objectivity, competence, due skill/care, courtesy, and confidentiality — always deferring to senior members' advice is not one of them, and would in fact undermine a member's own independent professional judgment.","src":"Chapter 3.2","sec":"3.2"},{"q":"Which of the following is a safeguard created by the profession against threats to independence, objectivity and integrity?","o":["Continuing professional education requirement","A corporate governance structure","Peer pressures towards integrity and objectivity","Partners' high regard for their careers and reputation","Emphasis on integrity and objectivity as hallmark qualities"],"a":0,"w":"Continuing professional development/education requirements are explicitly listed among the safeguards created by the profession, legislation or regulation — the other options describe informal cultural pressures or individual motivations, not profession-level safeguards.","src":"Chapter 3.5","sec":"3.5"},{"q":"Which of the sanctions listed below is NOT imposed by the Institute of Chartered Accountants of Nigeria for misconduct of its members?","o":["Reprimand","Payment of costs","Closure of practising office of the member","Suspension from membership","Removal of name from membership register"],"a":2,"w":"Physically closing a member's practising office is a regulatory/enforcement power beyond ICAN's disciplinary sanctions, which are professional-standing measures (reprimand, costs, suspension, removal from the register) rather than operational shutdown powers.","src":"Chapter 3","sec":"3.1"}],"theory":[{"q":"Explain the five categories of threat to the fundamental principles under the conceptual framework, giving one example of each.","marks":10,"a":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Threat","Example"],"rows":[["Self-interest","An auditor earning a large proportion of revenue from one client may be unwilling to issue an unfavourable report."],["Self-review","An auditor who also performs accountancy work for a client may be reviewing their own work when conducting the audit."],["Advocacy","Supporting a client in a legal case may lead to a perceived loss of independence."],["Familiarity","Acting for a client over a long period may make the auditor less critical of that client's reporting practices."],["Intimidation","A dominant client finance director may pressure junior team members not to report errors found in testing."]]}}],"src":"Chapter 3.4","sec":"3.4"},{"q":"Distinguish between a rules-based and a principles-based code of ethics, and explain which type the IFAC and ICAN codes follow.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"A **rules-based** code contains specific rules about how an accountant should act in specific situations."},{"p":"A **principles-based** code specifies general principles of ethical behaviour and requires the accountant to use judgment in deciding, in each case, whether a course of action is proper or ethical."},{"p":"Rules-based codes have three key weaknesses: they cannot anticipate every possible situation in advance; they require frequent updating as the business environment changes; and they cannot easily accommodate differing ethical views across countries and cultures. Both the **IFAC** and **ICAN** codes of ethics are **principles-based**."}],"src":"Chapter 3.6","sec":"3.6"}]}},{"n":4,"t":"Auditors' Liability","brief":"What it means for an auditor to be held liable, and under which of four distinct legal routes: negligence under common law, liability under contract, statutory liability under CAMA (misfeasance and breach of trust), and criminal liability under the Nigeria Criminal Code for knowingly making a false statement to deceive investors.","outcomes":["Appreciate how liability arises in audit work","Describe how liability occurs under common law","Outline how criminal liability arises in auditing"],"secs":[{"n":"4.1","t":"Introduction","b":[{"p":"Statutory auditors perform audits and sign audit reports — their opinion on the truth and fairness of financial statements. The auditor has responsibilities to shareholders and other third parties who may rely on the certified financial statements. If the auditor fails to meet the required standard of care, and a loss is consequently suffered by an affected party, remedy can be obtained against the auditor in a constituted court."},{"key":"Because others rely on an auditor's work, the auditor has a clear responsibility to work **honestly and carefully**: they must be honest — never certifying what they do not believe to be true — and must take reasonable care and skill before believing what they certify **is** true."},{"p":"What \"reasonable care and skill\" means in a given case depends on the circumstances, and is genuinely difficult to assess. What is clear is the causal chain that liability follows:"},{"ol":["an auditor may fail to exercise sufficient skill and care;","consequently, fraud or error may go undiscovered, or the auditor may fail to discover that the accounts do not show a true and fair view, or contain a material misstatement;","consequently, somebody relying on the auditor's work may lose money;","that loss of money flows directly from the auditor's failure to do their job properly."]},{"warn":"Auditors may be liable for **professional negligence** — an issue of growing concern. Some firms have resorted to professional indemnity insurance to safeguard their businesses, but in concrete terms, insurance does not actually **solve** the underlying problem — it only cushions its financial consequence."},{"key":"Auditor liability is not a single thing — it can arise through **four distinct routes at once**: (1) common law negligence; (2) the law of **contract**, since a client may sue to enforce the auditor's contractual duties; (3) the law of **tort**, for duty of care, especially where a **third party** is involved; and (4) **statute**, facing criminal penalty for deception and reckless negligence, or civil liability for damages. This chapter works through common law, contract, statute and criminal liability in turn."}]},{"n":"4.2","t":"Auditors' liability for negligence under common law","b":[{"key":"An auditor incurs common law liability only where **both** conditions hold: they are found negligent in performing their duty, **and** the client suffers a loss as a result of that negligence. Where a loss occurs but **no negligence** can be attributed to the auditor, they cannot be held liable for damages — loss alone is never sufficient."},{"def":{"t":"Common law","d":"the generally accepted code of conduct, or customs, in interpersonal relationships — based on whatever is common across the different cultural practices of the land."}},{"def":{"t":"Negligence","d":"some act or omission occurring because the person concerned (e.g. an auditor) failed to exercise the degree of reasonable skill and care that is reasonably expected in the circumstances of the case."}},{"key":"What is \"reasonable\" is **not** what a super-careful, expert auditor would do — it is what an **ordinary, skilful person** would do in the circumstances. This is a meaningfully lower bar than perfection, and is the standard the whole chapter turns on."},{"h4":"Liability under contract"},{"p":"On accepting appointment, auditors enter a contract imposing certain obligations, arising from its terms — both express and implied. For a statutory audit, company law (CAMA 2020) prescribes the auditors' duties, responsibilities, rights and the express terms of the audit; **contract cannot override statutory provisions**."},{"p":"The implied terms the law imputes into an audit contract are:"},{"ol":["the auditors have a duty to exercise reasonable care;","the auditors have a duty to carry out the required work with reasonable expediency; and","the auditors have a right to reasonable remuneration."]}]},{"n":"4.3","t":"Statutory liability","b":[{"p":"Auditors may be liable for financial damage in respect of the civil offences of **misfeasance** and **breach of trust** — normally where they have misused their position of authority for personal gain. The law makes the following provisions:"},{"ol":["Under **Section 415(1) of CAMA 2020**, a company's auditor shall, in performing their duties, exercise all such care, diligence and skill as is reasonably necessary in each particular circumstance;","Under **Section 415(2)**, where a company suffers loss or damage from the auditor's failure to discharge the fiduciary duty imposed by subsection (1), the auditor shall be liable for negligence, and the directors may institute an action for negligence against them in court;","Under **Section 415(3)**, if the directors fail to institute such an action, any member may do so after giving 30 days' notice to the company of their intention to institute it. For a company in the process of winding up, the auditor may be held liable **\"as an officer\"** of the company — and, if found guilty of misfeasance, may be required to contribute to the extent of the loss arising from the breach of trust."]},{"key":"Section 415(3)'s member-initiated route is a genuine **fallback mechanism**: it exists precisely so that a negligence claim against the auditor is not left entirely at the directors' discretion — since the directors themselves might be reluctant to sue an auditor they have an ongoing relationship with."}]},{"n":"4.4","t":"Criminal liability under Section 436 of the Nigeria Criminal Code","b":[{"warn":"If any promoter, director, officer, or **auditor** of a company makes, circulates, publishes, or concurs in making, circulating or publishing any written statement or account which, to their knowledge, is **false in a material particular**, in order to deceive or defraud any person into investing in that company — that promoter, director, officer or auditor is guilty of a **felony**, liable to **imprisonment for seven (7) years**."},{"key":"This is the only route in the chapter carrying a specific, fixed prison term — and note it requires **knowledge** that the statement is false (i.e. deliberate deception), distinguishing it sharply from the negligence-based common law and statutory routes above, which do not require intent, only a failure of reasonable care."}]},{"n":"4.5","t":"Chapter summary","b":[{"ol":["Every company, by law, should have its financial statements audited before circulation to members and other stakeholders.","The appointed auditor should be a professionally qualified accountant, duly licensed to practise.","The auditor, by virtue of appointment and in executing the assignment, should demonstrate professional skills and competencies.","Upholding requisite professional ethics — competence, fairness, due care, objectivity and independence — is among the required competencies.","The auditor also has a responsibility towards the client's compliance with laws and regulations.","Auditors can incur liability under **common law**, **CAMA**, and the **Criminal Code**, if they do not exercise care and due diligence in performing their duties."]}]},{"n":"4.6","t":"Study text questions — multiple-choice and short-answer","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with 10 questions — 5 multiple-choice and 5 short-answer — reproduced below in full with the official answers, each explained. The Auditing study text sets **no examination-type questions**; this bank is the whole of its end-of-chapter material."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"The questions, with the official answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["An auditor can be liable to a client or third parties **EXCEPT** for: (A) Negligence under common law; (B) Obeying a court order to release confidential information; (C) Section 643 of CAMA; (D) Section 436 of the Nigeria Criminal Code; (E) Breach of Section 368 of CAMA.","The auditor can take the following precautions to prevent him from being liable during his audit assignment **EXCEPT**: (A) Maintaining quality control, using a written audit programme and keeping proper working papers; (B) Minimising reliance on the client’s representation or even third party confirmation; (C) Giving snap advice, or where he does, making it clear that it is subject to limitation; (D) Agreeing the auditor’s duties and responsibilities precisely in a letter of engagement; (E) Ignoring the expectation gap.","An auditor who accepts an audit engagement and does not have any knowledge about the organisation should: (A) Engage financial experts familiar with the nature of the organisation; (B) Inform management that he first needs to attend an MCPE programme before the commencement of the audit; (C) Obtain knowledge of matters that relate to the nature of the organisation; (D) Refer a substantial portion of the audit to another member who will act as principal auditor; (E) Write to the organisation’s legal adviser for legal advice.","An auditor must exercise due professional care by (A) Obtaining professional experience and formal education; (B) Examining all available corroborating evidence; (C) Critically reviewing the judgment exercised at every level of supervision; (D) Reducing audit risk to the barest minimum; (E) Preventing fraud and irregularities.","Which of the following statements is **FALSE**? (A) An auditor may fail to exercise sufficient skill and care; (B) The audit engagement is a contract; (C) An auditor could be imprisoned if he becomes criminally liable; (D) An auditor is above all forms of liability under common law; (E) Making a misleading statement could lead to criminal liability.","\"Reasonable care and skill\" depends on the circumstances and is very difficult to assess in any given case. TRUE/FALSE.","An act or omission which occurs because a person (e.g. an auditor) failed to exercise that degree of reasonable skill and care which is reasonably expected of him is called ………","When an auditor is found negligent in the performance of his duty and the client suffers a loss as a result, the auditor will incur ……… and pay ………","An appointed auditor should be a professionally qualified accountant who has been duly licensed to ………","The law requires that the auditor has responsibility to shareholders and ……… who may place reliance on the financial statements examined by him."]}],"a":[{"ol":["**B** — obeying a court order. Disclosure **required by law** is a complete answer to a claim for breach of confidence; the auditor who complies with a court order incurs no liability for doing so.","**E** — ignoring the expectation gap. The gap between what users **believe** an audit does and what it actually does is a principal source of claims against auditors, so it must be actively **narrowed** — by explaining responsibilities in the engagement letter and in the audit report — not ignored.","**C** — obtain knowledge of the matters relating to the nature of the organisation. Obtaining an understanding of the entity and its environment is a **required** audit procedure and the foundation of risk assessment (§5.3).","**D** — reducing audit risk to the barest minimum *(the study text’s answer)*. Note that (C) — critical review of the judgment exercised at every level of supervision — is also a textbook description of due care, and many examiners would accept it; (E) is wrong because **preventing** fraud is the directors’ responsibility, not the auditor’s.","**D** — an auditor is emphatically **not** above liability under common law. Liability in negligence to the client arises in both contract and tort, and may extend to third parties where the *Caparo* proximity tests are satisfied (§4.2).","**TRUE.** There is no fixed standard; what is reasonable is judged against the conduct of a reasonably competent auditor in the same circumstances, which is why each case turns on its own facts.","**Negligence.**","**Liability**, and pay **damages** (compensation for the loss suffered).","**Practise** — i.e. who holds a current practising licence from his professional body and is not disqualified under s.403 of CAMA (§2.3.1).","**Third parties** — but only those third parties to whom a duty of care is owed on the *Caparo* principles: the auditor must know the identity of the party and the specific transaction for which the accounts will be used, and must know that they will be relied on without independent enquiry."]}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"A short chapter, but exam questions test the boundaries precisely: common law liability requires BOTH negligence AND a resulting loss — loss alone, without negligence, is never enough. Know the \"ordinary skilful person\" standard (not \"super-careful expert\") as the actual bar for reasonable care. Know the three implied contract terms. Know that CAMA s.415 gives shareholders (via s.415(3)) a fallback route to sue if the directors decline to. And know that criminal liability under s.436 of the Criminal Code specifically requires KNOWLEDGE of falsity — it is the one liability route in this chapter that is not simply about negligence.","errors":["Assuming any loss suffered by a client automatically makes the auditor liable — common law liability requires BOTH a finding of negligence AND a resulting loss; loss without negligence gives no claim.","Applying the \"super-careful expert\" standard when assessing reasonable care — the actual legal standard is what an ORDINARY, skilful person would do in the circumstances, a lower and more realistic bar.","Treating CAMA s.415 as the only route to hold an auditor liable — statutory, common law, contract, tort and criminal liability are all independent routes that can apply simultaneously to the same facts.","Believing criminal liability under s.436 can arise from ordinary negligence — it specifically requires the statement be false TO THE AUDITOR'S KNOWLEDGE, made to deceive or defraud; negligent (non-deliberate) error does not meet this bar.","Forgetting that contract cannot override statutory provisions — an engagement letter or other contract term cannot lawfully contract out of CAMA's statutory requirements."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"An auditor can be liable to client or third parties EXCEPT for:","o":["Negligence under common law","Obeying a court order to release confidential information","Section 643 of CAMA","Section 436 of Nigeria Criminal Code","Breach of Section 368 of CAMA"],"a":1,"w":"Disclosure compelled by a court order is a recognised legal exception to confidentiality (Chapter 3's §3.9) — complying with it is not itself a source of liability; it is the auditor doing exactly what the law requires.","src":"Chapter 4","sec":"4.1"},{"q":"The auditor can take the following precautions to prevent liability during an audit assignment EXCEPT:","o":["Maintaining quality control, using a written audit programme and keeping proper working papers","Minimizing reliance on client's representation or even third-party confirmation","Giving snap advice, or where he does, making clear it is subject to limitation","Agreeing the auditor's duties and responsibilities precisely in a letter of engagement","Ignoring the expectation gap"],"a":4,"w":"Ignoring the audit expectation gap is the opposite of a precaution — it leaves misconceptions about the auditor's role unaddressed, which is itself a source of liability exposure, not a safeguard against it.","src":"Chapter 4","sec":"4.1"},{"q":"An auditor who accepts an audit engagement and does not have any knowledge about the organization should:","o":["Engage financial experts familiar with the nature of the organization","Inform management that he first needs to attend the MCPE programme before commencement of the audit","Obtain knowledge of matters that relate to the nature of the organization","Refer a substantial portion of the audit to another member who will act as principal auditor","Write the organization's legal adviser for legal advice"],"a":2,"w":"Obtaining sufficient knowledge of the client's business — its industry, operations, products, financial performance and reporting environment — is the auditor's own direct responsibility before accepting or proceeding with an engagement (Chapter 2's client knowledge requirement), not something to be outsourced.","src":"Chapter 4","sec":"4.1"},{"q":"An auditor must exercise due professional care by","o":["Obtaining professional experience and formal education","Examining all available corroborating evidence","Critically reviewing the judgment exercised at every level of supervision","Reducing audit risk to the barest minimum","Preventing fraud and irregularities"],"a":3,"w":"Due professional care is exercised by reducing audit risk to an acceptably low level through properly planned and performed procedures — consistent with Chapter 1's treatment of audit risk and reasonable assurance.","src":"Chapter 4","sec":"4.1"},{"q":"Which of the following statements is FALSE?","o":["An auditor may fail to exercise sufficient skill and care","The audit engagement is a contract","An auditor could be imprisoned if he becomes criminally liable","An auditor is above all forms of liability under common law","Making a misleading statement could lead to criminal liability"],"a":3,"w":"This is false precisely because it contradicts the whole chapter: auditors are NOT above common law liability — they incur it whenever found negligent and a loss results from that negligence.","src":"Chapter 4.2","sec":"4.2"}],"theory":[{"q":"Distinguish between an auditor's common law liability and criminal liability under Section 436 of the Nigeria Criminal Code.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"**Common law liability** arises from negligence — a failure to exercise the degree of reasonable skill and care an ordinary skilful auditor would exercise in the circumstances — combined with a resulting loss to the client. It does not require any intent to deceive; an honest mistake made without reasonable care is still sufficient for liability."},{"p":"**Criminal liability under s.436** requires the auditor to make, circulate or publish (or concur in doing so) a written statement or account that is false **to their knowledge**, specifically in order to deceive or defraud someone into investing in the company. It therefore requires deliberate, knowing deception — not mere negligence — and carries a fixed penalty of imprisonment for seven years."}],"src":"Chapter 4.2","sec":"4.2"},{"q":"State the three implied terms the law imputes into an audit contract.","marks":3,"a":[{"ol":["The auditors have a duty to exercise reasonable care.","The auditors have a duty to carry out the required work with reasonable expediency.","The auditors have a right to reasonable remuneration."]}],"src":"Chapter 4.2","sec":"4.2"}]}},{"n":5,"t":"Audit Planning and Documentation","brief":"How an audit is actually organised before fieldwork begins: the aims and procedures of audit planning, the overall audit strategy vs. the detailed audit plan, the Audit Planning Memorandum, why and how the auditor builds knowledge of the client's business, the audit risk model (inherent, control and detection risk) and materiality, and the audit documentation itself — working papers, and the permanent vs. current audit file split.","outcomes":["Describe the aims of audit planning","Outline the audit planning procedures","Appreciate the need to have knowledge about a client's business before commencing actual audit work","Outline the aspects of a client's business that the auditor should know","List the sources from where knowledge of a client's business can be obtained","Understand the components of audit risk","Distinguish between risk-based, procedural, and other approaches to audit and review work","Describe and illustrate the contents of audit plans and audit programmes","Explain the importance of audit documentation required for different types of assignments","Differentiate between current and permanent audit files"],"secs":[{"n":"5.1","t":"Audit planning procedures","b":[{"def":{"t":"Audit plan","d":"the specific guideline followed when conducting an audit — helping the auditor obtain sufficient evidence, keep audit cost at a reasonable level, and avoid misunderstandings with the client."}},{"p":"The form and nature of planning required is affected by the enterprise's size and complexity, its commercial environment, its transaction-processing methods, and its reporting requirements."},{"def":{"t":"Audit planning","d":"the formulation of the general strategy for an audit, which sets the audit's direction, describes its expected scope and conduct, and guides the development of the audit programme."}},{"p":"Adequate audit planning aims at:"},{"ol":["establishing the intended means of achieving the audit's objectives;","assisting in the direction and control of the work;","helping ensure attention is devoted to critical aspects of the audit work;","ensuring the work is completed expeditiously;","facilitating review of the audit work; and","helping assign proper tasks to audit team members and co-ordinating outside experts."]},{"h4":"§5.1.1 Planning an audit of financial statements"},{"ul":["The auditor should plan the audit so the engagement is performed effectively and efficiently;","Planning involves establishing the overall audit strategy and developing an audit plan, to reduce audit risk to an acceptably low level;","Planning involves the engagement partner and other key team members, to benefit from their experience/insight and enhance the planning process's effectiveness/efficiency;","Adequate planning ensures appropriate attention to important aspects/areas; that potential problems are identified and resolved on a timely basis; and that the engagement is properly organised and managed;","Adequate planning also assists proper task assignment, facilitates direction/supervision/review of team members' work, and helps coordinate the work of component auditors and experts."]},{"key":"Planning is **not a discrete phase** of an audit — it is a **continual and iterative process**, often beginning shortly after (or in connection with) the completion of the previous audit, and continuing until the current engagement is complete."},{"h4":"§5.1.2 Preliminary engagement activities"},{"p":"At the beginning of the current engagement, the auditor should:"},{"ol":["perform procedures regarding continuance of the client relationship and the specific engagement;","evaluate compliance with ethical requirements, including independence; and","establish an understanding of the engagement's terms."]},{"key":"The purpose is to ensure the auditor plans an engagement for which: **independence and the ability to perform** the engagement are maintained; there are **no management-integrity issues** that would affect willingness to continue; and there is **no misunderstanding** with the client over the engagement's terms."},{"h4":"§5.1.3 The overall audit strategy"},{"def":{"t":"Overall audit strategy","d":"sets the scope, timing and direction of the audit, and guides development of the more detailed audit plan."}},{"p":"Establishing it involves:"},{"ol":["determining the engagement's scope-defining characteristics (financial reporting framework, industry-specific requirements, locations of entity components);","ascertaining the reporting objectives, to plan timing and required communications (interim/final reporting deadlines, key dates for management/governance communication); and","considering the factors determining the team's focus (materiality levels, preliminary high-risk areas, material components/balances, whether to test internal control effectiveness, recent significant developments)."]},{"p":"The overall audit strategy sets out clearly:"},{"ul":["the resources to deploy for specific areas (e.g. experienced staff for high-risk areas, expert involvement for complex matters);","the number of resources allocated (material locations, review of other auditors' work, the hour budget for high-risk areas);","when resources are deployed (interim stage or key cut-off dates); and","how resources are managed, directed and supervised (briefing/debriefing meetings, partner/manager review timing, quality control review completion)."]},{"note":"Although the auditor ordinarily establishes the overall strategy before the detailed plan, the two are **not strictly sequential** — they are closely inter-related, since changes in one often trigger consequential changes to the other. In audits of small entities, strategy-setting need not be complex or time-consuming — a brief memorandum from the previous audit's completion, updated through discussion with the owner-manager, can suffice."},{"h4":"§5.1.4 The audit plan"},{"p":"The audit plan is **more detailed** than the overall strategy, including the nature, timing and extent of procedures team members will perform to obtain sufficient appropriate audit evidence. It includes:"},{"ol":["a description of the nature, timing and extent of planned risk assessment procedures, sufficient to assess the risks of material misstatement;","a description of the nature, timing and extent of planned further audit procedures at the assertion level, for each material class of transactions, account balance and disclosure;","the plan for further audit procedures, reflecting whether to test control-operating effectiveness, and the nature/timing/extent of substantive procedures; and","other required audit procedures for the engagement."]},{"key":"Planning of specific further audit procedures **depends on the outcome of risk assessment procedures** — risk assessment planning ordinarily happens early, while detailed substantive/control-testing planning follows once those risk assessment results are known."},{"h4":"§5.1.5 Changes to planning decisions during the audit"},{"p":"The overall strategy and audit plan should be updated as necessary during the audit. Planning is continual and iterative — unexpected events, changed conditions, or contradictory audit evidence may require the auditor to modify the strategy/plan and the resulting nature/timing/extent of further procedures, re-evaluating planned procedures based on revised risk assessment."},{"h4":"§5.1.6 Direction, supervision and review of audit work"},{"key":"The nature/timing/extent of direction, supervision and review are planned based on the **assessed risk of material misstatement** — as that risk increases for a given audit area, the auditor ordinarily increases direction/supervision extent and timeliness, and performs a more detailed review, also factoring in the team's own capabilities and competence."},{"note":"In a small entity audited entirely by the engagement partner (possibly a sole practitioner), questions of directing/supervising a team simply do not arise — but the partner still needs to be satisfied the audit follows ISAs. Forming an objective view of judgments made can be practically difficult when the same individual performed the entire audit — for complex or unusual issues, it may be desirable to consult other experienced auditors or the professional body."},{"h4":"§5.1.7 Procedures of audit planning"},{"p":"The auditor considers the outline audit approach, including reliance on internal controls and any area needing particular attention, plus any agreed additional work. Preparatory procedures to consider:"},{"ol":["reviewing matters raised in the previous year's audit with continuing relevance;","assessing the effects of legislative/accounting-practice changes on the financial statements;","reviewing interim/management accounts and consulting management/staff (current trading circumstances, significant business/management changes);","identifying significant changes in accounting procedures (e.g. new accounting software);","determining the number, experience and special skills of audit staff needed, and their visit timing;","briefing the team on the client's affairs and the required work's nature/scope;","considering the need for expert help and involvement of auditors in group audits;","preparing the planning memorandum setting out the outline audit approach;","establishing materiality and risk factors;","setting out the audit programme of tests to be undertaken;","establishing initial sample sizes for testing;","setting staff allocation and a fee budget — used to control time spent, with major variances (under or over) investigated by the engagement manager;","informing the client of the auditor's expected attendance dates; and","liaising with joint auditors, where applicable, to allocate work and agree control/review procedures."]}]},{"n":"5.2","t":"Audit Planning Memorandum (APM)","b":[{"p":"It is essential that the auditor documents the overall audit plan to cover:"},{"ol":["the timing of the audit work;","changes in the client's business since the previous audit;","decisions taken as a result of the planning process;","assessment of the client's internal audit;","briefing instructions to the audit team;","control, supervision and review of the audit work; and","the staff and time budget."]},{"key":"Although the APM is prepared **before** audit work starts, planning does not end there — as the audit progresses, the auditor **reviews and revises** the plan as appropriate. Many firms now formalise this with a **Standard Audit Planning Memorandum**, used to record initial planning decisions and reviewed during the audit to suit the specific client and assignment."},{"h4":"Specimen structure of an Audit Planning Memorandum"},{"p":"A typical APM (here based on a specimen used by a Ghanaian audit firm) is organised into six numbered parts, plus a time budget:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Part","What it records"],"rows":[["**1. Job time table**","Planned vs. actual dates for: provisional audit timing, the planning meeting with the client, commencement and conclusion of the interim audit, and debtors/creditors circularisation."],["**2. Changes since previous audit**","Detailed comments on changes in the product range/terms of sale; in management structure or key financial personnel; in the accounting system (including changes resulting from previous management letters); and in external requirements (accounting standards, legislation, rules, etc.)."],["**3. Planning decisions**","The main changes to the audit programme from the previous period; areas where material errors were previously identified and the audit emphasis to place on them; which systems are to be flowcharted; which permanent-file sections need creating/updating; specialist assistance required; whether an audit committee is mandatory before the audit report is signed; a summary of schedules the client staff will provide; and other services the firm provides and their impact on audit nature/timing."],["**4. Internal audit department**","The extent to which audit tests will be reduced by relying on the internal auditor's work, and the liaison arrangements with internal audit at both the planning and final stage."],["**5. Briefing instructions**","Any required sequence of work; specific points needing particular attention or assigned to a specific person; and the date, names and levels of staff at the briefing meeting."],["**6. Supervision and review**","Particular stages/areas of difficulty where the senior refers back to the manager, and how/when the transaction audit is supervised and reviewed."],["**Time budget**","Planned hours by grade — e.g. Engagement Partner, Manager, Assistant Manager, Supervisor, Audit Seniors, Audit Assistants — plus an explanation of any significant variance against the prior year's budget and actual time."]]}}]},{"n":"5.3","t":"Knowledge of the client's business","b":[{"h4":"§5.3.1 Introduction"},{"key":"Knowledge-building happens in **two stages**: prior to accepting an engagement, the auditor obtains **preliminary** knowledge (industry, ownership, management, operations) sufficient to judge their ability and willingness to undertake the audit; after acceptance, they obtain **more detailed** knowledge sufficient to plan the audit, develop an effective approach, and understand events/practices with a significant effect on the financial statements or the audit work."},{"p":"What should be known about the client:"},{"ol":["general economic factors and industry conditions affecting the entity's business;","important characteristics of the entity, its business, financial performance and reporting requirements, including changes since the last audit;","the general level of competence of management, and recent management changes;","the accounting policies adopted and changes in them;","the effect of new legislation, accounting or auditing pronouncements;","the regulatory framework, if any; and","the auditors' cumulative knowledge of the accounting/internal control systems and any expected changes."]},{"note":"In succeeding periods, the auditor considers previously gathered information and performs procedures to identify **significant changes** since the last audit — client knowledge is cumulative across audits, not rebuilt from scratch each time."},{"h4":"§5.3.2 Sources of knowledge"},{"ol":["previous experience with the entity and its industry;","discussion with people within the entity (junior and senior managers);","discussion with internal audit personnel, and review of internal audit reports;","discussion with auditors (including predecessor auditors) and legal/other advisers who have served the entity or industry;","discussion with knowledgeable outsiders (industry economists, regulators, customers, suppliers, competitors);","industry publications (government, banks, regulators, financial newspapers);","legislation and regulations significantly affecting the entity;","visits to the entity's premises and plant facilities;","internal documents (minutes, shareholder materials, promotional literature, prior-year annual/financial reports, budgets, management reports, interim reports, policies, accounts, job descriptions, marketing/sales plans); and","industry-specific guidance (e.g. government/regulator circulars)."]},{"h4":"§5.3.3 Using the knowledge"},{"p":"Understanding the business, and using it appropriately, assists the auditor in:"},{"ol":["assessing risks and identifying problems;","planning and performing the audit effectively and efficiently; and","evaluating audit evidence."]},{"p":"Business knowledge informs judgment throughout the audit, for example when:"},{"ul":["assessing inherent and control risks;","considering business risks and management's response to them;","developing the overall audit plan and audit programme;","determining a materiality level, and assessing whether it remains appropriate;","assessing audit evidence's appropriateness and the validity of related assertions;","evaluating accounting estimates and management representations;","identifying areas needing special audit consideration and skill;","identifying related parties and related-party transactions;","recognising conflicting information (e.g. contradictory representations);","making informed enquiries and assessing the reasonableness of answers; and","considering the appropriateness of accounting policies and disclosures."]},{"key":"The engagement partner must ensure assistants assigned to the audit obtain sufficient business knowledge to carry out their delegated work — passed on initially via the APM or a briefing meeting, and subsequently as the audit proceeds. The partner must also ensure assistants understand the need to **actively share** additional information they encounter, not just receive it."},{"h4":"§5.3.4 Matters to consider"},{"p":"The matters relevant to knowledge of the client's business fall into five groups:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Group","Key matters"],"rows":[["**General economic factors**","Level of economic activity (growth/recession); interest rates and financing availability; inflation; government monetary/fiscal/trade policy; foreign currency rates and control."],["**Industry conditions**","Market and competition; cyclical/seasonal activity; product technology changes; business risk (e.g. high-tech, high-fashion, easy market entry); declining/expanding operations; adverse conditions (declining demand, excess capacity, price competition); key ratios/operating statistics; specific accounting practices/problems; environmental issues; regulatory framework; energy supply/cost; and unique practices (labour contracts, financing, accounting methods)."],["**Management and ownership**","Corporate structure (private/public/government); beneficial owners and related parties; the owner-director-management relationship; capital structure; organisational structure; management objectives/philosophy/strategy; planned or recent acquisitions/mergers/disposals; financing sources/methods; board composition and independence, meeting frequency, audit committee existence/scope, corporate conduct policy; operating management's experience/reputation/turnover, key financial personnel, incentive/bonus plans, use of forecasts/budgets, management pressures, information systems; internal audit function (existence, quality); and attitude to the internal control environment."],["**The entity's business — products, markets, suppliers, expenses and operations**","Nature of business (manufacturer, wholesaler, financial services, imports/exports); location of facilities; employment factors; products/services and markets (customers, contracts, payment terms, margins, warranties, order book, marketing strategy); key suppliers; inventories; franchises/licences/patents; important expense categories; R&D; foreign currency exposure/hedging; relevant legislation; information systems; and debt structure including covenants."],["**Financial performance**","Accounting policies; earnings and cash-flow trends; leasing and other financial commitments; credit line availability; off-statement-of-financial-position finance; foreign exchange/interest rate exposure; and comparison against industry trends."]]}},{"p":"A sixth group, **reporting environment**, covers the external influences affecting how directors prepare the financial statements: legislation; the regulatory environment; taxation; accounting requirements; measurement/disclosure issues peculiar to the business; audit reporting requirements; and the users of the financial statements."},{"key":"Knowledge of the client's business only becomes useful if it is **communicated to the whole audit team** — the engagement partner must ensure the team obtains sufficient knowledge (typically via planning documentation) and that staff regularly share subsequently gained knowledge with the rest of the team."}]},{"n":"5.4","t":"Audit risk assessment and planning for materiality","b":[{"h4":"§5.4.1 Introduction"},{"key":"The riskier the client's business, the more work the auditor must do before expressing an opinion. **Risk-based audit** is the system auditors use to concentrate resources on high-risk clients and high-risk areas of a client's business, rather than testing all areas in equal, undifferentiated detail — enabling cost-effectiveness in audits."},{"def":{"t":"Total audit risk","d":"the risk of giving an inappropriate opinion when the financial statements are materially misstated — specifically, the risk of issuing an unqualified opinion when the statements do not conform to the applicable financial reporting framework."}},{"key":"Total audit risk has **three components**: inherent risk, control risk, and detection risk. This three-way decomposition is the single most tested model in this chapter."},{"h4":"§5.4.2 Inherent risk"},{"def":{"t":"Inherent risk","d":"the susceptibility of an account balance or class of transactions to material misstatement — individually or aggregated with other misstatements — while assuming NO related internal controls exist."}},{"p":"The auditor assesses inherent risk at the financial statement level when developing the overall audit plan, then relates that assessment to material account balances/transaction classes when developing the audit programme."},{"warn":"In the absence of knowledge or information enabling a specific assessment of inherent risk for a material balance or transaction class, the auditor **assumes inherent risk is high** — the default under uncertainty is the conservative one."},{"p":"To assess inherent risk, the auditor uses experience and professional judgment to evaluate factors at two levels:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Level","Example factors"],"rows":[["**Financial statement level**","Integrity of management; management experience/knowledge and recent changes; unusual pressures on management (e.g. an industry with many business failures, or insufficient capital to continue); the nature of the entity's business (technological obsolescence potential, capital structure complexity, related-party significance, number/spread of locations); and industry factors (economic/competitive conditions, technology/consumer-demand/accounting-practice changes)."],["**Account balance/transaction-class level**","Susceptibility to misstatement (e.g. accounts needing prior-period adjustment or high estimation); complexity possibly requiring an expert; degree of judgment in determining balances; susceptibility of assets to loss/misappropriation (e.g. cash); unusual/complex transactions near period end; and transactions not subject to ordinary processing."]]}},{"h4":"§5.4.3 Control risk"},{"def":{"t":"Control risk","d":"the risk that material misstatement in an account balance or transaction class — individually or aggregated — could occur and NOT be prevented, or detected on a timely basis, by the accounting and internal control systems."}},{"p":"After understanding the accounting system and control environment, the auditor makes a **preliminary assessment of control risk** for material financial statement assertions — evaluating the likely effectiveness of the entity's accounting/internal control systems at preventing and correcting material misstatements."},{"key":"There is always **some** control risk, because of the inherent limitations of any internal control system. The more effective the systems are assessed to be, the lower the control risk assessment — and where satisfactory evidence from tests of control supports that effectiveness, the extent of substantive procedures may be reduced. Where systems are concluded **not** effective, the auditor treats it as an adverse preliminary assessment, and must obtain sufficient appropriate evidence **entirely** from substantive procedures."},{"p":"Where control risk is assessed at less than high, the auditor must **document the basis** for that conclusion. Common documentation techniques (used alone or in combination) are narrative descriptions, questionnaires, checklists and flowcharts — the more complex the entity's systems, the more extensive both the procedures and the documentation need to be."},{"note":"Management often reacts to high inherent risk by designing controls specifically to prevent/detect the resulting misstatements — so **inherent risk and control risk are often highly interrelated**, and assessing them entirely separately risks an inappropriate overall assessment. In practice, their effects (and the preliminary control risk assessment) are often performed **simultaneously**."},{"h4":"§5.4.4 Detection risk"},{"def":{"t":"Detection risk","d":"the risk that the auditor's substantive procedures (tests of detail or analytical procedures) would NOT detect a material misstatement that exists in an account balance or transaction class."}},{"key":"Regardless of the assessed levels of inherent and control risk, the auditor **always** performs some substantive procedures for material balances and transaction classes — the higher inherent and control risk are assessed, the **more** audit evidence must come from substantive procedures. This is the inverse relationship at the heart of the whole risk model: detection risk is the one component the auditor directly controls through the extent of substantive testing, to compensate for whatever inherent and control risk already exist."},{"p":"When both inherent and control risk are assessed as high, the auditor considers whether substantive procedures can still provide sufficient evidence to reduce detection risk (and hence total audit risk) to an acceptably low level. If detection risk for a material assertion cannot be reduced to an acceptable level, the auditor considers the implications for the audit report, and may consider **withdrawing** from the engagement."},{"p":"The auditor's risk assessment may change during the audit — e.g. if information from substantive procedures differs significantly from what was originally assessed. In such cases, planned substantive procedures are modified based on the revised risk assessment."},{"h4":"§5.4.5 Planning materiality"},{"p":"The auditor considers materiality and its relationship with audit risk throughout the audit."},{"def":{"t":"Material (ISA 320)","d":"financial information whose omission or misstatement could influence the economic decisions users take based on the financial statements."}},{"key":"Materiality depends on the **size of the item or error, judged in the particular circumstances of its omission or misstatement** — it provides a **threshold or cut-off point**, not a primary qualitative characteristic of information itself. The assessment of what is material is ultimately a matter of professional judgment."},{"p":"In designing the audit plan, the auditor sets an acceptable materiality level to detect quantitatively material misstatements — but **both amount (quantity) and nature (quality)** of misstatements matter. Qualitative examples: an inadequate/improper description of an accounting policy likely to mislead a user, or failure to disclose a breach of regulatory requirements likely to trigger operation-impairing regulatory restrictions."},{"note":"The auditor must also consider small, individually immaterial misstatements that **cumulatively** could become material — e.g. a monthly procedural error may signal a potential material misstatement if it recurs across several months."},{"p":"Materiality is considered at both the overall financial statement level and in relation to individual classes of transactions, balances and disclosures — legal/regulatory requirements and other relationships can mean different materiality levels apply to different aspects of the statements. Materiality should be considered when: (a) determining the nature, timing and extent of audit procedures; and (b) evaluating the effect of misstatements."},{"h4":"The relationship between materiality and audit risk"},{"p":"The auditor's understanding of the entity establishes a frame of reference for assessing risks of material misstatement and responding to them, and also helps establish materiality and evaluate whether that judgment remains appropriate as the audit progresses. This shapes decisions such as what items to examine, and whether to use sampling and substantive analytical procedures — selecting audit procedures that, together, can be expected to reduce audit risk to an acceptably low level."},{"note":"The auditor's assessment of materiality and risk may genuinely **differ** between initial planning and evaluating results — e.g. if procedures are performed before period end and actual results differ substantially from anticipated results, the assessment may change. Auditors may also **deliberately** set a lower acceptable materiality level during planning than intended for evaluating final results, to reduce the likelihood of undiscovered misstatements and provide a margin of safety."},{"h4":"Evaluating the effect of misstatements"},{"p":"The auditor assesses whether the **aggregate of uncorrected misstatements** identified during the audit is material. This aggregate comprises: (a) specific misstatements identified (including the net effect of uncorrected prior-period misstatements); and (b) the auditor's best estimate of other misstatements that cannot be specifically identified (projected errors)."},{"p":"If the auditor concludes the misstatements may be material, they consider reducing audit risk by extending procedures or asking management to adjust the financial statements. If management refuses to adjust, and extended procedures still cannot establish the aggregate is not material, the auditor should consider an appropriate modification to the auditor's report."},{"p":"If a material misstatement resulting from error is identified, the auditor should communicate it to the appropriate level of management on a timely basis, and consider the need to report it to those charged with governance."},{"p":"In the **public sector**, the auditor must additionally consider any relevant legislation/regulation affecting materiality assessment — materiality there is also based on an item's \"context and nature,\" including its **sensitivity** (compliance with authorities, legislative concern, public interest), not value alone."}]},{"n":"5.5","t":"Audit documentation: audit files and working papers","b":[{"h4":"§5.5.1 Introduction"},{"key":"The auditor should document matters important to (a) providing evidence supporting the audit opinion, and (b) evidencing that the audit was carried out to acceptable standards. Nothing enhances audit efficiency more than the careful, systematic compilation and maintenance of audit files and working papers, built up from the very start of the audit."},{"h4":"§5.5.2 Audit working papers"},{"def":{"t":"Audit working papers","d":"the materials auditors prepare or obtain and retain in connection with performing the audit — the record of planning and performance, supervision and review, and the evidence supporting the auditor's opinion. May be data stored on paper, electronic or other media."}},{"p":"Working papers should record the auditor's planning, the nature/timing/extent of procedures performed, the conclusions drawn from the evidence, and the auditor's reasoning on all significant matters requiring judgment."},{"p":"The form and content of working papers are affected by:"},{"ol":["the nature of the engagement;","the form of the auditor's report;","the nature and complexity of the entity's business;","the nature and condition of the entity's accounting and internal control systems;","the direction/supervision/review needs for assistants' work;","the specific methodology and technology the auditor uses; and","the client's chart of accounts and reporting requirements."]},{"note":"Standardised working papers (checklists, specimen letters, standard file organisation) may improve preparation/review efficiency. To improve efficiency further, the auditor may use schedules and analyses the entity itself prepared — but must obtain evidence that such information is properly prepared before relying on it."},{"p":"Working papers typically include:"},{"ol":["information on the client's legal and organisational structure;","extracts/copies of important legal documents, agreements and minutes;","evidence of the planning process, including audit programmes and their changes;","evidence of the auditor's understanding of the accounting/internal control systems;","evidence of inherent and control risk assessments, and any revisions;","evidence of the auditor's consideration of internal audit's work and conclusions;","analyses of transactions and balances;","analyses of significant ratios and trends;","a record of the nature, timing and extent of procedures undertaken, and their results;","details of procedures regarding companies whose statements are audited by other auditors;","copies of communications with other auditors, experts and third parties;","copies of correspondence with the entity, reports to management, and notes of discussions with management;","a summary of significant audit aspects (information available, amounts involved, management views, conclusions reached, how matters were resolved/treated); and","copies of the approved financial statements and the auditor's report."]},{"h4":"§5.5.3 Audit files"},{"key":"For recurring audits, working papers split into two distinct files: the **permanent audit file** (documents of continuing importance, needed across multiple audits) and the **current audit file** (information relating primarily to a single period's audit)."},{"p":"The **permanent file** typically contains (indexed for reference):"},{"ol":["statutory materials governing the enterprise's conduct, accounts and audit (e.g. the Companies Code, Stock Exchange Regulations) and its own rules/regulations;","copies of continuing-importance documents — the letter of engagement and extract of the appointing minutes; trade licences and royalty agreements; debenture deeds; leases; addresses of the registered office and other business locations; an organisation chart (departments, sections, authority-responsibility relationships, responsible managers/officers); lists of books/records and where kept, with responsible officers' designations/names/signatures; an outline organisational history (share capital, capital surplus/general reserve, prospectuses, important ratios); a list of significant accounting matters (policies on inventories, WIP, depreciation, R&D, etc.); notes of internal-control interviews/correspondence and past management letters; the client's internal audit and accounting instructions; a list of directors with shareholdings/service contracts; a list of property/investments with verification notes; and a list of the company's bankers, stockbrokers, solicitors, valuers and insurance brokers."]},{"key":"It is **essential** that the permanent file be updated **before** commencing a new financial year's audit — not treated as a static, set-once document."},{"p":"The **current file** contains matters relating to the current year's audit:"},{"ol":["a copy of the accounts being audited;","an index to the file;","a description of the internal control system (ICQ, flowcharts, or written description, with supervision documents);","cross-references to the internal control record and letter of weakness;","a schedule for each Statement of Financial Position item;","a schedule for each Statement of Profit or Loss and OCI item, showing its make-up;","a schedule of important statistics (output, sales composition, employment, accounting ratios);","letters of representation; and","letters to the client setting out internal control weaknesses."]},{"h4":"§5.5.4 Confidentiality, custody and ownership of working papers"},{"p":"The auditor should adopt appropriate procedures for maintaining the confidentiality and safe custody of working papers."},{"key":"There is **no specific statutory requirement** for the retention period of audit working papers — the auditor exercises judgment. It is considered advisable to retain working papers for **at least six years** from the completion of the relevant audit, and, before destroying them, to be satisfied there is unlikely to be any need to refer to them again."},{"warn":"Working papers are the **property of the auditor**, not the client — and are **not a substitute** for the entity's own accounting records."}]},{"n":"5.6","t":"Chapter summary","b":[{"ol":["The purposes of audit planning are to ensure appropriate attention is paid to different audit areas, potential problem areas are identified, and review is facilitated.","Planning procedures vary between audits, but generally adopt a systematic approach.","Planning is a continuous process, taking place throughout the audit.","Some firms have formalised the planning process using a standardised Audit Planning Memorandum (APM).","There may be risk involved in auditing some clients — the riskier the client, the more work the auditor plans to perform.","Risk-based audit is used to concentrate resources on high-risk clients and high-risk areas of each client's business.","Total audit risk consists of three components: inherent risk, control risk, and detection risk.","In planning the audit, the auditor must establish materiality levels to ensure material misstatements or omissions in the accounting records are discovered.","An overall materiality level is set based on evaluating the risks in the client's environment.","It is important for auditors to record their reasoning on significant matters requiring judgment.","Audit working papers are usually filed in two separate files: the Permanent file and the Current file."]}]},{"n":"5.7","t":"Study text questions — multiple-choice and short-answer","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with 10 questions — 5 multiple-choice and 5 short-answer — reproduced below in full with the official answers, each explained. The Auditing study text sets **no examination-type questions**; this bank is the whole of its end-of-chapter material."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"The questions, with the official answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["The following risks are relevant to the auditor **EXCEPT** (A) Inherent Risk; (B) Control Risk; (C) The possibility of fraud or misrepresentation involving collusion of staff and/or management; (D) Failure to draw the correct inference from audit evidence and the analytical review; (E) Risk of not being able to recover audit working papers.","Which one of the following is **NOT** a relevant consideration for determining whether a client is of high risk? (A) Evidence of client engagement in fraudulent or illegal activities; (B) The state of the economic sector in which the client operates; (C) Diversification of the client’s business into new areas to increase profitability; (D) The general abilities of the client management; (E) Evidence of management intentionally failing to record a material transaction.","The procedures outlined in an audit programme are designed to (A) Obtain audit evidence; (B) Detect errors or irregularities; (C) Protect the auditor against litigation; (D) Highlight movement in the accounts between years; (E) Ensure early completion of the audit.","In assessing control risk an auditor ordinarily selects from a variety of techniques including (A) Inquiry and analytical procedures; (B) Re-performance and observation; (C) Comparison and confirmation; (D) Inspection and verification; (E) Reassessment and re-performance.","Matters for auditors to consider in developing the overall plan, with particular regard to knowledge of the entity’s business, may include (A) General economic factors and industry conditions affecting the entity’s business; (B) Important characteristics of the entity, its business, principal business strategies, its financial performance and its reporting requirements, including changes since the previous audit; (C) The operating style and control consciousness of directors and management; (D) The auditor’s cumulative knowledge of the accounting and internal control systems and any expected changes in the period; (E) All of the above four cases.","Those documents which the auditors prepare or obtain, and retain in connection with the performance of their audit work, are called ………","The file that contains documents and matters of continuing importance, which will be required for more than one audit, is called the ………","The process of formalising audit arrangements ahead of the time of implementing them is called ………","A quantitative statement of the cost and revenue expectations of an audit assignment, which will enable the auditor to determine the profitability of the audit, is called the ………","Those schedules prepared by auditors setting out the procedures to be executed during the audit in order to obtain audit evidence from which the auditors draw conclusions on the financial statements are called the ………"]}],"a":[{"ol":["**E** — the risk of not being able to recover audit working papers is a **practice management and records** problem, not a component of audit risk. (A), (B) and (D) are inherent, control and detection risk respectively, and (C) is a source of inherent risk.","**D** — \"the general abilities of the client management\" *(the study text’s answer)*. The other four each point to a specific, assessable source of risk; the general ability of management is too vague to be a risk indicator on its own — what matters is management **integrity** and the control consciousness it sets.","**A** — to obtain audit evidence. Everything else listed is a by-product: the programme exists so that sufficient appropriate evidence is gathered to support the opinion.","**B** — re-performance and observation. These are **tests of control**: the auditor watches the control being operated, or performs it again independently, to see whether it works. Comparison, confirmation and analytical procedures are substantive techniques.","**E** — all four. Knowledge of the business is deliberately broad, running from the macroeconomic environment down to the attitude of individual directors (§5.3.4).","**Working papers** (§5.5.2).","**Permanent audit file** — as distinct from the **current audit file**, which holds matters relating to the one period being audited.","**Audit planning** (§5.1).","**Audit budget.**","**Audit programme.**"]}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"Total audit risk's three-component model — inherent × control × detection — is the single most examinable idea in this chapter, and it recurs throughout the rest of the subject (Chapters 6-7 build directly on it). Learn each component's definition precisely, including the specific phrase \"assuming no related internal controls\" for inherent risk (the thing that distinguishes it from control risk). Also know: the overall audit strategy vs. detailed audit plan distinction; the default assumption of HIGH inherent risk under uncertainty; materiality as a threshold/cut-off, not a primary qualitative characteristic; and the permanent-file vs. current-file split, including which specific documents belong in each.","errors":["Defining inherent risk without the \"assuming no related internal controls\" qualifier — that qualifier is precisely what distinguishes inherent risk from control risk; without it, the two definitions collapse into each other.","Assuming inherent and control risk should always be assessed strictly separately — the chapter is explicit that because management often designs controls specifically in response to high inherent risk, the two are frequently interrelated, and are often assessed together in practice.","Treating detection risk as something inherent to the client (like inherent/control risk are) rather than something the AUDITOR controls — detection risk is set by the extent of substantive testing the auditor chooses to perform, specifically to compensate for whatever inherent and control risk already exist.","Filing a document of continuing, multi-year relevance (e.g. the letter of engagement, the organisation chart) in the current file, or a single-period item (e.g. this year's Statement of Financial Position schedules) in the permanent file — the split is not arbitrary; it follows directly from whether the content is period-specific or ongoing.","Believing working papers must be retained indefinitely, or belong to the client — there is no fixed statutory retention period (six years is advisable, not mandatory), and working papers are explicitly the auditor's own property."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"The following risks are relevant to the auditor EXCEPT","o":["Inherent Risk","Control Risk","The possibility of fraud or misrepresentation involving collusion of staff and/or management","Failure to draw the correct inference from audit evidence and the analytical review","Risk of not being able to recover audit working papers"],"a":4,"w":"The risk of losing working papers is an administrative/operational risk, not one of the audit risk model's components or the recognised risk considerations relevant to forming the audit opinion.","src":"Chapter 5.4","sec":"5.4"},{"q":"Which one of the following is NOT a relevant consideration for determining whether a client is of high risk?","o":["Evidence of client engagement in fraudulent or illegal activities","The state of the economic sector in which the client operates","Diversification of the client's business into new areas to increase profitability","The general abilities of the client management","Evidence of management intentionally failing to record a material transaction"],"a":3,"w":"Stated neutrally, \"the general abilities of client management\" is too vague a factor by itself — the other four options are specific, concrete risk indicators (fraud evidence, sector conditions, business diversification, intentional non-recording), whereas management ability alone is a background characteristic rather than a direct risk signal.","src":"Chapter 5.4","sec":"5.4"},{"q":"The procedures outlined in an audit programme are designed to","o":["Obtain audit evidence","Detect errors or irregularities","Protect the auditor against litigation","Highlight movement in the accounts between years","Ensure early completion of the audit"],"a":0,"w":"The audit programme's procedures exist to obtain sufficient appropriate audit evidence — detecting errors, litigation protection, account movement, and early completion may all be incidental effects, but obtaining evidence is the programme's actual design purpose.","src":"Chapter 5.1","sec":"5.1"},{"q":"In assessing control risk, an auditor ordinarily selects from a variety of techniques including","o":["Inquiry and analytical procedures","Re-performance and observation","Comparison and confirmation","Inspection and verification","Reassessment and re-performance"],"a":1,"w":"Re-performance and observation are standard techniques for testing the operation of controls directly — the other option pairs describe techniques more associated with substantive testing or analytical review, not control risk assessment specifically.","src":"Chapter 5.4","sec":"5.4"},{"q":"Matters for auditors to consider in developing the overall plan, with particular regard to knowledge of the entity's business, may include","o":["General economic factors and industry conditions affecting the entity's business","Important characteristics of the entity, its business, principal business strategies, its financial performance and its reporting requirements, including changes since the previous audit","The operating style and control consciousness of directors and management","The auditor's cumulative knowledge of the accounting and internal control systems and any expected changes in the period","All of the above"],"a":4,"w":"Each of the first four options is independently a genuine matter the chapter lists under knowledge of the client's business — the question tests whether the reader recognises they are all valid simultaneously, not mutually exclusive.","src":"Chapter 5.3","sec":"5.3"}],"theory":[{"q":"Define inherent risk, control risk and detection risk, and explain how they relate to total audit risk.","marks":8,"a":[{"p":"**Inherent risk** is the susceptibility of an account balance or class of transactions to material misstatement, individually or aggregated with misstatements in other balances/classes, while assuming NO related internal controls exist."},{"p":"**Control risk** is the risk that material misstatement in an account balance or transaction class could occur and NOT be prevented, or detected on a timely basis, by the accounting and internal control systems."},{"p":"**Detection risk** is the risk that the auditor's substantive procedures would NOT detect a material misstatement that exists in an account balance or transaction class."},{"p":"Together, these three components make up **total audit risk** — the risk of giving an inappropriate opinion when the financial statements are materially misstated. The auditor cannot directly control inherent or control risk (which are properties of the client and its systems), but can control detection risk through the extent of substantive procedures performed — the higher inherent and control risk are assessed, the more substantive evidence is needed to keep total audit risk acceptably low."}],"src":"Chapter 5.4","sec":"5.4"},{"q":"Distinguish between the permanent audit file and the current audit file, giving two examples of documents kept in each.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"The **permanent file** contains documents and matters of continuing importance, required for more than one audit — e.g. the letter of engagement and appointing minutes extract, and the organisation chart."},{"p":"The **current file** contains matters relating primarily to a single period's audit — e.g. a copy of the accounts being audited, and the schedule for each Statement of Financial Position item."},{"p":"The permanent file must be updated before the start of each new financial year's audit, since its content is meant to carry forward across audits rather than being rebuilt each time."}],"src":"Chapter 5.5","sec":"5.5"}]}},{"n":6,"t":"Audit Evidence and Verification Procedures","brief":"What audit evidence actually is, how the auditor obtains it (financial statement assertions, the eight audit procedures, external confirmation, the work of an expert, management representations), and how it is applied to verify assets and liabilities on the Statement of Financial Position — closing with the firm-level safeguards of quality control and peer review.","outcomes":["Describe the sources and relative merits of different types of evidence available","Describe what is sufficiently appropriate audit evidence","Describe the financial statement assertions commonly reported on","Explain how the auditor uses assertions in obtaining audit evidence","Outline the audit procedures for obtaining audit evidence","Outline examples of situations where external confirmation may be used","Describe how the auditor should design external confirmation requests","Distinguish between positive and negative confirmation","Explain how the auditor should deal with responses from external confirmations","Explain the importance of management representation as audit evidence"],"secs":[{"n":"6.1","t":"Nature and scope of audit evidence","b":[{"key":"The auditor should obtain **sufficient and appropriate** audit evidence to be able to draw reasonable conclusions on which to base the audit opinion — sufficiency and appropriateness are two genuinely separate qualities, covered in detail below."},{"def":{"t":"Audit evidence","d":"all the information used by the auditor in arriving at the conclusions on which the audit opinion is based — including information in the accounting records underlying the financial statements, and other information."}},{"key":"Auditors are **not** expected to address all information that may exist — conclusions are ordinarily reached using sampling and other selection approaches, and the auditor ordinarily relies on evidence that is **persuasive rather than conclusive**. To obtain reasonable assurance, however, the auditor is never satisfied with evidence that is less than persuasive — professional judgment and scepticism determine the sufficiency and appropriateness needed."},{"h4":"§6.1.2 Types of audit evidence"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","What it covers"],"rows":[["**Accounting records**","Records of initial entries and supporting records — cheques, electronic fund transfer records, invoices, contracts, the general and subsidiary ledgers, journal entries and other unrecorded adjustments, worksheets and spreadsheets supporting cost allocations/computations/reconciliations/disclosures — potentially part of integrated systems sharing data across reporting, operations and compliance."],["**Financial statements prepared by management**","Management is responsible for preparing them from the accounting records. The auditor obtains some evidence by testing the records (analysis, review, re-performance, reconciliation), potentially establishing internal consistency — but accounting records **alone** never provide sufficient evidence for an opinion; other evidence is always needed."],["**Other audit evidence**","Minutes of meetings; third-party confirmations; analysts' reports; information from inquiry, observation and inspection; and other information developed by, or available to, the auditor permitting conclusions through valid reasoning."]]}},{"h4":"§6.1.3 Sufficient and appropriate audit evidence"},{"def":{"t":"Sufficiency","d":"the measure of the QUANTITY of audit evidence."}},{"def":{"t":"Appropriateness","d":"the measure of the QUALITY of audit evidence — its relevance and reliability in supporting, or detecting misstatements in, transaction classes, account balances, disclosures and related assertions."}},{"key":"Quantity needed is affected by the **risk of misstatement** (higher risk needs more evidence) and by **quality** (higher quality needs less) — sufficiency and appropriateness are therefore interrelated. But **merely obtaining more evidence cannot compensate for poor quality** — quantity is not a substitute for quality."},{"p":"A given set of procedures may be relevant to some assertions but not others — e.g. inspecting post-period-end receivable collection records may evidence existence and valuation, but not period-end cut-off appropriateness. Conversely, the auditor often obtains evidence from different sources/natures relevant to the **same** assertion — e.g. combining receivables ageing analysis with subsequent collection evidence for the valuation of the doubtful debts allowance."},{"warn":"Obtaining evidence for one assertion (e.g. physical existence of inventory) is **never a substitute** for obtaining evidence for another assertion (e.g. the valuation of that same inventory) — existence and valuation are independent questions requiring independent evidence."},{"p":"General reliability rules (subject to important exceptions):"},{"ol":["evidence is more reliable when obtained from independent sources outside the entity;","internally generated evidence is more reliable when related controls are effective;","evidence obtained directly by the auditor (e.g. observing a control) is more reliable than evidence obtained indirectly or by inference (e.g. inquiring about a control);","evidence is more reliable in documentary form than oral (a contemporaneous written meeting record beats a later oral account of what was discussed); and","original documents are more reliable than photocopies or facsimiles."]},{"note":"An audit rarely involves authenticating documentation, and the auditor is neither trained as, nor expected to be, an expert authenticator — but must still consider the reliability of photocopies, facsimiles, filmed, digitised or other electronic documents, including the controls over their preparation and maintenance."},{"p":"Where the auditor uses information the entity itself produced, they must obtain evidence of its **accuracy and completeness** — e.g. auditing revenue via standard prices applied to sales volume records requires considering both price-data accuracy and sales-volume-data completeness/accuracy. This may be tested concurrently with the audit procedure itself, via testing controls over the information's production, or, where needed, additional procedures such as CAATs recalculation."},{"key":"The auditor ordinarily obtains **more assurance** from consistent evidence obtained from different sources/natures than from any single item considered alone — and obtaining evidence from different sources may itself reveal that one particular item is unreliable. Where evidence from different sources is **inconsistent**, the auditor determines what additional procedures are needed to resolve the inconsistency."},{"p":"The auditor weighs the cost of obtaining evidence against the usefulness of the information obtained — but difficulty or expense alone is **never** a valid basis for omitting a procedure for which there is no alternative."}]},{"n":"6.2","t":"Obtaining audit evidence","b":[{"h4":"The use of assertions in obtaining audit evidence"},{"p":"In representing that the financial statements give a true and fair view, management implicitly or explicitly makes **assertions** about the recognition, measurement, presentation and disclosure of the financial statements' elements. The auditor uses assertions, in sufficient detail, as the basis for assessing risks of material misstatement and designing/performing further audit procedures responsive to those risks."},{"p":"Assertions fall into three categories:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Category","Assertions within it"],"rows":[["**Classes of transactions and events** (for the period)","**Occurrence** — recorded transactions/events have occurred and pertain to the entity; **accuracy** — amounts and data have been recorded appropriately; **cut-off** — transactions/events are recorded in the correct accounting period; **classification** — transactions/events are recorded in the proper accounts."],["**Account balances** (at period end)","**Existence** — assets, liabilities and equity interests exist; **rights and obligations** — the entity holds/controls the rights to assets, and liabilities are the entity's obligations; **completeness** — all balances that should have been recorded have been; **valuation and allocation** — balances are included at appropriate amounts, with valuation/allocation adjustments appropriately recorded."],["**Presentation and disclosure**","**Occurrence and rights and obligations** — disclosed events/transactions have occurred and pertain to the entity; **completeness** — all disclosures that should have been included have been; **classification and understandability** — information is appropriately presented/described, disclosures clearly expressed; **accuracy and valuation** — information is disclosed fairly and at appropriate amounts."]]}},{"note":"The auditor may use these exact assertions or express them differently, provided every aspect above is covered — e.g. combining \"transactions and events\" with \"account balance\" assertions, or folding cut-off into occurrence/completeness."},{"h4":"The use of audit procedures for obtaining audit evidence"},{"p":"The auditor performs audit procedures to:"},{"ol":["obtain an understanding of the entity and its environment, including internal control, to assess risks of material misstatement at the financial statement and assertion levels — called **\"risk assessment procedures\"**;","test the operating effectiveness of controls in preventing, or detecting and correcting, material misstatements — called **\"tests of controls\"**; and","detect material misstatements at the assertion level — called **\"substantive procedures\"** (tests of detail plus substantive analytical procedures)."]},{"key":"The auditor **always** performs risk assessment procedures — but these **alone** never provide sufficient appropriate evidence for the opinion; they must be supplemented by further procedures (tests of controls where relevant, and always substantive procedures)."},{"p":"Tests of controls are necessary in **two circumstances**: (1) where the risk assessment expects controls to operate effectively, the auditor must test them to support that assessment; and (2) where substantive procedures alone cannot provide sufficient appropriate evidence, tests of controls are required regardless."},{"warn":"Because the auditor's risk assessment is judgmental and may not identify every risk, and because internal control has inherent limitations (management override, human error, systems changes), **substantive procedures for material classes of transactions, account balances and disclosures are always required** — no amount of favourable control testing ever eliminates the need for substantive work."}]},{"n":"6.3","t":"Sources of audit evidence: the eight audit procedures","b":[{"p":"The auditor uses one or more of these procedures to obtain evidence — each may serve as a risk assessment procedure, test of control, or substantive procedure, depending on the context of its use:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Procedure","What it involves"],"rows":[["**Inspection of records/documents**","Examining internal or external records/documents in paper, electronic or other media — reliability varies by nature/source, and (for internal documents) the effectiveness of the controls over their production. Documents are **least reliable** if created and held by the entity (e.g. invoices); **more reliable** if created by third parties and held by the entity (e.g. a supplier's statement); **most reliable** if created by third parties and held by the auditor (e.g. debtor circularisation responses)."],["**Physical inspection of tangible assets**","Physical examination of assets — reliable for **existence**, but not necessarily for rights/obligations or valuation. Individual inventory item inspection ordinarily accompanies observation of the inventory count."],["**Observation**","Watching a process or procedure being performed by others (e.g. inventory counting, control activities) — evidences the performance of a process, but limited to the specific point in time observed, and by the fact that being observed may itself change how the process is performed."],["**Inquiry**","Seeking information from knowledgeable persons, inside or outside the entity — ranging from formal written to informal oral. Used extensively and often complements other procedures, but **alone is never sufficient** to address a material misstatement risk, nor to test control operating effectiveness."],["**Confirmation**","A specific type of inquiry — obtaining a representation of information or an existing condition directly from a third party (e.g. confirming receivables by communicating with debtors). Used for balances and their components, and also for confirming the absence of certain conditions (e.g. a \"side agreement\" affecting revenue recognition)."],["**Recalculation**","Checking the mathematical accuracy of documents/records — can be performed via CAATs (e.g. checking a file's summarisation accuracy electronically)."],["**Re-performance**","The auditor's own independent execution of procedures or controls originally performed as part of the entity's internal control (manually or via CAATs) — e.g. re-performing the ageing of accounts receivable."],["**Analytical procedures**","Evaluating financial information via plausible relationships among financial and non-financial data — including investigating fluctuations/relationships inconsistent with other information or deviating significantly from predictions (e.g. comparing monthly payroll costs against wage rises/starters/leavers/seasonality; sales vs. expenses month-on-month and against prior years; ageing inventory/debtors and debtor days/inventory turnover). Widely used, but limited by the predictability of relationships, data availability/relevance/comparability, and the auditor's own knowledge of the business."]]}},{"note":"Where accounting data exists only electronically (electronic commerce, image processing systems that scan and discard source documents), the auditor may need **Computer Assisted Audit Techniques (CAATs)** to carry out these procedures, and must consider the entity's data retention policy to ensure needed information remains available when required."}]},{"n":"6.4","t":"Reliability of third-party evidence: external confirmation","b":[{"p":"The auditor determines whether external confirmation is necessary to obtain sufficient appropriate evidence, considering the assessed risk of material misstatement and how evidence from other planned procedures will reduce that risk to an acceptably low level."},{"def":{"t":"External confirmation","d":"the process of obtaining and evaluating audit evidence through a representation of information or an existing condition, directly from a third party, in response to a request about a particular item affecting the financial statement assertions or disclosures."}},{"p":"Situations where external confirmations may be used:"},{"ul":["bank balances and other information from bankers;","accounts receivable balances;","inventories held by third parties at bonded warehouses for processing or on consignment;","property title deeds held by lawyers/financiers for safe custody or as security;","investments purchased from stockbrokers but not delivered at the reporting date;","loans from lenders; and","accounts payable balances."]},{"key":"Reliability depends on the auditor applying appropriate procedures across three stages: **designing** the request, **performing** the confirmation procedures, and **evaluating** the results — plus factors like the control exercised over requests/responses, respondent characteristics, and any restrictions in the response or imposed by management."},{"h4":"§6.4.2 Designing the request"},{"p":"Requests should be tailored to the specific audit objective, considering the assertions addressed and factors affecting reliability — the request's form, prior experience, the information's nature, and the intended respondent all directly affect the reliability of the resulting evidence."},{"h4":"§6.4.3 Positive vs. negative confirmations"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Positive confirmation","Negative confirmation"],"rows":[["Asks the respondent to reply **in every case** — either confirming agreement or filling in requested information. Ordinarily provides reliable evidence, but risks a respondent replying without actually verifying the information (undetectable by the auditor). A \"blank\" variant (omitting the stated amount, asking the respondent to fill it in) reduces this risk but tends to lower response rates.","Asks the respondent to reply **only if they disagree**. No response gives no explicit evidence that the request was even received and verified — so it ordinarily provides **less reliable** evidence than positive confirmation, and the auditor should consider supplementing it with other substantive procedures."]]}},{"p":"Negative confirmation may reduce risk to an acceptable level when: the assessed risk of material misstatement is lower; a large number of small balances is involved; a substantial number of errors is not expected; and the auditor has no reason to believe respondents will disregard the requests. A **combination** may be used — e.g. positive requests for a small number of large balances, negative requests for a large number of small balances."},{"h4":"§6.4.4 Management requests to withhold confirmation"},{"p":"If management asks the auditor not to seek confirmation on a matter, the auditor must consider whether valid grounds exist and obtain evidence supporting that validity. If the auditor agrees, alternative procedures must be applied instead. If the auditor **disagrees** and is prevented from confirming anyway, this is a **scope limitation**, with possible impact on the auditor's report."},{"key":"Applying professional scepticism here means considering whether the request has any implications for management's **integrity** — whether it may indicate the possible existence of fraud or error, and whether the proposed alternative procedures can genuinely provide sufficient appropriate evidence in its place."},{"h4":"§6.4.5-6.4.11 Process integrity, non-response and evaluation"},{"p":"Reliability is affected by the respondent's competence, independence, authority to respond, knowledge and objectivity — the auditor tries to direct requests to an appropriately knowledgeable, authorised individual. The auditor maintains **control** over the whole process (selecting recipients, preparing/sending requests, receiving responses) to minimise the risk of interception or alteration — ensuring the auditor sends the requests, they are properly addressed, and replies come directly back to the auditor."},{"p":"Where **no response** is received to a positive request, the auditor performs **alternative procedures** providing evidence for the same assertions — e.g. for receivables: examining subsequent cash receipts, shipping documentation for existence, and sales near period-end for cut-off; for payables: subsequent cash disbursements, third-party correspondence, and goods-received notes."},{"p":"The auditor also considers the **authenticity** of responses received (e.g. verifying a response by telephone call, requesting the original be mailed directly, validating electronic responses), documents oral confirmations in the working papers, and, for significant oral information, requests written confirmation directly."},{"p":"Where confirmation and alternative procedures together have not provided sufficient appropriate evidence, additional procedures are performed — considering the reliability of what was obtained, the nature and implications of any **exceptions** (which may indicate a misstatement requiring further investigation), and the evidence from other procedures. The overall results are finally evaluated together with all other audit evidence obtained for that assertion."},{"note":"The auditor may confirm balances at a date **prior to** the reporting date (e.g. where the audit must complete shortly after period end) — but must then obtain sufficient evidence that transactions in the intervening period have not been materially misstated, considering the need for further evidence covering the remainder of the period."}]},{"n":"6.5","t":"Specific examples of third-party confirmations","b":[{"p":"The most commonly used external verification procedures are debtors circularisation, bank confirmations, and confirmation of pending legal matters."},{"h4":"§6.5.1 Debtors circularisation"},{"def":{"t":"Debtors circularisation","d":"a verification procedure where trade debtors are contacted directly to confirm their balances — the normal means of checking whether debtors really owe the amount stated in the client's sales ledger."}},{"key":"Circularisation is essentially an act of the **client**, who alone can authorise third parties to divulge information to the auditor. If the client refuses to co-operate, the auditors must consider whether to **qualify their report**, since they may be unable to satisfy themselves by other means of the validity/accuracy of debtor balances."},{"p":"Ideally circularisation happens immediately after year end, to cover the actual year-end balances. Where timing constraints prevent this, prior year-end circularisation may be appropriate — recommended to be no more than **two months** before year end, especially where internal controls are strong enough for the auditor to gain reasonable assurance from reconciling the intervening period's movements."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Positive circularisation","Negative circularisation"],"rows":[["Requires confirmation of the outstanding balance **in every case**. Appropriate where: the auditor is not satisfied with sales/debtor control systems; other verification work showed unsatisfactory results. Its usefulness depends heavily on the auditor's tenacity in following up non-replies (telephone, personal calls).","Requires a reply only if the debtor disputes the balance. Simpler, needs no follow-up, but appropriate only where: the auditor already trusts the internal control governing sales/debtors; other verification already showed satisfactory results; and it is used with a large, carefully selected sample (otherwise many requests are simply discarded, negating the test's value)."]]}},{"p":"Sample selection requires scrutinising accounts individually to include special cases, such as: accounts with credit balances; accounts written off as bad, or under specific loss provision; active accounts with nil balances; accounts unpaid beyond the normal period; accounts habitually exceeding credit limits; accounts with balances materially smaller at the reporting date than usual (possibly artificially reduced to escape attention); accounts of debtors connected to the client or its officers; accounts with unusually favourable trade terms; and accounts in arrears that do not appear to be followed up by normal credit control."},{"p":"For **positive** circularisation, non-respondents must be followed up by all practical means — a second request after 2-3 weeks, then telephone (with client permission) if needed. Where follow-up is limited, alternative procedures include: checking post-date cash receipts and valid purchase orders; examining the account for specific invoices and confirming their validity; obtaining explanations for unpaid invoices when later ones have been paid; checking if the balance is growing (and why); and testing controls over credit notes and bad debt write-offs."},{"p":"Where circularisation is carried out **before** year end, additional procedures apply: reviewing/reconciling debtor control account entries for the intervening period; reconciling sales journal/invoice/dispatch note entries to the control account; checking credit entries for returns/allowances; sampling cash receipts for correct control account crediting; reviewing balances at both the circularisation and year-end dates and investigating unexpected movements (possibly sending further year-end requests to material debtors); analytical review comparing debtor ratios at both dates; and year-end cut-off tests in addition to those at the confirmation date."},{"key":"All circularisation results must be properly recorded and evaluated — every disagreement and non-reply followed up, with its effect on total debtors assessed. Differences that are merely invoices or cash in transit generally need no adjustment, but disputed amounts and genuine client errors might."},{"h4":"§6.5.2 Bank confirmations"},{"p":"Auditors rely heavily on bank confirmation for the completeness, existence, rights, obligations and valuation of bank balances. A standard bank confirmation letter requests:"},{"ol":["balances on all current, deposit and term accounts, noting any restrictions (blocked funds, compensating balances) — including a description of any account with no balance that operated during the year;","the name and closure date of any accounts closed during the year;","balances on advance/loan/overdraft accounts, with terms, interest, and any collateral/guarantees held;","liability for notes/bills discounted or deposited for collection;","guarantees given on the client's behalf, or given by the client for third parties;","a list of share certificates/securities deposited, and any lien/pledge/restriction on them;","interest/charges to be paid, and interest due, not yet included in the stated balances;","details of any other operations or matters in progress not otherwise covered; and","a list of officers authorised to sign cheques, and how their signatures must be combined."]},{"note":"Matters excluded from the **standard** bank letter, requiring a supplementary request where needed: copies of bank statements; copies of paying-in slips for specific lodgements; the make-up of those lodgements; lists of securities/title documents lodged with the bank (usually only relevant between banking organisations); interest paid to/by third parties and their names; insurance-related receipts; returned paid cheques; stopped cheques (not normally needed, since they clear through the banking system within the audit period anyway); third-party securities including director guarantees (requiring specific third-party authority); and outstanding forward foreign exchange contract details."},{"h4":"§6.5.3 Legal confirmation of pending legal matters"},{"p":"Confirmation from a client's legal advisers is often required for contingent liabilities from outstanding legal matters — pending lawsuits present problems of both **ascertainment** (does the claim exist) and **appraisal** (what might it cost)."},{"p":"Relevant audit procedures for verifying such claims exist (though not necessarily their likely amount):"},{"ol":["reviewing the claims-recording system and how claims reach management's attention;","discussing solicitor-instruction arrangements with the responsible official (e.g. head of legal, or the company secretary);","examining board/committee minutes for references to possible claims;","examining solicitors' bills and correspondence;","obtaining a list of matters referred to solicitors, with liability estimates, from the appropriate director/official; and","obtaining written assurance that the director/official is not aware of any referred matters beyond those disclosed."]},{"key":"The normal, proper source of confirmation for legal matters is the company's **own legal advisers** — requests should stay within the solicitor-client relationship, with the client asking that a copy of the reply go directly to the auditor. If the enquiries turn up nothing unexpected, the auditor would not normally qualify the report merely for lack of independent corroboration of the list's completeness. But if the auditor remains unable to obtain all the information required, **the report must be qualified**."},{"h4":"§6.5.4 Summary: assets/liabilities and their confirming third party"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Asset/Liability","Confirming third party"],"rows":[["Bank balance, security, bank loans, contingent liabilities, accrued interest","Bank"],["Debtors and creditors balances","Individual debtors and creditors"],["Staff loans, advances, mortgages, accrued interest","Individual staff members / financial houses"],["Assets held abroad, goods on consignment/sale-or-return, liabilities repayable abroad","Overseas agents, associate firms, agents, consignees, sale-or-return holders"],["Investments in subsidiary companies","Auditors of the subsidiaries"],["Goods held in bond or warehouse","Harbour board, warehouse company"],["Leased premises","Landlord"],["Pending litigation, possible damages/costs/legal fees outstanding","Solicitors"]]}}]},{"n":"6.6","t":"The reliability of audit evidence — a consolidated view","b":[{"p":"Reliability is influenced by source (internal or external) and nature (visual, documentary or oral). Useful generalisations:"},{"ol":["evidence from external sources (e.g. third-party confirmation) is more reliable than that from the entity's own records;","evidence from the entity's records is more reliable when the related accounting/internal control system operates effectively;","evidence obtained directly by the auditor is more reliable than evidence obtained by or from the entity;","documentary/written evidence is more reliable than oral; and","original documents are more reliable than photocopies, telexes or facsimiles."]},{"key":"Consistency across sources has a **corroborating effect**, making evidence more persuasive; where evidence is inconsistent, the auditor must determine what additional procedures are needed to resolve it. The cost-benefit relationship of obtaining evidence matters, but difficulty/expense alone never justifies omitting a necessary procedure — the same principle stated in §6.1, restated here as a general takeaway."}]},{"n":"6.7","t":"Using the work of an expert","b":[{"def":{"t":"Expert","d":"a person or firm possessing special skill, knowledge and experience in a particular field other than accounting and auditing."}},{"key":"The auditor's education and experience make them knowledgeable about business matters generally, but they are **not expected** to have the expertise of a person trained in another profession (e.g. an actuary or engineer). When using an expert's work, the auditor must still obtain sufficient appropriate evidence that the work is adequate for the audit's purposes — using an expert does not transfer responsibility for the opinion."},{"p":"An expert may be engaged by the entity, engaged by the auditor, employed by the entity, or employed by the auditor. Where the auditor uses the work of their own employee-expert, that work is used in the employee's capacity **as an expert**, not as an ordinary audit assistant — so the auditor applies the relevant procedures to their work/findings, but does not ordinarily need to re-evaluate their skill/competence for each engagement (unlike an outside expert)."},{"h4":"§6.7.1 Determining the need for an expert"},{"p":"Examples where an expert's report/opinion/valuation/statement may be needed:"},{"ul":["valuations of certain assets (land/buildings, plant/machinery, art, precious stones);","determining quantities or physical condition of assets (stored minerals, underground reserves, remaining plant life);","determining amounts using specialised techniques (e.g. actuarial valuation);","measuring work completed/to be completed on contracts in progress; and","legal opinions interpreting agreements, statutes and regulations."]},{"p":"Relevant considerations: the engagement team's own knowledge/experience of the matter; the risk of material misstatement based on the matter's nature/complexity/materiality; and the quantity/quality of other evidence expected to be available."},{"h4":"§6.7.2 Competence and objectivity of the expert"},{"p":"The auditor evaluates the expert's **professional competence** — considering professional certification/licensing/membership, and experience/reputation in the relevant field — and separately evaluates their **objectivity**."},{"warn":"The risk of impaired objectivity **increases** where the expert is: employed by the entity; or otherwise related to the entity (e.g. financially dependent on it, or holding an investment in it). If concerned about competence or objectivity, the auditor discusses reservations with management, considers whether sufficient appropriate evidence can still be obtained, and may need additional procedures or a different expert entirely."},{"h4":"§6.7.3 Scope of the expert's work"},{"p":"The auditor obtains evidence that the scope of the expert's work is adequate — often via reviewing written instructions from the entity to the expert, covering: the objectives/scope of the work; the matters the expert's report is expected to cover; the auditor's intended use of the work (including possible disclosure of the expert's identity/involvement to third parties); the expert's access to records/files; clarification of the expert's relationship with the entity; confidentiality; and the assumptions/methods to be used and their consistency with prior periods. Where these are not clearly set out, the auditor may communicate with the expert directly."},{"h4":"§6.7.4 Evaluating the expert's work"},{"p":"The auditor evaluates whether the substance of the expert's findings is properly reflected in, or supports, the financial statements — considering the source data used, the assumptions/methods used and their consistency with prior periods, and the results in light of the auditor's own business knowledge and other audit evidence."},{"key":"The **appropriateness and reasonableness of assumptions and methods** are the **expert's** own responsibility — the auditor typically lacks the expertise to challenge them directly, but must still understand them and consider whether they seem appropriate and reasonable given the auditor's own knowledge and other evidence. Where the expert's results are insufficient, or inconsistent with other evidence, the auditor resolves the matter — via discussion, additional procedures, possibly engaging another expert, or modifying the auditor's report."},{"h4":"§6.7.5 Reference to an expert in the auditor's report"},{"warn":"When issuing an **unmodified** report, the auditor should **not** refer to an expert's work — such a reference might be misunderstood as a qualification, or a division of responsibility, neither of which is intended. If the auditor's report is **modified** as a result of an expert's work, it may be appropriate to describe the expert's involvement (identity and extent) in explaining the modification — but only with the expert's **permission**; if refused and the auditor still believes reference is necessary, legal advice may be needed."}]},{"n":"6.8","t":"Management representation","b":[{"key":"The auditor should obtain evidence that management acknowledges its responsibility for the fair presentation of the financial statements, and has approved them — via minutes of governance meetings, a written representation, or a signed copy of the financial statements."},{"p":"Written representations should be obtained on matters **material** to the financial statements when other sufficient appropriate evidence cannot reasonably be expected to exist — reducing the possibility of misunderstanding, since oral representations confirmed in writing are less ambiguous. Requests may be limited to matters material individually or collectively; the auditor may need to inform management of their understanding of materiality for this purpose."},{"p":"The auditor should obtain written representation that management: (a) acknowledges responsibility for designing and implementing internal control to prevent and detect error; and (b) believes the effects of uncorrected misstatements aggregated by the auditor are immaterial, individually and in aggregate — with a summary of such items attached."},{"p":"Where management representations relate to material matters, the auditor should: seek corroborative evidence from inside or outside the entity; evaluate whether the representations seem reasonable and consistent with other evidence obtained; and consider whether the individuals making them can reasonably be expected to be well informed."},{"warn":"Management representations are **never a substitute** for other audit evidence the auditor could reasonably expect to be available — e.g. a management representation about an asset's cost is not a substitute for the cost evidence the auditor would ordinarily expect to obtain independently. If the auditor cannot obtain sufficient appropriate evidence on a matter with a material effect (or potential material effect), that is a **scope limitation**, even if management has made a representation on the matter."},{"p":"Some matters genuinely cannot be evidenced any other way — e.g. management's intention to hold an investment long-term, or to call up a debt before month-end balancing and re-grant the facility. In such cases, a written representation is the appropriate evidence. If a representation is **contradicted** by other evidence, the auditor investigates and, where necessary, reconsiders the reliability of other representations made."},{"h4":"Documentation and form"},{"p":"Working papers ordinarily include a summary of oral discussions, or written representations. A **written** representation is ordinarily more reliable than an oral one, and can take the form of: a representation letter from management; a letter from the auditor outlining their understanding of management's representations, acknowledged/confirmed by management; or relevant board minutes/a signed copy of the financial statements."},{"p":"A management representation letter should be addressed to the auditor, contain the specified information, and be appropriately dated and signed — ordinarily dated the same date as the auditor's report (though a separate letter on specific matters may be obtained during the audit, or after the report date, e.g. at a public offering). It is ordinarily signed by those with primary responsibility for the entity and its financial aspects (ordinarily the CEO and CFO), based on the best of their knowledge and belief. Separate representation letters may be sought from other individuals where appropriate (e.g. confirming the completeness of minute-keeping from the person responsible for it)."},{"h4":"Contents typically confirmed in a management representation letter"},{"p":"A specimen letter (illustrative, not a fixed standard — content varies by entity and period) would ordinarily confirm, to the best of management's knowledge and belief:"},{"ol":["no irregularities involving management or employees with a significant internal control role, or with a material effect on the financial statements;","all books of account, supporting documentation and meeting minutes have been made available;","completeness of information provided on the identification of related parties;","the financial statements are free of material misstatement, including omissions;","compliance with contractual agreements and regulatory requirements whose breach could have a material effect;","proper recording/disclosure of related-party balances/transactions, sale/purchase commitment losses, asset buy-back agreements, and pledged collateral;","no undisclosed plans that would materially alter asset/liability carrying values or classification;","no undisclosed plans that would cause excess/obsolete inventory, and no inventory stated above net realisable value;","satisfactory title to all assets, with no undisclosed liens/encumbrances;","all liabilities (actual and contingent) recorded/disclosed as appropriate, with guarantees given to third parties disclosed;","no undisclosed post-period-end events requiring adjustment or disclosure;","confirmation of settled litigation amounts, and no other expected claims;","no undisclosed compensating balance arrangements or other credit lines; and","proper recording/disclosure of capital stock repurchase options/agreements and reserved shares."]},{"h4":"Action if management refuses to provide representations"},{"warn":"If management refuses to provide a representation the auditor considers necessary, the auditor should discuss it with management. The refusal itself constitutes a **scope limitation**, and the auditor should express a **modified opinion**. The auditor should also re-evaluate reliance placed on any other management representations made during the audit, and consider whether the refusal has additional implications for the report."}]},{"n":"6.9","t":"Verification of assets and liabilities","b":[{"key":"The auditor has a duty to verify all assets and liabilities appearing in the Statement of Financial Position, **and** to ensure there are no other assets or liabilities that ought to appear but do not."},{"h4":"§6.9.1 Verification of assets: CAVEBOP and VEBOP"},{"p":"In verifying assets, the auditor seeks to establish six things, remembered by the mnemonic **CAVEBOP**:"},{"ol":["**C**ost;","**A**uthorization;","**V**alue;","**E**xistence;","**B**eneficial **O**wnership; and","**P**resentation in the accounts."]},{"key":"Cost and authorisation are ordinarily verified **once**, in the year of acquisition. For assets already on the books from prior years, the auditor therefore only needs the remaining four aspects — remembered as **VEBOP** (Valuation, Existence, Beneficial ownership, Presentation/disclosure). Assets acquired **during** the current year still need the full CAVEBOP treatment."},{"h4":"§6.9.2 Verification of trade debtors and creditors"},{"key":"Independent, externally derived confirmation minimises the risk of **collusion** between third parties and the company's own staff aimed at deceiving the auditor — as a general rule, independent documentary evidence is superior to documentary evidence compiled by company staff, regardless of their seniority."},{"p":"Circularising a carefully selected sample of debtors/creditors serves to: ensure the accuracy of sales/purchase ledger book entries and resulting balances; ensure proper functioning of the credit control system; identify disputed accounts; and ensure records are fully up to date. Where suppliers are circularised, the confirmations received support both the creditors' ledger balances and the monthly statements most suppliers send their customers anyway."},{"note":"It is essential that replies be sent **directly to the auditor at their own office** — otherwise there is a risk of tampering or suppression by client staff."},{"h4":"§6.9.3 Verification of inventory and work in progress"},{"key":"Management is responsible for ensuring reported stock figures represent stock physically in existence and include everything the entity owns; the auditor's duty is to obtain evidence of **ownership, existence and condition** at year end."},{"p":"Inventory receives particular audit attention because: it usually forms one of the largest Statement of Financial Position items (so a small error can be material); closing inventory directly affects cost of sales and hence profit/loss; it can be manipulated to hit a targeted profit figure; different valuation bases may be used; and there can be genuine valuation/existence/development-stage problems for specialised inventory (livestock, production-in-process, etc.)."},{"p":"Objectives of inventory taking: verify condition; verify existence; establish title; determine cut-off accuracy; ascertain internal control effectiveness over inventory; provide a basis for checking inventory record correctness; ensure adequate provision for slow-moving/obsolete inventory; provide a quantity basis for valuation; and determine valuation accuracy."},{"h4":"§6.9.4 Liability verification"},{"p":"A Statement of Financial Position groups liabilities under headings such as equity, reserves, creditors due after more than one year, provisions for liabilities/charges, creditors due within one year, and contingent liabilities. The auditor's duty: verify the existence of stated liabilities; verify the correctness of their money amounts; verify the appropriateness of their description and the adequacy of disclosure; and verify that **all** existing liabilities are actually included."},{"p":"General verification principles (applied per the specific circumstances):"},{"ol":["**Schedule** — request or prepare a schedule for each liability/class;","**Cut-off** — e.g. a trade creditor should not be included unless the goods were acquired before year end;","**Reasonableness** — of the liability itself;","**Internal control** — determine, evaluate and test relevant procedures;","**Previous-date clearance** — have prior-period liabilities all been cleared?","**Authority** — for all liabilities;","**Description** — adequacy of each liability's description in the accounts;","**Documents** — examine relevant invoices, correspondence, debenture deeds, etc.;","**Vouching** — the creation of each liability (e.g. receipt of a loan);","**Accounting policies** — appropriate and consistently applied;","**Disclosure** — everything needed for a true and fair view;","**External verification** — direct confirmation with creditors, where possible;","**Materiality** — a factor in every accounting/auditing decision;","**Post-reporting-date events** — particularly important for liabilities;","**Accounting standards** — liabilities accounted for per the relevant standards; and","**Risk** — assess the risk of misstatement."]},{"h4":"§6.9.5 Verification of non-current assets"},{"p":"Verification often begins with the **asset register**, which facilitates identifying assets during a physical check and forms an important part of internal control. It must be agreed to the financial records, and the auditor enquires about the extent/frequency of company officials' own reconciliation checks between records, register and physical assets."},{"p":"An asset register normally records, per asset: supplier's name; purchase date; original cost; estimated useful life; estimated residual value; a summary of repair expenditure to date; estimated replacement cost (updated annually); and eventual disposal details."},{"h4":"§6.9.6 Verification of intangible assets"},{"key":"All intangibles have a finite economic life and should therefore be **amortised**."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Intangible","Key verification points"],"rows":[["**Goodwill**","Arises on purchase of a business — examine the contract and allocation of purchase price to assets; normally remains in the accounts at cost."],["**Patents**","Examine the patent and its assignment (a patent can only be granted to an individual, e.g. an employee, who assigns it to the company); check payment of renewal fees and accounting for receivable royalties."],["**Trademarks**","Examine the patent office certificate; the assignment, if purchased; the reasonableness of capitalised design costs; and payment of renewal fees."],["**Copyrights**","Examine the document of title and assignment (if purchased); note that its life is the author's life plus 50 years."]]}},{"h4":"§6.9.7 Physical examination"},{"p":"Physical inspection ascertains the existence of assets and liabilities, supporting the auditor's conclusion on the fairness of related financial statement assertions — using professional judgment to determine sufficient evidence, considering the item's nature, the materiality of possible errors, the item's relative risk, and the quality/quantity of evidence available cost-effectively."}]},{"n":"6.10","t":"Quality control","b":[{"key":"Per the International Standard on Quality Control (ISQC), the audit firm establishes a system of quality control giving **reasonable assurance** that the firm and its personnel comply with professional standards and legal/regulatory requirements, and that the firm's reports are appropriate to the circumstances. The objectives are the same for every firm, but the exact nature/extent of procedures depends on the firm's size, practice, staff numbers and organisation."},{"p":"Procedures for controlling an audit plan: allocate work to staff, specifying completion timing for each aspect; and brief staff fully, determining the required performance standard and flagging possible problems."},{"p":"Audit risk areas demanding special attention:"},{"ol":["precise instructions that staff bring problems encountered, and anything putting them upon enquiry, to the team leader's attention;","thorough, meticulous completion of working papers recording all actions taken;","review of the work and working papers by a more senior partner, adding their own comments/observations/instructions alongside what staff recorded."]},{"note":"A completion checklist is commonly used — each staff member ticks off completed sections, followed by a final review by a partner (not necessarily on that assignment) exercising overall quality control."},{"h4":"Features of audit quality control"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Feature","What it involves"],"rows":[["**Hierarchy of control**","Communication flows (either way) through: partner → manager → audit senior → senior audit trainee → junior audit trainee. Ultimate responsibility for quality control rests with the relevant reporting **partner**."],["**Audit planning**","Scheduling audits (often clustered around common financial year-ends) to make maximum use of limited staff at minimum time/money cost, without compromising efficiency or quality."],["**Audit staff**","Wherever possible, the team should include experienced staff already familiar with that specific audit; new staff receive adequate training."],["**Communication with client/third parties**","Along three lines: the client's accounts department head (readiness to receive the team); the internal audit department (possible help/co-operation); and top management, year-round, for first-hand information on structural changes and legal matters without delay."],["**Timing**","Breaking the audit into work that can be done before, during and after the financial year-end, balancing promptness against audit cost."],["**Field work**","Proper execution of the audit plan, with adequate assessment and thorough evaluation of existing internal control/accounting systems to determine scope."],["**Reviews and consultation**","Before finalising the report, the reporting partner thoroughly reviews all field work — recordings, documents and evidence — alongside the letter of engagement and the audit completion checklist."],["**The audit report**","Should reflect the sum total of work and evidence gathered, worded to leave no doubt as to its meaning/implications, and state compliance (or otherwise) with regulatory requirements, accounting principles and auditing standards."]]}}]},{"n":"6.11","t":"Peer review","b":[{"p":"To mitigate litigation and losses arising from negligence, firms have taken measures to improve work quality — one of which is peer review."},{"def":{"t":"Peer review","d":"a process where firms agree to set up mechanisms to review each other's processes, identifying practice weaknesses that could be addressed to improve quality standards. It involves a review of one audit firm's systems, procedures and strategies by another audit firm of comparable size, reputation and standard."}},{"warn":"Peer review is **not common in Nigeria** — it is a new approach, limited by: potentially prohibitive cost; the legality/confidentiality of allowing others through a client's file being unprotected; and the inevitable exposure of one firm's procedures/standards to a competing firm."},{"p":"The reviewer is expected to report on: acceptance and retention of clients; means of engaging, training, allocating and motivating staff; independence, integrity and competence; and the conduct of individual audit and other engagements."}]},{"n":"6.12","t":"Chapter summary","b":[{"ol":["The financial statement assertions are existence, rights and obligations, occurrence, completeness, valuation, measurement, presentation and disclosure.","Auditors are required to obtain sufficient appropriate audit evidence on which to base audit opinions.","Substantive tests may be incorporated with other procedures like tests of control to obtain the required audit evidence.","Documentary evidence is least reliable if created and held by the entity, most reliable if created and held by third parties.","Among the key techniques used to obtain audit evidence are inspection, observation, enquiry, confirmation, computation, re-performance and analytical procedures.","Examples of third-party confirmations include debtors and creditors circularisation, and requesting bank references through a bank letter.","Management representations are also sought to provide evidence in key areas where other sources may be less reliable, normally in the form of a management representation letter."]}]},{"n":"6.13","t":"Study text questions — multiple-choice and short-answer","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with 10 questions — 5 multiple-choice and 5 short-answer — reproduced below in full with the official answers, each explained. The Auditing study text sets **no examination-type questions**; this bank is the whole of its end-of-chapter material."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"The questions, with the official answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["The examination of the documentary evidence in support of the transactions of an organisation is known as (A) Verification; (B) Observation; (C) Vouching; (D) Physical inspection; (E) Analytical review.","The **positive** method of circularising debtors can be used in the following situations **EXCEPT**: (A) Where there are numerous small accounts to be circularised; (B) Where there is a need to confirm the existence and amount of the debt; (C) Where the condition of the internal control system is not satisfactory; (D) Where the condition of the internal control system is satisfactory; (E) Where the amount of the debt is material.","\"Auditors should plan the audit work so as to perform the audit in an effective manner.\" (A) The statement is true; (B) The statement is not true; (C) Writing audit planning is enough; (D) Writing a management report will suffice; (E) Auditors’ prior year knowledge will suffice.","The following assertions about audit evidence are correct **EXCEPT**: (A) Evidence is more reliable when it is obtained from sources outside the entity; (B) All evidence is acceptable; (C) Evidence provided by original documents is more reliable; (D) The reliability of audit evidence is influenced by its source; (E) Internally generated evidence is reliable when control is effective.","Situations where external confirmations may be used include all but one of the following: (A) Account receivable balances; (B) Bank balances and other information from bankers; (C) Account payable balances; (D) Property agreed in the prior year audit; (E) Inventories held by third parties at a bonded warehouse for processing.","Information obtained by the auditor which enables him to arrive at the conclusions necessary for his opinion on financial statements is called ………","Written requests by a client to its debtors or creditors for confirmation of their outstanding balances as at a given date is called ………","When planning to use the work of an expert, the auditor should evaluate the ……… and ……… of the expert.","The representations by management, explicit or otherwise, that are embodied in the financial statements are called ………","State any **TWO** methods of obtaining audit evidence."]}],"a":[{"ol":["**C** — vouching. Note the distinction the examiner is testing: **vouching** is the examination of documentary evidence supporting **transactions**; **verification** is the confirmation of the existence, ownership, valuation and presentation of **assets and liabilities** at the reporting date.","**A** — numerous small accounts. The positive method asks every debtor to reply whether they agree or not, and is therefore reserved for **material balances, suspect balances and weak control environments**. Where there are many small accounts the **negative** method — reply only if you disagree — is used instead.","**A** — the statement is true, and is a direct restatement of the requirement in the planning standard.","**B** — \"all evidence is acceptable\" is plainly false. Evidence must be both **sufficient** (enough of it) and **appropriate** (relevant and reliable), and its reliability varies with its source and nature — which is exactly what the other four options describe.","**D** — property agreed in the prior year audit. External confirmation is a technique for obtaining evidence **from a third party about a current balance or condition**; property already verified last year is confirmed by inspecting title deeds and the asset register, not by circularisation.","**Audit evidence.**","**Circularisation** (external confirmation).","**Any two of:** professional competence; independence; experience; reputation — the two most often cited being **competence** and **objectivity/independence**.","**Assertions** — existence, rights and obligations, occurrence, completeness, valuation, measurement, and presentation and disclosure.","**Any two of:** physical inspection; observation (e.g. of inventory taking); enquiry (communicating with a third party or with management); confirmation; computation / re-performance; analytical procedures; inspection of records or documents."]}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"This chapter is the practical heart of the whole subject — the eight audit procedures (inspection, physical inspection, observation, inquiry, confirmation, recalculation, re-performance, analytical procedures) and the assertion categories (transactions/events, account balances, presentation/disclosure) are both directly examinable lists worth memorising verbatim. The reliability hierarchy for documentary evidence (least reliable: created and held by the entity; most reliable: created by third parties and held by the auditor) recurs constantly across MCQs. Also know: positive vs. negative confirmation and when each is appropriate; the CAVEBOP/VEBOP mnemonic for asset verification (and why cost/authorization drop out after the acquisition year); and that management representations can NEVER substitute for other reasonably-available evidence.","errors":["Treating \"sufficient\" and \"appropriate\" as synonyms — sufficiency is QUANTITY, appropriateness is QUALITY (relevance + reliability), and more quantity never compensates for poor quality.","Assuming risk assessment procedures alone can support the audit opinion — they never do by themselves; they must always be supplemented by tests of controls (where relevant) and substantive procedures, which are ALWAYS required regardless of how favourable the control assessment is.","Using positive confirmation for a large number of small, low-risk balances where a strong control environment already exists — that is precisely the negative-confirmation use case; the two methods are not interchangeable defaults.","Believing verification of one assertion (e.g. existence) satisfies another (e.g. valuation) for the same asset — each assertion requires its own independent evidence.","Forgetting that cost and authorization (the \"CA\" in CAVEBOP) apply only in the year of acquisition — for assets already on the books, only VEBOP (valuation, existence, beneficial ownership, presentation) needs re-verifying each year."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"The examination of the documentary evidence in support of the transactions of an organization is known as","o":["Verification","Observation","Vouching","Physical inspection","Analytical review"],"a":2,"w":"Vouching is precisely the examination of documentary evidence supporting recorded transactions — distinct from verification, which covers the broader existence/ownership/valuation of assets and liabilities generally.","src":"Chapter 6.3","sec":"6.3"},{"q":"The positive method of circularizing debtors can be used in the following situations EXCEPT:","o":["Where there are numerous small accounts to be circularized","Where there is need to confirm the existence and amount of the debt","Where the condition of the internal control system is not satisfactory","Where the condition of the internal control system is satisfactory","Where the amount of the debt is material"],"a":0,"w":"Numerous small accounts is precisely the scenario the NEGATIVE method suits — positive circularisation is reserved for material balances, weak controls, or a specific need to confirm existence/amount, not for large volumes of small, low-materiality balances.","src":"Chapter 6.5","sec":"6.5"},{"q":"\"Auditors should plan the audit work so as to perform the audit in an effective manner\" — is this statement true?","o":["The statement is true","The statement is not true","Writing audit planning is enough","Writing management report will suffice","Auditors' prior year knowledge will suffice"],"a":0,"w":"This is the chapter's own opening principle on planning (also established in Chapter 5) — planning exists specifically to enable the engagement to be performed effectively and efficiently.","src":"Chapter 6.1","sec":"6.1"},{"q":"The following assertions about audit evidence are correct EXCEPT:","o":["Evidence is more reliable when it is obtained from sources outside the entity","All evidence is acceptable","Evidence provided by original documents is more reliable","Reliability of audit evidence is influenced by its source","Internally generated evidence is reliable when control is effective"],"a":1,"w":"\"All evidence is acceptable\" directly contradicts the whole chapter's reliability framework — evidence varies enormously in reliability by source, nature and the circumstances under which it was obtained; it is never uniformly \"acceptable.\"","src":"Chapter 6.1","sec":"6.1"},{"q":"Situations where external confirmations may be used include all BUT ONE of the following:","o":["Accounts receivable balances","Bank balances and other information from bankers","Accounts payable balances","Property agreed in prior year audit","Inventories held by third parties at bonded warehouse for processing"],"a":3,"w":"A property balance already agreed in a PRIOR year's audit is not a current-period item requiring fresh external confirmation — the other four are all live examples the chapter lists of situations where external confirmation is actively used.","src":"Chapter 6.4","sec":"6.4"}],"theory":[{"q":"Distinguish between positive and negative external confirmation requests, and state the circumstances in which negative confirmation may be used.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"A **positive** confirmation request asks the respondent to reply in every case — either confirming agreement or filling in the requested information. It ordinarily provides more reliable evidence, but carries the risk that a respondent replies without actually verifying the information."},{"p":"A **negative** confirmation request asks the respondent to reply only if they disagree with the stated information. It provides less reliable evidence, since no response gives no explicit evidence the request was even received and checked."},{"p":"Negative confirmation may be used to reduce risk to an acceptable level when: the assessed risk of material misstatement is lower; a large number of small balances is involved; a substantial number of errors is not expected; and the auditor has no reason to believe respondents will disregard the requests."}],"src":"Chapter 6.4","sec":"6.4"},{"q":"Using the CAVEBOP mnemonic, state the six matters an auditor seeks to establish when verifying an asset, and explain why only four of them (VEBOP) apply in years after acquisition.","marks":6,"a":[{"ol":["Cost","Authorization","Value","Existence","Beneficial ownership","Presentation in the accounts"]},{"p":"Cost and authorization relate to the asset's **acquisition**, and are therefore ordinarily verified once, in the year the asset was acquired. In subsequent years, only the remaining four aspects — valuation, existence, beneficial ownership and presentation/disclosure (VEBOP) — need re-verifying, since cost and authorization do not change year to year for an asset already on the books."}],"src":"Chapter 6.9","sec":"6.9"}]}},{"n":7,"t":"Audit Tests and Sampling Procedures","brief":"How the auditor actually selects what to test (audit sampling, statistical vs. non-statistical, sample design/size/selection/evaluation), the substantive testing programmes for key transaction cycles and Statement of Financial Position items (including the notoriously difficult verification of inventory), analytical procedures, and the specialised audit procedures for subsequent events, contingencies and the going concern basis — closing with quality control at both the firm and individual-assignment level.","outcomes":["Explain the role sampling techniques play in auditing","Draw audit programmes appropriate for substantive testing for key audit areas","Verify the existence, valuation, ownership, presentation and disclosure of Statement of Financial Position items","Conduct analytical review procedures","Outline audit procedures relevant to events occurring after the reporting date and contingencies","Outline the indications for going concern difficulties","Describe the quality control procedures required at assignment level and at the firm level"],"secs":[{"n":"7.1","t":"Audit sampling","b":[{"p":"When designing audit procedures, the auditor determines appropriate means for selecting items for testing, to gather sufficient appropriate audit evidence."},{"h4":"Key definitions"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Term","Definition"],"rows":[["**Audit sampling**","Applying audit procedures to less than 100% of items within a class of transactions or account balance, such that all sampling units have a chance of selection — enabling the auditor to form or assist in forming a conclusion about the whole population. May be statistical or non-statistical."],["**Error**","Control deviations (tests of controls) or misstatements (tests of details); \"total error\" means the total deviation rate or total misstatement."],["**Anomalous error**","An error arising from an isolated event that has not recurred except on specifically identifiable occasions, and is therefore NOT representative of errors in the population."],["**Population**","The entire set of data from which a sample is selected and about which the auditor wishes to conclude — may be divided into strata (sub-populations), each examined separately."],["**Sampling risk**","The risk that the auditor's sample-based conclusion differs from the conclusion reached by testing the entire population."],["**Non-sampling risk**","The risk of an erroneous conclusion for any reason unrelated to sample size — e.g. relying on persuasive (not conclusive) evidence, using inappropriate procedures, or misinterpreting evidence and failing to recognise an error."],["**Sampling unit**","The individual items constituting a population (e.g. cheques on a deposit list, sales invoices, debtor balances) or a monetary unit."],["**Statistical sampling**","Sampling with BOTH: (i) random item selection; and (ii) use of probability theory to evaluate results, including measuring sampling risk. Lacking either characteristic makes it non-statistical sampling."],["**Stratification**","Dividing a population into subpopulations, each a group of sampling units sharing similar characteristics (often monetary value)."],["**Tolerable error**","The maximum error in a population the auditor is willing to accept."]]}},{"key":"Sampling risk has **two directions**, with opposite consequences: (i) concluding controls are more effective (or a material error does not exist) than is actually true — this affects audit **effectiveness** and is more likely to cause an inappropriate opinion; and (ii) concluding controls are less effective (or a material error exists) than is actually true — this affects audit **efficiency**, usually triggering unnecessary additional work. The mathematical complements of these risks are called **confidence levels**."},{"h4":"§7.1.2 Selecting items for testing"},{"p":"Three means are available: selecting all items (100% examination), selecting specific items, and audit sampling. The choice (or combination) depends on the risk of material misstatement for the assertion tested and audit efficiency — but the auditor must always be satisfied the method is effective in providing sufficient appropriate evidence."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Approach","When appropriate"],"rows":[["**Selecting all items**","Rare for tests of controls, more common for tests of details — e.g. a small number of large-value items, significant risk with no other sufficient means, or where automated/repetitive processing makes 100% examination cost-effective via CAATs."],["**Selecting specific items**","Based on the auditor's understanding of the entity, risk assessment, and population characteristics — e.g. high-value/key items, items over a certain amount, items chosen to obtain information, or items to test specific control activities. Subject to non-sampling risk. **Does NOT constitute audit sampling** — results cannot be projected to the whole population, so the auditor still considers whether the untested remainder needs its own evidence if material."],["**Audit sampling**","Applying either statistical or non-statistical sampling to a class of transactions or account balance, where results CAN be projected to the whole population."]]}},{"key":"For any sample to be **representative**, every item in the population must have an opportunity of being selected."},{"h4":"Statistical vs. non-statistical sampling"},{"p":"The choice is a matter of judgment about the most efficient means of obtaining sufficient appropriate evidence — e.g. for tests of controls, analysing the **nature and cause** of errors is often more important than a statistical count of their presence/absence, favouring non-statistical sampling in that case."},{"key":"Sample size is **not** a valid criterion for distinguishing statistical from non-statistical sampling — an approach can use elements of statistical technique (e.g. computer-generated random numbers) without meeting the full definition. Only where the approach genuinely has statistical sampling's characteristics are statistical measurements of sampling risk actually valid."},{"p":"Conditions favouring statistical sampling:"},{"ol":["the population must be **sufficiently large** (minimising the potential margin of error);","the system producing the records must be **sufficiently reliable** — without a system worthy of the name, it is dangerous to treat any recorded transaction as representative, and a vouching audit of nearly all transactions may be the only alternative;","all items within the population must be **homogeneous** — falling within the same materiality band, recorded the same way, and subject to the same internal checks/controls; and","all items must be **identifiable and accessible**, so each stands an equal chance of selection."]},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Advantages of statistical sampling","Disadvantages of statistical sampling"],"rows":[["States a definite confidence level, within a stated precision limit, that the whole population conforms to the sample result.","Auditing has never been a mathematical discipline — developed intuition has historically been more valuable than mathematical knowledge."],["Sample size is objectively determined given the accepted degree of risk.","Mathematically valid schemes take a long time, during which a good deal of representative random sampling could otherwise have been conducted."],["Fixing precision/confidence levels compels the auditor to clarify audit objectives.","Results in sample sizes much smaller than traditional practice — validity then depends on rigid adherence to statistical tenets and every team member's technical grasp of them."],["Results can be expressed in precise mathematical terms.","Many pre-implementation decisions are inherently subjective, making some auditors suspicious of the technique's validity (or how a court might view conclusions reached this way)."],["Bias in selecting sample units is largely eliminated.","Often needs back-up testing within the population anyway — large items, non-routine items, sensitive items."]]}},{"h4":"Judgmental (non-statistical) sampling"},{"def":{"t":"Judgmental sampling","d":"selecting a sample of appropriate size based on the auditor's judgment of what is desirable."}},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Advantages","Disadvantages"],"rows":[["In use for many years — lends itself to easy application through experience.","Unscientific."],["No special statistical knowledge required.","Wasteful — sample sizes are usually too large."],["No time wasted on mathematics.","No quantitative results obtained."],["The auditor can apply judgment — \"the sixth sense.\"","Personal bias in selection is unavoidable, and there is no real logic to sample size/selection."],["—","Selection can be imbalanced to suit the auditor's convenience — e.g. items near year end for cut-off — and conclusions from it are usually vague."]]}},{"h4":"§7.1.4 Designing the sample"},{"p":"The auditor considers the audit procedure's objectives and the population's attributes: (a) the specific objectives and best combination of procedures to achieve them, clarifying what constitutes an error and what population to sample; (b) what conditions constitute an error, by reference to the procedure's objective — e.g. in testing receivables existence via confirmation, a customer payment made before but received shortly after the confirmation date is **not** an error, and a mis-posting between customer accounts that does not affect the total receivables balance is not an error **for this specific procedure**, even though it may matter elsewhere (e.g. fraud assessment); and (c) an expected error rate (tests of controls) or expected error amount (tests of details) — if the expected error rate is unacceptably high, tests of controls are normally not performed at all; if the expected amount of error is high in tests of details, 100% examination or a large sample may be appropriate."},{"h4":"§7.1.5 Population"},{"p":"The population must be: (a) **appropriate to the objective**, including the direction of testing — e.g. testing for accounts payable overstatement uses the payables listing itself, but testing for **understatement** requires a different population entirely (subsequent disbursements, unpaid invoices, suppliers' statements, unmatched receiving reports); and (b) **complete** — e.g. conclusions about all vouchers require confidence that all vouchers were actually filed."},{"h4":"§7.1.6 Value weighted selection"},{"p":"Often efficient for tests of details (particularly overstatement testing): defining the sampling unit as individual **monetary units** making up a balance, then examining whichever items contain the selected monetary units. This directs audit effort toward larger-value items (which have a greater chance of selection) and can result in smaller sample sizes overall."},{"h4":"§7.1.7 Sample size"},{"key":"Sample size is affected by the sampling risk the auditor is willing to accept — the **lower** the acceptable risk, the **greater** the sample size needed. It can be determined via a statistically-based formula, or through professional judgment objectively applied."},{"h4":"§7.1.8 Selecting the sample — five methods"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Method","How it works"],"rows":[["**Random sampling**","Ensures all items have an equal chance of selection, e.g. via random number tables."],["**Systematic sampling**","A constant interval between selections, with a random start — the auditor must ensure the interval does not coincide with a pattern in the population."],["**Haphazard sampling**","An acceptable alternative to random selection, provided the auditor is satisfied the sample is not unrepresentative — care is needed to avoid bias toward easily located items."],["**Stratified sampling**","Divides the population into layers/strata, each tested separately (by random or interval methods) — appropriate where values vary widely; each stratum's relative materiality determines its own required confidence level and precision range."],["**Cluster sampling**","Suitable where data is grouped so that selecting a cluster is itself a sensible test — each cluster is numbered, one selected randomly, then tested fully or by further random/interval sampling. Most precise when each cluster is as varied internally, and as similar to other clusters, as possible. Useful when the population is too dispersed for other selection forms."]]}},{"h4":"§7.1.9-7.1.12 Performing, evaluating and concluding"},{"p":"If a selected item is unsuitable for the designed procedure (e.g. a voided cheque selected to test payment authorisation), an appropriately chosen **replacement** is examined instead — provided the original item genuinely does not constitute an error. Where suitable alternative procedures **cannot** be performed on a selected item (e.g. lost documentation), the auditor ordinarily treats that item as an error."},{"p":"For **tests of controls**, the auditor considers the direct effect of identified errors on the financial statements, and the effect on control effectiveness/audit approach (e.g. where an error results from management override). An **anomalous error** requires a high degree of certainty (via additional procedures) that it is genuinely non-representative — e.g. an error traced to a single day's computer breakdown, or an incorrect formula confirmed to be used at only one branch."},{"p":"For **tests of details**, the auditor **projects** monetary errors found in the sample to the whole population, comparing the projected total against **tolerable error** (itself an amount ≤ the materiality used for that class of transactions/balance)."},{"key":"If total projected error plus anomalous error is close to, but below, tolerable error, the auditor considers the persuasiveness of results in light of other procedures, recognising that a **different sample** might have produced a different estimate that exceeds tolerable error — additional evidence reduces this risk."},{"p":"If the evaluation indicates the population assessment needs revision, the auditor may: (a) request management investigate and adjust; (b) modify the nature/timing/extent of further procedures (e.g. extend the sample, test an alternative control, modify substantive procedures); and/or (c) consider the effect on the audit report."},{"p":"The steps of audit sampling, summarised: define the audit objective; define the population (must be complete); define the sample size; select items using the appropriate technique; test the selected items; and evaluate results — analysing errors against expected rates, estimating total population error, and ensuring the final conclusion is valid and clearly documented in the current file."}]},{"n":"7.2","t":"Substantive testing procedures","b":[{"key":"Substantive procedures detect material misstatement at the **assertion level**, and are performed **irrespective of** the assessed risk — the auditor always designs and performs substantive procedures for each **material** class of transactions, account balance and disclosure. Where a **significant risk** has been identified, the auditor performs substantive procedures specifically responsive to that risk."},{"p":"Substantive procedures related to the financial statement closing process always include: (a) agreeing the financial statements to the underlying accounting records; and (b) examining material journal entries and other adjustments made while preparing the financial statements."},{"h4":"§7.2.1 Nature of substantive procedures"},{"p":"Substantive analytical procedures suit large, predictable transaction volumes; tests of details generally suit assertions about account balances (existence, valuation). Designing substantive analytical procedures involves considering: their suitability given the assertions; the reliability of the underlying data; whether the expectation is sufficiently precise; and what amount of difference from expectation is acceptable."},{"h4":"§7.2.2 Substantive testing programmes for key audit areas"},{"p":"Illustrative audit programmes for three key transaction cycles:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Cycle","Objectives and key programme steps"],"rows":[["**Sales and receipts**","Objectives: confirm reliability/accuracy/completeness of the accounting; confirm all goods supplied were duly invoiced and recorded. Key steps: test the numerical sequence of dispatch notes and sales invoices; trace a sample from dispatch notes through to sales invoice, sales day book, inventory records and proof of delivery; check invoices against customer orders, credit procedures, price lists, calculations and postings; for receipts, trace to the cash book, paying-in slip and bank statement; cast/cross-cast the sales day book and control account; vouch a sample of credit notes against correspondence, authorisation and inventory records; confirm discounts match the client's trade terms; and scrutinise both books for large/unusual items throughout the year."],["**Purchases and payments**","Objectives: confirm reliability/accuracy/completeness of the accounting; confirm all incurred liabilities are recorded and represent goods/services actually received. Key steps: test the numerical sequence of goods-received notes and purchase invoices; verify authorisation, cash discounts, price agreement, delivery-note matching, and additions/calculations (including VAT) on a sample of invoices; check allocation to inventory/WIP records; cast/cross-cast the purchase day book and petty cash book; select miscellaneous cash-book payments and verify authorisation/allocation/vouching; and scrutinise the books for large/unusual items."],["**Payroll**","Objectives: ensure pay levels are in order; check accuracy/validity of deductions; confirm proper payments; test net-pay reasonableness; confirm reliability/accuracy/completeness of accounting entries; ensure correct PAYE/social security treatment. Key steps: select a sample of employees and check gross pay against hourly/piece-work/overtime rate lists, salary lists and employment contracts; check hours worked against time records for hourly staff, and output returns for piece workers; check PAYE/NSITF deduction additions and cross-cast to net pay; inspect receipts for cash payments; trace gross wages to income tax deduction and holiday pay records; trace a sample of net wages to the cash book; check wage-summary casts and postings to the nominal ledger; review payroll cost trends for unusual variation; trace joiners/leavers to ensure correct-period payments; and, for significant cash payments, consider attending to observe the pay-out."]]}}]},{"n":"7.3","t":"Verification of Statement of Financial Position items","b":[{"def":{"t":"Verification","d":"the process by which auditors seek confirmation of the existence, ownership, cost/valuation and appropriate presentation/disclosure of assets and liabilities — relying heavily on vouching work having been properly executed."}},{"p":"Specific verification objectives, in summary:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Objective","What the auditor establishes"],"rows":[["**Cost**","What the client actually paid, supported by supplier invoices/receipts, traced to the bank statement/cash book."],["**Authorization**","That acquisition passed through the company's proper approval procedures, evidenced by documents."],["**Valuation**","That assets appear at fair value (which may differ from original cost) and liabilities at the amount likely to be needed for settlement — including correct determination of depreciation or other value falls on an acceptable basis."],["**Existence**","Via physical verification wherever practicable, ideally on a random selection basis if 100% inspection is impractical."],["**Beneficial ownership**","That the client genuinely owns the asset / owes the liability — sometimes via documents like leases/deeds, sometimes only via corroborative evidence or outside representations."],["**Presentation/disclosure**","That items are appropriately presented, with full disclosure."]]}},{"note":"Verification procedures draw on the same techniques covered in Chapter 6: observation, inspection, enquiry and confirmation, computation, and analytical review."},{"h4":"Verification objectives and programmes for key assets/liabilities"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Item","Objectives and key programme steps"],"rows":[["**Non-current assets**","Objectives: ownership confirmed; self-constructed assets properly capitalised; cost/valuation correctly stated with consistent depreciation; physical existence verified; proper classification/presentation. Steps: obtain a summary reconciling to the prior year's Statement of Financial Position; test additions against suppliers' invoices/architects' certificates and capital expenditure authorisations; test disposal proceeds and disposal authority; verify original cost/depreciation elimination on disposal, and profit/loss calculations; inspect title deeds/leases; verify any revaluation's basis and the valuer's independence/qualifications; physically inspect samples; check depreciation rates are board-approved and reasonable; reconcile the asset register to the accounts; and verify adequate insurance cover."],["**Investments**","Objectives: documentary ownership evidence exists; period changes were bona fide transactions; the valuation basis is appropriate and consistently applied; amounts are properly classified/described. Steps: analyse investments and test clerical accuracy; obtain third-party confirmation or physically inspect securities; test interest/dividend income; check acquisitions/redemptions and capital distributions/bonus/rights issues; verify quoted prices at the reporting date; assess unlisted investment valuation; and confirm current-vs-long-term classification."],["**Debtors**","Objectives: confirm accuracy/collectability of the balance; confirm the doubtful debts provision is adequate. Steps: examine material ledger accounts and cast the balance list, tracing to the control account; select balances for direct confirmation via circularisation and investigate discrepancies; where no circularisation, check post-date payments; examine credit balances (ensuring proper authorisation and separate disclosure under creditors); verify the doubtful debts/discount provisions are reasonable; review credit control compliance; verify bad-debt write-off authorisation and prior recovery efforts; and examine post-year-end credit notes for genuine sales."],["**Cash at bank and in hand**","Objectives: confirm existence/accuracy of year-end balances; confirm proper classification/description. Bank steps: obtain and compare the year-end bank reconciliation against the cash book/bank statements; trace outstanding cheques/lodgements to subsequent clearance; scrutinise for dishonoured cheques; obtain a direct bank confirmation certificate; check overdraft security against facility agreements. Cash steps: count cash in the cashier's presence and reconcile; verify last-day receipts were banked or included in the cash balance; examine pre-/post-year-end cash counts and petty cash replenishment entries."],["**Trade creditors**","Objectives: confirm all existing/incurred creditors are recorded at the correct amount and represent genuine claims; confirm consistent, properly described/classified presentation. Steps: test extraction of the purchase ledger and reconcile against supplier statements (or circularise where unavailable); cast the balance list and trace to the control account; separate and reclassify debit balances under debtors; review goods-received-not-invoiced provisioning against delivery/goods-received notes; review major suppliers' terms for reservation of title; reconcile inter-company/inter-branch balances; and check classification/disclosure."]]}}]},{"n":"7.4","t":"Verification of inventories","b":[{"key":"Inventory has always been the asset posing the **greatest verification difficulties**, because: it is almost always **material**, especially for manufacturers; misstatement has a **direct, one-to-one effect** on reported profit (an inventory overstatement equally overstates profit); it is the **only** published-accounts item not itself arising from the double-entry process — its figure depends largely on physical inventory-taking, making it the item most susceptible to manipulation; for a manufacturer, it is not really one asset at all but several (raw materials, WIP, finished goods, etc.), each with its own difficulties; verification sometimes needs highly technical expertise beyond the auditor's own; items require categorisation (sound, defective, slow-moving, obsolete, scrap) that materially affects value, yet the auditor often lacks the competence to question that categorisation; inventory may be held across multiple locations needing **simultaneous** counting to avoid omission or double-counting from goods moving between sites; a moving production line or construction site complicates counting further, since work is unlikely to be halted specially for it; and some items pose basic identification/measurement problems (e.g. measuring a large volume of liquid — by volume or weight?)."},{"p":"Objectives of inventory verification: confirm all inventory/WIP belonging to the client has been brought into the accounts; confirm third-party inventory in the client's possession is separately identified and excluded; confirm obsolete/damaged/slow-moving inventory is separately identified and stated at realisable value; confirm the value stated is correctly calculated on a basis consistent with prior years; and confirm adequate security arrangements exist."},{"h4":"Audit programme for inventory quantity"},{"p":"Before the count: review the client's own counting instructions for adequacy, and note any weaknesses to raise with the audit manager. During the count: attend to observe, ensuring slow-moving/obsolete/damaged inventory is segregated, all locations are covered, third-party inventory is segregated, and inventory sheets are properly accounted for/dated/signed. After the count: complete the standard inventory-taking report; obtain details of the last goods inward/outward notes for cut-off testing; ensure all rough sheets are processed into the final sheets; check quantities tested at observation against final sheets; test extensions/additions; reconcile final sheets to the inventory summary and to any perpetual inventory records (bin cards, stores ledgers), investigating discrepancies; and reconcile the final accounts figure with perpetual/management-account totals, scheduling reasons for any divergence."},{"p":"For inventory held by **third parties**, obtain direct certificates identifying the stock's nature/quantity/purpose, confirming good condition and freedom from lien/charge, and any necessary deductions for slow-moving/damaged/obsolete stock."},{"h4":"Audit programmes for valuation"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Aspect","Key programme steps"],"rows":[["**Inventory valuation (general)**","Test final-sheet prices to purchase invoices; scrutinise bin cards/records for apparently obsolete stock; review slow-moving/obsolete items with the storekeeper and responsible officials; assess the appropriate value reduction given prior-year valuation and near-term sale prospects; and schedule the details for the manager's attention."],["**Work in progress (WIP)**","Test the reliability of the costing system (adequate stores-issue/labour-usage controls, reasonable overhead allocation confirmed against the actual — or normal, if below-capacity — rate, and a WIP valuation method consistent with prior years); compare selected WIP items' cost-plus-completion estimate against selling price, providing for anticipated losses; and, for long-term contracts, confirm the WIP calculation basis is permissible under the relevant accounting standard."],["**Overall inventory schedule**","Prepare a schedule summarising inventory/WIP by category with prior-year comparatives, reconciled to the Statement of Financial Position and final inventory sheets, stating the valuation basis and evidence obtained; state the proportion of value covered by observation/testing; compare key ratios (inventory/WIP to sales, raw material/WIP/finished goods to cost of sales) against the prior period, scheduling reasons for significant changes; and review the valuation on a current cost basis, discussing any material difference with directors."]]}},{"h4":"Attendance at inventory taking"},{"key":"It remains **management's** responsibility to ensure reported inventory figures represent inventory physically in existence and complete — this responsibility is **not reduced** merely because the auditor attends the physical count. It is the **auditor's** responsibility to obtain evidence enabling conclusions about the validity of the quantities underlying the reported figure — principally through test counts made by the auditor themselves, and reviewing the entity's inventory records/control systems to decide how much to rely on attendance."},{"p":"Where inventory is material and the auditor is relying on management's count as existence evidence, the auditor should **attend** the count — attendance is normally the best way of evidencing the proper functioning of management's counting procedures, and hence the existence/condition of inventory. WIP existence evidence often also comes from the count, though where WIP's nature makes counting impracticable, inspection still helps plan procedures and assess completion stage, even absent a formal count to attend."},{"h4":"Types of inventory taking"},{"p":"Physical verification may be a full count (or measurement) of everything at year end or a nearby date, a partial count statistically extrapolated, or continuous inventory-checking throughout the year (some businesses use continuous methods for some items and a full count for others). Evidence is most effective when the count happens **at** the year end; counts before/after are acceptable provided intervening movements can be examined/substantiated — the greater the interval, the greater the difficulty in substantiating the year-end figure (lessened by strong internal control and satisfactory records)."},{"key":"Where **continuous** inventory-checking is used, the auditor tests to confirm management: maintains adequate, up-to-date records; has satisfactory counting/test-counting procedures covering all inventory at least once a year; and investigates/corrects material differences between book records and physical counts."},{"h4":"Inventory-taking procedures: before, during and after"},{"p":"**Before**: review prior-year working papers and discuss inventory changes with management; discuss counting arrangements/instructions; understand the nature/volume of inventory and high-value items; consider location implications for control; review internal/accounting systems for difficulty areas (e.g. cut-off); consider internal audit involvement; ensure representative coverage of locations/inventories/procedures, with particular attention to high-value items; arrange third-party confirmation (or attendance at their premises, if their holding is material or they seem unreliable); and establish whether expert help is needed for specialised inventory. The auditor evaluates the adequacy of the client's own written instructions, seeking improvements where inadequate."},{"p":"**During**: ascertain whether client staff follow instructions properly, making own test counts and immediately raising unsatisfactory results with supervising management (possibly requesting a recount); select items both from count records and physical inventory, checking one against the other (particular attention to high-value items); include working-paper evidence (rough sheet copies, sheet-sequence details) for subsequent testing; determine whether procedures for identifying damaged/obsolete/slow-moving stock operate properly, gathering condition/age/usage/completion-stage information; assess cut-off procedure adequacy; and conclude whether the count is reliable enough as an existence basis, forming an overall impression of inventory levels/values for later reasonableness judgment."},{"p":"**After**: follow up the matters recorded during the count (using rough-sheet copies/test-count details to check the final sheets are accurate/complete); check third-party replies, follow up queries, and notify senior management of serious problems encountered."}]},{"n":"7.5","t":"Analytical procedures","b":[{"def":{"t":"Analytical procedures","d":"evaluations of financial information through a study of plausible relationships among financial and non-financial data — including investigating fluctuations/relationships inconsistent with other information, or deviating significantly from predicted amounts."}},{"p":"Analytical procedures compare the entity's financial information against: comparable prior-period information; anticipated results (budgets, forecasts, or an auditor estimate like depreciation); and similar-industry information. They also consider relationships among financial elements expected to follow a predictable pattern (e.g. gross margin %), and between financial and relevant non-financial information (e.g. payroll cost to headcount)."},{"p":"Analytical procedures are used for three purposes: as **risk assessment procedures**, to understand the entity/environment; as **substantive procedures**, where more effective/efficient than tests of details; and as an **overall review** at the end of the audit."},{"h4":"§7.5.2 As risk assessment procedures"},{"p":"Applied using both financial and non-financial information (e.g. sales vs. selling floor space, or vs. volume sold) — may reveal aspects of the entity the auditor was previously unaware of, helping determine the nature/timing/extent of further procedures."},{"h4":"§7.5.3-7.5.7 As substantive procedures"},{"p":"The choice between tests of details and substantive analytical procedures (or a combination) rests on judgment about relative effectiveness/efficiency. The auditor ordinarily enquires about the availability/reliability of information needed, and may use entity-prepared analytical data if satisfied it is properly prepared. Designing them requires considering:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Factor","What it involves"],"rows":[["**Suitability given the assertions**","Generally more applicable to large, predictable transaction volumes; relies on data relationships continuing absent known contrary conditions. Depends on the risk assessment (e.g. weak sales-order controls push toward tests of details for receivables) and whether tests of details are also applied to the same assertion (e.g. combining receivables ageing analysis with subsequent-receipts testing)."],["**Reliability of the data**","Influenced by source (more reliable if independent/external), comparability (broad industry data may need supplementing for a specialised producer), nature/relevance (whether budgets are set as expected results vs. aspirational goals), and controls over the data's preparation."],["**Precision of the expectation**","Depends on prediction accuracy (gross margin comparisons are ordinarily more consistent than discretionary expenses like R&D or advertising), the degree of disaggregation possible (more effective applied to individual operating sections than the whole entity), and the availability of both financial and non-financial information."],["**Acceptable difference from expectation**","Driven primarily by materiality and the desired assurance level — considering whether a combination of individually-acceptable differences could aggregate unacceptably. The higher the risk of material misstatement, the smaller the acceptable difference before investigation is required."]]}},{"h4":"§7.5.8-7.5.9 Overall review and investigating unusual items"},{"p":"Analytical procedures are applied at/near the audit's end, forming an overall conclusion on whether the financial statements as a whole are consistent with the auditor's understanding — corroborating conclusions from individual components, and potentially revealing a previously unrecognised risk requiring re-evaluation of planned procedures."},{"key":"When significant fluctuations or unexpected relationships are found, the auditor **investigates** — beginning with management enquiries, then corroborating the response against the auditor's own understanding/other evidence, and applying other procedures where management cannot provide an adequate explanation."},{"h4":"§7.5.10 Public sector perspective"},{"p":"Traditional business-entity relationships may not hold in government/public-sector audits — e.g. little direct revenue-expenditure relationship, or no relationship between asset expenditure and reported asset amounts where acquisition costs are not capitalised; industry comparative data may not exist. Other relationships (e.g. cost per kilometre of road construction, vehicles acquired vs. retired) may still be relevant — referring to private-sector data where appropriate, or building an in-house reference database."},{"h4":"§7.5.11 The practice of analytical procedures"},{"p":"Items commonly subjected to analytical procedures: creditors and purchases; inventories and cost of sales; fixed assets, depreciation, repairs and maintenance; loans and interest; investments and investment income; debtors and bad debt expense; and debtors and sales."},{"p":"Ratios commonly computed: gross profit margin; net profit margin; return on capital employed; current ratio; quick (acid test) ratio; debtors turnover period; creditors payment period; rate of inventory turnover; gearing ratio; price/earnings ratio; dividend per share; earnings per share; earnings yield; and return on net assets — plus any other ratio the client's specific circumstances call for (e.g. revenue per passenger-mile for an airline)."},{"h4":"§7.5.12 Other areas examined"},{"p":"Changes in products/customers/distribution/returns; the effect of inflation, industrial disputes, production-technology changes and activity levels on wages; explanations for major standard-costing variances (particularly overhead over/under-absorption, given its inventory-valuation effect); production/sales trends and their effect on obsolete-inventory provisions; changes in labour/overhead content of production costs; comparisons of rent, rates, interest and vehicle-running expenses against agreements/prior-year figures/known variations; review of the Statement of Profit or Loss and OCI for possible omissions; and variations from industry/local trends or known trading disturbances (strikes, depot closures, supplier failures)."}]},{"n":"7.6","t":"Auditing procedures for subsequent events","b":[{"def":{"t":"Subsequent event","d":"an event (favourable or unfavourable) occurring between the reporting date and the date the financial statements are approved by the board of directors."}},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Category","Definition and examples"],"rows":[["**Adjusting events**","Provide additional evidence of conditions existing at the reporting date, requiring changes to amounts in the financial statements. Examples: subsequent determination of purchase price/sale proceeds for assets bought/sold before year end; a valuation evidencing permanent property value diminution; evidence of permanent diminution in an unlisted long-term investment; post-date sale proceeds/evidence about inventory net realisable value; renegotiation of debtor amounts or debtor insolvency; subsidiary/associate dividend declarations relating to prior periods; new taxation rate information; insurance claims settled that were in negotiation at the reporting date; and discovery of errors/fraud showing the financial statements were incorrect."],["**Non-adjusting events**","Arise after the reporting date, concerning conditions that did NOT exist at that time — so they do NOT change amounts, but may need disclosure by way of notes if material enough to affect whether the statements mislead. Examples: mergers/acquisitions; reconstructions; share/debenture issues; major asset purchases/sales/losses; opening or closing significant trading activities (if unanticipated); property/investment value declines confirmed to have occurred after year end; foreign exchange rate changes; government actions (e.g. nationalisation); strikes/labour disputes; and pension benefit augmentation."]]}},{"p":"For each disclosed subsequent event, the notes should state: its nature, and an estimate of its financial effect (or a statement that estimation is impracticable)."},{"h4":"Auditing procedures"},{"p":"Procedures to identify subsequent events are performed as near as practicable to the audit report date: enquiring about management's own procedures for identifying such events; reading post-period-end minutes of member/board/audit-committee meetings; enquiring about matters discussed where minutes are not yet available; reading interim financial statements, cash flow forecasts and related management reports; and enquiring directly of management about relevant occurrences."},{"p":"Specific enquiries might cover: the status of subjective-judgment or preliminary-data items (e.g. ongoing litigation); new commitments/borrowings/guarantees; asset sales occurred or planned; new share/debenture issues or merger/liquidation plans; asset destruction (e.g. fire, flood); developments in risk areas/contingencies; and events calling into question accounting policies used, including the going concern presumption."},{"h4":"Events discovered after the audit report but before the financial statements are issued"},{"key":"The auditor has **no obligation** to actively enquire about the financial statements after the audit report date — but management would reasonably be expected to inform the auditor of material subsequent events discovered before the statements are actually issued."},{"p":"If the auditor becomes aware of such an event, they establish whether amendment is needed, discuss with management, and consider the report's implications. Where management **amends** the statements, the auditor performs necessary additional procedures, issues a new report dated no earlier than the amended statements' approval, and extends procedures to that new date. Where management **does not amend**, and the report has not yet been released, the auditor issues a **qualified report**; if the report has already been released, the auditor requests management not issue the statements — and if they are issued anyway, must act to prevent reliance on the auditor's report (possibly seeking legal advice)."},{"h4":"Events discovered after the financial statements have been issued"},{"p":"If, before the statements are laid before members, the auditor learns of an event that existed at the report date and would have changed the report had it been known, the same establish/discuss/consider-implications process applies. Where management **amends**: the auditor performs necessary procedures, considers stock-exchange publicity requirements and regulatory communication, reviews management's steps to inform prior recipients, and issues a **new report** — referencing the reason for amendment (in an explanatory paragraph or the report itself), referencing the earlier report, and dating the new report no earlier than the amended statements' approval. Where management **does not** take these steps and does not amend when the auditor believes they should, the auditor notifies management that action will be taken to prevent future reliance on the report, and may need legal advice."}]},{"n":"7.7","t":"Audit procedures for contingencies","b":[{"def":{"t":"Contingency","d":"a condition existing at the reporting date, whose outcome will be confirmed only by the occurrence or non-occurrence of one or more uncertain future events. Uncertainties inherent to ordinary accounting estimates (asset lives, bad debt amounts, inventory NRV, long-term contract outcomes, property/foreign currency valuation) do NOT fall within this definition."}},{"key":"A **material contingent loss** should be **accrued** where it is probable a future event will confirm a loss estimable with reasonable accuracy at approval date. A material contingent loss **not** accrued should be **disclosed**, unless the possibility of loss is remote. **Contingent gains should never be accrued** — a material contingent gain is only disclosed if realisation is probable, and once realisation becomes reasonably certain, it stops being a contingency and accrual becomes appropriate."},{"p":"Required disclosures for an unaccounted material contingency: its nature; the uncertainties affecting its ultimate outcome; and a prudent estimate of the financial effect (or a statement that estimation is impracticable) — for a contingent loss, the disclosed amount is reduced by any amount already accrued and any component whose loss possibility is remote (only the **net** amount need be disclosed), before taking account of taxation, with the taxation implications explained where needed for proper understanding."},{"p":"Auditing procedures for contingencies: reviewing the claims-recording system and how claims reach management/board attention; arrangements for instructing solicitors; examining board/committee minutes for claim references; examining solicitors' bills and correspondence (requesting confirmation of unbilled charges); obtaining a list of matters referred to solicitors with liability estimates; obtaining written assurance no other matters exist beyond those disclosed; considering contingencies from the prior year still applicable; and considering the nature of the client's business for likely contingency types (e.g. warranty claims)."}]},{"n":"7.8","t":"Audit procedures for the going concern basis","b":[{"key":"Financial statements are normally prepared assuming the entity is a **going concern**, unless stated otherwise. The auditor assesses whether this basis is appropriate, considers directors' written assertion on the matter, considers the adequacy of related disclosures, and factors both into their report."},{"h4":"§7.8.1 Is the client a going concern? Danger symptoms"},{"p":"Checklists of danger symptoms typically include: loan repayments falling due soon with refinancing not readily available; high/rising gearing; heavy/increasing reliance on short-term finance (trade credit, overdrafts); inability to take discounts, slowing creditor payment, or suppliers demanding cash terms; deferred normal purchases reducing inventory dangerously; substantial losses or declining profitability; capital expenditure switching to leasing; a net current liability position or declining current ratio; near-exhausted borrowing limits with no sign of reduced need; slowing debtor collection; rapid growth risking overtrading; dependence on a limited number of products/customers/suppliers; major capital project cancellations; and heavy dependence on an overseas holding company for finance/trade."},{"p":"Where such features cause concern, the auditor should at minimum: compare the client's cash flow forecast with available facilities for the next 12 months; obtain written confirmation from any holding company's board of its intention to support the subsidiary; and enquire into (with written evidence) any corrective steps the client is taking. If unable to be satisfied the client will continue in business, the auditor considers the going concern basis's validity, and the need for an appropriately worded qualification."},{"h4":"§7.8.2 Audit evidence"},{"p":"The auditor requests an adequate written assertion from the directors on the going concern basis's appropriateness — assessing adequacy by whether it is supported by an explanation of relevant matters/assumptions, and whether it considers circumstances over at least **one year** from the approval date. Absent an adequate assertion, the auditor considers the effect on the report."},{"p":"To gain sufficient appropriate evidence, the auditor may examine: systems for timely identification of future risk warnings; budget/forecast information and the reliability of the systems producing it; whether the underlying key assumptions seem appropriate; the sensitivity of budgets/forecasts to controllable and uncontrollable factors; the existence/adequacy/terms of borrowing facilities and supplier credit; and directors' plans for resolving any concerns — considering whether the plans are realistic and likely to be effectively implemented."},{"p":"The auditor may need to obtain **independent confirmation** of bank facilities and assess bankers' own intentions, particularly where: financial resources have a low margin; borrowing facilities are due for renewal soon; correspondence reveals the last renewal was difficult or came with new conditions; cash flow is projected to deteriorate significantly; security asset values are declining; or borrowing covenants have been (or may be) breached. Where the auditor cannot satisfy themselves on this despite deciding it necessary, they consider whether the inability (and its causes) need disclosure in the financial statements and/or reference in the audit report."},{"p":"Situations more likely to indicate a significant going concern concern: liabilities exceeding assets; default on loan terms or potential covenant breaches, plus significant liquidity/cash flow problems; major post-reporting-date losses/cash flow problems threatening continued existence; major litigation whose adverse outcome would imperil the company; denial/reduction of normal supplier credit terms; major debt repayments requiring refinancing for continued existence; outcome ranges wide enough that an unfavourable result affects going concern appropriateness; and fundamental market/technology changes the company cannot adequately adapt to."},{"note":"The auditor considers information relating to **beyond** one year from the approval date, when they become aware of it — discussing its relevance with the directors."},{"h4":"§7.8.3-7.8.4 Assessing disclosures and reporting"},{"p":"If the going concern basis is inappropriate, this affects how every other fundamental accounting concept is applied. If disclosures relevant to going concern appropriateness are inadequate, the auditor carefully considers whether the statements give a true and fair view, and whether a qualified opinion arising from disagreement over this disclosure is justified."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Scenario","Reporting response"],"rows":[["Significant concern exists, but the auditor does NOT disagree with the going concern basis being used.","Include an **explanatory paragraph** describing the matters giving rise to concern, referencing the relevant disclosures (typically under a \"Fundamental Uncertainty: going concern\" sub-heading) — this alone does NOT justify qualifying the report, provided the disclosures are adequate for a true and fair view."],["The auditor DISAGREES with the going concern presumption itself.","Issue a **modified opinion**, providing whatever additional information is considered necessary — even adequate disclosure of the reasons cannot, by itself, make the statements true and fair if the presumption is genuinely inappropriate."],["Significant concern exists, and disclosures are considered INADEQUATE for a true and fair view.","Issue a **modified opinion** arising from disagreement over the disclosure of these matters."]]}},{"key":"Where significant going concern concern exists, the auditor does not normally regard disclosures as adequate unless the financial statements include: a statement that they are prepared on the going concern basis; a statement of the pertinent facts; the nature of the concern; details of the matters giving rise to it; a statement of the directors' assumptions (clearly distinguished from the pertinent facts); where practicable, the directors' resolution plans; and details of any relevant actions already taken."},{"note":"In rare cases, directors may prepare financial statements on a basis **other than** going concern, to give a true and fair view. If the auditor considers this appropriate and the necessary disclosures are present, they include an explanatory paragraph describing the basis of preparation and referencing the relevant notes."}]},{"n":"7.9","t":"Quality control for audit work","b":[{"key":"Auditors urgently need internal quality control programmes — to improve client service, improve practice efficiency/profitability, and minimise exposure to legal liability risk. Every auditor should establish, maintain and **communicate** appropriate procedures, designed to ensure: staff associated with audit work are **fit and proper persons**; independence, resource availability and audit capability are considered before accepting/re-accepting an appointment; all principals/employees adhere to independence and confidentiality principles; and appropriate professional consultation and competence levels are maintained."},{"p":"Quality control operates at **two levels**: the audit firm, and the individual audit assignment."},{"h4":"§7.9.1 Quality control at the firm level"},{"p":"Firms establish policies/procedures ensuring all audits follow appropriate standards, communicated to personnel to provide confidence they are understood and implemented (including any specifically required by the relevant supervisory body's regulations). The nature/extent depends on practice size, geographic dispersion, organisation and cost-benefit considerations. Policies ordinarily incorporate:"},{"ol":["**Professional requirements** — personnel adhering to independence, integrity, objectivity, confidentiality and professional behaviour;","**Skills and competence** — personnel attaining and maintaining the required technical standards and professional competence;","**Acceptance and retention of clients** — evaluating prospective clients and reviewing existing ones on an ongoing basis, considering independence, ability to serve properly, and management's integrity;","**Assignment** — work assigned to personnel with the required technical training/proficiency;","**Delegation** — sufficient delegation, supervision and review at every level;","**Consultation** — occurring, when necessary, with appropriately expert persons within or outside the firm; and","**Monitoring** — the continued adequacy and operational efficiency of the policies themselves being monitored."]},{"h4":"§7.9.2 Quality control at the individual assignment level"},{"p":"The engagement partner applies the firm's policies as appropriate to each assignment. Delegated work must be **directed, supervised and reviewed** to provide confidence it is performed competently — with the required extent of each depending on the assistant's professional competence."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Element","What it involves"],"rows":[["**Direction**","Informing assistants of their responsibilities and the objectives of the procedures they perform — communicated via the audit programme, time budgets and the overall audit plan."],["**Supervision**","Monitoring the audit's progress (assistants' skill adequacy, work consistency with the plan/programme); becoming informed of and addressing significant accounting/auditing questions, modifying the plan/programme as needed; and resolving professional-judgment differences among personnel, considering the appropriate level of consultation."],["**Review**","Work by each assistant is reviewed by personnel of **at least equal competence**, checking: adherence to the audit programme; adequate documentation of work and results; resolution (or proper reflection) of significant audit matters; and that procedure objectives were achieved and conclusions are consistent with, and supported by, the work performed. Reviewed on a timely basis: the overall plan and programme; inherent/control risk assessments and any resulting modifications; documentation of substantive-procedure evidence and conclusions (including consultations); and the financial statements, proposed adjustments and proposed report."]]}},{"note":"For large, complex audits, the pre-issuance review process may include requesting personnel **not otherwise involved** in the audit to perform additional procedures — e.g. reading the annual report, or considering specific areas of audit judgment."}]},{"n":"7.10","t":"Chapter summary","b":[{"ol":["The sampling process and practices help auditors determine and identify the various ways of selecting items for testing.","Sampling enables the auditor to gather sufficient appropriate audit evidence from the population of transactions that occurred during the year.","The effects and differences between statistical and non-statistical sampling techniques enable the auditor to determine the strategy and choice of elements for testing.","Errors in the choice of samples can be evaluated with the knowledge gained at this stage.","Substantive procedures enable the auditor to conduct tests of details of transactions and relevant analytical procedures.","Analytical procedures ease the comparison of various indicators to establish expectations.","Audit programmes enable the auditor to establish the procedure for tests of details, and assign functions easily to assistants — a knowledgeable assistant or accountant using the audit programme can readily perform audit assignments.","Verification procedures establish the cost, authorisation, valuation, existence, beneficial ownership and presentation of assets and liabilities.","Entities are evaluated for their survival and continued existence as a going concern — the auditor uses information even after year end to evaluate this.","Firms set up procedures to strengthen their operations by instilling measures in their systems to guard against risk and ensure the delivery of desirable quality services."]}]},{"n":"7.11","t":"Study text questions — multiple-choice and short-answer","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with 10 questions — 5 multiple-choice and 5 short-answer — reproduced below in full with the official answers, each explained. The Auditing study text sets **no examination-type questions**; this bank is the whole of its end-of-chapter material."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"The questions, with the official answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["All those tests of transactions and balances, and other procedures such as analytical review, which seek to obtain audit evidence as to the completeness, accuracy and validity of all records is known as ……… (A) Substantive test; (B) Compliance test; (C) Walk-through test; (D) Depth test; (E) Random test.","Those tests which seek to provide audit evidence that internal control procedures are being applied as prescribed are known as (A) Audit evidence; (B) Substantive tests; (C) Compliance tests; (D) Procedural tests; (E) Investigating tests.","Which of the following tends to be most predictable for the purpose of analytical procedures applied as substantive tests? (A) Relationships involving statement of financial position accounts; (B) Transactions subject to management discretion; (C) Relationships involving income statement accounts; (D) Data subject to audit testing in the prior year; (E) Comparison of certificates of balance in the prior year.","An auditor wishes to perform tests of controls on cash disbursement procedures. If the controls leave no audit trail of documentary evidence, the auditor will most likely test the procedures by ……… (A) Inquiry and analytical procedures; (B) Confirmation and observations; (C) Analytical procedures and confirmation; (D) Observation and enquiry; (E) Comparison and confirmation.","A sample selection method where each item of the population has an equal chance of being selected is known as ……… (A) Stratified sampling; (B) Multistage sampling; (C) Random sampling; (D) Discovery sampling; (E) Judgmental sampling.","State **two** stages in statistical sampling as an auditing tool.","The risk that a material misstatement could occur in an account balance or class of transactions and not be detected by the accounting or internal control system is known as ………","The risk that the auditor will draw an invalid conclusion from his audit procedures is called ………","Selecting a sample of appropriate size on the basis of the auditor’s judgment of what is desirable is called ………","A sampling technique where each item of the population has an equal chance of being selected is called ………"]}],"a":[{"ol":["**A** — substantive tests. They test the **figures themselves**; compliance tests (also called tests of control) test whether the **controls** operated.","**C** — compliance tests (tests of control).","**C** — relationships involving **income statement** accounts. Income statement items represent transactions over a period and tend to follow stable relationships (gross margin, wages to hours, commission to sales); statement of financial position items are balances at a single date and are far more volatile, and items subject to **management discretion** are by definition unpredictable.","**D** — observation and enquiry. Where a control leaves no document behind — segregation of duties, for example, or a supervisor’s scrutiny — there is nothing to inspect, so the auditor must **watch it happening** and **ask** those who operate it.","**C** — random sampling. It is the defining feature of a **statistical** sample, and it is what permits the result to be projected over the population with a measurable confidence level.","**Any two of:** planning the sample; selecting the items to be tested; testing the items; evaluating the results of the test.","**Control risk.**","**Audit risk** *(the study text’s answer)*. More precisely this describes **detection risk** — the risk that the auditor’s own procedures fail — which is one of the three components of audit risk.","**Judgment sampling** (non-statistical sampling).","**Random sampling.**"]}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"This is the longest, most procedurally dense chapter in the subject — resist the temptation to memorise every audit-programme bullet; the exam tests concepts and definitions far more than exhaustive checklists. Prioritise: the statistical-sampling definition (random selection AND probability-theory evaluation — both required); the two directions of sampling risk and which affects effectiveness vs. efficiency; positive vs. negative confirmation logic (already met in Chapter 6, reapplied here to debtors); the CAVEBOP-style verification objectives; why inventory is uniquely hard to verify (the one-to-one profit effect, and being the only account not arising from double entry); the adjusting vs. non-adjusting subsequent event distinction; when a contingent loss is accrued vs. merely disclosed vs. ignored (and the asymmetric treatment of contingent gains); and the three going-concern reporting outcomes (explanatory paragraph only vs. two distinct grounds for a modified opinion).","errors":["Treating \"random selection\" alone as sufficient for statistical sampling — the definition requires BOTH random selection AND the use of probability theory to evaluate results (including measuring sampling risk); either alone makes it non-statistical.","Confusing the two directions of sampling risk — concluding things are BETTER than reality (an effectiveness risk, more likely to cause a wrong opinion) is a different, more dangerous risk than concluding things are WORSE than reality (an efficiency risk, causing wasted extra work).","Assuming selecting \"specific items\" (e.g. all balances over a threshold) counts as audit sampling — it explicitly does not, and results from it cannot be projected to the whole population.","Accruing a contingent GAIN the way a contingent loss is accrued — gains are never accrued while still contingent; only disclosed if probable, and only actually accrued once realisation is reasonably certain (at which point it is no longer a contingency at all).","Believing inadequate going-concern disclosure can be fixed with only an explanatory paragraph — an explanatory paragraph is the response when disclosures ARE adequate; inadequate disclosure requires a modified opinion instead."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"All those tests of transactions and balances and other procedures such as analytical review which seek to obtain audit evidence as to the completeness, accuracy and validity of all records is known as","o":["Substantive test","Compliance test","Walk-through test","Depth test","Random test"],"a":0,"w":"Substantive tests are precisely defined as procedures seeking evidence on the completeness, accuracy and validity of the accounting records themselves — distinct from compliance tests, which check whether CONTROL procedures are being followed.","src":"Chapter 7.2","sec":"7.2"},{"q":"Those tests which seek to provide audit evidence that internal control procedures are being applied as prescribed are known as","o":["Audit evidence","Substantive tests","Compliance tests","Procedural tests","Investigating tests"],"a":2,"w":"Compliance tests (tests of controls) specifically verify that prescribed control procedures are actually being applied — the auditing-terminology counterpart to substantive tests, which verify the records themselves.","src":"Chapter 7.1","sec":"7.1"},{"q":"Which of the following tends to be most predictable for the purpose of analytical procedures applied as substantive tests?","o":["Relationship involving statement of financial position accounts","Transactions subject to management discretion","Relationship involving income statement accounts","Data subject to audit testing in the prior year","Comparison of certificate of balance in prior year"],"a":2,"w":"Income statement (flow) relationships — such as gross margin percentages — tend to follow predictable patterns period to period, making them more suitable for analytical procedures than point-in-time balance sheet accounts or discretionary items.","src":"Chapter 7.5","sec":"7.5"},{"q":"An auditor wishes to perform tests of controls on cash disbursement procedures. If the controls leave no audit trail of documentary evidence, the auditor most likely will test the procedures by","o":["Inquiry and analytical procedures","Confirmation and observations","Analytical procedures and confirmation","Observation and enquiry","Comparison and confirmation"],"a":3,"w":"With no documentary trail to inspect or vouch, the only remaining ways to test that a control actually operates are to directly OBSERVE it being performed and to ENQUIRE of those who perform it.","src":"Chapter 7.1","sec":"7.1"},{"q":"A sample selection method where each item of the population has an equal chance of being selected is known as","o":["Stratified sampling","Multistage sampling","Random sampling","Discovery sampling","Judgmental sampling"],"a":2,"w":"Random sampling is defined precisely by this property — every item having an equal chance of selection, e.g. via random number tables.","src":"Chapter 7.1","sec":"7.1"}],"theory":[{"q":"Distinguish between adjusting and non-adjusting subsequent events, giving one example of each, and state what the financial statement notes should disclose for a disclosable subsequent event.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"**Adjusting events** provide additional evidence of conditions that existed at the reporting date, and require the financial statements' amounts to be changed — e.g. the subsequent determination of the sale proceeds of an asset sold before year end."},{"p":"**Non-adjusting events** arise after the reporting date and concern conditions that did NOT exist at that time — they do not change reported amounts, but may need note disclosure if material enough to affect whether the statements mislead — e.g. a merger or acquisition occurring after year end."},{"p":"For a disclosable event, the notes should state: the nature of the event, and an estimate of its financial effect (or a statement that such an estimate is not practicable to make)."}],"src":"Chapter 7.6","sec":"7.6"},{"q":"Explain the accounting treatment of a material contingent loss and a material contingent gain, and state why they are NOT treated symmetrically.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"A material contingent **loss** should be **accrued** if it is probable that a future event will confirm the loss and it can be estimated with reasonable accuracy. If not accrued, it should be **disclosed** unless the possibility of loss is remote."},{"p":"A material contingent **gain** should **never be accrued** while it remains a contingency — it should only be **disclosed** if realisation is probable. Once realisation becomes reasonably certain, the item is no longer treated as a contingency at all, and accrual becomes appropriate."},{"p":"The asymmetry reflects accounting prudence: recognising a possible loss early protects users from overstated profit/assets, while recognising a possible gain early would risk overstating profit/assets based on an outcome that has not yet been confirmed."}],"src":"Chapter 7.7","sec":"7.7"}]}},{"n":8,"t":"Internal Control System","brief":"What internal control is and why auditors rely on it: its definition, objectives and nine types, the five COSO-style components, its inherent limitations, how auditors ascertain/record/evaluate control systems (narrative notes, organisation charts, ICQs, flowcharts, ICEQs), the practical control considerations across every major transaction cycle, how small entities differ, reporting weaknesses to management, and internal audit's own role as a control factor.","outcomes":["Define internal control system","Describe the objectives of internal control systems and the need for effective internal control systems within the context of organizational objectives","Describe the different types of control systems that need to be in place to ensure the organization's success","Outline internal control considerations relevant to various aspects of business operations","Describe the problems associated with internal control systems of small companies","Describe the role of internal auditing in ensuring an effective internal control system","Explain the relationship between internal and external auditing"],"secs":[{"n":"8.1","t":"Definition, objectives, features and importance","b":[{"key":"Systems-based auditing only works if the auditor can **rely on internal controls** to reduce the volume of substantive testing — so examining the client's internal control practices, and recommending improvements where weaknesses appear, is central to the whole audit approach."},{"def":{"t":"Internal control (the operational standard/guideline)","d":"the whole system of controls, financial and otherwise, established by management to carry on the business in an orderly and efficient manner, ensure adherence to management policies, safeguard the assets, and secure as far as possible the completeness and accuracy of the records."}},{"key":"The definition's striking feature is its **all-embracing nature** — internal control concerns every area of corporate activity, and how individual controls **inter-relate**, not just isolated procedures. Individual components are called \"controls\" or \"internal controls.\""},{"p":"The four objectives of internal control (drawn directly from the definition):"},{"ol":["to carry on the business in an orderly and efficient manner;","to ensure adherence to management policies;","to safeguard the organisation's assets; and","to secure completeness and accuracy of the records."]},{"p":"The detailed nature of controls in any organisation depends on: the nature/size of the business; the number of administrative staff; the volume and materiality of transactions; the importance management itself places on internal control; management style (particularly trust in key personnel's integrity/honesty and their own supervisory ability); the enterprise's geographical distribution; and many other factors."},{"p":"Importance of internal controls to auditors: they prevent **and** detect errors, fraud or material misstatement; they ensure complete/adequate recording of transactions; they ensure recorded transactions are real, properly valued, in the correct period, properly classified, correctly authorised and posted; they support reliable financial reporting and compliance with laws/regulations/standards; and they give management reasonable assurance that goals important to the company (and equally important to the auditor) will be met."}]},{"n":"8.2","t":"Types of internal control","b":[{"p":"Internal controls fall into nine categories:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","What it covers"],"rows":[["**Organizational**","A clear plan of organisation that defines/allocates responsibilities and identifies reporting lines — delegation of authority/responsibility clearly specified, so every employee knows their precise powers, authority extent, and who they report to."],["**Segregation of duties**","No single person should record and process a complete transaction alone — involving several people reduces intentional manipulation/accidental error and adds a checking element. Functions to separate: initiation, authorization, execution, custody and recording."],["**Physical**","Physical custody of assets/documents, limiting access to authorised personnel only — access may be direct (entering a warehouse) or indirect (via documentation know-how). Especially important for valuable, portable, exchangeable or desirable assets (e.g. locking share certificates in a safe, restricting sensitive-area access with passes, restricting computer file access with passwords)."],["**Authorization and approval**","All transactions require authorisation/approval by an appropriate person, with specified limits — e.g. all credit sales approved by the credit control department; individual stationery purchases capped at a set amount."],["**Arithmetical and accounting**","Controls within the recording function checking transactions are properly coded, all included, correctly recorded and accurately processed — e.g. checking arithmetical accuracy, maintaining/checking totals, reconciliations, control accounts, trial balances, accounting for documents."],["**Personnel**","Ensuring the people operating a system are competent and motivated — since proper functioning depends on operator competence/integrity. Measures: selecting people with appropriate characteristics and training; matching staff level to task; appropriate remuneration/promotion; career development prospects."],["**Supervision**","All actions by all staff levels should be supervised, with supervisory responsibility clearly laid down and communicated to the person being supervised."],["**Management**","Controls exercised by management outside/above day-to-day system routine — overall supervisory control, review of management accounts, budget comparison, internal audit, and other special review procedures."],["**Acknowledgement of performance**","Persons performing data processing or other sensitive activities should acknowledge their work via signature/initials/rubber stamp (e.g. a checker initialling each invoice checked) — mitigating inherent control limitations, fraud (internal or external), and clever circumvention of computer controls."]]}}]},{"n":"8.3","t":"Scope and components of the internal control system","b":[{"key":"Internal control's scope extends **beyond** accounting-procedure control, encompassing five components: the control environment, risk assessment, control procedures, information and communication, and monitoring."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Component","What it is"],"rows":[["**The control environment**","The **foundation** of all other components — the overall attitude, awareness and actions of directors/management regarding internal control and its importance to the entity. A strong control environment complements specific control procedures but, by itself, does NOT ensure the whole system's effectiveness."],["**Risk assessment**","Management assessing business risk (factors/events/conditions that could prevent achieving business objectives) as part of designing and operating internal control. Effective risk assessment/response by management lowers control risk, meaning the auditor needs to accumulate less evidence. Conditions that raise business risk: new personnel, new technology, corporate restructuring, foreign operations."],["**Control procedures** (control activities)","Policies, procedures and specific actions by management/employees that help ensure management's directives are carried out — establishing a control policy and defining procedures to effect it. All control procedures should be analysed for risk."],["**Information and communication**","A prerequisite for achieving management objectives — every organisation should capture pertinent internal/external, financial/non-financial information, and communicate it (report production/distribution). For an audit, the auditor understands the information system and related business processes relevant to financial reporting."],["**Monitoring**","Assessing control design and operation on a timely basis, taking corrective action as needed. Examples: operating manager's reports and published financial statements, customer-complaint analysis, periodic comparison of recorded amounts to actual assets, and reviewing internal auditors' reports on control performance."]]}}]},{"n":"8.4","t":"Internal controls and their inherent limitations","b":[{"p":"Directors set up internal controls to ensure: transactions are executed per proper authorisation; all transactions are promptly recorded at correct values in the appropriate accounts per the relevant regulatory framework; asset access is permitted only per authorised procedures; and recorded assets are compared with physically existing assets at reasonable intervals, with differences reconciled."},{"warn":"Even so, any internal control system provides only **reasonable, not absolute**, assurance, because of inherent limitations:"},{"ol":["the requirement that a control's cost must not outweigh the potential loss from its absence;","most systematic controls target **routine** transactions rather than non-routine ones;","the potential for **human error** — carelessness, distraction, mistakes of judgment, misunderstanding instructions;","the possibility of a control being **abused/overridden** by the very person responsible for exercising it;","the possibility of controls being **bypassed through collusion** of two or more people (inside the organisation, or involving outsiders); and","the possibility that procedures become inadequate as conditions change, or that compliance deteriorates over time — particularly likely during business expansion."]},{"p":"Factors determining a system's success: the nature/size/volume of transactions; the enterprise's geographical distribution; controls exercised personally by individual management members; the cost of setting up controls against the benefit obtainable; and management's own attitude to controls."}]},{"n":"8.5","t":"Understanding the internal control system","b":[{"key":"In planning the audit, the auditor obtains and **documents** an understanding of the accounting system and control environment sufficient to determine the audit approach."},{"p":"Understanding the accounting system lets the auditor identify: major classes of transactions; how they are initiated; significant accounting records, supporting documents and financial statement accounts; and the accounting/financial reporting process from a significant transaction's initiation through to its inclusion in the financial statements."},{"p":"Factors affecting the nature/timing/extent of these understanding-building procedures: materiality; the entity's size/complexity; the assessed inherent risk; the complexity of computer systems; the type of internal controls involved; the nature of transaction documentation; and reporting requirements."},{"p":"Previous knowledge is updated via: enquiries of appropriate personnel at various organisational levels, referencing documentation like procedure manuals/job descriptions/systems descriptions; inspection of relevant documents/records; and observation of activities/operations, including IT functions, organisation, control-performing personnel, and how transactions are processed."}]},{"n":"8.6","t":"Ascertaining the system of controls","b":[{"p":"Six methods, generally used in combination:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Method","How it works"],"rows":[["**Examining previous audit work**","Beyond the first audit, prior audit files record the system as it operated last — requiring updating for any changes."],["**Examining the client's documentation**","Where procedure manuals exist, they source the described system — but the auditor must check the manual matches what is **actually practised**."],["**Interviews with client's staff**","Sitting with staff to find out how they carry out functions — revealing existing controls and indicating potential problems."],["**Tracing transactions**","Walk-through tests identifying where actual procedures diverge from intended ones — also builds overall process understanding and risk identification."],["**Reviewing documents and records**","E.g. management meeting minutes may reveal control issues needing further investigation; steering-group documentation for significant system changes is a useful source."],["**Observation of procedures**","Watching staff perform procedures (wages pay-out, goods receipt into stores) to ascertain the controls actually practised."]]}}]},{"n":"8.7","t":"Recording the system of controls","b":[{"p":"Four methods of recording: narrative notes, organisation charts, Internal Control Questionnaires (ICQ), and flowcharts/other diagrammatic presentation."},{"h4":"Narrative notes"},{"p":"Describes the system in words — appropriate for simple systems handled by only one or a few people. Should describe: functions performed and who performs them; documents used; document source/destination; document filing sequence; and types of accounting books kept."},{"key":"Advantage: simple to record and easy to understand. Disadvantages: difficult to note changes in the system; a complex system's notes take up disproportionate space; and it can be hard to tell if part of the system has been missed altogether."},{"h4":"Organization chart"},{"p":"Conveniently describes authority, responsibility, approval and reporting relationships — the organisation's formal relationships. It does **not**, however, specify individuals' precise duties."},{"h4":"Internal Control Questionnaire (ICQ)"},{"key":"ICQ remains the **longest-used** internal control assessment/recording technique — highlighting precisely where the system's strengths and weaknesses lie. It is a standardised, pre-printed document designed by the audit firm, comprising questions determining whether desirable controls are present, one set per major transaction cycle."},{"p":"Points worth noting about ICQ use:"},{"ol":["normally used if the client's size/complexity justifies it;","a complete ICQ has an effective life of roughly **3 years** (updating only needed meanwhile) — a new ICQ is needed after a major system change (e.g. manual to computerised);","should be completed by a **senior** audit staff member, after questioning the client's responsible officers;","observation and selected tests should confirm the ICQ accurately reflects actual day-to-day strengths/weaknesses;","the auditor should **not** rely on controls purely from this preliminary evaluation — further compliance tests are needed for reasonable assurance the controls actually function; and","questions should be formulated so a simple Yes/No answer indicates compliance/non-compliance — though this isn't always possible (e.g. needing named authorised cheque signatories, or a specific officer's expenditure authority limit)."]},{"p":"A specimen ICQ typically covers areas like purchases and trade creditors (e.g. are official orders issued showing supplier/quantity/price? Are orders authorised per authority limits? Are goods inspected on arrival?), cheque payments (is signing blank cheques prohibited? Is signing restricted to authorised officials? Are paid cheques returned by the bank monthly?), and cash payments (are all non-payroll payments made from an imprest? Are vouchers examined/authorised independently of the cashier? Is petty cash balance-checked at random by an independent official?) — each question answered Yes/No/N/A with a date."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Advantages of ICQ","Disadvantages of ICQ"],"rows":[["If drafted thoroughly, ensures all controls are considered; quick to prepare; easy to use and control (a reviewing manager/partner can easily see what has been done).","The client may overstate controls; may contain many irrelevant controls; can give the impression all controls carry equal weight; and may miss unusual controls that are nevertheless effective in that specific circumstance."]]}},{"h4":"Flowcharts"},{"p":"Flowcharts, supplemented by narrative notes, are kept in the permanent audit file, updated via periodic review — either adapted from the client's own, or drawn from scratch (the exercise of drawing itself teaches the auditor a great deal about how the system actually works). The auditor should not commit a flowchart to file until (via questioning/observation) reasonably satisfied it represents what actually happens — since subsequent audit tests largely follow the chart's pattern, an incomplete/inaccurate chart wastes significant time."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Document flowcharts","Information flowcharts"],"rows":[["More commonly used, relatively easy to prepare — follow every document from beginning to end, showing all operations/controls.","Prepared in reverse — starting from the entry into accounting records and working back to the transaction. Concentrate on significant information flows, ignoring unimportant items/document copies. Easy to understand, but require skill/experience to compile."]]}},{"p":"Rules of flowcharting: use one only when the system cannot readily be understood in words; keep it simple (conforming symbols each meaning one thing, top-to-bottom/left-to-right direction, no loose ends, main flow finishing bottom-right, minimal crossing lines); always provide a key unless using BSI symbols; always use a template (freehand is rarely acceptable); reflect the system as it actually is, weaknesses included; and include marginal comments via dotted flow lines/brackets where appropriate."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Advantages of flowcharts","Disadvantages of flowcharts"],"rows":[["Present the whole system (or part) without losing detail; simply depict cross-area relationships; highlight control features (or their absence) via designated symbols; easily incorporate references to other audit documents; facilitate later reference more readily than pure narrative; let new team members participate quickly after brief induction, saving time; are fairly easy to follow given their standard form; ensure the whole system is recorded (loose ends are apparent at a glance); and eliminate the need for extensive narrative.","Only suit standard systems — unusual transactions still need narrative notes; only useful while records/assets remain in flux (once static, e.g. non-current assets, they stop being useful); major amendments require redrawing; and time can be wasted charting audit-insignificant areas."]]}}]},{"n":"8.8","t":"Evaluating the internal control system","b":[{"key":"After ascertaining/recording the system, the auditor conducts a preliminary evaluation to decide whether to rely on it (reducing substantive testing) or disregard it as inefficient (requiring in-depth testing) — generally using the **Internal Control Evaluation Questionnaire (ICEQ)**."},{"def":{"t":"ICEQ","d":"a questionnaire determining whether appropriate control systems exist to prevent specific errors/omissions — answered using knowledge from the flowchart or ICQ, concentrating on the criteria existing controls **should** meet."}},{"p":"Illustrative ICEQ questions, by cycle: sales — are sales properly authorised/made to reliable payers, are all dispatched goods invoiced/recorded, will slow payers be chased and doubtful debts provided for, will the system neither over- nor understate debtors at period end; purchases — could goods/services be received without a liability being recorded, is a liability recorded only for authorised items at the proper amount, are all payments properly authorised; wages/salaries — are employees paid only for work done, at the correct gross/net amount, to the right person; inventories — is inventory safeguarded from physical loss, are records accurate/up to date, does recorded inventory exist and belong to the company, is cut-off reliable; non-current assets — do recorded assets exist and belong to the company, is capital expenditure/disposal authorised and reported, is depreciation realistic; investments — do recorded investments belong to the company and are they safeguarded, is income/rights/bonus issues properly received and accounted for; and management information/general controls — is the nominal ledger satisfactorily controlled, are journal entries adequately controlled, is there a clear authority structure, are systems operated by competent/supported staff, is internal audit (if any) adequate."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Advantages of ICEQ","Disadvantage of ICEQ"],"rows":[["Highlights areas of weakness requiring extensive substantive testing; answering ICEQs helps auditors identify important controls most likely to need control testing.","Can be drafted vaguely, leading to misunderstanding and important controls not being identified."]]}}]},{"n":"8.9","t":"Practical application of internal control","b":[{"p":"A summary of internal control considerations relevant to the key control risk areas: general financial arrangements; cash and cheques (received and paid); wages and salaries; purchases and trade creditors; sales and trade debtors; inventories and WIP; and non-current assets and investments."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Area","Key control considerations"],"rows":[["**General financial arrangements**","A properly integrated system of accounts/records; appropriate general financial/budgetary supervision, regular interim accounts and special reports; safeguarding (and, if necessary, duplicating and separately storing) important records; and engaging, training and allocating competent management/staff to specific duties, with cover arranged for absences."],["**Cash/cheques received by post**","Safeguards against mail interception before opening; a responsible person independent of the cashier appointed to open or supervise mail opening; cash/cheques adequately protected (e.g. restrictive crossing) and accounted for (e.g. post-lists compared independently against later records)."],["**Custody/control of money received**","Suitable persons appointed with clearly defined responsibilities at each collection/handling stage; defined arrangements for clearing cash offices/registers, agreeing collections with cash/sales records (ideally by someone independent of the receiving cashier), and investigating any cash shortage/surplus."],["**Recording of cash received**","A person responsible for records; practical limits on the receiving cashier's duties (e.g. no involvement in other books, funds, credit notes, cash payments); a nominated deputy for absences; and rules on when receipts are issued, serial-numbered and reconciled against records/paying-in slips."],["**Payment into banks**","How frequently banking occurs (preferably daily); who prepares paying-in slips (independent of the receiving/recording cashier); who makes the actual bank payment (not the slip-preparer); and whether all receipts are banked intact."],["**Cash balances**","Cash-float amounts and whether payments out of received cash are permitted; access restrictions on cash registers/offices; imprest-system rules and reimbursement methods; frequency of independent float checks; overnight safeguarding arrangements; fidelity guarantee/cash insurance suitability; and control of any funds held in trust for employees (e.g. sickness/holiday funds)."],["**Cheque payments**","Control over the supply/issue/safekeeping of blank cheques; who prepares cheques and on what authorising documents, with safeguards against double payment on the same document; authorised signatories, their limits, and minimum signatories per cheque; safeguards for mechanically signed/printed signatures; the extent of restrictive crossing and any exceptions; arrangements for obtaining and reviewing paid/returned cheques; and ensuring payment within discount periods."],["**Cash payments**","A responsible person authorising expenditure, with evidenced documentation; safeguards against double presentation of supporting vouchers; any limit on individual disbursement amounts; and rules on cash advances, IOUs and cheque cashing for staff."],["**Wages and salaries**","Who authorises engagement/discharge of employees and pay-rate changes; how personnel/pay-rate changes are recorded/controlled; how non-statutory deductions are authorised; arrangements for recording hours worked/output and their scrutiny before reaching the wages department; rules on pay advances and their recovery; handling of holiday pay; and who deals with pay queries."],["**Preparation of payroll**","The records used as a payroll basis and their authorisation; who prepares/checks/approves pay sheets and how individual responsibility is indicated at each stage; and procedures for non-routine circumstances (e.g. an employee's absence, or leaving mid-period)."],["**Payment of wages/salaries**","For cash payment: arrangements to obtain/safeguard the cash, safeguards against irregularities, who pays employees (independent of pay-sheet preparers), identity verification and distribution recording, and handling of unclaimed wages. For cheque/bank transfer: who prepares/signs cheques or transfer lists (mutually independent, and independent of pay-sheet preparers), and whether a separate wages bank account is maintained and regularly reconciled."],["**Purchases**","The procedure for inventory requisitions and who is responsible; preparation/authorisation of purchase orders (including tender-acceptance authorisation); special authorisation for capital items; goods-inward examination procedures (quantity/quality/condition) and their evidencing; a responsible goods-acceptance officer; and checking goods-inward records against authorised purchase orders."],["**Accounting for purchases**","Separate responsibility for checking supplier invoices, recording purchases/returns, maintaining supplier ledger accounts, checking supplier statements, and authorising payments; and ensuring, before payment, that goods were received matching the order and correctly priced/invoiced, that expenditure was properly allocated, and that payment was duly authorised."],["**Sales**","Ensuring goods are sold at current prices, with controlled discounts/special reductions; responsibility for and control over credit terms granted; who accepts customer orders and the procedure for production orders/dispatch notes; who prepares invoices/credit notes and how errors are prevented; special controls over free/special-terms dispatches; who authorises dispatch and how; independent recording of goods outward; and agreeing goods outward with orders/dispatch notes/invoices."],["**Accounting for sales**","Separate responsibility for recording sales returns, maintaining customer accounts, and preparing debtor statements; control over returns/price adjustments; cut-off procedures for goods dispatched-but-not-invoiced (and vice versa); arrangements for group/related-party sales; procedures for preparing/checking/dispatching debtor statements without interference; authorisation of discounts; who handles statement queries; procedures for reviewing overdue accounts; authorisation of bad debt write-offs; and an independently checked sales control account."],["**Inventories and WIP**","Goods-inward receiving/checking/recording arrangements; responsibility for safeguarding against theft/misuse/deterioration; control (via set inventory levels) and recording methods (ledgers, control accounts, bin cards); independent record-keeping and periodic reconciliation with the financial accounts; authorised/evidenced/recorded stock movement; handling of returnable containers; controlling inventory held by/for third parties; who physically checks inventory, how often (at least annually), and by whom (independent of storekeepers), with proper cut-off; the valuation basis adopted; periodic review and authorised write-off of damaged/slow-moving/obsolete stock; and controlling scrap/waste and disposal proceeds."],["**Non-current assets**","Who authorises capital expenditure and how evidenced; who authorises disposal/scrapping/transfer and how disposal proceeds are controlled; who maintains the asset register and how often it is agreed/physically verified; arrangements ensuring proper maintenance/use (periodic physical checks); depreciation accounting on inter-branch/company transfers; and how depreciation rates are authorised, evidenced and calculated/checked."],["**Investments**","Who authorises purchases/sales (ideally independent of cash/title-document custody) and how evidenced; maintenance of a detailed investment register, periodically agreed to the control account with physical title-document verification; and checking contract notes against authorised instructions, ensuring transfer charges are correctly calculated and rights/dividends/interest/capital repayments are received and accounted for."]]}}]},{"n":"8.10","t":"Control systems as they apply to small entities","b":[{"p":"Small entities implement control environment elements **differently**, not **worse** — the underlying concepts still apply, just less formally:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Component","How it differs in small entities"],"rows":[["**Control environment**","May lack a written code of conduct, instead cultivating integrity/ethics through oral communication and management example; governance may lack an independent/outside member."],["**Risk assessment process**","Still relevant regardless of size, but likely less formal/structured — financial reporting objectives may be implicit rather than explicit, with management aware of risk through direct personal involvement rather than a formal process."],["**Information systems**","Likely less formal, with active management involvement reducing the need for extensive procedure descriptions or sophisticated records — communication is often easier given fewer organisational levels and management's greater visibility/availability."],["**Control activities**","Similar underlying concepts, but less formal operation. Some control types become less relevant because management retains direct authority itself (e.g. personally approving credit sales, significant purchases, credit-line draw-downs). Segregation of duties often genuinely struggles with few employees — compensated either by careful responsibility assignment or management oversight of otherwise-incompatible activities."],["**Monitoring**","More likely informal, typically part of management's general operational oversight — close involvement often identifies significant variances/inaccuracies directly, prompting corrective action without a formal monitoring process."]]}}]},{"n":"8.11","t":"Reporting on internal control to management","b":[{"key":"At the conclusion of the internal control assessment, the auditor must communicate any weaknesses discovered to the client's management — typically via a **Management Letter**, **Letter of Weakness**, or **Internal Control Letter** (other report forms are also acceptable). Exactly when to send it, how far its content should extend, and what to do if major weaknesses are ignored, are all matters of professional judgment."},{"p":"Where the audit spans multiple visits, auditors normally report after the interim audit **and** after the final visit, especially if procedures need improving before the year end."},{"p":"Principal purposes: to comment on the design/operation of examined systems and suggest improvements; to provide constructive advice on potential economies/efficiency improvements; and to communicate other matters coming to attention (e.g. adjusted/unadjusted errors, or particular accounting policies/practices)."},{"h4":"Material weaknesses"},{"def":{"t":"Material weakness (in this context)","d":"a condition that may result in a material misstatement in the financial statements."}},{"key":"Material weaknesses must be reported **in writing**, on a timely basis, to directors/audit committee/appropriate management. If management has already detected and (in the auditor's view) appropriately corrected a weakness, no report is needed on that specific matter — but the auditor should still document the reasoning behind that conclusion in the working papers."},{"p":"Ordinarily a written report is used, though sometimes raising matters orally, followed by a file note recording the observations/responses, suffices. Where **no** material weaknesses are found, the auditor may choose not to issue a report at all — but may still need to inform management that none will be issued."},{"h4":"Addressee and content"},{"p":"The auditor considers who has power to act on the findings — ordinarily the board, audit committee or similar body. A **tiered** report may be appropriate: major points to the board, less important matters delegated to other management levels. A reply is ordinarily requested, indicating intended actions."},{"key":"The report must explain it is **not a comprehensive statement** of every weakness or possible improvement — only the matters that came to attention through the audit procedures actually performed. Referencing the audit approach helps management appreciate the nature/timing/extent of the work behind the report."},{"p":"Other (non-material) weaknesses may be included in the same report, a separate report, or raised orally with a supporting file note. To be effective, a report is issued immediately after the procedures giving rise to the observations are complete. An interim report must make clear the overall audit is only partially complete. Matters raised in **previous** reports must be followed up — where not effectively addressed, the auditor enquires why, and, if still significant, considers repeating the point (otherwise risking the impression the weakness was accepted as corrected)."},{"h4":"Third parties"},{"warn":"A report to management is a **confidential communication** — prior consent is needed before sharing it with a third party. Since auditors have little control over what happens to a report once dispatched (management may pass copies to banks or regulators), the report ordinarily states: it is prepared for the entity's **sole use**; must **not** be disclosed to a third party without the auditor's written consent; and **no responsibility** is assumed to any other person. Negligence liability to a third party ordinarily only arises where the auditor knowingly does work meant to be relied upon by that specific third party, who may then suffer loss as a result — an express liability disclaimer normally gives strong protection against unforeseen third-party liability."},{"h4":"Specimen management letter — structure"},{"p":"A typical management letter (illustrative) is organised as one entry per identified weakness, each following a consistent structure:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Element","Content"],"rows":[["**Present system**","What was actually observed during the audit — e.g. some departments ordering goods orally without a purchase requisition/order; a monthly purchase-ledger reconciliation that was skipped for two months; total debtors equating to roughly three months' sales against a stated four-week credit term."],["**Implication**","The consequence — e.g. unenforceable action against non-performing suppliers absent a written order; unexplained differences between supplier statements and ledger balances, implying a breakdown in invoice/payment batching and posting; or a high overdraft level with resulting interest cost and difficulty paying key suppliers on time."],["**Recommendation**","The suggested fix — e.g. centralising procurement responsibility with subsequent written confirmation of oral orders; performing the reconciliation regularly, by someone independent of the purchase ledger/cashier/nominal ledger functions; or adopting a more structured debt-collection system with standard letters and earlier statement dispatch."]]}}]},{"n":"8.12","t":"Internal audit as a control factor","b":[{"key":"The external auditor should consider internal auditing's activities and their effect (if any) on external audit procedures."},{"def":{"t":"Internal auditing","d":"an appraisal activity established within an entity, as a service to the entity, whose functions include, among other things, monitoring the internal control system."}},{"warn":"The **external auditor retains sole responsibility** for the audit opinion and for determining the nature/timing/extent of external audit procedures — but parts of internal audit's work may still be useful to the external auditor."},{"h4":"Scope and objectives of internal auditing"},{"p":"Internal auditing activities ordinarily include: **monitoring of internal control** (management is responsible for establishing adequate control, but often delegates specific review/monitoring/improvement-recommendation responsibility to internal audit); **examination of financial and operating information** (reviewing the means used to identify/measure/classify/report information, including detailed testing); and **review of economy, efficiency and effectiveness of operations**, including non-financial controls and compliance with laws/regulations/policies."},{"h4":"Relationship between internal auditing and the external auditor"},{"key":"Internal audit's objectives are set by **management** and differ from the external auditor's independent reporting role — though the means of achieving their respective objectives are often similar. Being **part of the entity**, internal audit can never achieve the same degree of independence the external auditor needs — and using internal audit's work **never reduces** the external auditor's own sole responsibility for the opinion. Every audit judgment remains the external auditor's own."},{"h4":"Understanding and preliminary assessment of internal auditing"},{"p":"The external auditor obtains sufficient understanding of internal audit activities to identify/assess risks of material misstatement and design further procedures. Effective internal auditing may modify the nature/timing and **reduce** the extent of external procedures — but can never **eliminate** them entirely; in some cases the external auditor may conclude internal audit has no effect at all on external procedures."},{"p":"Assessment criteria:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Criterion","What it examines"],"rows":[["**Organizational status**","Internal audit's specific status and its effect on objectivity — ideally reporting to the highest management level, free of other operating responsibility, with any management-imposed constraints carefully considered. Internal auditors must be free to communicate fully with the external auditor."],["**Scope of function**","The nature/extent of internal audit assignments, and whether management acts on its recommendations (and how that is evidenced)."],["**Technical competence**","Whether performed by adequately trained/proficient persons — e.g. reviewing hiring/training policies, experience and professional qualifications."],["**Due professional care**","Whether internal auditing is properly planned, supervised, reviewed and documented — e.g. the existence of adequate manuals, work programmes and working papers."]]}},{"h4":"Timing, liaison and coordination"},{"p":"When planning to use internal audit's work, the external auditor discusses internal audit's tentative plan as early as possible — ideally agreeing in advance the timing/extent of coverage, materiality levels, sample-selection methods, documentation, and review/reporting procedures. Effective liaison uses meetings at appropriate intervals, mutual access to relevant reports, and mutual notice of significant matters affecting the other."},{"h4":"Evaluating specific internal auditing work"},{"p":"Where the external auditor intends to use specific internal audit work, they evaluate and perform procedures on it to confirm its adequacy — considering: whether it was performed by adequately trained/proficient persons, with assistants' work properly supervised/reviewed/documented; whether sufficient appropriate evidence was obtained for reasonable conclusions; whether conclusions/reports are appropriate and consistent with the work performed; and whether any exceptions/unusual matters were properly resolved."},{"key":"The nature/timing/extent of procedures applied to internal audit's own work depends on the auditor's judgment about the area's risk, the internal audit assessment, and the specific work's own evaluation — possibly including re-examining items internal audit already examined, examining similar other items, and observing internal audit procedures. Conclusions on the evaluated work are recorded."}]},{"n":"8.13","t":"Chapter summary","b":[{"ol":["It is management's responsibility to ensure the existence and operation of an efficient internal control system — the auditor's role is to examine the system and determine how far it can be relied upon.","There are several ways of ascertaining and recording internal control systems.","The control environment means the overall attitude, awareness and actions of management regarding internal controls.","Control procedures are those established (in addition to the control environment) to achieve the entity's specific objectives.","Internal controls are meant to prevent risks from occurring, or minimise their impact.","Generally, the larger the entity, the more comprehensive the controls need to be.","Auditors rely on internal control to reduce the amount of substantive testing required.","Internal auditing is instituted as a control factor, and the internal audit department should be able to give management (the board) assurance about the internal control system's effectiveness."]}]},{"n":"8.14","t":"Study text questions — multiple-choice and short-answer","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with 10 questions — 5 multiple-choice and 5 short-answer — reproduced below in full with the official answers, each explained. The Auditing study text sets **no examination-type questions**; this bank is the whole of its end-of-chapter material."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"The questions, with the official answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Auditors obtain an understanding of the accounting system sufficient to enable them to identify and understand by documenting the following **EXCEPT** (A) Major classes of transactions in the entity’s operation; (B) How such transactions are initiated; (C) Significant accounting records, supporting documents and accounts in the financial statements; (D) The accounting and financial reporting process from the initiation of significant transactions and other events to their inclusion in the financial statements; (E) Their knowledge of any significant change in the composition of the board.","The nature, timing and extent of the procedures performed by auditors to obtain an understanding of the systems vary because of the following factors **EXCEPT** (A) Materiality considerations; (B) The size and complexity of the entity; (C) The type of products and/or services rendered by the entity; (D) Their assessment of inherent risk; (E) The type of internal controls involved.","Specific criteria for assessing the internal audit function **EXCLUDE** (A) Resources available; (B) Due professional care; (C) Scope of function; (D) Technical competence; (E) Number of staff in the internal audit section.","Which of the following is **NOT** a component of an internal control system? (A) Control environment; (B) Risk assessment; (C) Control activities; (D) Monitoring; (E) Management perspective.","Which of the following does **NOT** represent an inherent limitation of an internal control system? (A) Fraud; (B) Abuse of responsibility; (C) Fatigue; (D) Accountability; (E) Poor working conditions.","The overall attitude, awareness and actions of directors and management regarding internal controls and their importance in the entity is referred to as ………","Those policies and procedures, in addition to the control environment, which are established by management to achieve the entity’s specific objectives are known as ………","The type of audit test performed to obtain audit evidence about the effective operation of the accounting and internal control systems within an entity is called ………","State any **TWO** features of internal control.","List any **TWO** factors which management will consider in order to determine the extent to which internal controls are to be applied within an organisation."]}],"a":[{"ol":["**E** — a change in the composition of the board is relevant to the **control environment** and to risk assessment generally, but it is not part of documenting the **accounting system** itself.","**C** — the type of product or service. What the entity sells does not of itself change how much work is needed to understand its **systems**; size, complexity, materiality, inherent risk and the nature of the controls all do.","**E** — the raw headcount. The four established criteria are **organisational status** (and the resources that go with it), **scope of function**, **technical competence** and **due professional care**. A large department may still be incompetent or lack independence.","**E** — \"management perspective\" is not one of them. The five components are the **control environment**, the entity’s **risk assessment** process, **control activities**, the **information and communication** system, and **monitoring** of controls.","**D** — accountability is a **feature** that makes internal control work, not a limitation of it. The inherent limitations are human error and fatigue, collusion and fraud, management override, abuse of responsibility, the cost-benefit constraint, and the fact that controls are designed for routine rather than unusual transactions.","**The control environment.**","**Control procedures** (control activities).","**Tests of control** (compliance tests).","**Any two of:** segregation of duties; custody and control of assets; authorisation and approval; review of management accounts and comparison with budget; supervision of day-to-day transactions by a responsible official; allocation of responsibilities; checking of the arithmetical accuracy of the records.","**Any two of:** the nature, size and volume of transactions; the geographical distribution of the enterprise; the controls exercised personally by individual members of management; the cost of setting up the controls against the benefits obtained from them."]}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"This chapter is heavy on lists, but the exam mostly tests structure, not memorised detail: know the 5 components (control environment, risk assessment, control procedures, information/communication, monitoring) as a distinct, examinable set from the 9 TYPES of control (organizational, segregation of duties, physical, authorization, arithmetical, personnel, supervision, management, acknowledgement of performance) — these are two DIFFERENT classifications, easily confused. Know the 6 inherent limitations cold, and the ICQ-vs-ICEQ distinction (ICQ asks whether controls EXIST; ICEQ asks whether controls MEET the criteria they should). The \"reasonable, not absolute, assurance\" framing recurs throughout this subject — this chapter is where it is grounded in concrete mechanics.","errors":["Confusing the 5 COMPONENTS of internal control (control environment, risk assessment, control procedures, information/communication, monitoring) with the 9 TYPES of individual controls (organizational, segregation of duties, physical, etc.) — these are two separate classification schemes at different levels of the framework, not the same list.","Treating ICQ and ICEQ as interchangeable — an ICQ establishes whether desirable controls EXIST; an ICEQ evaluates whether existing controls actually MEET the criteria they should, to support a reliance decision.","Believing internal audit's work can reduce the external auditor's own responsibility for the opinion — using internal audit's work can modify or reduce (never eliminate) external procedures, but responsibility for the opinion always remains entirely with the external auditor.","Assuming attendance at inventory counting (Chapter 7) or a strong internal control system transfers responsibility away from management — internal control, like inventory count attendance, never reduces management's own underlying responsibility for the figures.","Treating small-entity control weaknesses (e.g. limited segregation of duties) as automatically deficient — the chapter frames this as a difference in FORMALITY, not necessarily effectiveness, since management's direct, close involvement can itself substitute for formal segregation in a small entity."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Auditors obtain an understanding of the accounting system sufficient to enable them to identify and understand, by documenting the following EXCEPT","o":["Major class of transactions in the entity's operation","How such transactions are initiated","Significant accounting records, supporting documents and accounts in the financial statements","The accounting and financial reporting process from the initiation of significant transactions and other events to their inclusion in the financial statement","Their knowledge of any significant change in the composition of the board"],"a":4,"w":"Board composition changes relate to general entity/governance understanding, not specifically to understanding the ACCOUNTING system itself, which the other four options all directly describe.","src":"Chapter 8.5","sec":"8.5"},{"q":"The nature, timing and extent of the procedures performed by auditors to obtain an understanding of the systems vary because of the following factors EXCEPT","o":["Materiality consideration","The size and complexity of the entity","The type of products and/or services rendered by the entity","Their assessment of inherent risk","The type of internal controls involved"],"a":2,"w":"The chapter's own list of factors is materiality, entity size/complexity, inherent risk assessment, computer system complexity, internal control type, documentation nature and reporting requirements — the specific type of product/service is not among them.","src":"Chapter 8.5","sec":"8.5"},{"q":"Specific criteria for assessing the internal audit function EXCLUDES","o":["Resources available","Due professional care","Scope of function","Technical competence","Number of staff in the internal audit section"],"a":4,"w":"The four recognised assessment criteria are organizational status, scope of function, technical competence, and due professional care — a raw headcount is not itself a recognised criterion; what matters is competence and adequacy, not staff numbers.","src":"Chapter 8.12","sec":"8.12"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a component of an internal control system?","o":["Control environment","Risk assessment","Control activities","Monitoring","Management perspective"],"a":4,"w":"The five components are control environment, risk assessment, control procedures (activities), information and communication, and monitoring — \"management perspective\" is not one of the recognised five.","src":"Chapter 8.3","sec":"8.3"},{"q":"Which of the following does NOT represent an inherent limitation of the internal control system?","o":["Fraud","Abuse of responsibility","Fatigue","Accountability","Poor working conditions"],"a":3,"w":"The six recognised inherent limitations relate to cost-benefit constraints, a focus on routine transactions, human error, override/abuse of responsibility, collusion, and changing conditions — \"accountability\" describes a governance concept, not a limitation of the control system itself.","src":"Chapter 8.4","sec":"8.4"}],"theory":[{"q":"Distinguish between an Internal Control Questionnaire (ICQ) and an Internal Control Evaluation Questionnaire (ICEQ).","marks":5,"a":[{"p":"An **ICQ** is a standardised, pre-printed document comprising a series of questions designed to determine whether desirable controls are PRESENT in the client's system — completed by questioning the client's responsible officers, with one section per major transaction cycle."},{"p":"An **ICEQ** is used AFTER the system has been ascertained and recorded (via flowchart or ICQ), and is designed to determine whether the existing controls MEET the criteria they should meet to prevent specific errors or omissions — directly supporting the auditor's decision on whether to rely on the system to reduce substantive testing."}],"src":"Chapter 8.7","sec":"8.7"},{"q":"State the six inherent limitations of an internal control system.","marks":6,"a":[{"ol":["The cost of a control must not outweigh the potential loss from its absence.","Most systematic controls target routine, rather than non-routine, transactions.","The potential for human error — carelessness, distraction, mistakes of judgment, or misunderstanding instructions.","The possibility that a person responsible for exercising a control abuses that responsibility by overriding it.","The possibility of controls being bypassed through collusion between two or more people, whether inside or outside the organisation.","The possibility that procedures become inadequate as conditions change, or that compliance deteriorates over time — particularly during business expansion."]}],"src":"Chapter 8.4","sec":"8.4"}]}},{"n":9,"t":"Public Sector Assurance","brief":"How auditing works in government: the appointment, powers, tenure and functions of the Auditor-General (at federal, state and local government level), the different audit types the Office of the Auditor-General performs (financial/regularity, economy and efficiency, effectiveness, value-for-money), the role of the Public Accounts Committee, internal audit's place in the public sector, and due process/public procurement law — the Public Procurement Act and the Bureau of Public Procurement.","outcomes":["Explain the appointment, powers and functions of the Auditor-General for the Federation, States and Local Government","Explain auditing for compliance with legislative and related authorities","Explain the role of the Public Accounts Committee (PAC) in the audit process","Explain internal audit in the public sector","Explain due process and the Public Procurement Act"],"secs":[{"n":"9.1","t":"Appointment, powers and functions of the Auditor-General","b":[{"h4":"§9.1.1 Appointment"},{"p":"The Auditor-General for the Federation is appointed by the **President**, on the recommendation of the Federal Civil Service Commission, subject to **Senate confirmation** — under Section 86 of the 1999 Constitution (as amended to 2011). The power to appoint a person to **act** in the office also rests with the President."},{"key":"At **State** level, the Governor appoints the State Auditor-General on the recommendation of the State Civil Service Commission, subject to confirmation by the relevant **House of Assembly** — and Local Government Auditors-General follow the same process."},{"h4":"§9.1.2 Acting capacity"},{"p":"No person may act in the office of Auditor-General for more than **six months**, except with the sanction of a Senate resolution."},{"h4":"§9.1.3 Tenure of office (Section 87)"},{"key":"The Auditor-General for the Federation can be **removed** by the President, acting on advice supported by a **two-thirds majority** of the Senate, praying for removal due to inability to discharge the functions of office — removable only for: (i) infirmity of mind, body, or other cause; or (ii) misconduct. The Auditor-General cannot otherwise be removed before the prescribed retiring age, except per this provision."},{"h4":"§9.1.4 Powers and functions"},{"p":"Per Financial Regulations No. 108 (December 2009), the Auditor-General for the Federation is the officer constitutionally responsible for auditing and reporting on public accounts of the Federation — including all persons/bodies entrusted with collecting, receiving, holding, issuing, selling, transferring or delivering any stamps, securities, stores or other government property — and for certifying the Federal Government's Annual Accounts. The Auditor-General examines the accounts to ascertain whether, in their opinion:"},{"ol":["the accounts have been properly kept;","all public monies have been fully accounted for, with rules/procedures sufficient to secure effective checks on revenue assessment, collection and proper allocation;","monies have been expended for the purposes appropriated, and expenditure was properly authorised; and","essential records are maintained, with rules/procedures sufficient to safeguard and control public property and funds."]},{"h4":"§9.1.5 Free access to books and accounts"},{"key":"The Auditor-General (and representatives) have **free access, at all reasonable times**, to all files, safes, documents, books and other records relating to the accounts of all Federal Ministries/Extra-Ministerial Departments/units, and can require/receive any information, reports and explanations deemed necessary from public service members."},{"p":"The Auditor-General may conduct **periodic checks** of all government statutory corporations, commissions, authorities and agencies (including any person/body established by an Act of the National Assembly). Reports must be submitted to each House of the National Assembly within **90 days** of receiving the Accountant-General's financial statements, and each House refers the report to its Public Accounts Committee for consideration."},{"warn":"In exercising these constitutional functions, the Auditor-General is **not subject to the direction or control of any other authority or person** — a deliberate independence guarantee, structurally analogous to the external auditor's independence in the private sector (Chapter 1)."},{"h4":"§9.1.6 Audit of public accounts"},{"key":"The Auditor-General does **not** audit (or appoint auditors for) government statutory corporations, commissions, authorities and agencies established by an Act of the National Assembly — instead, the Auditor-General: (i) provides such bodies with a **list of qualified external auditors** to choose from, and guidelines on fee levels; and (ii) **comments** on their annual accounts and the appointed auditor's reports."},{"h4":"§9.1.7 Auditing for compliance with legislative and related authorities"},{"p":"The Auditor-General performs the external audit of government financial statements and allied activities, encompassing both **pre-audit** and **post-audit** functions."},{"def":{"t":"Post-audit","d":"audit performed after the close of transactions in a particular period — a \"post-mortem\" audit, taking place after the end of the financial year."}},{"p":"Appointment, termination and mode of operation are governed by the Audit Act of 1956, the Nigerian Constitution, and the Financial Regulations of 2009 (as amended)."}]},{"n":"9.2","t":"The Office of the Auditor-General","b":[{"h4":"§9.2.1 Functions of the Office"},{"p":"The \"green paper\" on the Role of the Comptroller and Auditor-General lists four function types:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Audit type","What it examines"],"rows":[["**Financial and Regularity Audit**","A financial audit ensures accounting/financial control systems are efficient and operating properly, and financial transactions have been correctly controlled, authorised and accounted for. A regularity (compliance) audit verifies expenditure was incurred on approved services, in accordance with statutory and other governing regulations/authorities."],["**Economy and Efficiency Audit** (also called Value for Money Audit)","Measures how efficiently economic resources are employed — highlighting waste, extravagant or unrewarding expenditure, failures to maximise receipts, treasury-detrimental financial arrangements, and the weaknesses leading to them."],["**Effectiveness Audit** (also called Programme Results Audit)","Assesses whether programmes/projects undertaken to meet established policy goals have actually met their aims — aiming for a comprehensive audit of government activity."],["**Value-for-Money Audit** (also called Performance Audit)","Determines whether an entity acquires, manages or uses resources in the most economical/efficient manner, tracing the causes of inefficiency or uneconomical practice."]]}},{"def":{"t":"Value-for-money audit","d":"an objective, professional and systematic assessment of: (i) the nature and function of an authority's managerial systems/procedures; (ii) the economy and efficiency with which its services are processed; and (iii) the effectiveness of its performance in achieving objectives."}},{"p":"Five phases of a value-for-money audit:"},{"ol":["**Proposal** — justifying the study of a particular area, authorising initial resources, and determining initial analysis of financial statistics/audit costs/other performance indicators;","**Scoping** — gathering sufficient detail: working information, related legislation, and testing controls, alongside a comprehensive management-systems/objective review;","**Planning** — fully developing identified potential, properly analysing planning/control processes, and examining methods of reviewing operating results via control/reporting-system analysis;","**Implementation** — reporting audit results to those responsible for receiving/acting on them; and","**Evaluation** — assessing the audit result, methodology and audit staff performance, focused on efficiency/effectiveness review — aiming overall to identify system ineffectiveness and resource underutilisation."]},{"h4":"§9.2.2 Directorates in the Office of the Auditor-General"},{"p":"The Office comprises directorates including:"},{"ol":["**Treasury Accounts** — handles the audit of accounts and financial statements;","**State Accounts** — audits transactions of pay officers operating in various states;","**Public Enterprises** — oversees the audit of parastatals;","**Project Audit** — investigates justifications to pay officers operating in various states;","**Pension** — conducts pre-audit of gratuities and pensions;","**Losses and Investigations** — handles all cases of loss of funds; and","**Annual Report and Public Accounts Committee (PAC)** — handles all reports and links with PAC deliberations."]},{"h4":"§9.2.3 Audit queries and alarms"},{"def":{"t":"Audit query","d":"an observation or point raised by audit on a particular transaction, seeking further clarification."}},{"key":"Queries raised by **Internal Audit** are **pre-audit** queries; queries raised by the **Auditor-General** are **post-audit** queries. Queries are an important part of the financial control mechanism, and a valuable means of detecting/preventing errors, fraud, etc. — Civil Service Reform guidelines specify time limits for responding to queries, with possible sanctions for failing to respond."},{"p":"Matters on which queries may be raised fall into three classes:"},{"ol":["irregularities resulting in losses to government, through either fraud or negligence/incompetence of functionaries;","irregularities not directly/immediately causing loss to government, but infringing on budgetary and proper financial management; and","irregularities arising through poor or inefficient management/accounting, which may lead to government losses."]}]},{"n":"9.3","t":"Role of the Public Accounts Committee (PAC) in the audit process","b":[{"h4":"§9.3.1 Power to conduct investigation"},{"p":"Under the Constitution, each House of the National Assembly can, by resolution published in its journal or the Official Gazette, direct an investigation into: (a) any matter within its law-making power; and (b) the conduct of affairs of any person, authority, ministry or government department responsible for executing/administering laws enacted by the National Assembly, or disbursing/administering appropriated moneys."},{"key":"This investigative power is exercisable **only** to: make laws within its legislative competence (and correct defects in existing law); and expose corruption, inefficiency or waste in executing/administering laws or in disbursing/administering appropriated funds."},{"h4":"§9.3.2 Power as to matters of evidence"},{"p":"For investigations under Section 88 of the Constitution, the Senate, House of Representatives, or a Section-62 committee may: produce any evidence (written/oral, direct/circumstantial) it thinks necessary, examining witnesses whose evidence may be material/relevant; and require evidence to be given **on oath**."},{"key":"This committee is known as the **Public Accounts Committee (PAC)**, established to expose corruption, inefficiency or waste in the execution or disbursement/administration of appropriated funds. The PAC is empowered to:"},{"ol":["examine the audited accounts of the Federation and of all Federal offices/courts, and the Auditor-General's report on them, as referred to it;","examine: the accounts/reports of Ministries, Departments and Agencies; the audited accounts of statutory corporations/boards/other government bodies; and the causes of any excess over approved appropriations; and","procure any evidence (oral/written) and examine any persons deemed necessary, and require any person to produce relevant books/documents/records."]},{"note":"The PAC's composition is geared to function like a court of enquiry, empowered to examine civil and criminal cases regarding the allocation, disbursement and use of public funds by officers entrusted with them — carrying out government programmes and recommending action against officers found wanting."},{"h4":"§9.3.3 Basis of PAC activities"},{"p":"The PAC's major activity basis is the **Auditor-General's reports** — which state how government department/agency/ministry accounts were examined, what queries were raised, and what responses were given. Cases of prime importance (or that the Committee feels warrant proper examination) are picked out from the report; other cases come from matters the House refers to the Committee during deliberation."},{"h4":"§9.3.4 Problems of the PAC"},{"ol":["political instability (violent regime change, or political party change) causing continuous changes in Committee composition;","financial statements to be examined are often in **arrears**;","members are often not knowledgeable in accounting and financial reporting;","lack of co-operation/information from expected facilitators; and","resolutions are not always implemented."]}]},{"n":"9.4","t":"Internal audit in the public sector","b":[{"def":{"t":"Internal auditing (Institute of Internal Auditors)","d":"the independent appraisal activity within an organization for the review of accounting, financial and other operations, as a basis for protective and constructive service to management."}},{"h4":"§9.4.1 Objectives and scope"},{"p":"Internal audit assists management in effectively discharging its responsibilities, furnishing analysis, appraisals, recommendations and comments on the activities reviewed — potentially going beyond accounting/financial records to fully understand the operation under review. Internal audit exists in every Ministry/Department/Agency to carry out **pre-audit**."},{"def":{"t":"Pre-audit","d":"audit performed with the main objective of checking transactions before payment takes place, to ensure compliance with the rules governing each transaction."}},{"p":"To attain its overall objective, internal audit activities should include:"},{"ol":["reviewing/appraising the soundness, adequacy and application of accounting, financial and other administrative controls, and promoting effective controls at reasonable cost;","ascertaining the extent of compliance with established policies, plans and procedures;","ascertaining the extent to which department/ministry/agency assets are accounted for and safeguarded against losses of all kinds;","ascertaining the reliability of data developed/produced;","preserving the integrity of files storing transactions and related data; and","appraising the quality of performance in carrying out assigned responsibilities."]},{"h4":"§9.4.2 Operation of internal audit"},{"key":"Internal audit derives its powers from powers **delegated by the Auditor-General** — with full access to all relevant department/ministry/agency records, properties and personnel. Internal auditors carry out five types of audit:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Audit type","Focus"],"rows":[["**Operational Audit**","A particular activity within the establishment (e.g. an audit of Agricultural and Rural Development in a local government)."],["**Functional Audit**","A particular function (e.g. audit of salary preparation)."],["**Organizational Audit**","All activities within an establishment — including both operational and functional audit."],["**Investigation**","Ad-hoc assignments."],["**Management Audit**","Advising on policies introduced from time to time by the establishment."]]}},{"h4":"§9.4.3 Responsibilities of internal audit"},{"p":"Internal audit is responsible for: reviewing, evaluating and reporting on the adequacy (or otherwise) of installed/operated controls, and the extent to which they assure propriety, security, completeness and accuracy of the establishment's operations; performing the responsibility imposed by Civil Service Reforms — carrying out complete, continuous audit of accounts/records of revenue, expenditure and plant stores, and acting as inventory verifier where none exists; and confirming the reliability/accuracy of extracted financial information to management."},{"p":"Matters influencing setting up an internal audit unit: whether it is genuinely necessary given the circumstances of government (and Civil Service Reform guidelines/relevant laws); determining the unit's size/structure; preparing an audit manual; recruiting appropriate staff; and training/inducting staff."},{"key":"These same procedures apply equally to the other tiers of government — the state and local government levels."}]},{"n":"9.5","t":"Due process and public procurement","b":[{"h4":"§9.5.1 Due process"},{"p":"The Federal Government set up the **Budget Monitoring and Price Intelligence Unit (BMPIU)**, otherwise called the \"Due Process\" office — run as an operationally independent body under the Senior Special Assistant to the President on the Unit, staffed by project management/construction/procurement experts. It functions as the clearinghouse for all government contracts and procurement, operating under three strategies: goals, objectives and implementation — the goal being full compliance with laid-down guidelines/procedures for procuring capital/minor capital projects and associated goods/services."},{"def":{"t":"Due process","d":"the application of bureaucratic rules and regulations to managing a nation's economic resources — allowing approved official protocols to govern the spending of government funds/resources and the handling of government business."}},{"p":"Due process can also be defined as: a mechanism attesting that only projects passing proper implementation-packaging tests, and strictly adhering to the international competitive bid approach, receive public funding; firm adherence to laid-down rules/regulations in conducting national business, aiming for probity, accountability, transparency, effectiveness and efficiency; and the means by which ethical constraints are placed on administrative procedures/decision-making, preventing abuse of power and assuring financiers that budgeted/spent money is well-utilised and judiciously managed."},{"p":"Objectives of due process: harmonise/update public procurement policy and practice; ensure project conceptualisation, packaging and execution align with approved-budget targets; strictly enforce transparency, competition, efficiency and value for money in procuring public goods/works/services; ensure contract execution is monitored; and prevent extra-budgetary spending and contract-price inflation."},{"p":"Scope and coverage: entities at **all levels** of government, including enterprises owned/influenced by government; **all** goods and services, and any combination; coverage not limited to contracts above a threshold (though provisions may be more flexible for smaller contracts); and acquisition of any contracted item, including leases and rentals."},{"key":"Documents of importance to the auditor, needed to ascertain due process adherence: substantial/appropriate evidence of advertisement; the tender evaluation report; the contract award letter and agreement; and tender returns."},{"h4":"§9.5.2 The Public Procurement Act"},{"p":"The Public Procurement Act (Act No. 14 of 2007) established the **National Council on Public Procurement** (\"the Council\"), consisting of: the Minister of Finance (Chairman); the Attorney-General and Minister of Justice of the Federation; the Secretary to the Government of the Federation; the Head of Service of the Federation; the Economic Adviser to the President; six part-time members representing the Nigerian Institute of Purchasing and Supply Management, the Nigerian Bar Association, the Nigerian Association of Chambers of Commerce, Industry, Mines and Agriculture, the Nigerian Society of Engineers, civil society, and the media; and the Director-General of the Bureau, who serves as the Council's Secretary. Any co-opted person attending Council meetings has no casting vote and does not count toward quorum."},{"p":"Functions of the National Council on Procurement: consider, approve and amend the monetary/prior-review thresholds for applying the Act; consider and approve public procurement policies; approve appointment of the Bureau's Directors; receive and approve the Bureau's audited accounts; approve procurement-process changes adapting to modern technology; and give such orders/directives, and perform such other functions, as necessary to achieve the Act's objectives."}]},{"n":"9.6","t":"Establishment of the Bureau of Public Procurement (BPP)","b":[{"p":"The Bureau of Public Procurement (\"the Bureau\") is established as an agency that: is a **body corporate** with perpetual succession and a common seal; may sue and be sued in its corporate name; and may acquire, hold or dispose of movable/immovable property to carry out its functions under the Act."},{"h4":"§9.6.1 Objectives of the Bureau"},{"ol":["harmonising existing government policies/practices on public procurement, ensuring probity, accountability and transparency in the process;","establishing pricing standards and benchmarks;","ensuring fair, competitive, transparent, value-for-money standards/practices for procuring and disposing of public assets/services; and","attaining transparency, competitiveness and professionalism in the public procurement system."]},{"h4":"§9.6.2 Functions of the Bureau"},{"p":"The Bureau: formulates general procurement policies/guidelines for Council approval; publicises and explains the Act's provisions; certifies Federal Procurement prior to contract award (subject to Council thresholds); supervises implementation of procurement policies; monitors tendered-item prices and maintains a national standard-prices database; publishes major contract details in the procurement journal (print and electronic, with an archival system); maintains a national database of federal contractors/service providers; collates and archives federal procurement plans/information; undertakes procurement research/surveys; organises training/development for procurement professionals; periodically reviews the socio-economic effect of procurement policies, advising the Council; prepares/updates standard bidding/contract documents; prevents fraudulent/unfair procurement, applying administrative sanctions where necessary; reviews procurement/contract-award procedures of every entity the Act applies to; performs procurement audits, submitting reports to the National Assembly **bi-annually**; introduces/develops/maintains related database/technology; establishes a single internet portal as the primary source of public-sector procurement information; and co-ordinates training programmes to build institutional capacity."}]},{"n":"9.7","t":"Chapter summary","b":[{"ol":["This chapter provided full coverage of the statutory and constitutional provisions regarding the appointment and functions of the Auditor-General for the Federation.","The functions of the Public Accounts Committee, a unit of the National Assembly, were also detailed.","The information provided enables readers to fully understand the workings of the Office of the Auditor-General — at federal, state and equivalent local government level — together with the requirements for auditing public accounts and the supervisory powers of the National Assembly.","The procurement process and the Bureau of Public Procurement were also discussed."]}]},{"n":"9.8","t":"Study text questions — multiple-choice and short-answer","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with 10 questions — 5 multiple-choice and 5 short-answer — reproduced below in full with the official answers, each explained. The Auditing study text sets **no examination-type questions**; this bank is the whole of its end-of-chapter material."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"The questions, with the official answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Due process in the public sector involves which of the following? (A) Transparency in accounting for contracts; (B) Making use of a committee in approving contracts; (C) Making adequate arrangements in treating all audit queries into the decision-making process; (D) Allowing interested parties to provide input into the decision-making process; (E) Reporting over-inflated contracts to the National Assembly.","The following are documents necessary to ascertain that due process has been adhered to in the spending of public money **EXCEPT** (A) Substantial and appropriate evidence of advertisement; (B) Tender evaluation report; (C) Contract award letter and agreement; (D) Driving licence of the person quoting for the tender; (E) Tender returns.","The following matters are to be considered in designing an audit programme for government parastatals, corporations or ministries **EXCEPT** (A) General and service rules; (B) Organisational reporting structure; (C) Statutory law establishing the corporation, ministry or parastatal; (D) International passport of the parastatal’s general manager or permanent secretary of the ministry; (E) Internal control system in operation.","The National Council on Public Procurement has a board made up of the following **EXCEPT** (A) The Minister of Finance; (B) The Attorney-General and Minister of Justice of the Federation; (C) The Auditor-General for the Federation; (D) The Secretary to the Government of the Federation; (E) The Head of Service of the Federation.","Which of the following is **NOT** a major audit guide document the auditor will use in his compliance audit of government parastatals or ministries? (A) The Nigerian Constitution; (B) Civil service rules; (C) Gazettes; (D) CAMA 2020; (E) Treasury circulars.","In carrying out the audit of government parastatals there are two main checks which the auditor must adopt in his compliance tests. List them.","Who has the power to conduct periodic checks of all government statutory corporations, commissions and agencies, including all persons and bodies established by an Act of the National Assembly?","The power to appoint persons to act in the office of the Auditor-General for the Federation shall rest in ………","Who is the chairman of the National Council on Public Procurement?","An Act or Law passed by the National or State Assembly or Local Government authorising spending from the Consolidated Revenue Fund is called ………"]}],"a":[{"ol":["**D** — allowing interested parties to provide input into the decision-making process. Due process is about **openness and participation** in how public money is committed; the other options describe desirable practices but not the concept itself.","**D** — a driving licence has nothing to do with the procurement process.","**D** — the passport of an officer is irrelevant to the design of an audit programme.","**C** — the Auditor-General for the Federation is **not** a member. He could not be: as the external auditor of federal spending he must remain **independent** of the body that makes procurement policy, or he would be auditing decisions he had helped to take.","**D** — CAMA 2020 governs **companies**, not government ministries and parastatals, which are governed by the Constitution, the Act establishing them, the Financial Regulations, civil service rules, gazettes and treasury circulars.","**(a) Basic checks**, and **(b) special checks concerning legislative authorisation.**","**The Auditor-General for the Federation.**","**The President** — on the recommendation of the Federal Civil Service Commission, and subject to confirmation by the Senate.","**The Minister of Finance.**","**The Appropriation Act** (Appropriation Law at state level)."]}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"Public sector assurance introduces its own vocabulary layered on top of the general audit concepts from earlier chapters — the exam draws heavily on the specific appointment/removal mechanics (President + Senate confirmation for federal, two-thirds Senate majority to remove), the pre-audit/post-audit distinction (internal audit does pre-audit; the Auditor-General does post-audit), and the four types of public sector audit (financial/regularity, economy and efficiency, effectiveness, value-for-money) — memorise these as a set, since they map onto no private-sector equivalent covered elsewhere in the subject. Also know: the Auditor-General does NOT audit statutory corporations directly (only provides an approved-auditor list and comments on their accounts); the National Council on Public Procurement's membership does NOT include the Auditor-General; and the specific 90-day and six-month statutory time limits.","errors":["Assuming the Auditor-General personally audits every government statutory corporation, commission or agency — the Auditor-General explicitly does NOT audit these directly, only supplies a list of approved external auditors and comments on their accounts/reports.","Confusing pre-audit with post-audit — pre-audit (performed by internal audit) checks transactions BEFORE payment; post-audit (performed by the Auditor-General) is a \"post-mortem\" audit after the financial year closes.","Treating the four public-sector audit types (financial/regularity, economy and efficiency, effectiveness, value-for-money) as interchangeable — each has a genuinely distinct focus, from compliance (regularity) through to whether policy goals were actually achieved (effectiveness).","Believing the Auditor-General can be removed by simple presidential discretion — removal specifically requires presidential action supported by a two-thirds Senate majority, and only for infirmity or misconduct.","Listing the Auditor-General as a member of the National Council on Public Procurement — the Council's membership is the Minister of Finance, the Attorney-General, the SGF, the Head of Service, the Economic Adviser, six part-time representatives, and the Bureau's Director-General as Secretary; the Auditor-General is not among them."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Due process in the public sector involves which of the following?","o":["Transparency in accounting for contracts","Making use of a committee in approving contracts","Making adequate arrangements in treating all audit queries into the decision making process","Allowing interested parties to provide input into the decision making process","Report over-inflated contracts to the National Assembly"],"a":3,"w":"Due process is defined around ensuring probity, accountability, transparency, competition and fairness in procurement — allowing interested parties input into the decision-making process is the option that best captures this participatory, competitive ideal, consistent with the due process objectives of transparency and competition.","src":"Chapter 9.5","sec":"9.5"},{"q":"The following are documents necessary to ascertain that due process has been adhered to in the spending of public money EXCEPT","o":["Substantial and appropriate evidence of advertisement","Tender Evaluation report","Contract award letter and agreement","Driving License of the person quoting for the tender","Tender Returns"],"a":3,"w":"A tenderer's driving licence has no bearing on whether procurement due process was followed — the genuine due-process documents are evidence of advertisement, the tender evaluation report, the contract award letter/agreement, and tender returns.","src":"Chapter 9.5","sec":"9.5"},{"q":"The following matters are to be considered in designing an audit programme for government parastatals, corporations, or ministries EXCEPT","o":["General and service rules","Organizational reporting structure","Statutory law establishing the corporation, ministry, or parastatal","International passport of the parastatal's general manager or permanent secretary of the ministry","Internal control system in operation"],"a":3,"w":"A senior official's personal international passport is irrelevant to designing an audit programme — the genuinely relevant matters are the governing rules, reporting structure, establishing statute, and the internal control system.","src":"Chapter 9","sec":"9.4"},{"q":"The National Council on Public Procurement has a board made up of the following EXCEPT","o":["The Minister of Finance","The Attorney-General and Minister of Justice of the Federation","The Auditor-General for the Federation","The Secretary to the Government of the Federation","The Head of Service of the Federation"],"a":2,"w":"The Auditor-General for the Federation is explicitly NOT a member of the National Council on Public Procurement — the Council comprises the Minister of Finance (chair), the Attorney-General, the SGF, the Head of Service, the Economic Adviser, six part-time representative members, and the Bureau's Director-General as Secretary.","src":"Chapter 9.5","sec":"9.5"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a major audit guide document the auditor will use in a compliance audit of government parastatals/ministries?","o":["The Nigerian Constitution","Civil Service rules","Gazettes","CAMA 2020","Treasury circulars"],"a":3,"w":"CAMA governs private companies incorporated under company law — it is not the governing framework for public sector compliance auditing, which instead draws on the Constitution, Civil Service rules, Gazettes and Treasury circulars.","src":"Chapter 9","sec":"9.1"}],"theory":[{"q":"Distinguish between pre-audit and post-audit in the public sector, stating who ordinarily performs each.","marks":4,"a":[{"p":"**Pre-audit** is performed with the main objective of checking transactions **before** payment takes place, to ensure compliance with the rules governing each transaction — ordinarily carried out by **internal audit**, which exists in every Ministry/Department/Agency for this purpose."},{"p":"**Post-audit** is performed **after** the close of transactions in a particular period — a \"post-mortem\" audit taking place after the financial year ends — ordinarily carried out by the **Auditor-General**."}],"src":"Chapter 9.1","sec":"9.1"},{"q":"State the four types of audit the Office of the Auditor-General performs, with a brief description of each.","marks":8,"a":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Audit type","Description"],"rows":[["Financial and Regularity Audit","Checks that accounting/financial control systems operate properly and that transactions were correctly controlled, authorised and accounted for, in compliance with governing regulations."],["Economy and Efficiency Audit","Measures how efficiently resources are employed, highlighting waste and extravagant expenditure."],["Effectiveness Audit","Assesses whether programmes/projects have actually met their established policy goals."],["Value-for-Money Audit","Determines whether resources are acquired, managed and used in the most economical and efficient manner, tracing the causes of any inefficiency."]]}}],"src":"Chapter 9.2","sec":"9.2"}]}},{"n":10,"t":"IT-Based Audit Assurance","brief":"Auditing in a computerised environment: the six types of information technology system, the special challenges of small (PC-based) systems, how audit documentation and the audit approach itself change with IT, Computer Assisted Audit Techniques (CAATs), application vs. general controls in a computer environment, controls in online/real-time systems and database management systems, business/IT risk, and the current technology trends reshaping audit practice — big data, electronic transaction audit, artificial intelligence and machine learning.","outcomes":["Explain types of information technology systems that auditors encounter","State typical control challenges faced in the assurance of small IT-based systems","Explain documentation procedures used in the assurance of manual and IT-based systems","Explain the approach to the audit of IT-based systems with attention to audit trail and controls","Explain business systems and associated IT risks","Explain the concepts of IT audit activities and current trends in Information Technology (big data analytics, audit of electronic transactions, artificial intelligence, machine learning)"],"secs":[{"n":"10.1","t":"Information technology systems","b":[{"p":"There are six information technology systems an auditor encounters:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["System","What it does"],"rows":[["**Transaction Processing System (TPS)**","Turns transaction inputs into outputs — a transaction includes all purchases/sales of goods/services and business activities needed to run the entity. Originally called \"data processing\"; an improvement over manual handling."],["**Office Automation System (OAS)**","A web of tools/technologies for clerical and managerial tasks — recording, printing, mailing."],["**Knowledge Management System (KMS)**","Stores and extracts information to help employees/customers/management build knowledge and optimise collaboration — the shared information may itself be the product (e.g. in education) or about the product (e.g. commercial/industrial settings)."],["**Management Information System (MIS)**","Lets middle managers extract transaction data from a TPS, analyse it, and produce reports helping top management optimise planning/decision-making — including summarisation into the entity's financial statements."],["**Decision Support System (DSS)**","Software tools (e.g. Microsoft Office) assisting management decisions — spelling/grammar correction (Word), large-volume calculation/variance analysis/scenario mapping (Excel) — helping middle managers make informed decisions."],["**Executive Support System (ESS)**","Also called an \"expert system\" — proffers solutions to non-routine problems (e.g. taxation, investment, shareholding information), helping directors/owners monitor entity performance."]]}},{"key":"Understanding these six systems helps the auditor identify which information system is of **particular importance** to the audit versus which is secondary — but every IT system in use at the client is relevant to the auditor's overall knowledge of the client's business."}]},{"n":"10.2","t":"Challenges faced by small IT-based systems","b":[{"key":"Most PC-related control problems arise from the **departure from the formal structure** of a traditional data processing department — the world of the PC is more informal, lacking the controlled environment traditionally provided over acquisition, maintenance and distribution of computer information."},{"p":"Problems surrounding PCs fall under three headings, each creating difficulty assessing documentation, design-process adequacy, and the completeness/accuracy/authority of data and audit trails:"},{"h4":"§10.2.1 Lack of planning over acquisition and use"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Consideration","What it involves"],"rows":[["**Authorization**","A feasibility study examining requirements/costs/benefits; inviting supplier tenders and comparing responses; involving all interested parties throughout; ideally consulting other system users (e.g. the auditors themselves) for their opinion."],["**Suitability**","Risk that the client lacks the expertise to properly evaluate systems, causing compatibility/capacity problems that restrict future development (short of full replacement). Standard software packages purchased by first-time users raise this risk further, since they may not precisely fit the company's trading methods, and first-time users are unlikely to have the expertise to tailor them."],["**Support facilities**","The client should ascertain supplier/software-house support and enter a maintenance contract ensuring: prompt service (and backup facilities) on breakdown; bugs can be sorted out; minor modifications are possible; adequate systems documentation (program documentation, operator instructions, and a user manual) and operator manuals are provided; and operators receive adequate instruction."],["**Standards**","A formal data processing environment normally has hardware/software standards for controls and accounting principles — but with PCs, where ordering-to-operation may take only weeks, there is a real danger standards are never set. Strict discipline is needed to apply recognised systems-development controls and sufficient administration procedures."]]}},{"h4":"§10.2.2 Lack of documentary evidence"},{"p":"Many PCs operate in real time via VDUs, giving users direct computer access to input data, update files and make one-off enquiries — avoiding the need for edit programs and hard copy. Control can be enhanced by building in edit programs at the design stage, and incorporating a **user-usage file** logging user identification, the application involved, and records accessed/updated — periodically reviewed by a responsible official and the auditor. Manual controls (transactions only processed with a supporting initiating document, manual batching) may also be prudent."},{"h4":"§10.2.3 Lack of security and confidentiality"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Problem","Detail"],"rows":[["**Lack of segregation of duties**","The same person often prepares data, feeds it in, supervises processing, and is the end user — enhancing fraud opportunity via combined access to assets and their recording/disposal. Auditors may need extensive substantive verification work to compensate."],["**Lack of control over users**","PCs do not require a protected environment, so terminals are readily available to any user — necessitating controls preventing unauthorised use (locks, passwords, etc.)."],["**Lack of control over program alterations**","A lack of expertise may cause imprudent purchase decisions; conversely, expertise is easily acquired once a machine is operational (PCs use high-level languages, learnable quickly), risking undetected program alteration, or programs written without adequate testing while data is being processed. Stringent supervisory arrangements and programmed controls preventing unauthorised running are needed. Programs permanently etched onto silicon chips (ROMs) gain security since only specialist electronics engineers can alter them."]]}}]},{"n":"10.3","t":"Documentation procedures in IT-based systems","b":[{"p":"Computerised working paper packages ease documenting audit work, aiding preparation of working papers, lead schedules, extended trial balances and financial statements — automatically cross-referenced and balanced, with adjustments automatically updating all necessary schedules."},{"p":"Advantages of automated working papers: reduced risk of errors; neater, easier-to-review papers; substantial time saved (adjustments apply easily across all papers, including analytical summaries); no need to carry standard forms to audit locations (forms can be called up/completed on screen); and working papers can be transmitted for review via the internet."},{"p":"Combining office packages (Word, Excel, Access) can automate logical steps: **checking completeness** (is there evidence of supervision/review on each schedule? Is there an audit conclusion on each lead schedule? Have all points on \"audit point sheets\" been answered?); **checking accuracy** (are numeric totals consistent? Do they carry forward accurately to the lead schedule?); and **automated processing** (footing/cross-footing, carrying forward numeric data to lead schedules, highlighting proposed recommendations for management review, consolidating/reformatting material for the draft audit report). More generally, graphics production, image processing, desk-top publishing, and on-line/real-time file access can all assist."},{"key":"Software packages can also perform **analytical procedures** and **statistical sampling** — most useful when imputed directly from the client's own system, building cumulative year-on-year data, and able to determine sample sizes, generate random numbers, and evaluate sample-based test results."},{"p":"Decision support systems automate judgmental processes normally requiring a \"human\" auditor — notably automating checklists (internal control evaluation, statutory requirements, materiality estimation) that follow different logic paths depending on answers given, including omitting questions where appropriate."}]},{"n":"10.4","t":"Audit approach in IT-based systems","b":[{"p":"Audits are performed \"in a computer environment\" wherever computer-based accounting systems (large or small) process information supporting the financial statements — operated by the entity itself, or by a third party on its behalf."},{"def":{"t":"Computer Assisted Audit Techniques (CAATs)","d":"techniques performed with computers by the auditor, in two major categories: (a) use of audit software — computer programs examining the enterprise's computer file contents; and (b) use of test data — data used by the auditor to test the operation of the enterprise's own computer programs."}},{"key":"When auditing in a computer environment, the auditor needs a basic understanding of data-processing fundamentals, and technical computer knowledge/skill that may, depending on circumstances, need to be **extensive**."},{"h4":"Planning the use of CAATs"},{"p":"Points to weigh when choosing between CAATs and manual procedures:"},{"ol":["computer programs often perform functions with **no visible evidence** — making manual testing frequently impracticable;","where a manual/CAAT choice genuinely exists, the auditor weighs relative efficiency: the extent of test-of-control/substantive coverage each achieves, the CAAT's cost pattern, and its ability to incorporate several different tests at once;","under a short reporting time-scale, CAATs may be more efficient (quicker to apply) even if manual methods remain practicable and cheaper;","using a CAAT requires ensuring the necessary computer facilities/files/programs are available, and — since enterprises don't retain copies indefinitely — planning in good time to preserve copies and avoid **loss of audit trail**;","some CAATs need frequent auditor attendance/access; reliance on internal auditors' own CAATs depends on the assessed effectiveness/relevance of the internal audit function; and","where accounting records include computer data, the auditor needs access to it — current practice often uses auditor laptops to test client data collected from the client's system, without entering that system directly."]},{"key":"Where CAATs are used, particular attention is needed on: co-ordinating specialist-computer-skilled staff with the rest of the audit team; and approval/review of the technical work by someone with the necessary computer expertise. A CAAT may be used on copies of computer records/programs, provided reasonable assurance is gained that the copies are **identical to the originals**."},{"h4":"Internal control evaluation in a computer environment"},{"key":"The same internal-control principles apply as in any environment, with additional considerations. Controls split into **application controls** (specific to each transaction/standing-data system, ensuring completeness/accuracy of records and validity of entries from manual and programmed processing) and **general controls** (relating to the whole environment in which computerised operations occur, ensuring proper development/implementation of applications and program/data/operations integrity). Both may be manual or programmed."},{"p":"Where preliminary evaluation reveals absent or uncompensated-weak controls, the auditor moves directly to substantive procedures (possibly CAAT-assisted). Where controls may meet the auditor's objectives, tests of controls follow if reliance is wanted — influenced by cost-effectiveness, ease of testing, and reliability factors. Procedures for testing controls include: observing the control in operation; examining documentary evidence of its operation; repeat performance; and testing/re-performing specific program parts via CAATs for programmed controls."},{"h4":"Review, working papers and audit trails"},{"p":"CAATs (particularly audit software) can assist aspects like analytical review. Where a CAAT is used, working papers should show: the work performed, its results, the auditor's conclusions, how technical problems were resolved, and any recommendations for modifying the CAAT in future audits."},{"key":"An audit trail's original purpose was to preserve **paper** details of every processing stage — letting a transaction be followed stage-by-stage (e.g. a sales transaction traced through the customer order, delivery note, invoice and cheque/cash receipt) to confirm correct processing."},{"h4":"Around the computer vs. through the computer"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Approach","How it works"],"rows":[["**Around the computer** (traditional)","Auditors fulfil their function without detailed knowledge of what happens inside the computer — ignoring internal program procedures and concentrating solely on inputs/outputs (checking authorisation/coding/control totals of inputs, and outputs against source documents/clerical control totals). Consumes vast paper/computer/printer time, and does not reflect modern computerised accounting reality (computers manipulate/interpret data for management as much as record-keeping) — as system complexity has increased, audit trail has correspondingly been lost, and this approach is now frowned upon."],["**Through the computer** (modern default)","Examines the computer's detailed processing routines to determine whether controls adequately ensure complete/correct processing of all data — often requiring CAATs. Addresses typical problems as trails move away from hard copy: testing computer-generated totals absent detailed analysis, and testing output completeness absent control totals. The principal challenge is acquiring genuine understanding of electronic data processing and the computer itself."]]}}]},{"n":"10.5","t":"Computer Assisted Audit Techniques (CAATs) in detail","b":[{"p":"There is nothing mysterious about using a computer to help audit — most modern accounting systems allow ad-hoc reporting and sophisticated database querying; even limited systems let data be exported to a spreadsheet for sorting/recalculation; and most systems have search facilities far quicker than manual print-out searching, offsetting much of the so-called \"loss of audit trail\" (the trail is still there, just followed electronically)."},{"key":"Before running any audit software, the auditor checks the **identity and version** of the data files/programs used (client-supplied or auditor-supplied), normally via external evidence like control totals, file lengths, dates and other file properties."},{"h4":"Audit interrogation software"},{"p":"Performs the checks on data auditors would otherwise do by hand — particularly appropriate for substantive testing of transactions and balances, letting the auditor scrutinise large data volumes while concentrating skilled manual resources on investigating results rather than extracting information. Considerations: a basic understanding of data processing/the application, plus detailed knowledge of the audit software and files used; possibly a sound appreciation of systems analysis/operating systems, and programming-language experience if program code is used; how easily client data transfers to the auditor's PC; and whether the client can fully explain all the information their system produces."},{"h4":"Test data"},{"p":"Inputting test data and comparing actual results against pre-calculated expected results tests whether a system processes data correctly. The problem: any resulting data-file corruption must be corrected, which is difficult with modern real-time systems (whose built-in controls often prevent easy, mark-free removal of entered data) — consequently, test data is falling out of use as a CAAT."},{"h4":"Embedded audit facilities"},{"key":"Test data results would be completely distorted if the programs processing it were not the ones normally used (e.g. a fraudulent IT staff member substituting a correct-result version purely during the test). **Embedded audit facilities** — audit modules incorporated directly into the enterprise's own accounting system — allow continuous review instead."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Technique","How it works"],"rows":[["**Integrated Test Facility (ITF)**","Creates a fictitious entity (e.g. a department or customer) within the regular application; transactions are posted to it alongside real transactions, and the results are compared against a predetermined expected outcome. Care is needed to keep the fictitious entity out of actual financial reporting — the simplest, most secure method being reversing journal entries at appropriate cut-off dates. ITF lets management and auditors constantly check internal processing functions on both valid and invalid transactions."],["**System Control and Review File (SCARF)**","Relatively simple to build in — e.g. for a general ledger, each account carries two \"auditor\" fields: a Yes/No SCARF flag, and a monetary threshold, both settable only by the auditors. Transactions posted to a SCARF account exceeding its threshold are also written to a separate SCARF file, letting auditors monitor material transactions/sensitive accounts with ease."]]}},{"h4":"Simulation, program logic/coding, and knowledge-based systems"},{"p":"**Simulation** (\"parallel simulation\") prepares a separate program simulating the organisation's real system — real data is passed through both, e.g. re-performing controls like identifying missing items from a sequence. **Logical path analysis** draws flowcharts of program logic; **code comparison programs** compare the original specified program against the current one, detecting unauthorised amendments. **Knowledge-based systems** (\"decision support\" or \"expert\" systems) assist the auditor's own judgment/decisions, saving time/money by increasing procedural efficiency, easing record maintenance, reducing required staff numbers, and letting routine tasks go to technicians assisted by the expert system."}]},{"n":"10.6","t":"Controls in a computer environment","b":[{"key":"Application and general controls are **inter-related**: strong general controls add to the assurance obtainable from application controls, while unsatisfactory general controls can undermine strong application controls, or worsen already-unsatisfactory ones."},{"p":"Points influencing the auditor's approach: (a) before relying on programmed application controls, the auditor needs reasonable assurance the programs have operated properly, via evaluating/testing relevant general controls or other specific tests; (b) where a programmed procedure lacks effective application controls, the auditor may test the relevant general controls instead (manually or via CAATs) to limit substantive procedures; (c) systematic errors can arise from program faults or hardware malfunction — many should be prevented/detected by general controls over development, program/data integrity, and operations; and (d) reliance on general controls may be limited where they lack evidencing or are applied inconsistently (common with small computers) — in which case the auditor seeks assurance from manual application-control tests, or specific program-part tests instead."},{"h4":"Application controls"},{"p":"Specific requirements to achieve application control objectives: controls over the completeness/accuracy/authorisation of input; compliance with authorisation procedures; completeness/accuracy of processing; and maintenance of master files/standing data files (master files hold permanent information; standing data files hold current, repeatedly-used information, e.g. staff grades/wage rates)."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Application control area","Key techniques"],"rows":[["**Input — completeness**","Manual/programmed agreement of control totals; one-for-one checking of processed output to source documents; sequence checking; programmed matching against a control file of expected input; and procedures over resubmitting rejected inputs."],["**Input — accuracy**","Covers both value fields (e.g. invoice amounts) and reference fields (e.g. account number/date). Techniques: programmed check-digit verification; programmed reasonableness checks (including logical cross-file relationships); programmed existence checks against valid codes; and manual output scrutiny."],["**Authorization**","Ensures all transactions are authorised, and by someone empowered to do so — generally via clerical review (possibly supplemented by a programmed check for transactions exceeding authorisation limits), done after a control total is established or after processing, so unauthorised transactions cannot slip in post-review."],["**Processing**","Ensures all input data is processed; correct master/standing data files are used; each transaction is processed accurately; new/updated data is accurate and authorised; and output reports are complete/accurate. The same completeness/accuracy techniques used for input apply here too, but to the RESULTS of processing (e.g. a post-update batch reconciliation, not the pre-update one)."],["**Master files and standing data**","Similar techniques to transaction input, plus: more costly one-to-one checking (given these files' importance); cyclical review of all master/standing data files; record counts and hash totals used whenever master files are processed (a check that all records were processed, otherwise of no significance); and controls over deleting accounts holding a current balance."]]}},{"h4":"General controls"},{"p":"General controls must: ensure proper development of computer applications; prevent/detect unauthorised program changes; ensure program changes are adequately tested/documented; prevent/detect errors during program use; prevent unauthorised data-file amendments; ensure systems software is properly installed/maintained; ensure proper documentation is kept; and ensure continuity of operations."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["General control area","What the auditor considers"],"rows":[["**Development of applications**","System design standards; programming standards; documentation controls/standards; testing procedures; approval of development stages by users and computer management; internal audit involvement; segregation of duties (design vs. programming vs. operations); and training/supervision."],["**Preventing/detecting unauthorized program changes**","For maintenance: segregation of duties and staff training/supervision; job authorisation prior to processing; reviewed change records; password protection; virus checks; emergency modification procedures; backup-copy integrity; physical protection; offsite extra copies; and comparison of production programs to controlled copies. For execution: preventing access to programs while running; controls over utility program use; restricted computer/terminal access; job-accounting-report review (investigating unusual delays); and duty rotation."],["**Testing and documenting program changes**","Adequacy of testing procedures; documentation controls/standards; user and computer-management approval of changes; internal audit involvement and segregation of duties; and staff training/supervision."],["**Preventing/detecting execution errors**","Adequacy of operations controls in the systems software; job control procedure libraries; set-up/execution procedures; job scheduling and emergency backup procedures; and training/supervision — protecting against incorrect data files, wrong program versions, wrong-sequence running, incorrect responses to programme requests, and job control errors."],["**Preventing unauthorized data file amendments**","Depends on application controls over the files, how the file is maintained, and the file management software used. Considerations: job authorisation prior to processing; unauthorised-amendment detection; password protection and investigation of unauthorised access attempts; emergency modification procedures; backup integrity; physical protection; restricted utility program use; and segregation of duties."],["**Systems software installation/maintenance**","Systems software (operating system, teleprocessing monitors, database management systems, etc.) needs controls both exercised BY it and over it: amendment frequency, amendment procedures, access controls, and segregation of duties."],["**Proper documentation**","Aids efficient/accurate operation, application setup/amendment, and disaster recovery. Considerations: documentation quality; standards quality; enforcement of standards and internal audit involvement; updating procedures."],["**Continuity of operations**","Backup procedures (e.g. off-site extra program copies); testing of backup facilities/procedures; equipment protection against fire/hazards; separate power sources; emergency/disaster recovery procedures; and maintenance agreements/insurance."]]}}]},{"n":"10.7","t":"Controls in online and real-time systems","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["System","Definition"],"rows":[["**On-line systems**","Provide facilities for data to pass to/from the central computer via remote terminals."],["**Real-time systems**","A further development permitting immediate updating of computer-held files — input and file-update phases merge, and the system accepts individual transactions rather than batches. Often called \"one-write\" systems — the computerised equivalent of carbon-copy bookkeeping (sales day book, customer account, customer statement all prepared simultaneously)."]]}},{"p":"Control problems in real-time systems (concerned primarily with larger, multi-user systems — \"terminals\" meaning dumb terminals or networked PCs):"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Problem","Control response"],"rows":[["**Segregation of duties**","The same person often produces and processes the same information — supervisory controls should be strengthened to compensate for the reduced internal check."],["**Data file security**","Remote terminal access requires special controls against accidental/deliberate unauthorised reading, writing or destruction — partly physical (restricted terminal access, locked terminals/rooms when unused) and partly via the operating system (passwords/badges/keys, sometimes tiered — one for reading, another for writing, a third for both; restriction of certain users to certain files; logging of attempted violations, possibly with automatic shutdown). Application controls can add validity checks on input and reporting of unusual transactions."],["**Program security**","The same considerations as data file security apply equally to programs."],["**Back-ups**","Needed at least daily, plus separately weekly. A real-time system may need a cut-off time after which no further transactions post — since opening a file during backup can corrupt the process, and stray post-backup transactions could be lost if a restore is needed. Immediate file access does allow more sophisticated checks (e.g. extensive computer matching of input against held data), and lets users immediately correct certain error types, despite the abuse potential."]]}},{"h4":"Database Management Systems (DBMS)"},{"key":"DBMS are normally designed for real-time environments, letting data elements be accessed by different programs — avoiding the data duplication that inevitably occurs in traditional systems. Since data is normally stored **only once** and accessible to all users needing it, the principal control problems concern **authorising data amendments** and **restricting access**. An independent administration function should run day-to-day database operations, enhancing segregation of duties (independent of systems development, programmers, and the data processing manager)."},{"p":"DBMS-specific controls: preventing unauthorised program changes (restricted live-program-file access, password protection, restricted computer/terminal access, console-log maintenance reviewed by the data processing manager and an independent party like internal audit, periodic comparison of live programs to control copies); preventing/detecting operational errors (password-restricted terminal/field access, satisfactory application controls, operations manuals and user training, logs of unauthorised access attempts reviewed regularly, physical data protection, emergency-procedure training); and ensuring database integrity (restricted access to the data dictionary — the standard descriptions of data definitions/characteristics/inter-relationships — segregation of duties between the data processing manager, database administration and systems development, liaison between database administration and systems development for specification integrity, and preparation/updating of user manuals alongside the data dictionary)."},{"warn":"Auditing DBMS creates particular problems, since the two principal CAATs (test data and audit software) tend to work unsatisfactorily on DBMS programs/files — auditors may instead use embedded audit facilities, close liaison with internal auditors, and, ideally, involvement at the evaluation/design/development stages to identify audit requirements and control problems **before** implementation."}]},{"n":"10.8","t":"Business systems and associated IT risks","b":[{"p":"Clients relying on IT for key business activities face three main risk categories: natural disaster, general IT risks, and criminal IT risks."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Category","Examples"],"rows":[["**Natural disasters**","Floods, earthquakes, fire — damaging buildings/hardware, causing loss or corruption of business data records."],["**General IT threats**","Viruses (self-copying, spreading, disruptive programs); spam/scams/phishing (unsolicited emails fooling people into revealing details or buying fraudulent goods); malware (malicious disruptive programs); human error (incorrect processing, careless disposal, opening infected attachments); and hardware/software failure (power outages, data corruption)."],["**Criminal IT threats**","Denial-of-service attacks (preventing authorised website access); fraud (altering data for illegal benefit); hackers (illegal system break-ins); password theft (a common hacker tool); security breaches (online intrusion, physical break-ins); and staff dishonesty (theft of sensitive information, e.g. customer details)."]]}},{"p":"Main effects of IT risk: loss of confidential/sensitive data; loss of business continuity; brand/reputational damage; and regulatory fines for negligence."},{"key":"Preventive measures focus on **business continuity**: identifying/preventing/reducing risks; insuring against risks that cannot be controlled; and ensuring quick response/recovery if a risk materialises."},{"p":"Specific risk-and-inhibition pairs: Trojans (disguised harmful software) — install anti-virus software; Malware — install anti-virus software, use and regularly change strong passwords; Spam (bulk unsolicited email) — use inbox junk-email filters; Cookies (tracking software reporting movements back to the originating site) — regularly clear browser cookies; Identity theft (using client information to commit fraud) — store identity information securely and never share it with untrusted sources; Phishing (fake emails capturing sensitive data) — never open/click links in unsolicited emails; Hackers — keep software updated, use a firewall, and create non-disclosure agreements with employees; and Theft of IP/client data — complete security training with personnel, create security policies/procedures, and identify/secure valuable data."}]},{"n":"10.9","t":"Current trends in information technology","b":[{"h4":"§10.9.1 Big data analytics"},{"def":{"t":"Big data","d":"a mixture of structured, semi-structured and unstructured data collected by organisations, mined for information and used in advanced analytics applications (predictive modelling, machine learning)."}},{"key":"Big data is defined by its **three Vs**: **volume** (data comes from many environments, in large amounts), **variety** (frequently wide-ranging), and **velocity** (generated/collected/processed at high speed) — datasets so voluminous that traditional data processing software cannot manage them, yet valuable because they solve business problems."},{"p":"Big data helps: improve operational efficiencies; optimise product development; drive new revenue/growth opportunities; streamline resource management; and enable smart decision-making. High-performance analytics applied to big data accomplishes: determining root causes of failures/issues/defects; spotting anomalies faster/more accurately; swiftly converting data outcomes into insight; speedily recalculating entire risk portfolios/scenarios; sharpening deep-learning models' classification/reaction ability; and detecting fraudulent behaviour before it affects the organisation."},{"h4":"§10.9.2 Audit of electronic transactions"},{"p":"Electronic commerce has penetrated every facet of human endeavour, with cashless transactions consummated via digital platforms. The auditor observes four procedures:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Procedure","What it involves"],"rows":[["**Identify accepted payment methods**","Obtain a list of accepted payment types and process maps for each channel (credit/debit cards, mobile wallets, digital currencies like Bitcoin, ACH payments, wire transfers, intermediary payments like Interswitch/PayPal); review how cashless receipts flow into the company's bank account; and amend the audit programme to review them in detail based on how many cashless methods are used."],["**Evaluate roles and responsibilities**","Obtain a list of employees involved in receiving/recording/analysing/reporting cashless transactions; review how employee access to every connected technology platform is managed/monitored; and evaluate who handles each aspect of the cashless payment cycle, checking security and segregation of duties is adequate to prevent fraud/misstatement."],["**Test the reconciliation process**","Review prior sales reconciliations for accuracy and appropriate revenue recognition (especially challenging under new long-term-contract revenue recognition rules); and test accounting entries for accounts like inventory, deferred revenue and accounts receivable."],["**Analyze trends**","Cashless transactions create an electronic audit trail, letting the auditor analyse sales by payment channel over time and by employee/unit, comparing against prior years to uncover anomalies; and collect information on past payment problems and the remedial actions taken."]]}},{"h4":"§10.9.3 Artificial Intelligence (AI)"},{"def":{"t":"Artificial Intelligence","d":"the ability of a computer, or a computer-controlled robot, to perform tasks normally requiring human intelligence and discernment — the simulation of human intelligence in machines programmed to think like, and mimic the actions of, humans."}},{"p":"Examples: conversational bots; email spam filters; Netflix recommendations; robo-advisors; self-driving cars; and smart assistants (Siri, Alexa)."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Dimension","Weak AI","Strong AI"],"rows":[["Application","Narrow","Wide"],["Scope","Limited","Vast"],["Good at","Specific tasks","Human-level intelligence"],["Process","Supervised/unsupervised learning","Clustering and association"]]}},{"note":"Siri and Alexa are examples of weak AI; advanced robotics represents strong AI. AI aids human capabilities, simplifying effort and supporting better decisions — used by companies to improve process efficiency, automate resource-heavy tasks, and make data-based business predictions."},{"p":"AI's uses in data processing: searching within data and optimising for the most relevant results; logic-chains for if-then reasoning executing command strings based on parameters; pattern-detection identifying significant patterns in large datasets; and applied probabilistic models predicting future outcomes."},{"p":"Advantages of AI: constant (24-hour) availability; digital assistance; efficient communication; faster decisions; helps with repetitive work (no fatigue); improves security (digital surveillance); medical applications (diagnosis, drug dispensary, advisory); rational, data-based decision-making; and reduction in human error."},{"h4":"§10.9.4 Machine Learning (ML)"},{"def":{"t":"Machine Learning","d":"a form of AI that uses data to teach machines how to make inferences and decisions from past experience, rather than through explicit programming — recognising patterns and categorising past data to infer the meaning of current data and reach a conclusion without human assistance."}},{"key":"ML is both an **art** (reducing disparate problems to a set of acceptable prototypes) and a **science** (solving those problems and guaranteeing the recommended solutions) — helping businesses make better decisions, handle larger volumes, and save man-hours."},{"p":"Business applications: search-engine webpage ranking; Amazon attracting users to purchase more books; Netflix inviting additional video rentals; multilingual automatic translation (e.g. at the UN); speech recognition for verbal approvals; and face recognition for restricted-premises access."},{"p":"Types of ML: **Supervised Learning** (humans feed the machine data); **Unsupervised Learning** (the machine learns on its own, without human assistance); **Reinforcement Learning** (the machine is rewarded for doing the job as expected); **Deep Learning** (the human brain is simulated in the machine, so it learns on its own); and **Deep Reinforcement Learning** (incentivising/rewarding a Deep Learning network for expected performance)."}]},{"n":"10.10","t":"Chapter summary","b":[{"ol":["Computers have several uses in audit administration and management, including linked (cross-referenced) computerised working papers, statistical sampling, and analytical procedures.","Auditors may use a number of Computer Assisted Audit Techniques (CAATs), including audit interrogation software, test data, embedded audit facilities, simulation, logical path analysis, and code comparison programs.","Application controls should ensure the completeness, accuracy and authorisation of input; the completeness and accuracy of processing; and proper maintenance of master files and standing data.","General controls are required to ensure proper application development; prevent/detect unauthorised program changes; ensure all program changes are adequately tested/documented; prevent/detect errors during program use; prevent unauthorised amendments to data files; ensure systems software is properly installed/maintained; ensure proper documentation is kept; and ensure continuity of operations.","The majority of modern systems are real-time systems, where users have instant, direct access to data. Important real-time controls are segregation of duties, data file security, program security, and backups.","Many small computer systems use PCs — the major problems being lack of planning over acquisition/use, lack of documentary evidence, and lack of security/confidentiality.","Big data analytics, audit of electronic transactions, artificial intelligence and machine learning are current trends in information technology that impact the way audit is conducted."]}]},{"n":"10.11","t":"Study text questions — multiple-choice and short-answer","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with 10 questions — 5 multiple-choice and 5 short-answer — reproduced below in full with the official answers, each explained. The Auditing study text sets **no examination-type questions**; this bank is the whole of its end-of-chapter material."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"The questions, with the official answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["The possibility of erasing a large amount of information stored on a magnetic tape would most likely be reduced by using (A) File protection rings; (B) Check digits; (C) Completeness check; (D) Conversion verification; (E) Parity check.","Which of the following is **not** an example of CAATs? (A) System control and review file; (B) Integrated test facilities; (C) Read Only Memory; (D) Snapshot; (E) Mapping.","Computers are useful in audit administration to do the following **EXCEPT** (A) Footing and cross-footing; (B) Carrying forward numerical data to lead schedules; (C) Highlighting proposed audit recommendations for audit management review; (D) Consolidating materials in audit working papers in order to prepare the audit report; (E) None of the above.","Which of the following computer documentations can assist in obtaining an understanding of the internal control system? (A) Audit trail; (B) Computer log; (C) Record layout; (D) System flow chart; (E) Programme code analysis.","The loss of audit trail can be overcome through the following techniques **EXCEPT** (A) Special print-out; (B) Computer programme; (C) Alteration test; (D) Use of test pack; (E) By not keeping logs.","Controls over transactions and standing data appertaining to each computer-based accounting system, and therefore specific to each such application, are known as ………","When transactions can be followed stage by stage through a system to ensure that they have been processed correctly, there is an audit ………","The audit approach which ignores the procedures that take place within the computer programs and concentrates solely on the input and corresponding output is called ………","Controls which relate to the environment within which computer-based accounting systems are developed, maintained and operated, and which are therefore applicable to all applications, are known as ………","Which of the data processing functions should usually be set up to run and control the day-to-day operations of the database?"]}],"a":[{"ol":["**A** — file protection rings. These are a **physical** control that prevents writing to a tape at all. The others are data-validation controls: they check the accuracy of data being processed, but do nothing to stop a tape being overwritten.","**C** — Read Only Memory is a **hardware component**, not a computer-assisted audit technique. The other four are recognised CAATs for auditing **through** the computer.","**E** — \"none of the above\". The question is asking which task a computer **cannot** help with, and the answer is that it helps with all four, so there is no exception.","**D** — the system flow chart. It shows the **whole flow of data** through the system and where the controls sit, which is exactly what is needed to understand the control system. The others give detail about individual files, records or events rather than the system as a whole.","**E** — not keeping logs would **destroy** what remains of the trail, not restore it. The trail is recovered by producing special print-outs, writing audit software, and using test data.","**Application controls.**","**Trail** (audit trail).","**Auditing \"round\" the computer** — as distinct from auditing **through** the computer, which tests the processing itself using CAATs.","**General controls.**","**The administration function** — the database administrator."]}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"This chapter has the highest technical-vocabulary density of the subject. Focus first on the application-controls vs. general-controls distinction (specific-to-one-system vs. environment-wide) — it structures almost everything else in §10.6. Then know: the \"around the computer\" vs. \"through the computer\" audit-approach distinction and why the former is now discouraged; the two main CAAT categories (audit software, test data) plus the named techniques (ITF, SCARF, simulation, logical path analysis, code comparison); and the six IT systems (TPS/OAS/KMS/MIS/DSS/ESS) as a set worth being able to name and distinguish at a glance. For the \"current trends\" section, know the three Vs of big data and the weak-vs-strong AI distinction — these are new-syllabus content and likely to be tested directly rather than through scenario application.","errors":["Treating application controls and general controls as interchangeable — application controls are SPECIFIC to one transaction/standing-data system; general controls apply across the WHOLE computing environment. They are inter-related but distinct, and weak general controls can undermine even strong application controls.","Assuming \"around the computer\" auditing remains an acceptable modern default — the chapter is explicit that it is now frowned upon, precisely because increasing system complexity has eroded the paper audit trail it depends on.","Confusing test data (inputting data to see what a system does with it, now declining in use because of file-corruption risk) with embedded audit facilities like ITF/SCARF (built permanently into the live system, avoiding that exact problem).","Believing the three Vs of big data are volume, variety and VALUE — the correct third V is VELOCITY (how fast data is generated/collected/processed), not value.","Assuming DBMS can be audited with the same primary CAATs (test data, audit software) used elsewhere — the chapter explicitly notes these two standard CAATs tend to work unsatisfactorily on DBMS programs/files, requiring embedded audit facilities and early auditor involvement instead."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"The possibility of erasing a large amount of information stored on a magnetic tape would most likely be reduced using","o":["File protection rings","Check digits","Completeness check","Conversion verification","Parity check"],"a":0,"w":"File protection rings are a physical mechanism specifically designed to prevent accidental erasure/overwriting of magnetic tape — the other options address different concerns (data accuracy, completeness, conversion, transmission error detection).","src":"Chapter 10.6","sec":"10.6"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT an example of CAATs?","o":["System control and review file","Integrated test facilities","Read Only Memory","Snapshot","Mapping"],"a":2,"w":"Read Only Memory (ROM) is a hardware storage type, not an audit technique — SCARF and ITF are both named embedded audit facilities, while snapshot and mapping are recognised CAAT techniques.","src":"Chapter 10.5","sec":"10.5"},{"q":"Computers are useful in audit administration to do the following EXCEPT","o":["Footing and cross footing","Carry forward of numerical data to lead schedules","Highlight proposed audit recommendations for audit management review","Consolidate materials in audit working papers in order to prepare the audit report","None of the above"],"a":4,"w":"All four listed tasks — footing/cross-footing, carrying forward numeric data, highlighting recommendations, and consolidating materials for the draft report — are genuine automated-processing uses the chapter describes, so there is no genuine exception among them.","src":"Chapter 10.3","sec":"10.3"},{"q":"Which of the following computer documentation can assist in obtaining an understanding of the internal control system?","o":["Audit trail","Computer log","Record layout","System flow chart","Programme code analysis"],"a":3,"w":"A system flowchart, like its manual counterpart (Chapter 8), diagrammatically records how the system and its controls actually operate — the standard documentation tool for understanding internal control.","src":"Chapter 10","sec":"10.4"},{"q":"The loss of audit trail can be overcome through the following techniques EXCEPT","o":["Special print out","Computer programme","Alteration Test","Use of Test Pack","By not keeping logs"],"a":4,"w":"Not keeping logs is the opposite of a remedy — it actively worsens audit trail loss, rather than overcoming it; the other four are genuine techniques for recovering or substituting for a lost audit trail.","src":"Chapter 10.4","sec":"10.4"}],"theory":[{"q":"Distinguish between application controls and general controls in a computer environment, giving one example of each.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"**Application controls** relate to the transactions and standing data pertaining to a SPECIFIC computer-based accounting system, ensuring the completeness and accuracy of records and the validity of entries in that particular application — e.g. programmed check-digit verification on an input reference number."},{"p":"**General controls** relate to the WHOLE environment in which computerised accounting systems are developed, maintained and operated, applying across ALL applications — e.g. password protection restricting access to live program files."},{"p":"The two are inter-related: strong general controls enhance the assurance an auditor can place on application controls, while weak general controls can undermine otherwise-strong application controls."}],"src":"Chapter 10.4","sec":"10.4"},{"q":"Explain the Integrated Test Facility (ITF) and System Control and Review File (SCARF) as examples of embedded audit facilities.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"An **Integrated Test Facility (ITF)** creates a fictitious entity (e.g. a department or customer) within the regular live application. Test transactions are posted to this fictitious entity alongside genuine transactions, and the results produced by normal processing are compared against a predetermined expected outcome. Care must be taken (e.g. reversing journal entries at cut-off) to ensure the fictitious entity never becomes part of actual financial reporting."},{"p":"A **System Control and Review File (SCARF)** flags specific accounts (via an auditor-set Yes/No field) with a monetary threshold, also set only by the auditors. Transactions posted to a flagged account above its threshold are automatically written to a separate SCARF file, letting auditors monitor material transactions or sensitive accounts continuously and with ease."},{"p":"Both are embedded permanently within the live accounting system, avoiding the data-corruption risk that affects standalone test data."}],"src":"Chapter 10.5","sec":"10.5"}]}},{"n":11,"t":"Reports on Financial Statements","brief":"The auditor's final deliverable: the eight basic elements every audit report contains, the unmodified (clean) report's full wording, and the modified-report framework — emphasis of matter (which does not affect the opinion), and the three true modifications (qualified, disclaimer, adverse), each tied to whether the cause is a scope limitation or a disagreement with management, and how material/pervasive that cause is.","outcomes":["Describe the purpose, significance and content of the audit report","Choose which type of audit report is appropriate to specific situations"],"secs":[{"n":"11.1","t":"Basic elements of the auditor's report","b":[{"key":"The auditor reviews and assesses the conclusions drawn from audit evidence as the basis for expressing an opinion — considering whether the financial statements were prepared per an acceptable financial reporting framework (IFRS, or relevant national standards/practices), and whether they comply with necessary statutory requirements. The report must contain a **clear written expression of opinion** on the financial statements **taken as a whole**."},{"p":"The auditor's report has eight basic elements, in this layout:"},{"ol":["**Title**;","**Addressee**;","**Opening/introductory paragraph** — identifying the financial statements audited, and stating the respective responsibilities of management and the auditor;","**Scope paragraph** — describing the nature of the audit: a reference to ISAs (or relevant national standards/practices), and a description of the work performed;","**Opinion paragraph** — a reference to the financial reporting framework used (identifying its country of origin, if not IFRS), and the actual expression of opinion;","**Auditor's signature**;","**Auditor's address**; and","**Date of the report**."]},{"key":"A measure of **uniformity** in report form/content is desirable — it helps the reader's understanding and helps unusual circumstances stand out when they occur."},{"h4":"Title"},{"p":"An appropriate title is needed — often \"**Independent Auditor's Report**,\" distinguishing it from reports issued by others (entity officers, the board, or auditors not bound by the same ethical requirements)."},{"h4":"Addressee"},{"p":"Addressed as circumstances/local regulations require — ordinarily to the **shareholders** of the entity being audited."},{"h4":"Opening (introductory) paragraph"},{"p":"Identifies the financial statements audited (including their date and period covered), and states: the financial statements are **management's responsibility**; and the auditor's responsibility is to **express an opinion** on them based on the audit."},{"key":"Financial statements are **management's representations** — their preparation requires management to make significant accounting estimates/judgments and determine appropriate accounting principles/methods, within whichever financial reporting framework management chooses or is required to use. The auditor's role is, by contrast, purely to **audit** those statements and express an opinion."},{"pre":"We have audited the accompanying statement of financial position of ABC Company as of 31 December 2021, and the related statements of comprehensive income, changes in equity and cash flows for the year then ended. These financial statements are the responsibility of the Company's management. Our responsibility is to express an opinion on these financial statements based on our audit."},{"h4":"Scope paragraph"},{"def":{"t":"Scope (of the audit)","d":"the auditor's ability to perform audit procedures deemed necessary in the circumstances."}},{"p":"States the audit was conducted per ISAs (or relevant national standards/practices) — assuring the reader the audit met established standards. Unless otherwise stated, the standards followed are presumed to be those of the country shown by the auditor's address. The paragraph states the audit was **planned and performed to obtain reasonable assurance** that the financial statements are free of material misstatement, describing the audit as including:"},{"ol":["examining, on a test basis, evidence supporting the financial statement amounts and disclosures;","assessing the accounting principles used;","assessing significant estimates management made; and","evaluating the overall financial statement presentation."]},{"p":"It also states the audit provides a **reasonable basis** for the opinion."},{"pre":"We conducted our audit in accordance with International Standards on Auditing (or refer to relevant national standards or practices). Those standards require that we plan and perform the audit to obtain reasonable assurance about whether the financial statements are free of material misstatement. An audit includes examining, on a test basis, evidence supporting the amounts and disclosures in the financial statements. An audit also includes assessing the accounting principles used and significant estimates made by management, as well as evaluating the overall financial statements presentation. We believe that our audit provides a reasonable basis for our opinion."},{"h4":"Opinion paragraph"},{"key":"Clearly indicates the financial reporting framework used (with country of origin, if not IFRS), and states the auditor's opinion on whether the financial statements **\"give a true and fair view\"** (or **\"present fairly, in all material respects\"**) per that framework — the two phrasings are **equivalent**, both indicating the auditor considers only matters **material** to the financial statements."},{"p":"The financial reporting framework is set by IFRS, rules from recognised standard-setting bodies (e.g. the Financial Reporting Council of Nigeria), and a country's own developed general practice, with appropriate regard to fairness and local legislation. The framework reference is stated in terms like: \"…in accordance with International Financial Reporting Standards (or title of framework, with country of origin)…\" — helping users understand the reporting basis used, and, where statements are prepared specifically for use in another country, prompting the auditor to consider whether the entity disclosed the framework used appropriately. The opinion paragraph may also need to cover compliance with other statutory/legal requirements, beyond the true-and-fair opinion itself."},{"pre":"In our opinion, the financial statements give a true and fair view of (or present fairly, in all material respects) the financial position of the Company as of 31 December 2021, and of the results of its operations and its cash flows for the year then ended in accordance with International Financial Reporting Standards (or [title of financial reporting framework with reference to the country of origin]) (and comply with…)."},{"h4":"Auditor's signature"},{"key":"The report is signed in the **personal name of the engagement partner**, with the Institute's stamp affixed and the report sealed — the signer must state their **FRC (Financial Reporting Council) number** after their name. The report is ordinarily in the **firm's** name (on the firm's letterhead), since the firm assumes audit responsibility."},{"p":"Signing procedure: the engagement auditor signs; writes their full name; includes their FRC number; states the firm's name and city; affixes the Institute's stamp; and affixes their seal."},{"h4":"Auditor's address"},{"p":"Names a specific location — ordinarily the city where the auditor maintains the office responsible for the audit."},{"h4":"Date of the report"},{"key":"Dated as of the **audit's completion date** — informing the reader the auditor considered the effect of events/transactions they became aware of up to that date. Since the auditor reports on the financial statements **as prepared/represented by management**, the report should **not** be dated earlier than the date management signed or approved the financial statements."}]},{"n":"11.2","t":"The unmodified (unqualified) audit report","b":[{"key":"An unqualified (clean) opinion is expressed when the auditor concludes the financial statements give a true and fair view (or are presented fairly, in all material respects) per the identified framework — implicitly also indicating any accounting-principle changes (and their application/effects) have been properly determined and disclosed."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration — a full unqualified auditor's report","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Reproduce a complete unqualified auditor's report to the members of ABC Plc, combining every basic element covered in §11.1."}],"a":[{"pre":"AUDITOR'S REPORT\nAudit Report to the Members of ABC Plc\n\nWe have audited the accompanying statement of financial position of ABC Company as of 31 December 2021, and the related statements of income, and cash flows for the year then ended. These financial statements are the responsibility of the Company's management. Our responsibility is to express an opinion on these financial statements based on our audit.\n\nWe conducted our audit in accordance with International Standards on Auditing (or refer to relevant national standards or practices). Those Standards require that we plan and perform the audit to obtain reasonable assurance about whether the financial statements are free of material misstatement. An audit includes examining, on a test basis, evidence supporting the amounts and disclosures in the financial statements. An audit also includes assessing the accounting principles used and significant estimates made by management, as well as evaluating the overall financial statement presentation. We believe that our audit provides a reasonable basis for our opinion.\n\nIn our opinion, the financial statements give a true and fair view of (or present fairly, in all material respects) the financial position of the Company as of 31 December 2021, and of the results of its operations and its cash flows for the year then ended in accordance with the International Financial Reporting Standards.\n\nSignature\n\nPPP Chartered Accountants\nVictoria Island\nLagos\nDate: 1 October 2025"}]}}]},{"n":"11.3","t":"The modified audit report","b":[{"p":"An auditor's report is \"modified\" in the following situations:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Category","Types"],"rows":[["**Matters that do NOT affect the opinion**","Emphasis of Matter."],["**Matters that DO affect the opinion**","Qualified opinion; Disclaimer of opinion; or Adverse opinion."]]}},{"key":"Uniformity in the form/content of each modified-report type furthers user understanding — **ISA 705, \"Modifications to the Opinion in the Independent Auditor's Report,\"** provides suggested wording for both the unqualified opinion and modifying phrases used in modified reports."},{"h4":"§11.3.1 Matters that do not affect the opinion — Emphasis of Matter"},{"p":"In certain circumstances, the report may add an **emphasis of matter paragraph**, highlighting a matter affecting the financial statements that is already more fully discussed in a note — this addition does **not** affect the opinion. The paragraph is preferably placed **after** the opinion paragraph, and ordinarily states the opinion is not qualified in that respect."},{"key":"The auditor **should** modify the report with such a paragraph for a material **going concern** problem. The auditor **should consider** doing so for any other significant **uncertainty** (whose resolution depends on future events, and which may affect the financial statements) — an uncertainty being a matter whose outcome depends on future actions/events not under the entity's direct control."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration — emphasis of matter for a significant uncertainty","open":true,"q":[{"p":"How does an emphasis of matter paragraph, for a pending lawsuit whose outcome cannot presently be determined, appear within an otherwise unqualified opinion?"}],"a":[{"pre":"In our opinion, the financial statements give a true and fair view of (or present fairly, in all material respects) the financial position of the Company as of 31 December 2021, and of the results of its operations and its cash flows for the year then ended in accordance with International Accounting Standards/International Financial Reporting Standards.\n\nWithout qualifying our opinion, we draw attention to Note X to the financial statements. The Company is the defendant in a lawsuit alleging infringement of certain patent rights and claiming royalties and punitive damages. The Company has filed a countersuit, and preliminary hearing and discovery proceedings on both actions are in progress.\n\nThe ultimate outcome of the matter cannot presently be determined, and a provision for any liability that may result has been made in the financial statements."}]}},{"warn":"Adding an emphasis-of-matter paragraph is **ordinarily adequate** to meet the auditor's reporting duty on a going concern problem or significant uncertainty. But in **extreme cases** — e.g. multiple uncertainties significant to the financial statements — the auditor may consider a **disclaimer of opinion** more appropriate than an emphasis of matter paragraph."},{"p":"An emphasis of matter paragraph may also cover matters **other than** those affecting the financial statements — e.g. a material inconsistency in other information within a document containing the audited statements, where the entity refuses to amend it, or additional statutory reporting responsibilities."},{"h4":"§11.3.2 Matters that affect the opinion"},{"key":"An unqualified opinion cannot be expressed where, in the auditor's judgment, the effect of a matter is or may be **material** to the financial statements, and either: (a) there is a **limitation on the scope** of the auditor's work; or (b) there is a **disagreement** with management over accounting policy acceptability, its application method, or disclosure adequacy."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Cause","Possible opinions"],"rows":[["**(a) Limitation of scope**","Qualified opinion, or disclaimer of opinion."],["**(b) Disagreement with management**","Qualified opinion, or adverse opinion."]]}},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Opinion type","When expressed"],"rows":[["**Qualified opinion**","When an unqualified opinion cannot be expressed, but the effect of the disagreement or scope limitation is NOT so material and pervasive as to need an adverse opinion or disclaimer — expressed as being **\"except for\"** the effects of the matter qualified."],["**Disclaimer of opinion**","When the possible effect of a scope limitation is SO material and pervasive that the auditor cannot obtain sufficient appropriate evidence, and is accordingly unable to express any opinion at all."],["**Adverse opinion**","When the effect of a disagreement is SO material and pervasive that a mere qualification would not adequately disclose the misleading or incomplete nature of the financial statements."]]}},{"key":"The pattern across both causes is identical: **mild** → qualified; **severe, from a scope limitation** → disclaimer (auditor cannot form an opinion at all); **severe, from a disagreement** → adverse (auditor actively concludes the statements are misleading). A scope limitation never leads to an adverse opinion, and a disagreement never leads to a disclaimer — the type of cause determines which severe outcome applies."},{"h4":"§11.3.3 Disclosures in a modified opinion"},{"key":"Whenever the opinion is anything other than unqualified, the report must include a **clear description of all substantive reasons**, and (unless impracticable) a **quantification of the possible effect(s)** on the financial statements — ordinarily set out in a separate paragraph **preceding** the opinion or disclaimer paragraph, possibly referencing a fuller discussion in a note."}]},{"n":"11.4","t":"Circumstances that may result in a modified opinion","b":[{"h4":"§11.4.1 Limitation of scope"},{"p":"A scope limitation may be **entity-imposed** (e.g. engagement terms specifying the auditor will not carry out a procedure the auditor believes necessary) — where the limitation is severe enough that the auditor believes a disclaimer would be needed, the auditor ordinarily would **not accept** such a limited engagement as an audit engagement at all (unless required by statute), and a statutory auditor would not accept it where the limitation infringes their statutory duties."},{"p":"A scope limitation may also arise from **circumstances** — e.g. appointment timing preventing observation of a physical inventory count, inadequate accounting records, or inability to carry out a desirable procedure. Here, the auditor first attempts **reasonable alternative procedures** to obtain sufficient appropriate evidence supporting an unqualified opinion."},{"key":"Where a scope limitation genuinely requires a qualified opinion or disclaimer, the report should **describe the limitation** and indicate the possible adjustments that might have been found necessary, had the limitation not existed."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration — qualified opinion for a scope limitation (inventory not observed)","open":true,"q":[{"p":"The auditor was engaged after the year-end inventory count date, and the company's records did not allow inventory quantities to be verified by any alternative means. How is this reflected in a qualified auditor's report?"}],"a":[{"pre":"Except as discussed in the following paragraph, we conducted our audit in accordance with International Standards on Auditing (or refer to relevant national standards or practices)…\n\nWe did not observe the counting of the physical inventories as of 31 December, 2021 since that date was prior to the time we were initially engaged as auditors of the company. Owing to the nature of the company's records, we were unable to satisfy ourselves as to inventory quantities by other audit procedures.\n\nIn our opinion, except for the effects of such adjustments, if any, as might have been determined to be necessary, had we been able to satisfy ourselves as to physical inventory quantities, the financial statements give a true and fair view of (or present fairly, in all material respects) the financial position of the Company as of 31 December 2021, and of the results of its operations and its cash flows for the year then ended in accordance with the International Financial Reporting Standards."}]}},{"key":"Where the scope limitation is severe enough to require a **disclaimer**, the report omits the statement of the auditor's own responsibility, either omits or amends the scope paragraph, adds a paragraph describing the limitation, and closes with the explicit statement that **no opinion is expressed** — e.g. \"...because of the significance of the matters discussed in the preceding paragraph, we do not express an opinion on the financial statements.\""},{"h4":"§11.4.2 Disagreement with management"},{"p":"Disagreements may cover the acceptability of accounting policies selected, their method of application, or disclosure adequacy. Where material, the auditor expresses a **qualified** or an **adverse** opinion."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration — qualified opinion for disagreement (inappropriate accounting policy)","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Management has provided no depreciation at all in the financial statements — a practice the auditor considers non-compliant with International Accounting Standards. How does the qualification paragraph read?"}],"a":[{"pre":"As discussed in Note X to the financial statements, no depreciation has been provided in the financial statements, which practice, in our opinion, is not in accordance with International Accounting Standards. The provision for the year ended 31 December 2024 should be N[xxx] based on the straight-line method of depreciation using annual rates of 5% for the building and 20% for the equipment. Accordingly, the fixed assets should be reduced by accumulated depreciation of N[xxx], and the loss for the year and accumulated deficit should be increased by N[xxx] respectively.\n\nIn our opinion, except for the effect on the financial statements of the matter referred to in the preceding paragraph, the financial statements give a true and fair view of (or present fairly, in all material respects) the financial position of the Company as of 31 December 2024, and of the results of its operations and its cash flows for the year then ended in accordance with International Accounting Standards/International Financial Reporting Standards."}]}},{"note":"A disagreement can equally concern **inadequate disclosure** rather than an inappropriate accounting method — e.g. an undisclosed debenture agreement restricting future dividend payments — with the same \"except for the omission of the information…\" qualified-opinion structure applying."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration — adverse opinion for disagreement","open":true,"q":[{"p":"The disagreement with management is so material and pervasive that a mere qualification would not adequately disclose the misleading nature of the financial statements. How does the opinion paragraph read?"}],"a":[{"pre":"In our opinion, because of the effects of the matters discussed in the preceding paragraph(s), the financial statements do not give a true and fair view of (or do not present fairly) the financial position of the Company as of 31 December 2024, and of the results of its operations and its cash flows for the year then ended in accordance with International Accounting Standards (and do not comply with…)."}]}}]},{"n":"11.5","t":"Chapter summary","b":[{"ol":["Auditors are required to make specific reference in the audit report as to: the truth and fairness of the state of the company's affairs at period end; the truth and fairness of the Statement of Profit or Loss and Other Comprehensive Income; and whether the accounts have been properly prepared in accordance with CAMA (as amended).","CAMA requires certain matters to be specifically reported on **by exception**: proper accounting records kept; accounts agreeing with accounting records/returns; all information and explanations received; directors' emoluments correctly disclosed; loans and other transactions in favour of directors/related parties correctly disclosed; and information in the directors' report being consistent with the accounts.","The main elements of an unqualified audit report are: a title identifying the addressee; an introductory paragraph identifying the financial statements audited; sections on directors'/auditors' responsibilities; the basis of the auditors' opinion; and a clear statement of opinion.","Auditors may qualify their opinion on grounds of limitation of scope or disagreement with management, which may be material or fundamental (pervasive).","Auditors are principally concerned with the correct treatment and disclosure of inherent and fundamental uncertainties, which relate to uncertain future events.","Auditors should include an explanatory paragraph in their audit report if fundamental uncertainties exist."]}]},{"n":"11.6","t":"Study text questions — multiple-choice and short-answer","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with 10 questions — 5 multiple-choice and 5 short-answer — reproduced below in full with the official answers, each explained. The Auditing study text sets **no examination-type questions**; this bank is the whole of its end-of-chapter material."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"The questions, with the official answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["When there is a disagreement that is material but not fundamental to a financial statement, the auditor will issue a/an (A) Disclaimer opinion; (B) Adverse opinion; (C) Subject to opinion; (D) Except for; (E) Qualified.","Which of the following is the term used to describe a statement where the auditor draws the attention of users of the financial statements to a particular issue without the intention of qualification? (A) Unqualified report; (B) Audit opinion; (C) Exception report; (D) Qualified opinion; (E) Emphasis of the report.","An auditor concludes that a client’s illegal act, which has a material effect on the financial statements, has not been properly accounted for or disclosed. Depending on the materiality of the effect, the auditor should express either (A) An adverse opinion or a disclaimer of opinion; (B) A qualified opinion or an adverse opinion; (C) A disclaimer of opinion or an unqualified opinion; (D) An unqualified opinion with a separate explanatory paragraph or a qualified opinion; (E) None of the above circumstances.","For which of the following events would an auditor issue a report that does **not** make any reference to consistency? (A) A change in the method of accounting for inventories; (B) A change from an accounting principle that is not generally accepted to one that is generally accepted; (C) A change in the life span used to calculate the provision for depreciation expense; (D) Management’s lack of reasonable justification for a change in accounting principle; (E) A change in the asset revaluation policy.","Which of the following statements is a basic element of the auditor’s standard report? (A) The disclosure provides reasonable assurance that the financial statements are free of material misstatement; (B) The auditor evaluates the overall internal control; (C) An auditor includes assessing significant estimates made by management; (D) The financial statements are consistent with those of the prior period; (E) The audit is conducted in accordance with international statements on auditing.","When the auditor concludes that the financial statements give a true and fair view, he will express ……… opinion.","Under what situations will an auditor’s report be considered to be modified?","The paragraph stating the factual statement of what the auditor did in the audit is called the ……… paragraph.","The part of the auditor’s report which clearly indicates the financial reporting framework used to prepare the financial statements is called the ……… paragraph.","Mention **TWO** circumstances that will make an auditor issue a qualified audit report."]}],"a":[{"ol":["**C** — a \"subject to\" opinion *(the study text’s answer)*. **Note:** modern terminology under ISA 705 no longer uses \"subject to\"; a material but not pervasive disagreement now produces a **qualified (\"except for\") opinion**, which is option (D). Both describe the same situation; learn the modern wording but know what the older term means.","**E** — an **emphasis of matter**. It is **not a qualification**: the opinion remains unmodified, and the paragraph simply directs the reader to something already properly disclosed in the financial statements that is fundamental to understanding them.","**B** — a qualified opinion or an adverse opinion. This is a **disagreement**, not a limitation of scope: the auditor has the evidence and disagrees with the treatment. A disagreement that is material gives a **qualified** opinion; one that is **pervasive** gives an **adverse** opinion. Disclaimers arise only from limitations of scope.","**C** — a change in the estimated useful life of an asset is a **change in accounting estimate**, not a change in accounting **policy**, so it raises no consistency question. The other four are all changes of policy or of their justification.","**C** — the scope paragraph states that the audit includes **assessing the significant estimates made by management** and evaluating the overall presentation. (E) is close but wrongly worded — the report refers to International Standards on **Auditing**, not \"international statements\".","**An unqualified** (unmodified) opinion.","**Two situations:** (a) matters that **do not affect** the auditor’s opinion — an emphasis of matter paragraph; and (b) matters that **do affect** the auditor’s opinion — a qualified opinion, a disclaimer of opinion, or an adverse opinion.","**Scope** paragraph.","**Opinion** paragraph.","**Any two of:** a **limitation of scope** on the auditor’s work that is material but not pervasive (records destroyed, a confirmation refused, inability to attend an inventory count); a **disagreement with management** that is material but not pervasive (an inappropriate accounting policy, inadequate disclosure, a misstated balance); inability to obtain sufficient appropriate audit evidence over a material item; and non-compliance with legislation or accounting standards that management will not correct."]}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"This chapter is the natural culmination of the whole subject — every other chapter builds toward the opinion expressed here. Learn the modified-opinion decision tree as a single flowchart: is the CAUSE a scope limitation or a disagreement? Is the EFFECT mild, or material-and-pervasive? That two-question test determines whether the outcome is unqualified, qualified (\"except for\"), disclaimer (scope-limitation-only), or adverse (disagreement-only) — memorising this mapping is worth more than memorising any single report's exact wording. Also know: emphasis of matter NEVER changes the opinion itself (it is added information, not a qualification); \"give a true and fair view\" and \"present fairly, in all material respects\" are equivalent phrasings; and the report must never be dated earlier than when management approved the financial statements.","errors":["Treating an emphasis of matter paragraph as a form of qualification — it explicitly does NOT affect the opinion; the report ordinarily states directly that the opinion is not qualified in that respect.","Assuming a scope limitation can lead to an adverse opinion, or a disagreement can lead to a disclaimer — the chapter's own framework pairs each CAUSE with only two possible outcomes: scope limitation → qualified or disclaimer; disagreement → qualified or adverse. The severe outcomes never cross over.","Believing the auditor's report can be dated before management approved the financial statements — since the auditor reports on statements AS PREPARED BY management, the report date can never precede that approval date.","Omitting the FRC (Financial Reporting Council) number from the signature description — this specific requirement (the signing partner's FRC number, alongside the Institute stamp and seal) is a genuine Nigerian-practice detail worth remembering precisely.","Confusing \"true and fair view\" and \"present fairly, in all material respects\" as somehow different standards — the chapter is explicit these are EQUIVALENT phrasings of the same opinion."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"When there is disagreement that is material but not fundamental to a financial statement, the auditor will issue a/an","o":["Disclaimer opinion","Adverse opinion","Subject to opinion","Except for opinion","Qualified opinion"],"a":2,"w":"\"Subject to\" is the older terminology (predating the ISA clarity project) for a qualification that stops short of adverse/disclaimer severity — functionally equivalent to what this chapter calls a qualified (\"except for\") opinion, expressed for a disagreement that is material but not so pervasive as to require an adverse opinion.","src":"Chapter 11.3","sec":"11.3"},{"q":"Which of the following is the term used in describing a statement where the auditor draws the attention of users of financial statements to a particular issue without the intention of qualification?","o":["Unqualified report","Audit opinion","Exception Report","Qualified opinion","Emphasis of the report"],"a":4,"w":"This describes an emphasis of matter paragraph — added to highlight a matter already covered in a note to the financial statements, explicitly without affecting the opinion.","src":"Chapter 11.3","sec":"11.3"},{"q":"An auditor concludes that a client's illegal act, which has a material effect on the financial statements, has not been properly accounted for or disclosed. Depending on the materiality of the effect on the financial statements, the auditor should express either","o":["An adverse opinion or a disclaimer of opinion","A qualified opinion or an adverse opinion","A disclaimer of opinion or an unqualified opinion","An unqualified opinion with a separate explanatory paragraph or a qualified opinion","None of the above circumstances"],"a":1,"w":"An improperly accounted-for or disclosed matter is a DISAGREEMENT with management, not a scope limitation — disagreements lead to either a qualified opinion (material) or an adverse opinion (material and pervasive), never a disclaimer.","src":"Chapter 11.3","sec":"11.3"},{"q":"For which of the following events would an auditor issue a report that does not make any reference to consistency?","o":["A change in the method of accounting for inventories","A change from an accounting principle that is not generally accepted to one that is generally accepted","A change in the life span used to calculate the provision for depreciation expense","Management's lack of reasonable justification for a change in accounting principle","A change in the assets revaluation policy"],"a":2,"w":"A change in an estimated useful life is a change in an ACCOUNTING ESTIMATE, not a change in accounting PRINCIPLE — only changes in accounting principle trigger a consistency reference; estimate changes (like a revised depreciable life) do not.","src":"Chapter 11","sec":"11.2"},{"q":"Which of the following statements is a basic element of the auditor's standard report?","o":["The disclosure provides reasonable assurance that the financial statements are free of material misstatement","The auditor evaluates the overall internal control","An auditor includes assessing significant estimates made by management","The financial statements are consistent with that of the prior period","The audit is conducted in accordance with international statements on auditing"],"a":2,"w":"The scope paragraph explicitly states the audit includes assessing the significant estimates management made — this is one of the four described components of the scope paragraph's content, verbatim from the chapter.","src":"Chapter 11.1","sec":"11.1"}],"theory":[{"q":"Distinguish between a qualified opinion, a disclaimer of opinion, and an adverse opinion, stating the circumstances in which each is expressed.","marks":9,"a":[{"p":"A **qualified opinion** is expressed when an unqualified opinion cannot be given, but the effect of a disagreement or scope limitation is not so material and pervasive as to require an adverse opinion or disclaimer — expressed as \"except for\" the effects of the matter concerned."},{"p":"A **disclaimer of opinion** is expressed when the possible effect of a **scope limitation** is so material and pervasive that the auditor cannot obtain sufficient appropriate evidence, and is accordingly unable to express any opinion at all."},{"p":"An **adverse opinion** is expressed when the effect of a **disagreement** is so material and pervasive that a mere qualification would not adequately disclose the misleading or incomplete nature of the financial statements."},{"p":"A scope limitation can only ever lead to a qualified opinion or a disclaimer (never adverse); a disagreement can only ever lead to a qualified opinion or an adverse opinion (never a disclaimer)."}],"src":"Chapter 11.3","sec":"11.3"},{"q":"State the eight basic elements of the auditor's report, in order.","marks":8,"a":[{"ol":["Title","Addressee","Opening/introductory paragraph","Scope paragraph","Opinion paragraph","Auditor's signature","Auditor's address","Date of the report"]}],"src":"Chapter 11.1","sec":"11.1"}]}},{"n":12,"t":"Audit Completion","brief":"How an audit is properly closed out: the final review of draft financial statements, completing field work, drafting the report, issues documentation and the completion checklist, the engagement partner's sign-off, debriefing, reporting to the board and management, considering reappointment, and the detailed \"true and fair view\" assessment framework — for the financial statements as a whole, for assets, for liabilities, and for the Statement of Profit or Loss. Closes with two other assurance engagements: forensic audit and due diligence.","outcomes":["Understand the procedures involved in disengaging from the audit","Understand the stages of the audit completion process","Understand when a true and fair view may or may not be given","Have a basic understanding of other assurance engagements"],"secs":[{"n":"12.1","t":"Audit completion procedures","b":[{"p":"The procedures for completing an audit in an orderly, proper manner fall under nine headings:"},{"ol":["final review of draft financial statements;","completion of field work;","drafting the audit report;","issues documentation;","audit completion checklist;","the engagement partner's sign-off;","debriefing;","reporting to the board and to management; and","consideration of the desirability of reappointment."]}]},{"n":"12.2","t":"Final review of draft financial statements","b":[{"key":"The auditor carries out a final review of the draft financial statements, alongside conclusions drawn from other evidence, to provide a reasonable basis for the audit opinion — reviewing all matters relating to the period to ensure proper presentation and disclosure."},{"p":"The final review covers:"},{"ol":["that all audit programmes were followed through in each transaction cycle;","that audit working papers were complete, with covering memos, properly referenced and cross-referenced;","that all audit queries raised were attended to;","that exceptional ratios from analytical review were investigated;","that the management letter contains all observations/issues not satisfactorily cleared;","that matters carried forward from the prior year have been reviewed and treated;","that comparative figures agree to prior-year financial statements;","that letter of representation contents were properly appraised and referenced;","that the entity's existence as a going concern is assessed; and","that all subsequent events are reviewed."]}]},{"n":"12.3","t":"Completion of field work","b":[{"key":"The engagement manager ensures all field work is complete and reviewed (except as flagged in Issues Documentation) before files go to the engagement partner for final review — satisfied that: adequate working paper review is evidenced in the file; and all review notes have been satisfactorily cleared and removed from the files, with any document of substance affecting the opinion transferred instead to the Issues Documentation."}]},{"n":"12.4","t":"Drafting the audit reports","b":[{"p":"The draft financial statements presented to the engagement partner for review should include the **draft audit report** — using the standard wording for an unqualified opinion, and ensuring all relevant circumstances have genuinely been taken into account before that wording is used."}]},{"n":"12.5","t":"Issues documentation","b":[{"key":"To assist the partner's efficient review and clearance of the audit, the team prepares **Issues Documentation** — bringing together all unresolved matters from various sections, aiding the partner's decision on the opinion's final wording. The engagement partner may evidence clearance of the opinion by **signing off** the issues documentation itself."}]},{"n":"12.6","t":"Audit completion checklist","b":[{"p":"A manual or electronic **audit completion checklist** helps ensure all relevant matters are attended to before the report is signed. When Issues Documentation is first presented to the engagement partner for **initial** clearance, some matters (e.g. the subsequent events review) are likely still incomplete — the audit manager goes through the checklist to ensure every aspect of the work is finished before presenting the Issues Documentation (with the checklist) for **final** clearance."}]},{"n":"12.7","t":"The engagement partner's sign-off","b":[{"p":"It is useful to record information on the signing of the financial statements' master copy — the audit file may also record the location/movement of draft and signed copies, the number needed for printing, and who copies should be sent to."},{"key":"The partner's sign-off **authorises acceptance for issuance** of the reports."}]},{"n":"12.8","t":"Debriefing","b":[{"def":{"t":"Debriefing","d":"a meeting held in the auditor's office, among the audit team, to share and discuss information on the success or failure of the audit just completed."}},{"p":"Objectives of debriefing at the audit's end:"},{"ol":["ensuring all matters usable in the report to the board/management are identified and included;","assisting the assignment, training and development of the audit team;","improving the efficiency and effectiveness of future audits; and","letting key players obtain important information leading to future performance improvement."]}]},{"n":"12.9","t":"Reporting to the board and management","b":[{"p":"Beyond the audit report on the financial statements, the auditor draws together conclusions and other matters appropriate for the report to the board, and other reports to management."},{"key":"For public limited companies, the auditor's report passes through the **Audit Committee**, which stands between the auditors and the Board of Directors — this is also known as the **domestic report**."}]},{"n":"12.10","t":"Consideration of the desirability of reappointment","b":[{"key":"The auditor considers whether the client's circumstances have changed such that continuing as auditor becomes undesirable — a decision needed **before** signifying willingness to be reappointed at the company's AGM. The auditor should be alert for conditions that would have caused **rejection** of the client, had they existed when the appointment was originally accepted. In case of doubt, the engagement partner should consult other partners."},{"p":"The auditor also obtains assurance that necessary subsequent events have been appropriately dealt with, covering the auditor's responsibilities for events occurring:"},{"ol":["between the period end and the date of the auditor's report;","between the date of the auditor's report and the issue of the financial statements; and","after the financial statements have been issued, but before they are laid before members at the AGM."]},{"note":"This three-way split is the same subsequent-events framework covered in full in Chapter 7 (§7.6) — repeated here because it forms part of the audit-completion checklist itself, not a new topic."}]},{"n":"12.11","t":"Truth and fairness assessment of financial statements","b":[{"key":"\"True and fair view\" is **central to auditing** — without it, the audit opinion would lack universal meaning."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Term","Meaning"],"rows":[["**True**","The financial statement contains no falsehoods — it is free from material misstatement, meaning transactions were correctly transferred to the financial statement."],["**Fair**","The financial statement presents the entity's situation and financial performance accurately and faithfully — impartial, unbiased, and representing the commercial substance of its transactions."]]}},{"key":"A true and fair view therefore means the financial statements are **truly prepared and fairly presented** per prescribed accounting standards — the auditor reports on the bookkeeping, the application of principles/policies, and an evaluation of management's decisions, all of which together affect the financial statements."},{"p":"Presenting a true and fair view requires compliance with: (a) generally accepted accounting practices, as prescribed by IFRS, applied **consistently** year to year; and (b) all relevant legislation."},{"h4":"§12.11.1 Where a true and fair view may not be given"},{"warn":"A true and fair view may **not** be given, for example, where:"},{"ol":["the figures do not fully/properly disclose their substance, or are misleading/ambiguous;","the financial statements are prepared on a basis inconsistent with previous periods, absent adequate disclosure;","the financial statements do not conform with accounting policies required by CAMA 2020, relevant accounting standards (e.g. IFRS), or those acceptable to the auditor; and","the figures' presentation is so complicated as to be difficult to interpret or understand."]},{"h4":"§12.11.2 True and fair presentation of the financial statements as a whole"},{"p":"To reach an opinion on true and fair view, the auditor should be satisfied that:"},{"ol":["all information materially affecting the view given is properly disclosed;","a proper balance is struck between completeness of disclosure and the summarisation needed for the statements to be clear and readily understandable;","the information ensures a reader's conclusions would be justified and consistent with the entity's business circumstances;","the statements fairly reflect the **commercial effect** of underlying transactions/balances — not merely their legal form; and","the presentation has not been unduly influenced by management's desire to present facts in an unreasonably favourable or unfavourable light."]},{"h4":"§12.11.3 True and fair presentation of assets"},{"p":"The auditor must be satisfied that assets:"},{"ol":["exist, and are the entity's property;","have not been charged/pledged (or, if so, that position is properly disclosed); and","are stated at amounts according with accounting policies required by CAMA 2020 (or other relevant legislation) and relevant Accounting Standards (e.g. IFRS), or otherwise acceptable to the auditor, and consistently applied."]},{"h4":"§12.11.4 True and fair presentation of liabilities"},{"p":"The auditor must ascertain that:"},{"ol":["all material liabilities at the Statement of Financial Position date are included;","all material contingent liabilities are referred to in the notes, unless the possibility of loss is remote or adequate provision has been made where losses are probable;","adequate provision has been made for known/expected losses; and","they are stated at amounts according with accounting policies required by CAMA 2020 (or other relevant legislation) and relevant Accounting Standards, or otherwise acceptable to the auditor, and consistently applied."]},{"h4":"§12.11.5 Results shown by the Statement of Profit or Loss and Other Comprehensive Income"},{"p":"The auditor should ensure the results:"},{"ol":["represent profit or loss properly attributable to the period; and","are stated at amounts according with accounting policies required by CAMA (or other relevant legislation) and relevant Accounting Standards, or otherwise acceptable to the auditor, and consistently applied."]},{"key":"Note the deliberate **parallel structure** across §12.11.3-12.11.5 — existence/inclusion, proper treatment of contingencies/charges, and consistent-policy valuation recur as the same underlying test applied to assets, liabilities, and results in turn."}]},{"n":"12.12","t":"Other assurance engagements","b":[{"h4":"Forensic audit"},{"p":"Current practice, and the growing number of court cases involving professional accountants, means accountants carrying out audit engagements may need to give evidence in court — requiring skills in reviewing/investigating matters and presenting evidence, and presenting financial information to substantiate a court position."},{"def":{"t":"Forensic audit","d":"the process of auditing and presenting reports in a manner admissible as evidence in court — requiring investigative skill and appropriate grounding in law and evidence presentation, integrating auditing and investigative skills to uncover fraud, embezzlement or other financial misconduct. Key objectives: detecting/preventing fraud, gathering evidence for court use, and assisting in resolving financial disputes."}},{"p":"Forensic engagements typically include: investigating financial fraud/misrepresentation; valuing businesses/assets in disputes; assessing economic damages; analysing bankruptcy/insolvency cases; and examining money laundering activities."},{"key":"A forensic auditor must: be **independent and objective**; collect/preserve evidence in a **legally acceptable** manner; maintain confidentiality; prepare detailed reports able to **withstand legal scrutiny**; and provide **expert witness testimony** if required."},{"p":"The forensic audit process, in five steps:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Step","What it involves"],"rows":[["**1. Planning and preparation**","Understand the client's needs and the nature of the suspected issue; define objectives, scope and methodology."],["**2. Evidence collection**","Obtain relevant documents (accounting records, emails, contracts); use interviews, surveillance and other investigative techniques."],["**3. Data analysis**","Analyse financial data for irregularities/fraud patterns; use forensic technology tools for data mining and reconstruction."],["**4. Reporting**","Document findings clearly and factually; prepare reports suitable for court submission."],["**5. Court proceedings**","Present findings as an expert witness; respond to cross-examination and scrutiny."]]}},{"p":"Forensic audit techniques: **data mining** (systematic analysis of large datasets for anomalies); **Benford's Law analysis** (detecting irregularities in numerical data); **link analysis** (mapping relationships between entities involved in fraud); and **forensic technology** (software tools for evidence gathering/analysis)."},{"key":"Evidence qualifying for forensic audit must be **properly documented and safeguarded** against tampering or loss."},{"p":"Forensic audit reports must: be comprehensive, accurate and unbiased; include scope, methodology, findings and conclusions; and be clear and understandable to non-accountants. To qualify for presentation in legal proceedings, forensic auditors must: adhere to applicable laws/regulations; follow professional codes of ethics; maintain objectivity and independence; and protect the confidentiality of sensitive information."},{"p":"Challenges in forensic audit: limited access to necessary information; the complexity of financial transactions; the evolving nature of fraud techniques; and maintaining neutrality under pressure."},{"note":"Forensic audit plays a crucial role in maintaining financial-system integrity and assisting legal proceedings — a forensic auditor's expertise bridges auditing principles and legal requirements, ensuring transparency, accountability and justice."},{"h4":"Due diligence"},{"p":"Investors acquire and dispose of investments from time to time, based on assessing recent, up-to-date information on the target entity's state of affairs — done by assigning other auditors to review the target's accounting and management structure."},{"def":{"t":"Due diligence","d":"the review and evaluation of a target entity that enables an investor to decide on a proposed investment."}},{"p":"Objectives of due diligence: determine the entity's strengths/weaknesses; ascertain its economic worth; evaluate the financial injection needed to achieve the planned investment's objective; and ascertain the investment's worthwhileness."},{"p":"The due diligence procedure: review the enterprise's financial statements; prepare and compare its ratios against industry and other standards; make adjustments to the financial statements; review management projections and expectations; make cash flow projections; project the business over a period; and advise on the investment's viability."}]},{"n":"12.13","t":"Chapter summary","b":[{"ol":["This chapter discussed the procedures involved in completing assurance engagements.","The scope of work involves the final review of financial statements — including drafting of reports, debriefing, reappointment, and assessing the truth and fairness of the financial statements.","Other assurance engagements include forensic audit and due diligence."]}]},{"n":"12.14","t":"Study text questions — multiple-choice and short-answer","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with 10 questions — 5 multiple-choice and 5 short-answer — reproduced below in full with the official answers, each explained. The Auditing study text sets **no examination-type questions**; this bank is the whole of its end-of-chapter material."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"The questions, with the official answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Which of the following procedures would be **least** effective in the audit of contingent liabilities? (A) Examining invoices for professional services; (B) Reviewing asset verification procedures; (C) Reviewing a bank confirmation letter; (D) Reviewing customer confirmation; (E) Reading the minutes of the board of directors.","Which of the following best defines a \"subsequent event\" for reporting purposes — an event that occurs subsequent to the (A) Date of the auditor’s report; (B) Statement of financial position date but prior to the date of the directors’ report; (C) Statement of financial position date; (D) Date of the auditor’s report, and concerns contingencies that are not reflected in the financial statements; (E) Statement of financial position date but prior to the date of the auditor’s report.","For which of the following will the auditor **not** accept responsibility in expressing an opinion on the financial statements? (A) Compliance with legal requirements; (B) The preparation of financial statements; (C) The appropriateness of the comparative figures included in the account; (D) The reliability of the opening balances; (E) The consistency of the application of accounting policies.","Which of the following is **NOT** a basic element of an audit report? (A) Director’s signature; (B) Title; (C) Date of the report; (D) Auditor’s signature; (E) Address.","Which of the following statements is **NOT** part of the financial statements? (A) The chairman’s statement; (B) Statement of Cash Flows; (C) Statement of Value Added; (D) Statement of Accounting Policies; (E) Statement of Financial Position.","To assist the engagement partner in the review of the financial statements and clearance of the audit, the audit team have to prepare ……… documentation.","That procedure used in ensuring that all relevant matters are attended to before the audit report is signed is called the ………","The meeting held in the auditor’s office with the engagement team at the conclusion of the field work is called the ………","In the course of his audit engagement, the professional accountant may be required to present evidence in court. What skills are required for this?","Mention any **TWO** of the circumstances addressed in the concept of \"true and fair view\" used in audit reporting."]}],"a":[{"ol":["**D** — reviewing customer confirmations. Debtors’ circularisation confirms amounts **owed to** the entity; it says nothing about contingent **liabilities**. The other four are standard sources: solicitors’ invoices reveal litigation, bank letters disclose guarantees and discounted bills, and board minutes record the decisions that create contingencies.","**E** — after the reporting date but **before the date of the auditor’s report**. That is the period in which the auditor has an **active duty** to perform procedures designed to identify such events. After the report is signed the duty becomes passive.","**B** — the preparation of the financial statements, which is the **directors’** responsibility. (Compare chapter 1 question 3, which makes the same point: this is the single most frequently repeated idea in the whole subject.)","**A** — the director’s signature. The directors sign the **financial statements**; the **auditor** signs the audit report on them. The basic elements are title, addressee, introductory paragraph, management’s and auditor’s responsibilities, scope, opinion, other reporting responsibilities, signature, date and address.","**A** — the chairman’s statement, for the reason given at chapter 2 question 2: it is published with the accounts but is not one of them, and is not audited.","**Issues** documentation — the schedule of matters arising that the partner must consider and clear before signing.","**Audit completion checklist.**","**Debriefing** meeting.","**Investigative skill** and **evidence presentation** skill.","**Any two of:** proper disclosure in the financial statements; compliance with relevant legislation; adherence to standards (e.g. IFRS); judgments and decisions being appropriate; proper books of account having been kept."]}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"This closing chapter is where the whole subject converges — the \"true and fair view\" assessment framework (§12.11) is the practical endpoint every earlier chapter has been building toward, so learn its parallel structure across assets/liabilities/results rather than memorising three unrelated checklists. Know the completion sequence in order (final review → field work completion → draft report → issues documentation → completion checklist → partner sign-off → debriefing → board/management reporting → reappointment consideration), and the true/fair definitional split (true = free of material misstatement; fair = commercial substance, impartially presented) as a pair the exam tests directly. For the two \"other assurance engagements,\" know forensic audit's 5-step process and due diligence's distinct investment-decision purpose — they serve genuinely different objectives (litigation support vs. investment appraisal) despite both being assurance work beyond the standard statutory audit.","errors":["Treating \"true\" and \"fair\" as synonyms — \"true\" specifically means free from material misstatement (factual accuracy); \"fair\" specifically means faithfully representing commercial substance without bias. A statement can, in principle, satisfy one test and still fail the other.","Assuming the audit completion checklist and Issues Documentation are the same thing — the checklist ensures every aspect of the WORK is finished; Issues Documentation collects UNRESOLVED matters specifically to guide the partner's wording of the opinion. They serve related but distinct purposes and are typically presented together only at final clearance.","Confusing forensic audit with a standard statutory audit — forensic audit's explicit, defining purpose is producing evidence ADMISSIBLE IN COURT, requiring legal grounding and expert witness readiness that an ordinary financial statement audit does not.","Treating due diligence as identical to a statutory audit performed on someone else's behalf — due diligence exists specifically to inform an INVESTMENT decision (ascertaining economic worth, needed financial injection, investment worthwhileness), not to express a true-and-fair opinion for shareholders.","Believing the engagement partner's sign-off happens before field work and review are fully complete — sign-off is explicitly the FINAL step, authorising acceptance for issuance only once the completion checklist and Issues Documentation have both been cleared."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Which of the following procedures would be LEAST effective in the audit of contingent liabilities?","o":["Examining invoices for professional services","Reviewing Assets verification procedures","Reviewing a bank confirmation letter","Reviewing customer confirmation","Reading the minutes of the board of directors"],"a":3,"w":"Customer (debtor) confirmations verify receivable balances, not contingent liabilities — the other four (legal/professional invoices, asset verification, bank confirmations, and board minutes) are all genuine sources of evidence about contingent liabilities, as covered in Chapter 7's contingencies procedures.","src":"Chapter 12","sec":"12.10"},{"q":"Which of the following best defines \"subsequent event\" for reporting purposes? An event that occurs subsequent to the","o":["Date of the auditor's report","Statement of financial position date but prior to the date of the directors' report","Statement of financial position date","Date of the auditor's report and concerns contingencies that are not reflected in the financial statements","Statement of financial position date but prior to the date of the auditor's report"],"a":4,"w":"A subsequent event is defined as occurring between the reporting date and the date the auditor's report is signed — consistent with the definition already established in Chapter 7.","src":"Chapter 12.10","sec":"12.10"},{"q":"For which of the following will the auditor NOT accept responsibility in expressing an opinion on the financial statements?","o":["Compliance with legal requirements","The preparation of financial statements","The appropriateness of the comparative figures included in the accounts","The reliability of the opening balances","The consistency of the application of accounting policies"],"a":1,"w":"Preparing the financial statements is management's responsibility, not the auditor's — a principle established repeatedly across this subject, from Chapter 1's §1.6.6 onward.","src":"Chapter 12","sec":"12.11"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a basic element of the audit report?","o":["Director's signature","Title","Date of the report","Auditor's signature","Address"],"a":0,"w":"The audit report is signed by the AUDITOR (in the engagement partner's personal name, with their FRC number), never by a director — directors have no signing role in the auditor's own report.","src":"Chapter 11.1","sec":"11.1"},{"q":"Which of the following statements is NOT part of the financial statements?","o":["The chairman's statement","Statement of Cash Flow","Statement of Value Added","Statement of Accounting Policies","Statement of Financial Position"],"a":0,"w":"The chairman's statement is a separate, non-statutory document, distinct from the Directors' Report and the other statutory financial statement components — consistent with the same point established in Chapter 2's treatment of CAMA's required financial statement components.","src":"Chapter 2.12","sec":"2.12"}],"theory":[{"q":"Distinguish between the meanings of \"true\" and \"fair\" in the expression \"true and fair view,\" and state what compliance is required for a financial statement to present a true and fair view.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"The financial statement is **true** when it contains no falsehoods — i.e. when it is free from material misstatement, meaning transactions were correctly transferred to the financial statement."},{"p":"The financial statement is **fair** when it presents the entity's situation and financial performance accurately and faithfully — impartial, unbiased, and representing the commercial substance (not merely the legal form) of the entity's transactions."},{"p":"A true and fair view requires compliance with: (a) generally accepted accounting practices, as prescribed by IFRS, applied consistently from year to year; and (b) all relevant legislation."}],"src":"Chapter 12.11","sec":"12.11"},{"q":"Distinguish between forensic audit and due diligence, stating the primary objective of each.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"**Forensic audit** is the process of auditing and presenting reports in a manner admissible as evidence in a court of law — integrating auditing and investigative skills to uncover fraud, embezzlement or other financial misconduct. Its primary objectives are detecting/preventing fraud, gathering court-usable evidence, and resolving financial disputes."},{"p":"**Due diligence** is the review and evaluation of a target entity, undertaken to enable an investor to decide on a proposed investment — determining the entity's strengths/weaknesses, its economic worth, the financial injection needed, and the investment's overall worthwhileness."},{"p":"The key distinction: forensic audit serves litigation/legal purposes; due diligence serves investment decision-making. Neither is a standard statutory audit expressing a true-and-fair opinion to shareholders."}],"src":"Chapter 12.12","sec":"12.12"}]}}]});
